self.registry={"en/about/overview.html":{"title":"What Theia is","content":"Theia does coding tasks with a team of AI agents. You describe the task in plain words. Theia writes the code in a copy of your repository, checks and reviews it, and puts the result on a separate git branch.\nThe full name of the platform is Aletheia. The command is theia.\nWhat Theia does\nRuns a coding task: theia run. The work happens in a copy of the repository; your current branch does not change.\nMakes a plan before the work: theia plan. You can read the plan, ask for changes and approve it.\nBuilds a code index — a short card for every file: theia index.\nKeeps project memory — facts and decisions: theia mem.\nAnswers questions from memory, the index and past runs: theia ask.\nConnects to Claude Code and Codex through hooks and an MCP server.\nRecords every step in an event log. The log is protected by a hash chain, so tampering shows.\nTheia has no model of its own. Models work through CLIs installed on your machine: Claude Code ( claude ) or Codex ( codex ). Theia calls them engines ( harness ). A settings file decides which engine and model work in each role. See Models for tasks.\nHow a run works\nA run is one execution of a task. It goes like this:\nTheia creates a run branch theia/run-…. The branch starts from the base: the upstream of your current branch (for example, origin/main ). Without an upstream it uses origin/HEAD, then your local HEAD.\nTheia makes a separate copy of the repository for each task.\nThe implementer changes files in the copy. Theia makes the commits, not the model.\nCheck ( verify ). If you passed commands with --verify, Theia runs them and the exit code decides. Without commands, a model checks the work. A failure sends the task back to the implementer. After two implementer attempts, a fresh implementer ( fresh_implementer ) with a clean context makes the third. The third failure in a row is stop_and_fix: the task goes to the lead.\nThe reviewer looks at the change: approves it, asks for rework or discards it.\nMerge ( merge ). Theia merges the task's work into the run branch with plain git.\nWhen a step fails, the arbiter decides: try again, or hand the task to the lead. The lead can restart the task with a new direction, accept the work, check it again, discard the task or ask you a question.\nEach step of a task is a phase. A role answers it with a verdict — a short word such as verify_pass or approve. A simplified map:\nThe track decides where a task starts:\nfast_path — the default. The task goes straight to the implementer.\nfull_spec — the planner works first, then the implementer.\nRoles Role Who does the work What it does\nlead\na model\nDecides what happens to a stuck task\nplanner\na model\nSplits work into tasks: the full_spec track and theia plan\nimplementer\na model\nChanges code in the repository copy\nfresh_implementer\na model\nThe same implementer with a clean context. Takes the task after two implementer attempts. In the output it is preceded by the mark!! anomaly fresh_perspective … dispatches=2\nverifier\nyour --verify commands, or a model\nChecks the result\nreviewer\na model\nApproves, asks for rework or discards\narbiter\na model\nDecides: continue or hand over to the lead\nmerger\nTheia itself (git)\nMerges the task's work into the run branch\nindexer\na model\nWrites the cards of the code index\nWhere the result goes\nThe run's work is on the branch theia/run-<6 characters>. Your current branch does not change.\nTake the result into your branch with git merge. How to do it, and why git switch to the run branch fails, is in Your first task.\nAt the end Theia prints a summary. halted: completed only means the work stopped. Tasks may still have been discarded. Check each task's outcome: merged means the work landed, discarded means it did not. If the run had anomalies, marks such as stop_and_fix, the line gets a tail ·!! 2 anomalies.\nCode index, memory and questions\nThe code index is a card per file: what it is for, what it relates to, which decisions it holds. A model writes the cards. Theia gives them to agents in every request as the blueprint.\nMemory holds short facts about the project: decisions, constraints, agreements. Facts are never overwritten: a new fact replaces an old one, and the old one stays in history.\ntheia ask answers a question from memory, the index and recent runs, and names its sources.\nSee Code index, memory and questions.\nMoney and budget\nEvery model request is a gateway call. Theia records the price of each call and adds them up.\nA run has a budget in US dollars. The default is 10. The --budget flag changes it; 0 removes the limit.\nWhen the budget runs out, the run pauses. You can continue it with a new budget.\nCommands that do not call a model cost nothing: for example theia show, theia replay, theia index status, theia mem add.\nWhere data lives\nThe store is the.theia folder. In a git repository it sits at the root of the working copy.\nWhat Where\nEvent log, plans, memory, index\n.theia/store.db\nProject settings\n.theia/config/\nTask copies and the run branch\n.theia/runs/\nCheck command output and other files\n.theia/objects/\nTheia adds.theia/ to.gitignore when a run starts. Where the store goes outside a git repository is described in Store and project roots.\nSee also\nSystems and run modes\nInstall\nYour first task\nGlossary","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/install/install.html":{"title":"Install","content":"How to get access to the releases, install Theia with one command, and check the install.\nAccess to releases\nTheia's files live in the private GitHub repository dokimelabs/theia-releases. You can download them only with a GitHub token ( PAT ). Your organization or your Theia vendor gives you the token.\nA suitable token:\nis a fine-grained GitHub token;\nhas access to the repository dokimelabs/theia-releases;\nhas read access to the repository contents ( Contents: read ).\nThe token expires. When it does, ask for a new one and save it with theia setup --pat <token>.\nYou need the token to install, update, and run another Theia version. You do not need it to work on tasks. Where Theia keeps the token and how to replace it is described in Keys and access.\nInstall with one command\ncurl -fsSL https://dokimelabs.github.io/get-theia/install.sh | sh\nThe installer asks for the token:\nGitHub PAT for dokimelabs/theia-releases (input hidden):\nPaste the token and press Enter. The characters are not shown. The question also works with curl … | sh: the installer reads the answer straight from the terminal.\nThe installer needs curl, tar, and sha256sum or shasum. macOS and common Linux distributions already have them.\nWhat the installer does\nDetects your system and CPU. On an unsupported system it stops and names the reason: see Platforms and run modes.\nTakes the token from the THEIA_PAT variable or asks for it.\nFinds the latest release. It never picks a pre-release build (suffix -pre.N ) on its own. Today the latest release is newer than 0.2.201: without a pinned version the installer installs a different version from the one this documentation describes. How to pin a version is described in A specific version.\nDownloads the archive for your system: 210–230 MB.\nChecks the SHA-256 checksum against the release's checksums.txt. If the list is missing or the checksum does not match, the install stops.\nUnpacks the archive and runs theia version. If the program does not start, the install stops and the previous version stays in place.\nPuts the version in ~/.local/share/theia/releases/<version>/ and creates the link ~/.local/bin/theia.\nCreates the link ~/.local/share/theia/vendor/current to the vendor files ( vendor ) from the archive, if that link does not exist yet.\nWrites the token to ~/.config/theia/config.yaml with mode 0600. If the file does not exist, it creates it. If the file exists but has neither a token nor a github: section, it appends the token. If the file already has a token, it keeps the old one. If there is a github: section without a token, it writes nothing and prints config exists with a github: section — run: theia setup --pat <token>.\nIf ~/.local/bin is not on your PATH, it prints NOTE: add …/.local/bin to PATH.\nIf the settings file has no OpenAI key ( apiKey ), it offers to run setup: run interactive setup now? [Y/n]. Any answer that does not start with n or N runs theia setup --interactive. Enter runs it too. The offer appears only when the installer has a terminal: in CI it does not.\nAt the end the installer prints done — try: theia version && theia doctor; then cd your project and run: theia.\nNote\nThe installer does not delete other versions. Each unpacked version takes about 900 MB.\nWhere the files go What Where\nThe program and its libraries\n~/.local/share/theia/releases/<version>/\nThe theia command\nThe link ~/.local/bin/theia to the program\nVendor files: prompts, rules, skills\n~/.local/share/theia/vendor/current\nGitHub token\n~/.config/theia/config.yaml, mode 0600\nCache: downloaded versions, embedding models, index cache, version check stamp\n~/.cache/theia — appears later, while Theia runs\nStores for folders outside git\n~/.local/share/theia/stores/ — appear later, on theia init outside git\nIf XDG_CONFIG_HOME is set, the settings file is $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/theia/config.yaml. On macOS Theia may keep settings, data and cache in ~/Library folders: see macOS: the settings folder can switch.\nInstaller variables Variable What it sets Default\nTHEIA_PAT\nThe GitHub token. The installer does not ask for it. Needed when there is no terminal\nAsked in the terminal\nTHEIA_VERSION\nThe exact version, with or without v. The version must exist in the release repository: see A specific version\nLatest release\nXDG_CONFIG_HOME\nThe folder that holds theia/config.yaml\n~/.config\nXDG_CACHE_HOME\nThe cache folder from which the installer deletes an old copy of the version it installs. On macOS Theia's cache may live in ~/Library/Caches/theia: the installer does not clean that folder\n~/.cache\nThe variable must reach sh, not curl. Put it after the |, as in A specific version.\nThe installer does not read THEIA_GH_TOKEN or GH_TOKEN.\nThe install folder is always ~/.local/share/theia/releases, even when XDG_DATA_HOME is set.\nInstall in CI\nCI has no terminal, so the installer cannot ask for the token. Pass the token from a CI secret in THEIA_PAT:\ncurl -fsSL https://dokimelabs.github.io/get-theia/install.sh | THEIA_PAT=<token> sh\n<token> is the GitHub token. Do not write it into script files: take it from your CI secret store.\nWithout a token and without a terminal the installer stops with:\nget-theia: error: no PAT — set THEIA_PAT or run interactively (the releases repo is private)\nA specific version\nBy default the latest release is installed. To install a specific version, set THEIA_VERSION:\ncurl -fsSL https://dokimelabs.github.io/get-theia/install.sh | THEIA_VERSION=<version> sh\n<version> is a version number from the release repository, with or without v.\nOld versions are removed from the release repository\nThe repository keeps only the two latest releases, the latest pre-release build and pinned versions. Release 0.2.201 has already been removed: on a fresh machine the command with THEIA_VERSION=0.2.201 fails with release v0.2.201 not found in dokimelabs/theia-releases (check the version and the PAT).\nWorkaround: first see which versions exist, then pin one of them. The list is on the releases page; you need access to the repository. Or use the GitHub CLI ( gh ), if it is logged in to GitHub with access to the repository:\ngh release list -R dokimelabs/theia-releases\nIf there is nothing to pin, install without THEIA_VERSION: the installer takes the latest release. It is newer than 0.2.201 and may behave differently from what is described here.\nA pre-release build installs only when you type the full version, with the -pre.N suffix. Such builds are not yet verified for real work.\nIf the version does not exist, the installer prints release v<version> not found in dokimelabs/theia-releases (check the version and the PAT).\nReinstall to recover\nIf Theia stopped working after an update, run the installer again. You can name a version that worked: see A specific version.\nThe installer:\nreplaces that version's folder completely;\nswitches the link ~/.local/bin/theia only if the new program starts;\ndoes not touch the settings file.\nA new token is not saved\nIf the settings file already has a token, the installer does not change it, even if you typed a different one. Save a new token with theia setup --pat <token>: see theia setup.\nPATH\nThe theia command lives in ~/.local/bin. Check that your shell finds it:\ncommand -v theia\nExample output:\n/Users/you/.local/bin/theia\nIf the command prints nothing, add the folder to PATH:\nmacOS (zsh)\nLinux (bash)\necho 'export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\"' >> ~/.zshrc\necho 'export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\"' >> ~/.bashrc\nThen open a new terminal window. Run the command once: each run appends another line to the file.\nCheck the install\ntheia version\nExample output:\n0.2.201\nThe link must point to the installed version:\nls -l ~/.local/bin/theia\nExample output:\nlrwxr-xr-x@ 1 you staff 56 Sep 13 17:55 /Users/you/.local/bin/theia -> /Users/you/.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia\nCheck the environment:\ntheia doctor\nThe line harnesses: FAIL means you are logged in to neither Claude Code nor Codex. Every line is explained in the theia doctor reference.\nNext: First setup.\nWindows\nVersion 0.2.201 has no files for Windows: see Platforms and run modes.\nSee also\nFirst setup\nUpdate and choose a version\nUninstall\nKeys and access","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/tui/overview.html":{"title":"TUI screens and elements","content":"Theia shows a full-screen text interface ( TUI ) in the terminal. This page walks through each screen, what its parts mean, and the glyphs and colours.\nThe keys are on the page Navigation and hotkeys. How to leave a screen without stopping a task is on the page Leave without stopping a task.\nNote\nThe examples come from test runs with a stand-in model named fake. It answers at once and changes no files. That is why the examples show fake, zero cost, and tasks that end as discarded. With a real engine you see its name (for example, codex ) and real costs. Screen frames were captured in a window 120 columns wide unless stated otherwise; empty lines inside a screen are removed. Some long rows are shortened.\nWhich screen opens Command Screen\ntheia with no command, in a set-up project\nHome screen\ntheia with no command, before the first setup\nSetup questions, one per line. See First setup.\ntheia run --task \"…\" in a terminal\nLive run tree. This is the default view of theia run.\ntheia run --task \"…\" --tui\nThe same live run tree.\ntheia show <runId> --tui\nStored run browser.\nA set-up project means two things: your user settings file exists, and the project has a Theia store.\nThe TUI needs a terminal for its output and for your keys. Without one (for example, when output goes to a file), Theia prints a notice and switches to plain lines:\nview: --tui needs an interactive terminal (a TTY for output and keys) — degrading to --plain\nWith --non-interactive, theia run never opens the TUI:\nview: --tui is interactive; --non-interactive degrades to --plain\nIf you pipe the task text into theia run, the TUI still opens and reads your keys from the terminal.\ntheia show <runId> without --tui prints a short summary instead of the TUI. The default view of theia run is set by the ui.defaultView key in policy.yaml; see policy.yaml settings.\nHome screen\nRun theia with no command in the project folder. The home screen shows setup problems, the state of the code index, your recent runs, and a box for a new task.\nExample:\ntheia — ~/projects/app harnesses: FAIL (neither codex nor claude is authenticated — one is required)\nembeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d) — FAIL (not fetched and autoDownload is off — theia setup --fetch-em…\ntheia: 0 indexed · 1 missing recent runs: ✔ run2akdqcdg2s223jpw4ncc54 completed $0.0000 task ▸ ▏ Enter run ·? help · q quit\nPart What it shows\ntheia — ~/projects/app\nThe current folder.\nProblem lines\nOnly the problems that theia doctor finds: warnings and failures. With no problems, this part is empty. Theia checks the engines before it draws the screen, so the screen can take a few seconds to appear.\ntheia: 0 indexed · 1 missing\nThe state of the code index. · N live is added when some runs have no end record. · N question waiting (or questions ) is added when runs wait for answers.\nrecent runs:\nUp to five recent runs, newest first: glyph, run id, outcome, cost. With no runs: recent runs: none yet.\ntask ▸ ▏\nThe box for a new task. ▏ is the text cursor.\nEnter run ·? help · q quit\nKey hints.? adds the full theia help text under the screen.\nThe glyph in front of a recent run:\nGlyph Outcome Meaning\n✔\ncompleted\nThe run has ended. This does not mean its work was merged: check the tasks with theia show <runId>.\n◆\npaused\nThe run is paused and can be resumed.\n⣽\nin flight\nThe run has no end record. It is running now, or its process was killed.\n·\nprobe, ask\nA one-off question, not a task run.\n✖\nanything else, for example abandoned\nThe run ended in another way.\nA fork shows forked· before its outcome, for example forked·completed.\nThe home screen has no colours and no minimum size. In a small window its lower lines are cut off, including the task box. The keys are in Home screen keys.\nEnter in the task box fails in 0.2.201\nIn release 0.2.201, typing a task and pressing Enter does not start a run. The home screen closes, and Theia prints:\nrun run2akdqcdzy6223oud53ns4c task tsk2akdqce2gw225ovuhksitw (fast_path)\nkernel: no vendored prompts — the composition root must call setVendorPrompts from the resolved vendor dir (reinstall, or set THEIA_VENDOR_DIR); prompts do not exist in code (THE-065)\nTheia exits with code 1. Setting THEIA_VENDOR_DIR, as the message suggests, does not help. The failed attempt leaves an unfinished run and a branch theia/run-….\nStart tasks with theia run --task \"…\" instead. To remove what the failed attempt left, follow Clean up after the task box.\nClean up after the task box\nRun these commands in the project folder. You need the run id from the error output, for example run2akdqcdzy6223oud53ns4c.\nClose the run as abandoned:\ntheia run --abandon <runId>\nExample output:\nabandoned run2akdqcdzy6223oud53ns4c — workspaces kept; reclaim with 'theia prune'\nRemove the hidden working copy of the run branch:\ngit worktree remove.theia/runs/<runBody>/trunk\n<runBody> is the run id without the leading run, for example 2akdqcdzy6223oud53ns4c.\nDelete the branch:\ngit branch -d theia/run-<suffix>\n<suffix> is the last six characters of the run id, for example 53ns4c.\nExample output:\nDeleted branch theia/run-53ns4c (was 236fdee).\nAfter that, theia show <runId> reports halted: abandoned, and the home screen no longer counts the run as live. More about closing runs: Control a run. More about run branches: Run results.\nLive run tree\ntheia run in a terminal opens the run tree. It shows the run, its tasks, and the steps of each task. The screen updates while the run works.\nExample: a run while its --verify command works:\n⣾ running · run run2akec5ebn2222agikedmpm · fast_path · $0.0000/$10.00 · 1 call · 7 events 5s\ntasks: 1 running · 0 done · anomalies 0 · idx ⚠1 [@ event 7/7 · live]\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ⣾ ▼ tsk…hb2ddoq5 [fast_path] verify r1 $0.0000 5.7s · ├╴smoke ✔ ├╴workspace @ ac69078 (copy) ✔ ├╴▶ r1 implement implementer → ready 1 evidence 0.1s ⣾ ╰╴r2 verify … 5.6s ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow:round • e err • m steer • p prompt • +/- verbosity=1 •? • q stop\nFrom top to bottom the screen has: the header (two lines), the tree, the detail pane (after Enter), live activity lines (while a model call works), and the bottom line.\nHeader\nThe first line holds the status, the run id, the track, money and counters. The time since the start is on the right.\nPart Meaning\n⣾ running\nThe run status: running; stopping (you asked to stop, the current step is finishing); paused; completed; or stopped (<reason>), for example stopped (abandoned).\nrun run2akec5ebn2222agikedmpm\nThe full run id. Use it with theia show and theia run --resume.\nfast_path\nThe track of the run.\n$0.0000/$10.00\nMoney spent and the budget. Without a budget it reads $0.0000 spent.\n1 call · 7 events\nModel calls so far, and records in the event log.\nThe second line holds task counts, warnings, and the position in the log.\nPart Meaning\n1 running · 0 done\nTasks that are working, and tasks whose work was merged.\nN failed, N frozen, N pending\nShown only when not zero: discarded tasks; stuck tasks that wait for the lead; tasks that wait for other tasks.\nanomalies 0\nWarnings that Theia raised about the run.\nctx ⚠N\nWarnings about a very large prompt.\nidx ⚠1\nCode index entries that were stale when the run started.\n[@ event 7/7 · live]\nYou see the newest state of a working run. In the browser and on the \"run ended\" screen it reads · head, or has no word after you step back in history.\nTree\nEach row is one item: the state glyph first, then the tree lines, the fold mark, the label, and facts on the right. What the glyphs mean is in Glyphs and colours.\nRow Example Meaning\nTask\n⣾ ▼ tsk…hb2ddoq5 [fast_path] … verify r1 $0.0000 5.7s\nShort task id and track. On the right: the current phase, rounds so far, cost, time. If the task waits for another task: blocked on tsk….\nTask text\n· ├╴smoke\nWhat the task asks for.\nNote\n✔ ├╴workspace @ ac69078 (copy)\nThe task works in a copy of the repository made from this commit. Other notes: context injected (N entries), pre-task gates: N run, M failed, note <name>.\nRound\n✔ ├╴▶ r1 implement implementer → ready … 1 evidence\nOne finished round: the phase, the role and its verdict. On the right: the number of evidence items.\nRunning round\n⣾ ╰╴r2 verify …\nThe step that works now.\nModel call\n✔ │ ├╴· gateway fake ok $0.0000\nOne model call inside a round: transport, result, cost.\nEvidence\n· │ ╰╴test_run 74d01a0c… — exit 0: sleep 40\nProof for a verdict, for example the result of a --verify command.\nTool\n$ <command>, ✎ <file>, ⚙ <tool> <arguments>\nA command, a file change or another tool inside a model call. These rows appear only with a real engine; they were not captured for this guide.\nSteering\n» steer [msg] please keep it small\nA note you sent with m.\nAnomaly\n‼ anomaly <kind>\nA warning. The same warning repeated in a row folds into one line with ×N.\nOther\ngateway calls (unattributed) ×N, changeset (N merges), forked from …, (no tasks yet)\nModel calls not tied to a task; the result of a run over several roots; the origin of a fork; a run with no tasks yet.\n▼ means the row is open, ▶ means it is closed. ├╴, ╰╴ and │ draw the tree.\nMerged tasks and all rounds start closed. A discarded task, a round with a failure or a question ( ✖, ◆, ‼ ), and any row that contains one start open.\nTimes on the right are dim for finished steps and bright yellow while a step works. Steps shorter than 0.05 s show no time.\nDetail pane\nPress Enter to open the details of the row under the cursor. The pane opens under the tree. The separator lines name the panes, and · focus marks the pane that gets the arrow keys.\nExample: the details of a task in a working run:\n⢿ running · run run2akec5ebn2222agikedmpm · fast_path · $0.0000/$10.00 · 1 call · 7 events 7s\ntasks: 1 running · 0 done · anomalies 0 · idx ⚠1 [@ event 7/7 · live]\n──┤ tree · focus ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ⢿ ▼ tsk…hb2ddoq5 [fast_path] verify r1 $0.0000 7.3s · ├╴smoke ✔ ├╴workspace @ ac69078 (copy) ✔ ├╴▶ r1 implement implementer → ready 1 evidence 0.1s ⢿ ╰╴r2 verify … 7.2s ──┤ detail ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\ntask 2akec5ec5k222ahb2ddoq5 desc: smoke track: fast_path root: null deps: [] baseSha: ac690782f0aa607b7eb03abaf11c72ae0b6f4e2d phase: verify costUsd: 0 phases: ready → implement ready → verify ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow:round • e err • m steer • p prompt • +/- verbosity=1 •? • q stop\nExample: the details of a round in the browser, in a window 100 columns wide:\n✔ completed · run run2akec5ebn2222agikedmpm · fast_path · $0.0000/$10.00 · 4 calls · 23 events\ntasks: 0 running · 0 done · 1 failed · anomalies 0 · idx ⚠1 [@ event 23/23 · head]\n──┤ tree · focus ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ✖ ▼ tsk…hb2ddoq5 [fast_path] discarded r6 $0.0000 40s · ├╴smoke ✔ ├╴workspace @ ac69078 (copy) ✔ ├╴▶ r1 implement implementer → ready 1 evidence 0.1s ✔ ├╴▶ r2 verify verifier → verify_pass 1 evidence 40s ✔ ├╴▶ r3 review reviewer → approve 1 evidence ✖ ├╴▼ r4 merge merger → merge_fail 1 evidence 0.1s · │ ╰╴command precheck:2akec5ec5k222ahb2ddoq5 — no commits on the task branch ✔ ├╴▶ r5 arbitrate arbiter → arb_escalate 1 evidence\n──┤ detail ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nverdict implementer → ready note: fake-green implementer evidence: command fake-green:implementer synthesized (simulator run) fork from here: theia fork run2akec5ebn2222agikedmpm --at evt2akec5ecng222ah4trvfl2 ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow • e err • [ ] seek • +/- verbosity=1 •? • q quit\nRow The details show\nTask\nThe id, text, track, dependencies, base commit, phase, cost, and phases:, the history of phase changes.\nRound\nverdict <role> → <result>, the note, the evidence list, and questions ( Q: ) if there were any. In the browser also fork from here: with a ready theia fork command.\nModel call\ngateway call and the stored fields of the call: kind, latencyMs, model, role, blobRef and others.\nEvidence\nevidence <kind>, ref: and note:. For a commit reference also inspect: git show <sha>.\nTool\ntool <name>, (FAILED) when it failed, and its fields.\nSteering\nsteering [msg] and the text. For answers to questions: Q: … → \"…\".\nAnomaly\nanomaly <kind>, repeated ×N and the fields.\nIn a small window the pane is only three lines high. To scroll it, see Detail pane keys.\nLive activity lines\nWhile a model call works, dim lines appear above the bottom line. They show who works, the last tool, the time, and the number of tools. When a call waits to retry after an error, the middle part names the wait and the reason. Format, taken from the source code (the stand-in model answers at once, so these lines were not captured):\n⋯ <role> tsk…<id> (<model>) <last tool or \"call in flight…\"> · <time> [<N> tools]\n⋯ <role> tsk…<id> (<model>) ↻ retry <N>/<M> in <seconds>s — <reason> · <time> [<N> tools]\nUnder the ⋯ … line, indented by two spaces, come up to seven earlier tools of that call, newest first (eight most recent together with the top line): $ <command> is a command, ✎ <file> a file change, ⚙ <tool> another tool. A failed tool has! after its glyph, for example $! <command>. The newest tool is on the top line and is not repeated below it:\n⋯ <role> tsk…<id> (<model>) $ <newest command> · <time> [3 tools] ✎ <changed file> $! <failed command>\n+ shows more of these lines and - fewer: 2, 6 or 10, but never more than a third of the screen.\nBottom line\nThe bottom line shows key hints. It lists only the keys that work now, and only the hints that fit the width. Example from a working run in a window 120 columns wide:\n↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow:round • e err • m steer • p prompt • +/- verbosity=1 •? • q stop\nThe full set is longer: between p prompt and +/- verbosity there is also w wrap • v view. When the whole line does not fit, Theia drops w wrap • v view first, then +/- verbosity; the keys themselves always work. In a working run at 120 columns w wrap • v view are already gone. The \"run ended\" screen and the browser have no m steer • p prompt, so at 120 columns they fit:\n↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow • e err • [ ] seek • w wrap • v view • +/- verbosity=1 •? • q quit\nf follow:round: the cursor follows new activity at the level of rounds. Without:round, the cursor stays where you put it.\nverbosity=1: how much of the tree is shown (0, 1 or 2).\n[ ] seek: seek through the history. Only on the \"run ended\" screen and in the browser.\nq stop: in a working run, q stops the run. On the \"run ended\" screen and in the browser the hint reads q quit.\nThe bottom line changes in two cases:\nWhile you type a steering note or an answer, it becomes the input line. See Steering input.\nAfter some keys it shows a one-time message until the next key, for example no call in flight or no stored body for this row.\n\"Run ended\" screen\nWhen the run ends, the tree stays on the screen so you can look around. A line with ■ appears above the key hints.\n✔ completed · run run2akec7tvvw223q2p7c2mn4 · fast_path · $0.0000/$10.00 · 4 calls · 21 events 37s\ntasks: 0 running · 0 done · 1 failed · anomalies 0 · idx ⚠1 [@ event 21/21 · head]\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ✖ ▼ tsk…3kuvboaw [fast_path] discarded r6 $0.0000 30s · ├╴input demo ✔ ├╴workspace @ e6acb06 (copy) ✔ ├╴▶ r1 implement implementer → ready 1 evidence 0.1s ✔ ├╴▶ r2 verify verifier → verify_pass 1 evidence 30s ✔ ├╴▶ r3 review reviewer → approve 1 evidence ✖ ├╴▼ r4 merge merger → merge_fail 1 evidence 0.1s · │ ╰╴command precheck:2akec7twgc223q3kuvboaw — no commits on the task branch ✔ ├╴▶ r5 arbitrate arbiter → arb_escalate 1 evidence ✔ ╰╴▶ r6 escalated lead → lead_discard 1 evidence ■ run ended — 1 discarded · browse the tree; q quits ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow • e err • [ ] seek • w wrap • v view • +/- verbosity=1 •? • q quit\n■ run ended — 1 discarded counts the tasks by their last phase. For a paused run the line also gives the resume command.\nThe tree, the detail pane, v and the other view keys work. m and p do nothing.\nThe keys [ and ] change only the event counter here; the tree does not go back. See Seek through the history.\nPress q or Ctrl+C to leave. Theia returns to the normal terminal screen and prints the summary.\nExample output after q:\ntsk2akec5ec5k222ahb2ddoq5 → discarded (6 rounds) smoke\nhalted: completed · 4 gateway calls · $0.0000\naudit chain: intact\nbudget remaining: 10\nrun branch theia/run-kedmpm — no merges landed (branch left at base for inspection)\nmaintenance: 0 summaries refreshed, 0 orphan generations GCed\nhalted: completed means the run has ended, not that the work landed (see halted ). The task line gives the result: discarded here.\naudit chain: intact means the audit chain of the run log is unbroken.\nThe work of merged tasks is on the run branch.\nThe exit code is 0 when every task was merged or the run is paused, otherwise 1.\nmaintenance: 0 summaries refreshed, 0 orphan generations GCed is the cleanup after the run: memory summaries are refreshed and old search generations are deleted. No model is called for it.\nThis screen does not appear if you pressed q or Ctrl+C while the run worked. Then the TUI closes as soon as the run pauses. See Leave without stopping a task.\nStored run browser\ntheia show <runId> --tui opens a saved run in the same tree. Use it to look at a run later and to step through its history.\ntheia show <runId> --tui\n<runId> is the run id, for example run2akec5ebn2222agikedmpm. Theia prints it when a run starts, and the home screen lists recent ones.\nExample:\n✔ completed · run run2akec5ebn2222agikedmpm · fast_path · $0.0000/$10.00 · 4 calls · 23 events\ntasks: 0 running · 0 done · 1 failed · anomalies 0 · idx ⚠1 [@ event 23/23 · head]\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ✖ ▼ tsk…hb2ddoq5 [fast_path] discarded r6 $0.0000 40s · ├╴smoke ✔ ├╴workspace @ ac69078 (copy) ✔ ├╴▶ r1 implement implementer → ready 1 evidence 0.1s ✔ ├╴▶ r2 verify verifier → verify_pass 1 evidence 40s ✔ ├╴▶ r3 review reviewer → approve 1 evidence ✖ ├╴▼ r4 merge merger → merge_fail 1 evidence 0.1s · │ ╰╴command precheck:2akec5ec5k222ahb2ddoq5 — no commits on the task branch ✔ ├╴▶ r5 arbitrate arbiter → arb_escalate 1 evidence ✔ ╰╴▶ r6 escalated lead → lead_discard 1 evidence » steer [msg] please keep it small ✔ note post_run_maintenance ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow • e err • [ ] seek • +/- verbosity=1 •? • q quit\nThe frame was captured in a window 100 columns wide, so the bottom line has no w wrap • v view hints; the keys still work.\nHow the browser differs from the live tree:\nIt has no time since the start, no activity lines, and no m or p.\nThe cursor starts on the first row with a problem.\n[ and ] step through the history, and the tree changes with them. See Seek through the history.\nq or Ctrl+C closes the browser with exit code 0.\nWarning\nThe browser reads the run once, when it opens. It does not follow a run that is still working: the screen stays the same until you close the browser and open it again.\nA run whose process was killed has no end record, so the browser shows it as running for good. Resume such a run or close it: see Runs left open.\nWhen you step back in history, the time of a step that was still working at that moment is counted up to the last record of the whole log. It can look much longer than the step really took.\nWithout a terminal, theia show <runId> --tui prints view: --tui needs a TTY — degrading to the summary and then the summary.\nSteering input\nIn a working run, press m to send a short note to the run ( steering ). The bottom line becomes an input line. Its label says who gets the note: the task under the cursor or the whole run.\nsteer → tsk…hb2ddoq5 ▸ please keep it small▏\nAfter Tab the keys go back to the tree, and the draft stays, dimmed:\nsteer → tsk…hb2ddoq5 ▸ please keep it small · ⇥ back to input\nAfter Enter the note is saved in the run log and appears in the tree:\n» steer [msg] please keep it small\nWith the cursor on a task or inside it, the label is steer → tsk…<id> ▸. Elsewhere it is steer → run ▸.\nThe note reaches the next step that starts for that task or run. A note to a finished task goes to the whole run.\nEsc cancels the note. An empty note is not sent.\nWhile the input line has the keys, q types a letter and Ctrl+C does nothing. See Steer a working run.\nOnly this screen delivers a note to a run started with theia run --task. Notes from another terminal do not reach it. See Watch from another terminal.\nClarify form\nNot checked live\nThis form is described from the source code. The test runs for this guide could not produce a question, so the form was not seen on screen.\nWhen a role asks clarifying questions during a working run, the detail pane becomes a form, and the bottom line reads answer ▸. The questions come one at a time:\nclarify — task …<id> (1/2)? <question> (<why the role asks>) type the answer below · Enter next (empty = skip) · Esc decline remaining\nType the answer and press Enter to go to the next question. An empty answer skips the question.\nAfter the last question the answers go to the run. Theia also saves them in project memory, so the same question is not asked again.\nEsc declines the remaining questions. If you answered none, the task stops as escalated ( ◆ ), the same as with --non-interactive.\nThe keys are in Answer questions.\nFull text and prompt viewers\nThe tree shows short labels. Two keys open the full stored text in your editor or in less:\nv opens the full text of the row under the cursor. For a model call it is the whole exchange with the model.\np opens the prompt of a model call that is still working. It works only in a working run.\nTheia writes the text to a temporary file and opens it with the program from $EDITOR. Without $EDITOR it uses less. When you close the program, the TUI comes back.\nExample: v on a model call row, shown in less:\n### system\nYou are the IMPLEMENTER. Execute the task in the working directory: make the changes, keep the diff minimal and surgical, run nothing destructive. …\n/var/folders/…/T/theia-blob-62a278c5331a.txt (END)\nWhen there is nothing to open, the bottom line says why:\nMessage Meaning\nno stored body for this row\nThis row has no stored text ( blob ).\nno call in flight\np: no model call works now.\nN calls in flight — cursor a task row to pick one\np: several calls work; put the cursor on a task first.\nno prompt blob for this call (blob store absent or content blocked)\np: the working call has no stored prompt: the blob store is off, or the content was blocked.\nbody withheld (<class> detected)\nThe text was not stored because it contained protected data (see data class ).\nblob absent (recorded elsewhere)\nThe text is not in this store.\nno $EDITOR or less available\nThere is no program to open the text.\nWarning\nv opens the text of model call rows. It does not open the output of a --verify command: on a test_run evidence row it says no stored body for this row, although Theia stored that output.\np was checked only when no call was working ( no call in flight ). Opening the prompt of a working call was not checked.\nTheia never deletes the temporary files. They keep full prompts and answers, which can include your code. See Delete the temporary files.\nHelp overlay\nPress? in the tree to see all keys and glyphs. Any key closes the help; that key does nothing else.\n↑↓ / j k move over visible nodes ← → / h l collapse / expand (← on a leaf: jump to parent) L / H expand / collapse every fold at once Enter zoom: open/close the detail viewport Tab cycle focus tree ⇄ detail (focused detail scrolls) g / G first / last node e hop to the next ✖/◆/‼ node f follow mode (cursor tracks newest activity) F cycle follow altitude: task → round → tool (F6) [ ] { } seek −1/+1/−10/+10 events (browse) Home/End 0 $ genesis / head (browse) w wrap the cursored row across lines (toggle) v view the full command/output body in $EDITOR (or less) + / - verbosity 0..2: condense to headers ⇄ inline tool detail m steer the live run (appends an audited user.note) p open the IN-FLIGHT call's prompt (pre-call blob, live) q / Ctrl+C browse: quit · live: graceful stop, twice = hard stop? this help glyphs: ✔ done-good ✖ failed ◆ frozen ⣽ active ◌ pending ‼ anomaly » steering · task text ▼▶ fold\nThe help does not scroll. In a window 24 lines high its last line is cut off.\nGlyphs and colours Glyph Meaning Colour\n⣾ ⣽ ⣻ ⢿ ⡿ ⣟ ⣯ ⣷\nWorking now; the glyph turns\nyellow\n●\nWorking now, with the animation turned off\nyellow\n✔\nDone without a failure: a merged task, a model call that worked, a round whose verdict is not a failure word\ngreen\n✖\nFailed: a discarded task; a round with discard, merge_fail, verify_fail, cant_do, algedonic or no_progress; a failed call or tool\nred\n◆\nStuck: the task waits for the lead ( escalated ), or a role asked questions\nyellow\n◌\nWaiting: for another task, or because the run does not work now\ndim\n‼\nA warning (anomaly)\nred\n»\nYour steering note\ngreen\n·\nTask text or an evidence row; the whole row is dim\ndim\n▼ / ▶\nThe row is open / closed\n—\n‖\nIn the header: stopping or paused\nyellow\nThe cursor row has a grey background. With NO_COLOR set, the cursor row starts with ❯ instead.\nTimes on the right are dim for finished steps and yellow while a step works.\nThe separator of the pane that has the keys is bold; the other separators are dim.\nA ✔ on a round is not success\nA round gets ✔ whenever its verdict is not a failure word. The example on this page shows ✔ r5 arbitrate arbiter → arb_escalate and ✔ r6 escalated lead → lead_discard, yet the task was discarded. Judge a task by its own row: discarded with ✖, or merged with ✔.\nTo turn off colours or the animation, see Colours, animation and fonts.\nOther views: --cli and --plain\nTwo views of theia run do not take over the screen:\n--plain prints one line per event. Use it for logs and scripts.\n--cli shows a short board that redraws in place: a line per task and a status line.\nExample of the --cli board:\nrun …zey6dqww $0.0000/$10.00 · 1 calls · 7 events tsk…zeuk275l fast_path verify r1 implementer→ready “fake-green implementer”\n— elapsed 0s\nIn 0.2.201 the --cli board overwrites the run id\nThe board leaves an extra copy of its first line on the screen and overwrites the line run <runId> task <taskId> that Theia prints at the start. The board shows only the end of the id, for example …zey6dqww, while theia show and theia run --resume need the full one. Workaround: the home screen ( theia with no command) lists full ids under recent runs:. If you need the id in a log, start the run with --plain: there the line run run… task … stays.\nMore about the views: theia run.\nSee also\nNavigation and hotkeys\nLeave without stopping a task\nYour first task\nControl a run\nGlossary","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/config/overview.html":{"title":"Settings files","content":"Theia has no settings screen and no theia config command. All settings live in YAML files. This page shows where the files are, which one wins, how a file is built and how to check what Theia really uses.\nWhere settings live What Where What is inside Who writes it\nProject settings\n.theia/config/*.yaml in the store\nManifests of any kind: routing, models, limits, rules\ntheia init creates policy.yaml and registry.yaml; you add more\nYour personal defaults\nconfig/*.yaml in your user folder\nManifests of kinds Policy, Pricing, TransportRegistry. They apply to every project\nyou\nKeys and personal options\nconfig.yaml in your user folder\nGitHub token, OpenAI key, update channel, dev root for --dev. Not a manifest\ntheia setup, the installer\nRole prompts\n.theia/prompts/\nText added to agent prompts\nyou\nRules for one root\n<root>/.theia/config/ and <root>/.theia/prompts/\nOnly Context manifests, run.preTask and gateway.harnessConfig\nyou\nIn a git repository the store is the.theia/ folder at the top of the working copy. Outside git it can live in your user folder. See Store and project roots.\nOnly files that end in.yaml or.yml are read. Other files in.theia/config/ are ignored.\ntheia init writes policy.yaml and registry.yaml only when they do not exist. Running init again never changes them.\nKeys are described in Keys and access, rules and prompts in Rules and prompts.\nYour user folder\nThe user folder depends on the operating system.\nmacOS\nLinux\nWindows\nTheia knows two folders:\nnative: ~/Library/Application Support/theia;\nold style: ~/.config/theia.\nTheia uses the native folder. The old-style folder wins only when it exists and the native folder does not. The installer creates ~/.config/theia. So on a Mac set up with the installer your settings are in ~/.config/theia.\nCheck which of the two folders exist:\nls -d ~/Library/Application\\ Support/theia ~/.config/theia\nExample output on a Mac set up with the installer:\nls: /Users/you/Library/Application Support/theia: No such file or directory\n/Users/you/.config/theia\n~/.config/theia.\n%APPDATA%\\dokimelabs\\theia. This path is taken from the source code and was not checked on Windows.\nWhen XDG_CONFIG_HOME is set, Theia uses $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/theia on every system. See Environment variables.\nThe user folder holds config.yaml (keys) and the config/ folder (your manifests).\ntheia help always names ~/.config/theia/config.yaml. On a Mac without that folder the real file is ~/Library/Application Support/theia/config.yaml.\nWarning\nIf your Mac uses ~/.config/theia, do not create ~/Library/Application Support/theia. As soon as that folder exists, Theia switches to it and no longer sees the old config.yaml with your GitHub token.\nWhich setting wins\nFrom the weakest to the strongest:\nBuilt-in defaults.\nYour manifests in config/*.yaml of the user folder.\nProject manifests in.theia/config/*.yaml. Files are read in file name order; a later file wins.\nThe.theia/config/ folder of a root — only for tasks in that root and only for a few keys. See Rules for one root.\nCommand line flags, for example theia run --budget.\nExample. The user folder has config/budget.yaml:\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: my-defaults } spec: run: maxUsdPerRun: 20\nNow every project gets a run budget of 20 US dollars. If a project sets maxUsdPerRun: 3 in.theia/config/, that project uses 3. The flag --budget 2 sets 2 for one run. The run prints the value it uses.\nExample output (the project file set 3):\nazcw6r3f run.created budget=$3 track=fast_path\nMerging goes only one level deep\nTheia merges files key by key inside each section: spec.run, spec.gateway, spec.ui, spec.maintenance, spec.memory. Anything deeper is not merged. It is replaced as a whole.\nA stronger file replaces the whole map\nEvery nested map and list is replaced as a whole:\nin spec.gateway: routing, models, harnessConfig, maskOverrides, embeddings;\nin spec.run: rulesSoT, mcpBelt, resources, jail, preTask, poolSizes.\nWhen a stronger scope sets one of them, all entries of that map from the weaker scope are lost. Nested fields you leave out take the built-in defaults, not the values of the weaker scope.\nTwo more examples of the same rule:\nThe user folder sets rulesSoT: { enabled: true, maskFiles: [NOTES.md] }, the project sets rulesSoT: { enabled: true }. In the task copy CLAUDE.md is masked (the default list) and NOTES.md is left alone.\nThe project file emb.yaml sets embeddings: { provider: openai } and policy.yaml next to it sets embeddings: { autoDownload: false }. policy.yaml wins (it comes later in file name order), and theia doctor shows local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2: the provider value is lost.\nExample. The user folder has config/routing.yaml:\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: my-routing } spec: gateway: routing: \"*\": claude implementer: claude-write fresh_implementer: claude-write\nThe project file.theia/config/policy.yaml only adds a route for the reviewer:\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: local } spec: gateway: routing: reviewer: codex-terra\nResult: only reviewer has a route. The \"*\" route from the user folder is gone, and the run cannot start the implementer:\nnkrv3s5f verdict nyryakoz implementer → no_progress (0 evidence) “gateway fatal: gateway: no route for role 'implementer' (se…”\nFix: write the whole map in the file that must win.\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: local } spec: gateway: routing: \"*\": claude implementer: claude-write fresh_implementer: claude-write reviewer: codex-terra\nManifest format\nEvery settings file except config.yaml is a manifest with four top-level keys:\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: my-defaults } spec: run: maxUsdPerRun: 20\napiVersion — always theia.dev/v1alpha1.\nkind — the type of the file, see the table.\nmetadata.name — any name. Theia does not use it.\nspec — the settings themselves.\nkind What it sets Where it is allowed Details\nPolicy\nrouting, models, limits, index and memory options\nuser folder, project, root (two keys)\npolicy.yaml settings\nTransportRegistry\ntransports that routing may use\nuser folder, project\nModels for tasks\nPricing\nmodel prices for cost accounting\nuser folder, project\nModels for tasks\nContext\nrules and texts added to prompts\nproject, root\nRules and prompts\nWorkspace\nseveral repositories in one store\nproject only, one file\nStore and project roots\nTheia reads files strictly: every key must be known, every value must have the right type.\nMistakes in settings files\nA broken file stops every command that reads the settings: theia run, theia plan, theia index status, theia mem search, theia ask and others. The command prints one config: … line and exits with code 1. There is one exception, see the warning below.\ntheia index status --brief hides settings errors\ntheia index status --brief is a status line for hooks and panels. It never fails: on any settings error it prints theia: no index and exits with code 0. It hides Workspace manifest errors the same way. Do not judge your settings by this command.\nHow to see the error:\ntheia index status without --brief prints the config: … line and exits with code 1;\ntheia doctor shows it in the embeddings line:\nembeddings: FAIL (policy unreadable: config: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.run: unrecognized_keys))\nHow to read invalid manifest (<path>: <code>): the path leads to the wrong place from the top of the file, and a number in the path is a list index counted from zero ( spec.roots.0 is the first root). The code names the kind of mistake.\nMistakes in a Policy manifest Message Cause Fix\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.run: unrecognized_keys)\nAn unknown key in spec.run, for example the typo maxUsdPerRn. The message names the section, not the key\nCheck every key of the section against policy.yaml settings\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec: unrecognized_keys)\nAn unknown section in spec\nOnly run, gateway, ui, maintenance, memory are allowed\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.run.sandboxTier: invalid_enum_value)\nA value that is not in the list, for example sandboxed\nUse one of the allowed values of the key\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.run.maxUsdPerRun: invalid_type)\nText instead of a number, for example \"ten\"\nWrite the number without quotes\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.run.maxUsdPerRun: too_small)\n0 or a negative number\nIn the file the budget must be above 0. For no limit use the flag theia run --budget 0\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.run.maxRoundsPerTask: too_big)\nA number above the upper bound (here 100)\nLower the value to the bound from the table\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.run.resources.memoryMax: invalid_string)\nWrong format, for example 3GB\nA number and one letter K, M, G or T: 3G, 512M\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.gateway.maskOverrides.phone: invalid_enum_value)\nAn unknown detector\nOnly pem_private_key, api_key, card_number, seed_phrase, email\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.gateway.embeddings.provider: invalid_enum_value)\nA provider that is not in the list\nOnly local or openai\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.gateway.harnessConfig.arbiter.model_reasoning_effort: invalid_union)\nA Codex setting whose value is a list or a map\nOnly text, a number or true / false\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (kind: invalid_union_discriminator)\nAn unknown kind, for example kind: Policies\nOne of the kinds from the table above\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (apiVersion: invalid_literal)\nA wrong schema version\nAlways apiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (metadata: invalid_type)\nmetadata is missing or is not a map\nAdd metadata: { name: local }\nMistakes in Workspace, Context and TransportRegistry manifests Message Cause Fix\nconfig: ws3.yaml: second Workspace manifest (one per workspace)\nTwo kind: Workspace files in.theia/config/\nKeep one\nconfig: ws2.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.roots.0.name: invalid_string)\nA root name that breaks the rules\nLower-case Latin letters, digits, _ and -, up to 40 characters, first character a letter or digit\nconfig: ws2.yaml: invalid manifest (spec: custom)\nTwo roots with the same name, or primary names a root that is not in the list\nMake the names different; put a name from roots into primary\nconfig: ctx.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.entries.0.name: invalid_string)\nA Context entry name that breaks the rules\nLower-case Latin letters, digits, _ and -, up to 64 characters\nconfig: ctx.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.entries.0.inject: custom)\ninject: {} with no condition\nGive roles, tracks or pathTriggers, or use inject: always\nconfig: ctx.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.entries.0.inject: invalid_union)\nA wrong condition, for example tracks: [slow]\nTracks are only fast_path and full_spec\nconfig: ctx.yaml: duplicate context entry 'x'\nTwo Context files with the same entry name\nRename one entry\nconfig: registry2.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.transports.0.dataClasses.0: invalid_enum_value)\nA data class that is not in the list\nOnly restricted and permitted\nMessages that carry a full file path are longer, so they are shown separately.\nBroken YAML (here an unclosed list in spec.run ):\nconfig: zz-bad.yaml: YAML parse failed: Flow sequence in block collection must be sufficiently indented and end with a ] at line 6, column 1:\nA Workspace or Context manifest in the user folder. These kinds are allowed only in the project's.theia/config/:\nconfig: <user folder>/config/ws.yaml: kind Workspace is workspace-scoped — move it to the workspace home's.theia/config/ (a user-scope Workspace would re-root every project)\nconfig: <user folder>/config/ctx.yaml: kind Context is workspace-scoped — move it to the workspace home's.theia/config/ (a user-scope Context entry's relative sources have no root to resolve against)\nA root path from the Workspace manifest does not exist:\ntheia: workspace root 'gone' does not exist: <path> (fix the Workspace manifest)\nMistakes in a root overlay are described in Rules for one root.\nWhat the checks do not catch\nRole names inside gateway.routing, gateway.models, gateway.harnessConfig and run.poolSizes are not checked. A misspelled role such as implementor is silently ignored, and the real role uses the \"*\" route. The values of gateway.harnessConfig are checked only by type.\nTool names in run.mcpBelt.tools are not checked when the file loads. The error appears when the run's MCP server starts. See policy.yaml settings.\nA missing file in a Context entry is found only when a run starts: context: entry 'gone' names missing file docs/NOPE.md.\nWhat a role really uses\nThere is no theia config command. theia doctor checks logins and keys, but it does not show routing or models. Use these ways.\nRead the files:.theia/config/policy.yaml and the config/ folder of your user folder. Keep the merge rule in mind.\nLook at the gateway lines of a run. Every model call prints one line: role, @, transport, result and cost. You see them live in --plain mode and later in theia show:\ntheia run --task \"<text>\" --plain\ntheia show <runId> --plain\n<text> is your task. <runId> is the run id that theia run prints.\nExample output. This test run sent every role to fake, a built-in test transport. In your runs you see claude, claude-write, codex-write and other transports:\nyzgujp5d gateway implementer@fake → ok $0.0000\nyknnslxu gateway reviewer@fake → ok $0.0000\nThe model is not printed in these lines. How to see it: Models for tasks.\nSee also\nModels for tasks\npolicy.yaml settings\nKeys and access\nStore and project roots\ntheia doctor","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/integrations/claude-code.html":{"title":"Claude Code","content":"This page connects Theia to Claude Code. After that, Claude sees your project memory at the start of every session, gets matching memory with each prompt, and can plan and start Theia runs through the /theia skill.\nWhat you get\nOne command, theia hooks install claude, sets up four things in the project folder:\nHooks — commands that Claude Code runs by itself at certain moments. They pass project memory to Claude and save the end of the chat before Claude Code compresses it.\nA status line at the bottom of Claude Code: how fresh the code map is and how many runs are working.\nThe Theia MCP server — a tool server that Claude calls. It gives Claude 22 tools: code map, memory, audit, runs and plans.\nThe /theia skill — instructions for Claude on how to plan, start and watch Theia runs together with you.\nEverything goes into the project folder. Your home folder and Claude Code's own settings do not change.\nBefore you start\nTheia is installed, and the project has a store ( theia init ). See First setup.\nClaude Code is installed.\nOpen a terminal in the folder that holds.theia/. The command does not look for a store in parent folders.\nInstall\nGo to the project folder — the one that holds.theia/.\nRun:\ntheia hooks install claude\nExample output:\ninstalled hooks: SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, PreCompact, Stop → ~/projects/app/.claude/settings.json\nstatusline wired: theia index status --brief\nai rules: none detected\nmcp server registered: theia serve → ~/projects/app/.mcp.json (control tools included)\n/theia skill installed → ~/projects/app/.claude/skills/theia/SKILL.md\nThe ai rules: … line lists AI rule files that Theia found, such as AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md.\nStart Claude Code in the same folder. See First start in Claude Code.\nRunning the command again is safe. It changes nothing and prints:\nhooks already installed (~/projects/app/.claude/settings.json unchanged)\nstatusline: existing statusLine preserved (not overwritten)\nai rules: none detected\nmcp server: existing theia entry preserved\n/theia skill: existing file preserved\nFrom a subfolder, the command stops with exit code 1:\ntheia hooks: no.theia/ in ~/projects/app/sub — run 'theia init' first\nExtra arguments are skipped without a message. For example, theia hooks install claude --trust is a normal install: the --trust flag works only for codex.\nAn invalid.mcp.json stops the install halfway\nIf.mcp.json is not valid JSON, the command first writes the hooks and the status line into.claude/settings.json. Then it stops with exit code 1 and does not install the skill:\ntheia hooks: ~/projects/app/.mcp.json is not valid JSON — fix it first (never clobbered)\nFix.mcp.json and run the command again. An invalid.claude/settings.json stops the command before any change.\nWhat changes in the project\nThe command writes exactly three files. It keeps what is already in them: your own hooks, your own status line and other MCP servers.\n.claude/settings.json\nFour hooks and a status line. The SessionStart hook and the status line look like this; the other three hooks have the same shape:\n{ \"hooks\": { \"SessionStart\": [ { \"hooks\": [ { \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"/Users/you/.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia mem context\", \"timeout\": 30 } ] } ] }, \"statusLine\": { \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"/Users/you/.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia index status --brief\" } }\nyou stands for your user name. On Linux the path starts with /home/you.\n.mcp.json\n{ \"mcpServers\": { \"theia\": { \"command\": \"/Users/you/.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia\", \"args\": [ \"serve\", \"--home\", \"/Users/you/projects/app\" ] } } }\nClaude Code starts this server for the project. It offers all 22 tools, run control included. The tool list is on the MCP server page.\n.claude/skills/theia/SKILL.md\nThe /theia skill, about 4 KB of text. It starts like this:\n---\nname: theia\ndescription: Plan, run, watch, steer and answer theia agent runs from this session (theia MCP control tools).\n---\nWhat the skill does: The /theia skill.\nNote\ntheia init writes a.gitignore that lists only.theia/. So.claude/ and.mcp.json show up as new files in git status. Both files hold absolute paths of your machine. On a colleague's machine these paths do not exist, so think before you commit them.\nHooks: what runs and when\nThe moments below come from the Claude Code documentation.\nClaude Code event When Claude Code runs it Command What Theia does\nSessionStart\na session starts\ntheia mem context\nPrints the first 40 lines of the project blueprint and the 8 newest memory facts, up to 160 characters each. Claude gets this text as context.\nUserPromptSubmit\nyou send a prompt\ntheia mem recall\nFinds up to 6 memory entries that match the prompt and passes them to Claude. Prompts shorter than 8 characters are skipped.\nPreCompact\nbefore Claude Code compresses the chat\ntheia mem checkpoint\nSaves the last 16 KB of the chat file to memory as a checkpoint.\nStop\nClaude finishes a response — after every response\ntheia mem checkpoint --final\nThe same save.\nWhat Claude gets at the start of a session (example):\n<theia-context>\nRecent project memory:\n- [constraint] Never call the billing service from the web frontend directly\n- [decision] Payments API always rounds totals to two decimals before tax\n</theia-context>\n(index freshness: theia: 0 indexed · 1 missing)\nWhat Claude gets with a prompt about rounding (example):\n<theia-memory>\n- [constraint] Never call the billing service from the web frontend directly\n- [decision] Payments API always rounds totals to two decimals before tax\n</theia-memory>\nGood to know:\nThe hooks never block Claude Code. If Theia fails, it writes the error to stderr and exits with code 0. This is the fail-open mode; the sign of an error is a line like theia mem context (fail-open): … in stderr.\nSaved chat text is masked: a card number is stored as [CARD NUMBER].\nEach hook takes about 0.1 s on a small project. Each has a 30-second limit.\nIf maintenance.indexPolicy is auto and the code map is out of date, the session-start hook also starts a background index build. That build calls models and spends money within maintenance.indexBuildBudgetUsd. The default value alert never spends. See policy.yaml.\nThe commands themselves: theia mem context, theia mem recall, theia mem checkpoint.\nMemory grows with every response\nThe Stop hook runs after every Claude response, not once per session. Each time it saves up to 16 KB of the chat as a new memory entry. Repeats are not removed, so project memory grows fast in long sessions. Saved chat pieces show up in theia mem search with ids that start with chk.\nIf you do not want this, delete the Stop entry from.claude/settings.json. The PreCompact hook still saves the chat before compression. Running theia hooks install claude again adds the Stop entry back.\nStatus line\nClaude Code shows the output of theia index status --brief at the bottom of the window. Example output:\ntheia: 0 indexed · 1 missing\nWhile a run is working:\ntheia: 0 indexed · 1 missing · 1 live\nN indexed and N missing describe the code map: files in it and files not yet in it. Other parts, such as stale, are explained in theia index status.\nN live — runs that are working now. N questions waiting — runs that wait for an answer.\nOutside a project with a store, the line is theia: no index.\nDetails:\nIf.claude/settings.json already has a statusLine, Theia keeps yours and prints statusline: existing statusLine preserved (not overwritten).\nClaude Code updates the status line after events in the session. Theia does not set a refresh timer, so the run count can lag while the session is idle.\nA run that was killed from outside still counts as live.\nFirst start in Claude Code\nStart claude in the project folder.\nClaude Code asks you to approve the project MCP server theia from.mcp.json. Until you approve it, Claude Code does not start it. You can see the state from the terminal:\nclaude mcp list\nExample output before approval:\nChecking MCP server health… theia: /Users/you/.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia serve --home /Users/you/projects/app - ⏸ Pending approval (run `claude` to approve)\nApprove the server. According to the Claude Code documentation, the /mcp command in a session shows the server state, and Claude sees the tools as mcp__theia__<tool>, for example mcp__theia__run_start.\nClaude Code applies its workspace trust rules to project hooks.\nThe /theia skill How to call it\nType /theia and your request in the Claude Code prompt. According to the Claude Code documentation, Claude can also use the skill on its own when a request fits it, and a new or edited skill works without a restart.\nThe skill only describes the order of work. The work itself goes through the Theia MCP server, so approve the server first.\nWhat it lets Claude do\nPlan. Claude sends the full task text to plan_create. Planning takes minutes. Then Claude shows you the tasks, their order, the check commands and the plan id.\nWait for your approval. Claude calls plan_approve only after you say yes. After any change to the plan, it shows the plan again and asks again. This rule lives in the skill text; the tool itself does not check it.\nStart a run with run_start: from an approved plan, or from a short task text. If the budget is above the default, Claude asks you first.\nWatch with run_wait in a loop. Claude reports merges, rework with its reasons, and warnings. It stays quiet during routine steps.\nPass questions on. When a run asks a clarifying question, Claude shows it to you word for word and sends your answer with run_answer.\nSteer. run_msg sends a steering note, run_probe asks a read-only question about the run, run_stop asks for a pause.\nSeveral runs. run_list gives an overview of all runs.\nHand work to Theia. Claude may offer to pass a multi-file change to Theia instead of editing files itself. It asks first, because runs spend your model budget.\nCheck the result. The result is a run branch theia/run-…. Claude reads it with git and its own tools. run_status shows why each task closed, run_diagnose looks for the cause of a failure, run_audit checks the audit chain.\nTools it uses\nplan_create, plan_show, plan_approve, run_start, run_wait, run_list, run_status, run_msg, run_answer, run_probe, run_stop, run_diagnose, run_audit. Their parameters are on the MCP server page.\nExample requests You type What Claude does, following the skill\n/theia plan: add CSV export to the orders page and the ticket text\nplan_create, shows the plan, asks for approval\nyes, run it\nplan_approve, then run_start with the plan id, then run_wait\nwhat's running?\nrun_list: runs, cost, questions waiting\nwhy was task 2 discarded?\nrun_status and its closedBy field\nreview what theia wrote\ngit log and git diff on the run branch, then its own review\nWhat works in 0.2.201 Step State\nStart a run ( run_start ) and get its id\nworks\nWatch ( run_wait ), overview ( run_list ), state ( run_status )\nworks\nNote ( run_msg ) and pause ( run_stop ) for a run started by run_start\ndoes not work\nQuestions from a run started by run_start reach Claude\ndoes not work\nNote and pause after theia run --resume … --interaction bus\nworks\nPlanning ( plan_create, plan_approve ), answers ( run_answer ), run_probe\nnot checked for this guide\nRun control does not reach new runs\nrun_msg and run_stop have no effect on a run that was started fresh. That includes every run started by run_start. The tools still reply reaches the live run at its next dispatch boundary and honored at the next dispatch boundary (in-flight work lands first), but the run ignores the note and does not pause. Questions from such a run never reach Claude.\nThe cause is in the 0.2.201 code: a fresh run does not connect the control bus that carries notes, stop requests and answers. Only theia run --resume connects it. The --interaction bus flag has no effect on a fresh run either. This is a bug of the version, see Limitations.\nControl works after the run is paused and continued with theia run --resume. See Steer a run from Claude.\nSteer a run from Claude\nThis order was checked end to end.\nStart the run: ask Claude, or run theia run yourself.\nPause the run once. Find its process:\npgrep -fl \"theia run\"\nExample output:\n76003 /Users/you/.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia run --task Pause probe --verify sleep 20 --non-interactive --plain\nSend an interrupt signal to that process. Replace <pid> with the number from the first column:\nkill -INT <pid>\nThe run finishes the step in progress and pauses. Its output ends with:\npaused — resume with: theia run --resume run2akdqkvcug222iaxzvmt6b\nFor a run that Claude started, this output goes to the log file.theia/control/run-<number>.log, and run_status shows \"tail\": \"paused\".\nContinue the run so that it listens to Claude. Use the id from the paused — resume with line and keep the terminal open:\ntheia run --resume <runId> --interaction bus --non-interactive --plain\nNow run_msg and run_stop reach the run. After run_stop, the run finishes the step in progress and pauses again; its log shows run.paused rounds=2 (resumable).\nWarning\nDo not pause with Ctrl+C in the terminal where the run works. Ctrl+C also stops a check command that is running at that moment, and Theia records a false check failure. kill -INT from another terminal sends the signal to Theia only. More on pauses: Control a run.\nAfter a Theia update\nThe hooks, the status line and.mcp.json store the path of one version: …/releases/0.2.201/theia. The theia command in your terminal is the link ~/.local/bin/theia, which the installer and theia self update point at the new version (see Update ). The files in your project keep the old path.\nWhat happens after an update:\nClaude Code keeps running the old version while its folder exists.\ntheia hooks install claude does not fix the path. It adds a second set of four hooks with the new path, and it keeps the old status line and the old.mcp.json entry. Both sets of hooks then run on every event.\nIf the old version's folder is deleted, the old hooks fail. Claude Code shows a hook error and goes on.\nTwo checked fixes. Choose one.\nOption A: point the files at ~/.local/bin/theia. Later updates then need no changes.\nmacOS\nLinux\nsed -i '' 's#/.local/share/theia/releases/[^/]*/theia#/.local/bin/theia#g'.claude/settings.json.mcp.json\nOpen.claude/settings.json and.mcp.json in an editor. Replace /.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia with /.local/bin/theia everywhere: in the four hooks, the status line and the server command.\nAfter option A, do not run theia hooks install claude again: it would add a second set of hooks with a version path.\nOption B: remove and install again. Remove Theia's entries by the steps in Undo, then run theia hooks install claude. The files get the path of the new version. Repeat this after each update.\nUndo\nThere is no theia hooks uninstall command. Remove the integration by hand.\nIn.claude/settings.json, delete the four hook entries whose command ends with theia mem context, theia mem recall, theia mem checkpoint and theia mem checkpoint --final. Delete statusLine if its command ends with theia index status --brief. Keep your own entries.\nIn.mcp.json, delete the theia entry under mcpServers. If no other servers are left, delete the file:\nrm.mcp.json\nDelete the skill:\nrm -rf.claude/skills/theia\nCheck that nothing is left. The command prints nothing when the file is clean:\ngrep -n \"theia\".claude/settings.json\nClaude Code keeps its own record that you approved the server. Its documentation describes the claude mcp reset-project-choices command for that.\nMemory saved by the hooks stays in the store. To remove Theia completely, see Uninstall.\nWhat does not exist\nNo theia hooks uninstall command.\nNo pull request creation and no push. A run leaves a local branch; you merge it or open a pull request yourself.\nNo Slack, webhook or Jira integration. Azure DevOps exists only as a text file among the shipped files, not as an integration.\nTHEIA_SKILL, THEIA_MCP_TOOLS and THEIA_CONTROL_TOOLS are names inside Theia's code, not environment variables. Setting them changes nothing. To change the skill, edit.claude/skills/theia/SKILL.md: Theia never overwrites this file.\nSee also\nMCP server\nCodex\ntheia hooks install claude\nControl a run\nCode index, memory and questions","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/commands.html":{"title":"All commands","content":"A short summary of every theia 0.2.201 command: what it does, whether it spends money on models, and where to read more. This page also covers global flags, exit codes and the general argument rules.\nHow a call is built\ntheia [global flags] <command> [subcommand] [arguments]\ntheia help prints the help text and exits with code 0. There is no per-command help: theia help run prints the same general help.\nAn unknown command prints the same help and exits with code 1.\ntheia with no command, in a terminal, opens the first-run wizard or the home screen. Without a terminal, for example in a script, it prints the help.\nGlobal flags may appear anywhere on the line, not only before the command. Theia strips them before it parses the command.\nCommands\nThe \"Models\" column shows whether a command calls a model. Such calls cost money or use up your subscription limits.\nCommand What it does Models Details\ntheia init\nCreates the.theia store for a project\nno\ninit\ntheia setup\nWrites user settings: GitHub token, OpenAI key, update channel\nno\nsetup\ntheia self update\nUpdates Theia to the newest version of its channel\nno\nself update\ntheia version\nPrints the version\nno\nversion\ntheia doctor\nChecks engines, keys, embeddings and the store\nno\ndoctor\ntheia run\nRuns a task, continues ( --resume ) or closes ( --abandon ) a run\nyes\nrun\ntheia plan\nMakes a plan and manages it\nonly plan --task\nplan\ntheia msg\nSends guidance to a run\nno\nmsg\ntheia answer\nLists a run's questions and answers them\nno\nanswer\ntheia probe\nAsks a question about a run without changing it\nyes\nprobe\ntheia show\nShows a stored run\nno\nshow\ntheia replay\nRe-reads a run's log and checks the audit chain\nno\nreplay\ntheia diagnose\nNames the likely cause of a failure from a rule table\nno\ndiagnose\ntheia compare\nCompares two runs\nno\ncompare\ntheia fork\nBranches a run off at a chosen step\nno, until the fork is continued\nfork\ntheia audit verify\nChecks the hash chains of the event log\nno\naudit verify\ntheia prune\nDeletes the data of finished runs\nno\nprune\ntheia index\nBuilds the code index and searches it\nindex build\nindex\ntheia mem\nWorks with project memory\nonly mem maintain --llm\nmem\ntheia ask\nAnswers a question from memory, the index and runs\nyes, when records were found\nask\ntheia hooks install\nConnects Theia to Claude Code, Codex or git\nno\nhooks\ntheia serve\nStarts an MCP server for other tools\ndepends on the tool called\nserve\ntheia jail\nRuns a command in a sandbox, Linux only\nno\njail\ntheia eval\nRuns platform benchmarks\ndepends on the subcommand\neval\nNote\nEmbeddings (number vectors that capture meaning) for search are computed locally by default and cost nothing. If the settings choose the openai provider, index reindex and mem reindex pay for OpenAI embeddings. See Code index, memory and questions.\nGlobal flags Flag What it does\n--x-release <version>\nRuns the given Theia version instead of the installed one. --x-release=<version> works too. Theia looks for the version in its cache and downloads it when a GitHub token is set. If the version cannot be obtained, the command fails; it never falls back to the current version.\n--dev [path]\nFor platform developers only. Runs Theia from a source tree: the given path, or dev.root in ~/.config/theia/config.yaml (written by theia setup --dev-root <path> ). --dev=<path> works too.\nThe THEIA_X_RELEASE environment variable works like the --x-release flag. theia --version and theia -V do the same as theia version.\n--dev without a path and without dev.root in the settings stops the command with exit code 1. It does not print the help:\n--dev: no development root — pass a path (theia --dev <path> …) or set dev.root in ~/.config/theia/config.yaml (theia setup --dev-root <path>)\nWarning\n--x-release does not see versions placed by the installer and downloads them again. One unpacked version takes about 900 MB.\nA wrong THEIA_X_RELEASE value breaks every command, even theia version. To fix it, remove the variable: unset THEIA_X_RELEASE.\nSee Update and pick a version.\nExit codes Code Meaning\n0\nThe command succeeded. For theia run it also means a pause: after the first Ctrl+C, when the budget ran out, or when a loop guard fired\n1\nAn error or refusal: wrong arguments, no store found, a run's task was discarded ( discarded ), theia doctor found a problem\n130\ntheia run: hard stop with a second Ctrl+C\n143\ntheia run: the process was killed with SIGTERM, for example by kill. Theia does not catch this signal. The run stays open and can be continued with --resume\nFor some commands code 1 has its own meaning:\ntheia replay — the audit chain of the run is broken.\ntheia audit verify — at least one checked log is broken.\ntheia index status — the index is out of date.\nCommand pages describe these cases.\nGeneral rules\nA flag that takes a value uses the next word: --budget 5.\nEvery command skips unknown flags without a warning. A typo such as --budjet 3 keeps the default value. The run's budget is visible in the run.created line at the start of the output. There are two exceptions: theia msg and theia probe put an unknown flag into the text of the note or question, so theia msg <runId> --foo hello records the note --foo hello. In theia answer, every word without -- after --question <id> counts as an answer.\nTheia validates only the values of run --track, run --interaction, prune --older-than, prune --keep and ids. Other values are taken as they are: --budget abc removes the money limit, see --budget.\nIds start with a prefix: run — run, task — tsk, plan — pln, memory fact — fct. After the prefix come 22 characters. Commands accept the full id or those 22 characters without the prefix.\nOnly show --task, msg --task, fork --at and answer --question accept a short tail of an id.\nThere is no \"list runs\" command. A run id appears in the run run… task tsk… line at the start of a run, in the resume with: … hint on pause, and on the home screen (last 5 runs). theia prune --keep 0 --dry-run prints the full ids of every finished run and deletes nothing. theia audit verify prints ids without run, mixed with the logs of plans and probes. theia show of a fork names the parent in the forked from line, and theia fork names the new run in the drive it: line. See How to find the run id.\n@path in the text of run, plan, msg and probe inserts the file's content into the request. See the glossary: @path inclusion. ask does not support it.\nSee also\nWhat Theia is\nTroubleshooting\nGlossary","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/guides/first-task.html":{"title":"Your first task","content":"This page walks you through one task from start to finish. You create the store, check the setup, run a task with a check, read the result and take it into your own branch.\nReal runs cost money\nA run calls real models through your Claude Code or Codex login. Each run has a budget: $10, unless you set --budget or run.maxUsdPerRun in policy.yaml. When the budget runs out, the run pauses. See When the budget runs out.\nWhat you need\nTheia is installed, and theia version prints 0.2.201. See Install.\nA git repository with at least one commit.\nA logged-in harness, the program that talks to the model: Claude Code ( claude ) or Codex ( codex ). See Keys and access.\nA command that proves the task is done, for example your test suite.\nTheia does not change your checkout. It works in copies of the repository and on a separate branch.\nStep 1. Create the store\nGo to the root of the repository and run:\ntheia init\nTheia creates the store: the folder.theia/ in the root of the repository. It adds.theia/ to.gitignore and writes the settings to.theia/config/policy.yaml. The first line of the output says which harness theia chose. Example output without that line:\nstore: ~/projects/app/.theia (worktree root)\ninitialized ~/projects/app/.theia (harness: codex — switch any time: gateway.routing in.theia/config/policy.yaml)\nai rules: none detected\n.gitignore now shows up in git status. Commit it together with your next change. More about the command: theia init.\nStep 2. Check the setup\nRun the check from the same folder. theia doctor looks only at.theia in the current folder.\ntheia doctor\nAt least one harness line must say ok. Example output, shortened:\ncodex subscription: unavailable (harness-codex: not authenticated (codex login status: exit 1))\nclaude code: ok\nlexical index: ok (tantivy binding loads).theia store: ok\nvendor deliverables: ok — 9ff60d108678 verified (21 files); matches this release\nIf neither harness is logged in, the line harnesses says FAIL (neither codex nor claude is authenticated — one is required), and the exit code is 1. Log in to Claude Code or Codex first. All lines: theia doctor.\nStep 3. Build the code index (optional)\nTheia gives the roles a map of your code, the intent index. A run works without it, but prints a warning at the start:\nindex: 1 stale — plan quality degrades; run 'theia index build' to refresh\nTo see what is missing, run this command. It makes no model calls.\ntheia index status\nExample output for a new store:\nentries: 0 (0 current)\nmissing (1): hello.js\nrun 'theia index build' to refresh\ntheia index build creates the map. It calls a model once for each new or changed file, so it costs money. See Code index, memory and questions and theia index build.\nStep 4. Run the task\nGive the task in plain words, a command that checks the result, and the mode: code right away or plan first.\ntheia run --task \"<text>\" --verify \"<command>\" [--track fast_path|full_spec]\n--task is the brief: what to do. Without the flag Theia reads the text from standard input when that is not a terminal: theia run < task.md. With no text in either place it prints the syntax hint and exits with code 1.\n--verify is a shell command, your definition of done. Theia runs it after each attempt, in the task's copy of the repository. Exit code 0 means the check passed; any other code sends the task back for another attempt. You can give --verify several times; the commands run in order. Without the flag a model judges the result. Why you need it and what to pass: Why --verify matters.\n--track is the mode, or track: where the task starts. fast_path sends the task straight to the implementer; it is the default. full_spec runs the planner first, and it may split the task into subtasks. Theia rejects any other value: run: unknown track 'foo' (supported: full_spec, fast_path), exit code 1. Which one to choose: Choosing the track.\nA small, clear task takes the short track. You can leave --track out; fast_path applies on its own:\ntheia run --task \"Add a greeting.txt file with the text hello\" --verify \"test -f greeting.txt\" --track fast_path\nA task that spans several files or is worded in general terms goes with planning:\ntheia run --task \"Add a greeting.txt file with the text hello\" --verify \"test -f greeting.txt\" --track full_spec\nThe rest of this page follows the short track.\nWhat theia does:\nIt creates the run branch theia/run-<6 characters> from the base commit, see The base.\nIt copies the repository into a workspace under.theia/runs/.\nThe implementer changes files in the copy, and theia commits the changes.\nThe verifier runs your --verify command.\nThe reviewer looks at the change.\nTheia merges the task into the run branch.\nIf a step fails, the task goes back for another attempt, or to the arbiter and the lead. How the roles work together: What Theia is. All options: theia run.\nWhy --verify matters and what to pass\n--verify is your definition of done. Theia counts an attempt as successful only when the command exits with code 0. Without it a model, the verifier, judges the result: it reads the change and answers pass or fail on its own. That is one more model call, and it is an opinion, not a check.\nWhen you need --verify:\nthe result has a checkable sign: tests pass, the project builds, a file exists, the linter is quiet;\nyou want the task to count as done only when that sign holds.\nWhat to pass:\nthe project's test command: npm test, pytest, go test./...;\na build or a type check: npm run build, tsc --noEmit;\na simple file check, as in the example above: test -f greeting.txt;\nseveral commands in order: --verify \"npm run lint\" --verify \"npm test\". After the first failure the rest are skipped.\nWhen you can do without it: the task has no checkable result, for example rewriting a text. Then the model checks.\nHow it works:\nthe command runs through bash -lc in the task's copy of the repository. The copy includes untracked files such as node_modules and.env, so tests run the same way as for you;\neach command gets 5 minutes. It runs on your machine with no sandbox. Do not pass commands that change anything outside the project;\na failure sends the task back to the implementer for another attempt. After two implementer attempts, a fresh implementer ( fresh_implementer ) makes the third: the same role with a clean context. After three failures in a row stop_and_fix fires: the task goes to the lead, who may discard it.\nExample output with --plain when the check fails three times in a row. Model call lines are removed:\nanqfe5n7 task.phase boxcavfh ready → verify\nar76o37h verdict boxcavfh verifier → verify_fail (1 evidence) “$ test -f greeting.txt → exit 1 (no output)”\naiuukpwb task.phase boxcavfh verify_fail → implement\napvoujmf task.phase boxcavfh ready → verify\na7zuqbcp verdict boxcavfh verifier → verify_fail (1 evidence) “$ test -f greeting.txt → exit 1 (no output)”\nbx27ia7m task.phase boxcavfh verify_fail → implement\nannvmhhk!! anomaly fresh_perspective taskId=2akec5u34o222lboxcavfh dispatches=2\na63ykvyi verdict boxcavfh fresh_implementer → ready (1 evidence) “fake-green fresh_implementer”\nbb72z2rm task.phase boxcavfh ready → verify\nbkhqmm5w verdict boxcavfh verifier → verify_fail (1 evidence) “$ test -f greeting.txt → exit 1 (no output)”\nbhzhceq7!! anomaly stop_and_fix taskId=2akec5u34o222lboxcavfh fails=3\nba5xeri6 task.phase boxcavfh stop_and_fix → escalated\nbzg6ojuk verdict boxcavfh lead → lead_discard (1 evidence) “fake-green lead”\navgop7oy task.phase boxcavfh lead_discard → discarded\nverify_fail … “$ test -f greeting.txt → exit 1 (no output)” shows the command, its exit code and the start of its output. The task goes back to implement.\n!! anomaly fresh_perspective … dispatches=2 is an anomaly, a mark that the approach changes: fresh_implementer makes the third attempt.\n!! anomaly stop_and_fix … fails=3 and stop_and_fix → escalated mark the third failure in a row; the task is with the lead. Here the lead discarded it: lead_discard → discarded.\nWithout --verify the same spot shows a model call gateway verifier@… → ok and its verdict verifier → verify_pass.\nMore: --verify, verify, Sandbox.\nChoosing the track: --track fast_path or --track full_spec\n--track sets the phase a task starts in. The default is fast_path.\nTrack What happens When to choose it\nfast_path\nThe task goes straight to the implementer.\nA small, clear change: one file or one function, and the brief leaves no questions.\nfull_spec\nThe planner works the task through first. It may split it into subtasks or ask you a question. Then the implementer works.\nThe task touches several files or modules, changes a contract, a data schema or authorization, or is worded in general terms.\nExample:\ntheia run --task \"Add a greeting.txt file with the text hello\" --verify \"test -f greeting.txt\" --track full_spec\nThe track shows in the output: run.created … track=full_spec, [full_spec] in the task line, and the task's first step is start → plan:\njimv4iti run.created budget=$10 track=full_spec\njciukom6 task.created j4h6tge6 [full_spec] Add a greeting.txt file with the text hello\njf2x6o6g task.phase j4h6tge6 start → plan\njzz3chzl verdict j4h6tge6 planner → ready (1 evidence) “fake-green planner”\nihdtcnzk task.phase j4h6tge6 ready → implement\nfull_spec costs at least one extra planner call, and noticeably more if the planner splits the task into subtasks.\nThe planner may ask a clarifying question. On a terminal you answer in place. With --non-interactive or without a terminal nobody can answer, and the task freezes. See Answer a question.\nNo plan approval happens here: Theia executes the plan right away. To read and approve a plan before spending money on implementation, use Plan before you run.\nMore: --track, fast_path, full_spec.\nStep 5. Watch the run\nOn a terminal theia run opens the TUI: a live tree of tasks, rounds and events. See Screens and elements. When the run ends, the screen stays so you can look around. Press q to leave. Theia then prints the closing lines from step 6.\nWithout a terminal, for example in CI, or with --plain, theia prints one line per event:\ntheia run --task \"Add a greeting.txt file with the text hello\" --verify \"test -f greeting.txt\" --plain\nExample output, shortened. Model call lines and progress lines are removed.\nrun branch: theia/run-4lejtj (base HEAD (no origin remote) @ 0de439e)\nxlgyzyuh run.created budget=$10 track=fast_path\nxbdpahtl task.created xsd2vfzc [fast_path] Add a greeting.txt file with the text hello\nw6ifinhv task.phase xsd2vfzc ready → implement\nrun run2akdqiyvvs222jx34lejtj task tsk2akdqiyyco222jxsd2vfzc (fast_path)\nwsrf7wre task.phase xsd2vfzc ready → verify\nwc32sshz verdict xsd2vfzc verifier → verify_pass (1 evidence)\nw6rtwvwr task.phase xsd2vfzc verify_pass → review\nw53cac3p task.phase xsd2vfzc approve → merge\nx6gw5prf verdict xsd2vfzc merger → merged (1 evidence)\nxkooar53 task.phase xsd2vfzc merged → merged\nxfarrzzv run.finished halted=completed rounds=4\nrun run2akdq… task tsk2akdq… shows the run id and the task id. Other commands need the run id.\ntask.phase … ready → implement means the task moved to a new phase.\nverdict … verifier → verify_pass is the verdict of a role.\nEach model call adds a line gateway <role>@<transport> → ok $<cost>. Verdicts of models end with a short note from the model.\nLines with live show progress. They are not saved.\nStep 6. Read the closing lines\nExample, same run:\ntsk2akdqiyyco222jxsd2vfzc → merged (4 rounds) Add a greeting.txt file with the text hello\nhalted: completed · 2 gateway calls · $0.0000\naudit chain: intact\nbudget remaining: 10\nrun branch theia/run-4lejtj @ 8d6868e — 2 merges ahead of base; review/PR from it (your checkout was not touched)\nLine Meaning\ntsk… → merged (4 rounds) …\nThe final phase of each task and its number of rounds. merged means the change is on the run branch. discarded means the task ended without a result.\nhalted: completed\nWhy the run stopped, see halted. completed means nothing is left to do. Check the task lines: a task can still be discarded. paused means you can continue the run. If the run had anomalies, for example stop_and_fix, the line is longer: halted: completed · 4 gateway calls · $0.0000 ·!! 2 anomalies.\n2 gateway calls · $0.0000\nThe number of model calls ( gateway calls ) and their cost. The examples on this page cost $0 because they were recorded with a free test model.\naudit chain: intact\nThe run's event log is complete and unchanged, see audit chain.\nbudget remaining: 10\nDollars left from the run's budget.\nrun branch … — 2 merges ahead of base\nWhere the result is. The number counts commits after the base, not merges. no merges landed means there is nothing to take.\nAfter the closing lines Theia prints two more housekeeping lines. Example:\nr6t5yzzg task.note [post_run_maintenance]\nmaintenance: 0 summaries refreshed, 0 orphan generations GCed\nThis is the post-run maintenance: Theia refreshes memory summaries and removes old search generations. No model is called. These lines say nothing about the task result.\nThe exit code is 0 when every task ended merged, and 1 when any task did not. All codes: Exit codes.\nStep 7. Take the result into your branch\nThe result is on the run branch. Your current branch is unchanged. List the run branches:\ngit branch --list 'theia/run-*'\nA + before a name means that the branch is checked out in another worktree, see the note below.\nLook at the change and merge it from your own branch. Replace <suffix> with the end of your run branch name, for example 4lejtj, and main with your branch:\ngit log --oneline main..theia/run-<suffix>\ngit diff main...theia/run-<suffix>\ngit merge theia/run-<suffix>\nExample output of git log:\n8d6868e Merge branch 'ai/2akdqiyyco222jxsd2vfzc' into theia/run-4lejtj\n9e56eff theia: Add a greeting.txt file with the text hello (attempt 0)\nExample output of git merge:\nUpdating 0de439e..8d6868e\nFast-forward greeting.txt | 1 + 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+) create mode 100644 greeting.txt\nYou can also push the run branch and open a pull request. Theia itself never pushes.\nWhy git switch fails\nThe run branch is checked out in a hidden git worktree.theia/runs/<run folder>/trunk. Git does not allow one branch in two worktrees, so git switch theia/run-4lejtj fails:\nfatal: 'theia/run-4lejtj' is already used by worktree at '~/projects/app/.theia/runs/2akdqiyvvs222jx34lejtj/trunk'\nMerge the branch as shown above, or remove the worktree first, see step 8.\nStep 8. Clean up\nWhen you no longer need the run branch, remove the worktree first and then the branches. Show the worktrees and their branches:\ngit worktree list\nExample output:\n~/projects/app 8d6868e [main]\n~/projects/app/.theia/runs/2akdqiyvvs222jx34lejtj/trunk 8d6868e [theia/run-4lejtj]\nThen run:\ngit worktree remove.theia/runs/<run folder>/trunk\ngit branch -d theia/run-<suffix>\ngit branch -d ai/<task>\n<run folder> is the folder name from git worktree list: the run id without the run prefix.\nai/<task> is the task's own branch. Theia keeps it after the merge as a record. git branch --list 'ai/*' lists these branches.\nExample output:\nDeleted branch theia/run-4lejtj (was 8d6868e).\nDeleted branch ai/2akdqiyyco222jxsd2vfzc (was 9e56eff).\ngit branch -d refuses a branch that is not merged into your current branch. Use -D only if you want to drop that work.\ntheia prune frees disk space from old finished runs, see theia prune. It does not delete theia/run-* or ai/* branches.\nThe base: unpushed commits are not included\nWarning\nA run does not start from your local HEAD. It starts from the upstream of your current branch, for example origin/main. Without an upstream it uses origin/HEAD, and only without a remote the local HEAD. Commits that you have not pushed are not in the copy, and the check runs without them.\nThe first line of the run shows the base. Example with one commit that was not pushed:\nrun branch: theia/run-6wf2n7 (base origin/main @ 8d6868e)\nTo include your local commits, add --base HEAD:\ntheia run --task \"Add a greeting.txt file with the text hello\" --verify \"test -f greeting.txt\" --base HEAD\nExample output:\nrun branch: theia/run-uia3ca (base HEAD @ 49446c2)\n--base accepts any git ref: a branch, a tag or a commit. Use it when the run needs commits that are not in the upstream, or when the work must start from another branch. Without the flag the order from the note above applies. If the ref does not resolve in the repository, Theia creates no run and exits with code 1:\nrun: --base 'nosuchref' does not resolve in ~/projects/app (fetch it, or name a reachable ref)\nSee --base.\nSee also\nControl a run\nRun results\nPlan before you run\ntheia run\nTroubleshooting","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/index.html":{"title":"Theia 0.2.201 user guide","content":"This is the guide to the theia CLI, version 0.2.201. It shows how to install Theia, run a task, use the TUI screen, set up models and connect Theia to Claude Code and Codex.\nNote\nCommands and arguments were checked on release 0.2.201 on macOS (Apple silicon). Commands that change data or call models ran in an isolated sandbox. Linux behaviour is described from the release source code. Where a command works differently from what theia help says, a warning sits next to it.\nQuick start\nInstall Theia — see Install.\nSign in to Claude Code or Codex and check your setup with theia doctor — see First setup.\nGo to the root of your git repository and run theia init — same page.\nStart a task with theia run — see Your first task.\nTake the result from the run branch theia/run-… — see Run results.\nSections Section What is inside\nAbout the platform\nWhat Theia is, systems and run modes, known limitations\nInstallation\nInstall, first setup, update, uninstall\nWorking with tasks\nYour first task, plan, control a run, results, code index and memory\nTUI screen\nScreens, hotkeys, leave without stopping a task\nConfiguration\nSettings files, models, keys, policy.yaml, environment variables, rules, sandbox, store\nIntegrations\nClaude Code, Codex, MCP server, Git, metrics\nReference\nAll commands, troubleshooting, glossary\nHow to read commands\nBlocks tagged bash are ready to paste into a terminal as a whole.\nBlocks tagged text show syntax or output. Do not paste them.\nIn syntax, <runId> is a value you fill in. A part in [ ] is optional. a|b means \"one of these values\".\nOutput examples are shortened. Paths like ~/projects/app stand for your real paths.\nWords that Theia itself prints ( fast_path, halted, merged ) appear as they are and are explained in the glossary. Words with several meanings and misleading statuses have their own sections there: Words with several meanings and Misleading statuses.\nSome output examples show the fake transport. It is a test transport that calls no model; the commands were checked with it. In your output you will see your own transport there, for example claude-write or codex-write.","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/about/platforms.html":{"title":"Platforms and run modes","content":"This page lists the systems Theia 0.2.201 runs on, what to install first, and the modes you can run theia in.\nSupported systems System CPU Status in 0.2.201\nLinux\nx86_64\nSupported\nLinux\naarch64 (arm64)\nSupported\nmacOS\nApple silicon (arm64)\nSupported\nmacOS\nIntel (x86_64)\nNot supported, the installer refuses\nWindows\nany\nNot supported in 0.2.201: this release has no Windows files\nWSL\nx86_64, aarch64\nNot verified\nOther systems and CPUs\n—\nNot supported\nCheck your system and CPU:\nuname -sm\nExample output on a Mac with Apple silicon:\nDarwin arm64\nOn an Intel Mac the installer stops with this message:\nget-theia: error: Intel macOS (darwin/x86_64) is not supported — releases target darwin/aarch64 (Apple silicon) and linux x86_64/aarch64\nFor other CPUs the installer prints unsupported architecture, for other systems unsupported OS.\nOn each system Theia is a single executable with Node.js built in. You do not need to install Node.js.\nWindows and WSL\nWarning\nRelease 0.2.201 has no files for Windows: the release build covers only Linux x86_64, Linux aarch64 and macOS Apple silicon. This version's installer stops on Windows with Windows is a planned first-class target; not yet supported. The source tree has an install.ps1 script, but it expects a ready-made Windows archive. In 0.2.201 there is no such archive, so install.ps1 cannot work.\nNewer releases do ship a Windows archive. This documentation covers 0.2.201 only.\nUnder WSL the installer sees Linux and picks the Linux file. Theia under WSL has not been verified.\nWhat you need Program Why Required\ngit\nThe store is created at the repository root; runs work in copies of the repository\nYes\nclaude (Claude Code) or codex (Codex CLI), logged in\nModels work through them\nYes, at least one\nbash\nCheck commands from --verify run through bash -lc\nYes\ncurl, tar, sha256sum or shasum\nThe installer needs them\nYes, to install\ntar\nUnpacks archives for theia self update and --x-release\nYes, to update\npodman or docker\nThe container sandbox, if the policy enables it\nNo\ntmux\nKeep a task running when the terminal is closed\nNo\nbubblewrap ( bwrap )\nThe theia jail sandbox on Linux\nNo\nTheia calls Claude Code and Codex engines ( harness ). Theia checks that you are logged in with a subscription. It does not accept a Claude Code set up with an API key only.\nCheck that the programs are installed:\ngit --version\nclaude --version\ncodex --version\ntmux -V\nExample output:\ngit version 2.50.1 (Apple Git-155)\n2.1.268 (Claude Code)\ncodex-cli 0.154.0\ntmux 3.7c\nIf a program is missing, the shell reports that the command is not found. One engine is enough: claude or codex.\ntheia doctor checks the engine logins and the rest of the environment. How to log in to Claude Code and Codex is described in Keys and access.\nDisk space: one installed version takes about 900 MB. See Update and choose a version.\nRun modes Mode How to start it What it needs When the need is not met\nFirst-setup wizard\ntheia with no arguments, environment not set up\nA terminal for input and output\nPrints the help text\nHome screen\ntheia with no arguments, environment set up\nA terminal for input and output\nPrints the help text\nScreen interface ( TUI )\ntheia run in a terminal, or theia run --tui\nA terminal for output and a keyboard, no --non-interactive\nTheia switches to --plain and says so\nBoard\ntheia run --cli\nA terminal for output\nTheia switches to --plain and says so\nEvent stream\ntheia run --plain\nNothing\n—\nNo questions\ntheia run --non-interactive\nNothing\n—\nMCP server\ntheia serve\nAn MCP client that starts the server itself: Claude Code, Codex\n—\nScripts and CI\nAny command without a terminal\nNothing\ntheia with no arguments prints the help text; a run switches to --plain\n\"Environment set up\" means: the user settings file exists and Theia finds a project store. See First setup.\nMessages when Theia switches to --plain:\nview: --tui needs an interactive terminal (a TTY for output and keys) — degrading to --plain\nview: --cli needs a TTY — degrading to --plain\nview: --tui is interactive; --non-interactive degrades to --plain\nThe third message appears only in a terminal. Without a terminal, --tui --non-interactive prints the first message: the terminal check runs first.\nWith --non-interactive Theia asks no questions in any mode. All run flags are in the theia run reference.\nHow to keep a task running without an open terminal: Leave a task running.\nThe MCP server is described in MCP server.\nTo run one command in another Theia version, use --x-release: see Update and choose a version.\nThe --dev flag is for platform developers only: it runs Theia from a source tree. Without a source tree the flag fails with an error: see For platform developers.\nSee also\nInstall\nFirst setup\nSandbox\ntheia doctor","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/about/limitations.html":{"title":"Known limitations of 0.2.201","content":"Some features of this version do not work, or work differently from what you might expect. This page lists them and the ways around them. Error messages and step-by-step fixes are in Troubleshooting.\nSystems\nNo Windows build. The installer refuses. The install.ps1 script expects an archive that the release does not have. WSL was not tested.\nIntel Macs are not supported. Builds exist only for macOS on Apple silicon and for Linux (x86_64 and aarch64).\nThe theia jail sandbox does not work on macOS. So the jailed-full sandbox tier is not available on macOS. Resource limits ( run.resources ) work only on Linux with systemd-run. See Sandbox.\nInstall and update\nThe --dev flag does not work in an installed version. Without a path it prints --dev: no development root — pass a path (theia --dev <path> …) or set dev.root in … and exits with code 1; it does not print the help. The flag is for platform developers — see For platform developers.\n--x-release downloads a version again, even when the installer already placed it. One unpacked version takes about 900 MB. After theia self update the cache keeps one more full copy.\nA wrong THEIA_X_RELEASE value breaks every command, even theia version. Remove the variable: unset THEIA_X_RELEASE.\ntheia self update compares versions as strings (seen in the code). After an update, check the version with theia version. How to go back to the previous version is in Update and pick a version.\ntheia setup deletes keys it does not know from config.yaml. Do not keep your own entries in that file.\nOn macOS the settings folder can \"move\". If ~/Library/Application Support/theia ever appears, Theia stops reading ~/.config/theia, and the GitHub token with it. See First setup.\nRunning the installer again does not replace a saved GitHub token. Set a new token with theia setup --pat.\nThe first-run wizard creates.theia in the current folder, even in a repository subfolder or in a folder without git. theia init puts the store at the repository root, so run it from the root.\nRunning theia init --harness … again does not change the engine, although it prints the new name. Change the engine in policy.yaml — see Models for tasks.\nRuns\nSteering and answers do not reach a new run. theia msg, theia answer, the --interaction bus flag and the MCP tools run_stop, run_msg, run_answer work only with a run continued through theia run --resume. The working order is in Control a run.\nA run starts from the upstream branch, not from your local HEAD. Commits you have not pushed yet do not get into the run. To include them, pass --base HEAD.\nCtrl+C in --plain and --cli modes kills the check command. Theia records a false verify_fail with exit null. The q key in the TUI does not do this.\ntheia run --resume continues even a closed run — one closed with --abandon. Before resuming, check theia show <runId>: if it says halted: abandoned, start a new run instead.\nA refused theia run --from-plan leaves traces. When the plan is not approved, or the word after the flag is not a plan id ( terryid: malformed id '…' ), a run branch theia/run-…, a folder in.theia/runs/ and an unfinished run are created anyway. theia prune does not remove them.\ntheia run silently skips unknown flags. A non-numeric --budget value gives no error either: Theia prints budget=$NaN and, at the end, budget remaining: NaN. No limit applies, but Theia does not call it unmetered.\n--max-rounds 0 and --max-rescopes 0 on the command line remove the limit, while maxRescopes: 0 in policy.yaml forbids rescoping instead (seen in the code).\nhalted: budget is not about money. It is an internal cap of 1000 verdicts. When the money runs out, the run pauses: halted: paused.\nExit code 0 also means a pause. This includes the case when no role can work: no route to a model, or a blocked secret in the task text.\ntheia show --task works only for merged tasks. For a discarded task it says the branch was not found.\ntheia audit verify with a scope that does not exist answers intact.\ntheia prune does not delete theia/run-* branches, git worktree records or probe scopes. Delete the branches yourself; git worktree prune clears the worktree records ( git worktree list marks them prunable ).\nA run fork ( fork ) has its own rules. Its default budget is $5, whatever the settings say. When you continue a fork, merges go into the branch currently checked out in your working copy. A fork of a finished run made without --at cannot be continued.\nTHEIA_LOG_FILE can stay empty when a run lasts less than a second.\nThe paths @/…, @./… and @../… in a task text are not inserted and give no error. Write the path from the current folder without./, for example @src/app.ts.\nTUI screen\nThere is no key to leave the screen and keep the run going. q pauses the run. Closing the terminal window kills Theia and the check command with no pause. How to keep a task running is in Leave without stopping a task.\nnohup theia run … & does not survive closing the terminal window. Run inside tmux, or in the background with < /dev/null & and disown. See Leave without stopping a task.\nA run stopped hard looks alive: running, in flight, · N live. Continue it with --resume or close it with --abandon.\nCtrl+C does nothing while you type a steering note. Press Esc or Tab first, then q.\nThe task box on the home screen does not work. Enter fails with kernel: no vendored prompts and leaves an open run and a branch behind. Start tasks with theia run --task.\ntheia run --resume <runId> --tui draws an incomplete tree: it holds only the events after the resume.\nSeeking on the finished-run screen only changes the event counter. Real seeking works in theia show <runId> --tui.\nThe v key does not open the output of check rows — only the texts of model calls.\nAfter kill -TERM the terminal stays in the alternate screen with the cursor hidden.\nTemporary theia-blob-*.txt files are never deleted. They hold model request texts and sit in the system temp folder.\nIn a small window lines get cut, and the terminal too small message is garbled. The minimum window size is 20×5 characters.\nCode index, memory and questions\nThe index and theia ask work only in a git repository. In a folder without git they fail with a git error.\ntheia index build does not refresh search. After a build, run theia index reindex, or index search and ask will not see the new cards.\ntheia index with no subcommand runs a full build and can spend money.\nSearch fails when a vector index is published but the local embedding model is not available. This affects index search, mem search, mem asof and ask. There is no word-only fallback in this case.\ntheia mem add does not mask secrets. Do not put keys or tokens into facts.\ntheia mem supersede does not check the new id. Even with a typo, the old fact disappears from search, and the replacement cannot be undone.\nThere is no command to delete a fact. A fact can only be replaced; the old one stays in history. E-mail addresses are not masked in memory.\nThe date 2026-09 in theia mem chunk --date is taken as the whole year. Write the month in words: September 2026.\nA failed reindex leaves an unfinished search folder behind, and theia mem maintain does not delete it.\nCards copied from the shared cache are not counted in written or current in the theia index build report. Only the rate: line shows them.\n--root root in a single-root project creates separate memory. mem search, the hooks and ask do not see it.\ntheia ask almost always calls a model. If the store has at least one fact or run, the answer goes through a model.\nSetting memory.sentenceGraph to h0 or h1 turns off vector search.\neval swebl, eval memory and eval demo create are tools for platform developers. They download data, call models and expect paths from the development setup.\nSettings and keys\nSettings are merged only one level deep. The gateway.routing, gateway.models and gateway.embeddings sections from project settings replace the same sections from user settings as a whole.\nA settings error names the section, not the key: spec.run: unrecognized_keys. A typo in a role name inside routing or models is silently ignored.\nAn invalid policy.yaml breaks every command except theia doctor. It prints all its lines and hides the error in the embeddings: FAIL (policy unreadable: …) line. See Troubleshooting.\ntheia doctor does not show routes or models. It looks only at.theia in the current folder and says docker ready even when Docker is not running. A lexical index: FAIL line does not change the exit code (seen in the code).\nThe dataClasses field of transports has no effect.\nSecret detectors know only five kinds of data: private keys, API keys, card numbers, seed phrases and e-mail addresses. API keys are recognized only by the start sk-, AKIA, ghp_ or gho_. Other tokens, for example github_pat_…, go unnoticed.\nA task text with a blocked secret is still saved in.theia/store.db as it is, although it never goes to a model.\ngateway.harnessConfig works only for Codex.\nThe code does not read the rules and skills shipped with Theia. The run.rulesSoT setting hides AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md from the engine but does not insert them into requests.\nThe budget counts Claude calls at list price, even when you work on a subscription (seen in the code).\nIntegrations\nHooks, the status line and.mcp.json point at the file of one specific version ( …/releases/0.2.201/theia ), not at ~/.local/bin/theia. After an update, Claude Code and Codex keep running the old version. Running theia hooks install claude again adds a second set of hooks and does not fix the path. Replace the path with ~/.local/bin/theia, or remove the old entries and install the hooks again — see Claude Code.\nThere is no hooks uninstall command. Integrations are removed by hand. The steps are on the Claude Code, Codex and Git pages.\nThe Claude Code Stop hook fires after every response. Each time it saves up to 16 KB of the conversation into memory and does not remove repeats. Project memory grows. To stop this, delete the Stop entry from.claude/settings.json; installing the hooks again brings it back.\nThe /theia skill cannot control a new run. run_msg and run_stop have no effect on a run started with run_start, although they reply that everything was delivered. Questions from such a run never reach Claude.\nA run killed from outside still shows as live in run_list, run_status and the status line.\nCodex gets a reduced MCP server: 5 tools, no run control and no /theia skill. The server and hooks start working only after two trust confirmations in Codex.\ntheia hooks install git silently does nothing when git has core.hooksPath set. In a linked git worktree the command fails with ENOTDIR.\nAn invalid.mcp.json stops theia hooks install claude halfway with code 1.\ntheia serve --metrics listens on all network interfaces. A second server on the same port crashes. /metrics shows only the server's own counters; run metrics leave only through OTLP.\nThe ops/observability stack is not part of the release. It exists only in the source repository. The Grafana panel for the theia_pii_total metric is always empty.\nrun_start with a wrong plan id leaves an empty theia/run-* branch.\nThere is no pull request creation, no branch push, no Slack, webhooks or Jira. Azure DevOps exists only as a text file in the shipped set.\nSee also\nTroubleshooting\nSystems and run modes\nControl a run","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/install/first-setup.html":{"title":"First setup","content":"After installing, you need three steps: log in to an engine, create a store in your project, and check the environment. This page covers the first-setup wizard, the home screen, and the commands theia setup, theia init and theia doctor.\nQuick path\nLog in to Claude Code or Codex. How to do it is described in Keys and access.\nGo to the repository root and create the store:\ncd \" $(git rev-parse --show-toplevel) \" theia init\nCheck the environment from the repository root:\ntheia doctor\nExample output of theia init:\nstore: ~/projects/app/.theia (worktree root)\ninitialized ~/projects/app/.theia (harness: codex — switch any time: gateway.routing in.theia/config/policy.yaml)\nai rules: none detected\nIf you are logged in to neither engine, the first line is:\nharness: neither codex nor claude answered its doctor probe — seeding codex routing anyway; authenticate one and see `theia doctor`\nSave a GitHub token for updates with theia setup --pat <token>: see The theia setup command.\nFirst-setup wizard\nIf you run theia with no arguments in a terminal and the environment is not set up yet, a wizard opens. The environment is not set up when there is no user settings file, or Theia finds no store in the current folder or above.\ntheia\nThe wizard creates the store in the current folder\nThe wizard creates.theia right in the folder where you ran theia. It does not look for the repository root, and outside git it does not put the store in your user folder. Outside git the wizard also creates a.gitignore file with the line.theia/ in that folder. theia init works differently: in a git repository it always puts the store at the root.\nIf you run the wizard in a subfolder, you get a separate store for that subfolder only. Run the wizard from the repository root. Or answer n to the store question and run theia init from the root.\nThe wizard asks its questions one by one. Its first output line states the rule: theia setup — Enter accepts the default, empty answers skip. Enter accepts the default; an empty answer skips the question.\nQuestion What to answer\nGitHub PAT for theia releases (updates + version pinning; Enter to skip):\nA GitHub token for updates. The input is hidden. You can skip it\nOpenAI API key for embeddings (semantic search; Enter to skip):\nAn OpenAI key. Needed only if embeddings come from OpenAI. Not asked when OPENAI_API_KEY is set\ninitialize a theia workspace in <folder>? [Y/n]\nCreate a store in the current folder\nadditional root repos for a multi-root workspace (comma-separated paths; Enter for single-root):\nPaths to sibling repositories if you want a workspace with several roots. Usually Enter\nharness [codex/claude] (Enter = codex):\nThe engine. In brackets the wizard prints the engine it found by login: Enter = claude when you are logged in to Claude Code only. Enter accepts it\nThe wizard asks only for what is missing. It leaves saved keys alone.\nIf you skip both keys, the wizard still writes the settings file, empty ( {} ). That is how it remembers the general step is done.\nIf you answer n to the store question, the wizard offers it again the next time you run theia in that folder.\nIf you name sibling repositories, the wizard creates a workspace manifest: see Several roots.\nExample of the end of the wizard:\ninitialized ~/projects/app/.theia (harness: claude)\nai rules: none detected\nno harness is authenticated yet — sign in with one of: codex login (OpenAI subscription) claude /login (Anthropic subscription — run `claude` once)\nthen `theia doctor` confirms.\ndone — try: theia doctor, then theia run --task \"…\"\nIf you are already logged in to an engine, the login hint is replaced by the line authenticated harnesses: ….\nHome screen\nWhen both the settings file and a store exist, theia with no arguments opens the home screen. Example on a machine with no engine login and no index:\ntheia — ~/projects/app\nharnesses: FAIL (neither codex nor claude is authenticated — one is required)\ntheia: no index\nrecent runs: none yet\ntask ▸ ▏\nEnter run ·? help · q quit\nAt the top: only the problems from theia doctor. With no problems, these lines are absent.\nThen: the code index state and up to five recent runs. The index line depends on the state: theia: no index while there is no index, or, for example, theia: 0 indexed · 1 missing.\nYou can type a task in the task ▸ line. Enter starts theia run --task with that text. This is real model work: it uses your subscription limits.\n? shows and hides the help. q, Esc or Ctrl+C close the screen. q and? work only while the task line is empty; Esc and Ctrl+C always work.\nThe screen also opens from a project subfolder: Theia looks for the store upward.\nThe theia setup command\ntheia setup saves user settings. It never prints token or key values.\nCommand What it saves\ntheia setup --pat <token>\nA GitHub token for updates and other versions\ntheia setup --openai-key <key>\nAn OpenAI key for OpenAI embeddings\ntheia setup --channel release\nThe update channel: release or prerelease\ntheia setup --interactive\nAsks for the token and the OpenAI key in the terminal\nThere are also --dev-root (platform developers only) and --fetch-embeddings (download the local embedding model). All flags are in the theia setup reference.\nExample: set the update channel back to the default.\ntheia setup --channel release\nExample output:\nwrote update.channel to ~/.config/theia/config.yaml (mode 0600)\nThe path in the output shows which settings file Theia uses. On macOS it can be in ~/Library/Application Support/theia: see macOS: the settings folder can switch.\ntheia setup rewrites the settings file\ntheia setup and the wizard rewrite config.yaml completely. Only four known keys stay: github.pat, openai.apiKey, dev.root and update.channel. Anything you added by hand is lost. An invalid update.channel value is removed too. The file mode goes back to 0600.\nUser settings file System Where config.yaml lives\nLinux\n~/.config/theia/config.yaml\nmacOS after the installer\n~/.config/theia/config.yaml\nmacOS, if the folder ~/Library/Application Support/theia exists\n~/Library/Application Support/theia/config.yaml\nIf XDG_CONFIG_HOME is set, the file is $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/theia/config.yaml on any system. The keys in the file and the environment variables that override them are described in Keys and access.\nmacOS: the settings folder can switch\nOn macOS Theia has two possible settings folders:\n~/Library/Application Support/theia — the usual macOS folder for programs;\n~/.config/theia — the old folder. The installer creates it.\nThe rule: if ~/Library/Application Support/theia exists, Theia reads settings from it and ignores the old folder. If it does not exist but ~/.config/theia does, Theia uses the old folder.\nThe installer creates ~/.config/theia, so things normally work. But if ~/Library/Application Support/theia appears, Theia stops seeing your token and other settings.\nThe folder can appear:\nif you created it by hand;\nif you delete ~/.local/share/theia and then run theia init in a folder outside git: Theia creates ~/Library/Application Support/theia itself.\nAfter that the data folder changes too: theia self update puts new versions in ~/Library/Application Support/theia/releases and looks for the vendor files in ~/Library/Application Support/theia/vendor/current. The installer still writes to ~/.local/share/theia.\nThe sign: theia doctor prints:\nuser config: absent (~/Library/Application Support/theia/config.yaml — theia setup)\ngithub pat: absent (release download/self update unavailable)\nCheck which folders exist:\nls -ld \" $HOME /Library/Application Support/theia\" \" $HOME /.config/theia\"\nExample output when both exist:\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 3 you staff 96 Sep 13 19:50 /Users/you/.config/theia\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 2 you staff 64 Sep 13 19:50 /Users/you/Library/Application Support/theia\nIf one folder is missing, ls reports it with No such file or directory.\nHow to fix it:\nThe folder ~/Library/Application Support/theia is empty: delete it. rmdir deletes only an empty folder, so the command is safe:\nrmdir \" $HOME /Library/Application Support/theia\"\nAfter that theia doctor again prints user config: present and github pat: present.\nThe folder already has files: save your settings again, and they go to the new folder: theia setup --pat <token>. Set any other keys you had the same way.\nProject store: theia init\nThe store is the.theia folder where Theia keeps the event log, memory, code index and project settings. Create it once per project, from the repository root:\ncd \" $(git rev-parse --show-toplevel) \" theia init\nIn a git repository theia init puts the store at the root of the working copy, even when you are in a subfolder.\nOutside git the store goes into your user folder and the folder itself stays clean. Such a workspace does not fully work yet: see Store and project roots.\ntheia init adds the line.theia/ to.gitignore.\nThe engine is chosen for you: codex if you are logged in to Codex, otherwise claude if you are logged in to Claude Code. With no login, codex is chosen. To choose explicitly: theia init --harness claude.\nWarning\nIf the store already exists, running theia init --harness … again does not change the engine, although the output names the new one. How to switch the engine is described in Models for tasks.\nAll flags are in the theia init reference.\nCheck: theia doctor\ntheia doctor\nRun theia doctor from the project root. The store, embeddings and rules lines look only at.theia in the current folder.\nWhat to look at first:\nLine Good Bad\ncodex subscription, claude code\nAt least one ok\nBoth unavailable and the line harnesses: FAIL\n.theia store\nok\nnot initialized (theia init)\nvendor deliverables\nok — … verified\nFAIL\nExample output, the engine lines:\ncodex subscription: unavailable (harness-codex: not authenticated (codex login status: exit 1))\nclaude code: ok\nThe exit code is 0 when there are no errors and 1 when a FAIL line counts as an error. Every line is in the theia doctor reference.\nSee also\ntheia init\ntheia setup, self update, version\ntheia doctor\nKeys and access\nStore and project roots\nFirst task","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/install/uninstall.html":{"title":"Uninstall","content":"There is no uninstall command. Delete the files by hand: first in your projects, then the user folders.\nThe data cannot be recovered\nUninstalling erases the run log, plans, memory and code index. You cannot get them back. If you need the data, copy the folders first.\nBefore you start\nTake the run results you need into your own branches: the theia/run-… branches are deleted below. How to take a result is described in First task.\nCheck the stores for folders outside git. They live in your user folder and are deleted with it. The list is in the workspaces.yaml registry: see Store and project roots.\nClose every running theia.\nFiles in projects\nDo these steps at the root of each repository where you ran theia init.\nThe store\nList the repository copies that runs created:\ngit worktree list\nExample output:\n~/projects/app c692273 [main]\n~/projects/app/.theia/runs/2akdqjtwvk223xgvjuh7qx/trunk c692273 [theia/run-juh7qx]\n~/projects/app/.theia/runs/2akdqjtyqo2223vwumszit/trunk c692273 [theia/run-umszit]\nThe next command deletes the.theia folder in the current folder: the event log, plans, memory, code index, project settings and task copies.\nrm -rf.theia\nRemove git's records of copies that no longer exist:\ngit worktree prune\nTheia added the line.theia/ to.gitignore. Delete it by hand if you no longer need it.\nRun branches\nRuns leave branches behind:\ntheia/run-… — the run branch with the result;\nai/… — the branch of a task whose work was merged.\nList them:\ngit branch --list 'theia/run-*' 'ai/*'\nExample output:\n+ theia/run-juh7qx\n+ theia/run-umszit\nThe + sign means the branch is checked out in a repository copy. After rm -rf.theia and git worktree prune it disappears.\nWarning\nThe pattern ai/* matches every branch whose name starts with ai/, including your own. Check the list before you delete.\nThe next command deletes every branch in that list without asking. Run it after rm -rf.theia and git worktree prune: git does not delete a branch that a copy still uses.\ngit branch -D $(git branch --list 'theia/run-*' 'ai/*' --format= '%(refname:short)' )\nExample output:\nDeleted branch theia/run-juh7qx (was c692273).\nDeleted branch theia/run-umszit (was c692273).\nIf there are no such branches, git prints fatal: branch name required. It means there is nothing to delete.\nIntegration files\nIf you connected Theia to Claude Code, Codex or git, files remain in the project. There is no hooks uninstall command: remove Theia's entries by hand.\nFile Created by What to delete\n.claude/settings.json\ntheia hooks install claude\nTheia's hooks and its status line ( statusLine )\n.mcp.json\ntheia hooks install claude\nThe theia server entry\n.claude/skills/theia/\ntheia hooks install claude\nThe whole folder\n.codex/hooks.json\ntheia hooks install codex\nTheia's hooks\n.codex/config.toml\ntheia hooks install codex\nTheia's server block\n~/.codex/config.toml\ntheia hooks install codex --trust\nThe project trust block\n.git/hooks/post-commit\ntheia hooks install git\nTheia's line\nThe file ~/.codex/config.toml belongs to Codex. If CODEX_HOME is set, it is $CODEX_HOME/config.toml.\nThe next command deletes only the /theia skill folder:\nrm -rf.claude/skills/theia\nWhat exactly to remove from the other files is described in:\nClaude Code\nCodex\nGit\nUser files\nYou can run these commands from any folder. What each folder holds:\nFolder Contents\n~/.local/share/theia\nreleases/ — installed versions; vendor/ — vendor files; stores/ — stores for folders outside git\n~/.config/theia\nconfig.yaml — the settings file with the GitHub token; workspaces.yaml — the registry of stores outside git; config/ — your policy manifests for all projects\n~/.cache/theia\nreleases/ — downloaded versions; models/ — embedding models; index-cache/ — the code index cache; last-update-check — the version check stamp\nmacOS\nLinux\nDelete the link to the program:\nrm ~/.local/bin/theia\nThe next command deletes all installed versions, the vendor files, and the stores for folders outside git:\nrm -rf ~/.local/share/theia\nThe next command deletes the settings folder (the file with the GitHub token, the workspaces.yaml registry, the config/ manifests) and the cache (downloaded versions, embedding models, the index cache, the version check stamp):\nrm -rf ~/.config/theia ~/.cache/theia\nThe next command deletes Theia's folders in the standard macOS locations. They can also hold settings, stores and cache:\nrm -rf \" $HOME /Library/Application Support/theia\" \" $HOME /Library/Caches/theia\"\nDelete the link to the program:\nrm ~/.local/bin/theia\nThe next command deletes all installed versions, the vendor files, the stores for folders outside git, the settings folder, and the cache:\nrm -rf ~/.local/share/theia ~/.config/theia ~/.cache/theia\nIf you set XDG_CONFIG_HOME, XDG_DATA_HOME or XDG_CACHE_HOME, theia folders also live inside those folders. Delete them there.\nIf you set THEIA_MODELS_DIR, THEIA_INDEX_CACHE_DIR or THEIA_VENDOR_DIR, the models, the index cache or the vendor files live in those folders. Delete them there.\nIf you added ~/.local/bin to PATH only for Theia, delete that line from ~/.zshrc or ~/.bashrc.\nYour Claude Code and Codex logins stay: they are those programs' own settings.\nCheck\ncommand -v theia\nIf the command prints nothing, theia is no longer on your PATH.\nSee also\nInstall\nStore and project roots\ntheia hooks","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/install/update.html":{"title":"Update and choose a version","content":"How to update Theia, roll back to a previous version, and run another version for one command.\nVersions and channels\nShow the current version:\ntheia version\nExample output:\n0.2.201\nThe version number grows with every release.\nPre-release builds get the suffix -pre.N, for example 0.2.201-pre.1. Such a build counts as earlier than the release with the same number.\nThe update channel decides which versions theia self update gets.\nChannel What you get\nrelease\nReleases only. The default\nprerelease\nReleases and pre-release builds. The builds are not verified for real work: the channel is for platform developers\nSet the channel back to the default:\ntheia setup --channel release\nNew version notice\nOnce a day, before a command, Theia checks for a new version. If there is one, it prints a grey line like this:\ntheia v<new> is available (running v<current>) — theia self update\nThe check needs a GitHub token. Without a token there is no check.\ntheia version and theia with no arguments do not run the check.\nThe time of the last check is kept in the file last-update-check in the cache folder: ~/.cache/theia or ~/Library/Caches/theia.\nIf the network is down, Theia stays silent and goes on. On the release channel the check waits at most 1.5 seconds. On the prerelease channel there is no such limit.\nIf your version is newer than the latest one, there is no notice.\nUpdate: theia self update\ntheia self update\nWhat the command does:\nTakes the GitHub token from THEIA_GH_TOKEN or from the settings file.\nFinds the latest version in your channel.\nIf it equals the current version, prints already up to date (v<version>) and updates only the vendor files. On the prerelease channel the line ends with, prerelease channel.\nOtherwise downloads the archive into the cache and checks the checksum if the release has checksums.txt.\nCopies the version into the data folder: ~/.local/share/theia/releases/<version>/. Then points the link ~/.local/bin/theia at it.\nUpdates the vendor files ( vendor ): prompts, rules and skills. Their archive is downloaded only when the set of files changed.\nWithout a token the command does not work:\nself update: no GitHub PAT — theia setup --pat <token> (or THEIA_GH_TOKEN)\nAfter the update, check the version:\ntheia version\nCheck the version after an update\ntheia self update compares versions as strings, not by number order. If your version is newer than the latest in the channel, for example because you installed a pre-release build, the command installs an older version. After an update, check theia version.\nExample: on 0.2.201 with 0.2.255 as the latest release, the command installs 0.2.255. That is a normal forward update.\nOld versions stay in ~/.local/share/theia/releases/. There is no rollback command: see Roll back.\nThe data folder does not always match the installer's folder. theia self update honours XDG_DATA_HOME, and on macOS it writes to ~/Library/Application Support/theia when that folder exists. The installer always writes to ~/.local/share/theia. See macOS: the settings folder can switch.\nIf a release has no checksums.txt, theia self update installs the version without checking the checksum. The installer refuses in that case.\nIf theia was not installed by the installer but is a plain file, the command replaces that file in place.\nIf updating the vendor files fails, the program itself stays updated.\nDisk space\nOne unpacked version takes about 900 MB.\ntheia self update and --x-release leave another copy of the version in the cache: another 900 MB or so.\nHow much the installed versions take:\ndu -sh ~/.local/share/theia/releases\nExample output for two versions:\n1.4G\t/Users/you/.local/share/theia/releases\nHow much the version cache takes:\nmacOS\nLinux\ndu -sh ~/Library/Caches/theia/releases ~/.cache/theia/releases\nUsually only one of these folders exists. For the other, du prints No such file or directory.\ndu -sh ~/.cache/theia/releases\nYou can delete the cache folder releases. The installed versions in ~/.local/share/theia/releases stay. If --x-release needs a version again, Theia downloads it again.\nYou can delete an old version together with its folder in ~/.local/share/theia/releases. Do not delete the version that ~/.local/bin/theia points to: check the link with ls -l ~/.local/bin/theia.\nRoll back\nThere is no rollback command. There are two ways.\nInstall the version you need with the installer. Set the version in THEIA_VERSION: see A specific version.\nPoint the link at a version that is already on disk. List the versions:\nls ~/.local/share/theia/releases\nExample output on a machine with two installed versions:\n0.1.14\n0.2.201\nSwitch the link:\nln -sfn ~/.local/share/theia/releases/<version>/theia ~/.local/bin/theia\n<version> is a folder name from the list. Then check the result with theia version.\nWarning\nUse only a version from the list. If the folder does not exist, the link points nowhere and the theia command stops starting. Then run the command again with a correct version, or reinstall Theia.\nAnother version for one command: --x-release\nThe --x-release flag runs one command in another Theia version. The installed version does not change.\ntheia --x-release <version> <command> [args]\n<version> is a version number: 0.1.14, v0.1.14, or with a suffix, for example 0.2.201-pre.1.\nPut the version right after the flag. The form --x-release=<version> also works.\nThe flag can go anywhere in the command.\nThe THEIA_X_RELEASE variable does the same for every command while it is set.\nExample: show another version's number by running version in it.\ntheia --x-release <version> version\nThe output is that version's number, for example 0.2.249.\nThe version must be cached or present in the release repository\nThe release repository keeps only the two latest releases, the latest pre-release build and pinned versions. Older versions, including 0.2.201 and earlier, have been removed. For such a version --x-release on a fresh machine fails with an error like release v<version> is not available: GitHub API 404 ….\nWorkaround: check the available versions on the releases page or with gh release list -R dokimelabs/theia-releases, and name one of them. A version that is already in the cache keeps working after it is removed from the repository.\nHow it works. Theia looks for the version in the cache, in the folder releases/<version>. If it is not there, Theia downloads it with your GitHub token. It checks the checksum only if the release has checksums.txt with a line for the archive. Then it runs the command in that version and returns its exit code. Another version may behave differently from 0.2.201.\nThe version is downloaded again\n--x-release looks for the version only in the cache. It does not see versions that the installer put in ~/.local/share/theia/releases, and downloads them again: a 210–230 MB archive and about 900 MB on disk.\nIf the version is not cached and there is no token, Theia does not run the current version instead. It stops:\nrelease v0.1.14 is not available: not cached and no GitHub PAT configured — cannot download looked in cache: ~/Library/Caches/theia/releases/0.1.14 fix: check the version tag, configure a GitHub PAT (theia setup --pat <token> or THEIA_GH_TOKEN), and ensure network access to github.com — theia never silently falls back to the running version.\nA bad THEIA_X_RELEASE value breaks every command\nIf THEIA_X_RELEASE holds something that is not a version number, every command fails, even theia version:\ninvalid release version 'garbage' (expected e.g. 1.2.3 or v1.2.3)\nRemove the variable and check:\nunset THEIA_X_RELEASE\ntheia version\nIf the variable is set in your shell startup file ( ~/.zshrc or ~/.bashrc ), delete the line there too.\nFor platform developers\nThe --dev flag and the dev.root key (command theia setup --dev-root ) are only for developers of Theia itself: they run the platform's source code from a source tree.\ntheia --dev <path> <command> [args]\ntheia --dev=<path> <command> [args]\ntheia --dev <command> [args]\n<path> is a folder with a Theia source tree. Without a path, the dev.root key from the settings file is used.\nA bare --dev takes the next word as a path only if such a folder exists. Otherwise the word is treated as the command.\nThe tree must contain apps/theia/src/main.ts and an installed tsx package. Then Theia runs the sources through tsx.\nWithout a source tree the flag does not start Theia. It exits with code 1 and one of these errors:\n--dev: no development root — pass a path (theia --dev <path> …) or set dev.root in ~/.config/theia/config.yaml (theia setup --dev-root <path>)\n--dev: /tmp/nowhere does not look like a theia dev tree (missing apps/theia/src/main.ts)\n--dev: tsx is not installed in <path> — run pnpm install there first\n--dev= requires a path\nThe first error: neither a path nor dev.root. The second: the folder holds no source tree. The third: tsx is not installed in the tree. The fourth: the --dev= form with an empty value. The error names the path ~/.config/theia/config.yaml; on macOS the settings file can be in another folder: see User settings file.\nSee also\nInstall\ntheia setup, self update, version\nUninstall\nKeys and access","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/guides/control-run.html":{"title":"Control a run","content":"This page shows how to pause, stop, continue and close a run. It also covers how to give a run instructions, ask about it, and what happens when the money runs out.\nFind the run id\nMost commands on this page need the run id. You find it here:\nAt the start of --plain output: run run2akdqiyvvs222jx34lejtj task tsk2akdqiyyco222jxsd2vfzc (fast_path).\nIn the pause hint: paused — resume with: theia run --resume run2akdqcg6bs222ldyelou46.\nOn the home screen: run theia without arguments in the project folder. See Screens and elements.\nIn theia audit verify: one line per log in the store, without the prefix — runs, plans ( pln… ) and probe records. theia show tells a run apart.\nA run id is run plus 22 characters. Commands accept the full id or the 22 characters alone. A shorter part does not work.\nPause a run\nPausing lets the current step finish, starts nothing new and keeps the run ready to continue.\nIn the TUI: press q or Ctrl+C once. The running step, including a check command, finishes, then the run pauses and the TUI closes. See Leave without stopping a task.\nWith --plain or --cli: press Ctrl+C once.\nTheia exits with code 0. Example output with --plain:\nstopping gracefully — in-flight verdicts will land; resume with: theia run --resume 2akdqbnjho2237q4cxhpts\nru3vcsya verdict rcnssu2r verifier → verify_fail (1 evidence) “$ sleep 10 → exit null (no output)”\nraqsutuk task.phase rcnssu2r verify_fail → implement\nrnh5e7zc run.paused rounds=2 (resumable)\ntsk2akdqbnjww2237rcnssu2r → implement (2 rounds) slow verify one\nhalted: paused · 1 gateway calls · $0.0000\naudit chain: intact\nbudget remaining: 10\npaused — resume with: theia run --resume run2akdqbnjho2237q4cxhpts\nCtrl+C also stops the check command\nWith --plain and --cli the terminal sends Ctrl+C to the running check command as well. The command dies, and theia records a failed check: verify_fail … exit null. The task goes back to implementation. This failure counts toward the limit of 3 failed checks in a row. After --resume the task simply tries again. In the TUI, q and Ctrl+C are keys, so the check finishes normally.\nStop at once\nPress Ctrl+C a second time before the run has paused. In the TUI, press q or Ctrl+C a second time. Theia kills its child processes and exits with code 130. The step that was running is lost. Everything recorded before it stays.\nExample output with --plain:\nhard stop: swept 3 child process(es)/group(s); resume with: theia run --resume 2akdqcgx6c223a47ovfyt3\nIn the TUI nothing is printed on this path: the screen handles the second press itself, kills the child processes and exits with code 130 without a message. With --plain and --cli there is another trap: if the first Ctrl+C killed the check command, the run pauses at once, and the second press arrives after the pause — you only get a pause. A hard stop happens when the step survived the first press.\nAfter a hard stop theia show <runId> reports halted: (still open), see (still open). You can still continue the run: --resume prints resuming run … (live; budget …), and theia msg in this state answers noted — this run appears live: ….\nContinue a run\ntheia run --resume <runId> [options]\nTheia continues from the run log. Finished steps are not repeated. A step that was cut off starts again.\nExample output:\nrun branch: theia/run-elou46 (resumed @ 74a5168)\nresuming run run2akdqcg6bs222ldyelou46 (paused; budget 10.00 usd remaining)\nThese options work with --resume: the view options ( --plain, --cli, --tui ), --non-interactive, --budget, --verify, --max-rounds, --max-rescopes. --verify replaces the run's check commands.\n--task, --track, --branch and --base are ignored.\nIf nothing is left to do, theia refuses: resume: all tasks terminal (finished) — nothing to resume, exit code 1.\nAll details: theia run --resume.\nClose a run without a result\ntheia run --abandon <runId>\nExample output:\nabandoned run2akdqbnjho2237q4cxhpts — workspaces kept; reclaim with 'theia prune'\nThe run gets halted: abandoned, see abandoned. Its files stay until theia prune. --abandon does not stop a run that is still going, so stop it first.\nWhy: to close a paused or hard-stopped run that you will not come back to. Without it the run stays unfinished, and theia prune never removes it.\nWhat to pass: the run id, full or its 22 characters.\nOn failure: for a run that already finished theia answers abandon: run already finished — nothing to do (prune reclaims finished runs), exit code 1. For an unknown run it is abandon: no events for <runId>, for an id in the wrong format terryid: malformed id '…', exit code 1.\n--resume still runs an abandoned run\nIn 0.2.201 theia run --resume does not refuse a run that you closed with --abandon. It runs it again. The sign: the line resuming run … (finished; budget …) instead of (paused; …). Before you resume, check the run with theia show <runId>. If it says halted: abandoned, do not resume it. Start a new run instead.\nGive a run an instruction\ntheia msg adds your instruction to the run log. The roles see it in their next prompts.\ntheia msg <runId> \"<text>\"\ntheia msg <runId> --task <taskId> \"<text>\"\nA run started with theia run --task does not hear msg\nIn 0.2.201 a run started with theia run --task or theia run --from-plan does not read notes from theia msg while it is running. The reason: the control bus, a poll of the log at the boundary of every step, is attached only on the --resume path. A fresh run starts without it. Theia still answers noted — this run appears live: it folds cross-process notes at its next dispatch boundary …, but the run does not use the note. The note is not lost: it stays in the log and enters the prompts at the next --resume. The same limit applies to theia answer, --interaction bus and the MCP control tools. All of them work for a run that you continued with theia run --resume. To steer a run in the TUI, use the m key, see Navigation and hotkeys.\nThis flow works:\nPause the run: Ctrl+C once, or q in the TUI.\nSend the instruction:\ntheia msg <runId> \"Use the file name greeting.txt\"\nExample output:\nnoted — folds into guidance on: theia run --resume run2akdqcg6bs222ldyelou46\nContinue the run:\ntheia run --resume <runId>\nAfter the resume the note is part of every prompt of the run. While the resumed run is going, new notes from theia msg reach it at its next step.\nTo send a note to one task only, add --task with the task id or its last characters. Example output:\nnoted for task …dlyndt2n — folds into its guidance only\nIf the task has already ended or the id is unknown, the note goes to the whole run: task …nosuch is unknown — rerouted to run-level guidance (the lead sees it; the original addressee is recorded on the note).\ntheia show <runId> --plain shows the note as a line user.note [msg] <text>. For a finished run theia answers noted — run already finished; the note stays on the audit trail (and guides any fork).\nAnswer a question\nThe planner and the lead may ask you a clarifying question. Usually the question appears in the terminal: as a form in the TUI, or as a text prompt with --plain. With --non-interactive or without a terminal nobody can answer. Then the task freezes, and a second unanswered question in a row pauses the run.\ntheia answer lists and answers questions that a run sends over the control bus:\ntheia answer <runId>\ntheia answer <runId> --question <questionId> \"<answer 1>\" [\"<answer 2>\" …]\nWithout --question the command only lists the unanswered questions. Example output when there is nothing to answer:\nno pending questions\n--question answers one question: pass its id from the list and the answers in order, one per item of the question. If there is no such question, or it was already answered, theia answers answer: no pending question 'nosuch' on this run and exits with code 1.\nWarning\nIn 0.2.201 only a run continued with theia run --resume sends questions to the control bus, and only when the bus is on: with the flag --interaction bus or the setting run.interaction: bus in policy.yaml. The run waits up to 600 seconds for an answer. If it no longer waits, theia answer does not reach it. Give the information with theia msg and resume the run, as described in Give a run an instruction. A question on the control bus could not be produced in our tests of this version.\nAsk about a run\ntheia probe <runId> \"<question>\" [--budget <usd>]\nTheia makes one model call about the state and history of the run. It does not change the run. The default budget of the call is $1. The answer comes first, then a line like this:\n[probe run2akdqckmao223pmxknw6vb of run2akdqck6vw2234dhcxwrue @ …d5kzqxny — $0.0000; parent untouched]\nprobe works for a running, paused or finished run. Details: theia probe.\nWhen the budget runs out\nEach run has a budget in US dollars: --budget <usd>, or run.maxUsdPerRun in policy.yaml, 10 by default. --budget 0 removes the limit.\nWhen the money is used up, theia refuses the next model call and pauses the run. The exit code is 0, because this is not an error. Example output, shortened:\n5yzerdqt!! anomaly budget_freeze taskId=2akdqciobc223v4nlhprjn role=implementer\n5snrbkl5 verdict 4nlhprjn implementer → no_progress (0 evidence) “gateway fatal: gateway: usd budget exhausted for this run (…”\n46ye2kee task.phase 4nlhprjn no_progress → escalated\n4qa5h7dj run.paused rounds=1 (resumable)\npaused — resume with: theia run --resume run2akdqcio5w223v4bg3eng3\nTo continue with new money:\ntheia run --resume <runId> --budget <usd>\nExample output:\nresuming run run2akdqcio5w223v4bg3eng3 (paused; budget 5.00 usd remaining)\n--budget sets the amount for the rest of the run. It does not add to the old budget.\nhalted: budget in the closing lines means something else: the run reached the internal limit of 1000 verdicts.\nWarning\nTheia does not check the value of --budget. A value that is not a number removes the limit. The start line then shows budget=$NaN.\nSee also\ntheia run: all options\ntheia msg, answer, probe\nLeave without stopping a task\nRun results\nTroubleshooting","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/guides/results.html":{"title":"Run results","content":"This page shows how to check what a run did: the summary and the events, the change of each task, a diagnosis, comparing runs, and cleaning up old runs. To take a change into your branch, see Your first task.\nSummary\ntheia show <runId>\nExample output:\ntsk2akdql5ywk223qptn2mqab → merged (4 rounds) [greet] Create greeting.txt with the text hello\ntsk2akdql5ywk22bqovrzs5yx → merged (4 rounds) [docs] Mention greeting.txt in README.md\nhalted: completed · 4 gateway calls · $0.0000\naudit chain: intact\nYou get one line per task with its final phase, the reason the run stopped, the number of model calls with their cost, and the state of the audit chain. What each line means: Read the closing lines. Where to find the run id: Find the run id.\nAll events\ntheia show <runId> --plain\ntheia show <runId> --cli\ntheia show <runId> --tui\n--plain prints every saved event, the same lines as theia run --plain, and then the summary. Progress lines with live are not saved, so they do not appear.\n--cli prints the final board. Example:\nrun …dhcxwrue $0.0000/$10.00 · 2 calls · 16 events tsk…dumszl6j fast_path merged r4 merger→merged\n— halted: completed\naudit chain: intact\n--tui opens the run tree in the TUI, see Screens and elements. Without a terminal theia prints view: --tui needs a TTY — degrading to the summary and shows the summary.\nWhat a task changed\ntheia show <runId> --task <taskId> [--diff]\n<taskId> can be the last characters of the task id. Theia shows the commits of the task and a summary of the changed files. --diff adds the full diff. Example output with --diff:\ntask tsk2akdql5ywk223qptn2mqab — ai/2akdql5ywk223qptn2mqab @ f87e49cecb50 (base a67ee9e260d3)\nattempts: f87e49c theia: [greet] Create greeting.txt with the text hello (attempt 0) greeting.txt | 1 + 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+) diff --git a/greeting.txt b/greeting.txt\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..ce01362\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/greeting.txt\n@@ -0,0 +1 @@\n+hello\nWarning\n--task works only for tasks that were merged. For any other task theia prints show: audit branch ai/<task> not found in <repository> (in-place run, or the branch was pruned) and exits with code 1, even if nothing was pruned. The copy of such a task stays in.theia/runs/<run folder>/ws-<task> until theia prune. You can look at it with git -C <path> log and git -C <path> diff.\nWhy the run ended this way\ntheia diagnose <runId>\nTheia checks the run log against a fixed list of rules. It makes no model call. Example output for a run whose task was discarded, shortened:\nroot cause: rejected-by-review\nwhy: a task terminated discarded with no anomaly — the work was produced and judged wrong, not blocked\nfingerprint: 0b0ec207de4ff4b3… memory: 2akdqckhcs223li2axo3ft\nanomalies: none\ntaxonomy: —\ngateway: 4 calls, $0.0000 task 2akdqckb7s225cdjcvcjj5 → discarded; verdicts: ready→verify_pass→approve→merge_fail→arb_escalate→lead_discard evidence[command] precheck:2akdqckb7s225cdjcvcjj5 (no commits on the task branch)\nThe last lines show the chain of verdicts of each task and the evidence behind them. Theia saves the diagnosis in memory, so it recognises the same failure next time. All root causes: theia diagnose.\nFor a successful run no rule fires, and the first lines look like this. It is not an error:\nroot cause: inconclusive\nwhy: no rule matched the evidence — the honest arm; inspect the trace, then extend the table\nReplay the log\ntheia replay <runId>\nReplay reads the run log again and rebuilds the final state. It runs nothing and calls no model. Example output:\nevents: 16; chain: intact tsk2akdqck7ds2234dumszl6j → merged\nverdicts: 4; gateway calls: 2\nsigma: {}\nThe exit code is 1 when the log was changed.\nTry an alternative and compare\nA fork is a new run that copies the history of an existing run up to a chosen event and continues from there, optionally with a new instruction.\ntheia fork <runId> --at <eventId> --with \"<instruction>\"\ntheia run --resume <forkId>\ntheia compare <runId> <forkId>\n--at is the fork point. Use it to repeat the run from a chosen step, for example from the attempt before a bad verdict. <eventId> must be a task.verdict or task.phase event. The 8 characters at the start of a line in theia show <runId> --plain are enough. Without --at the whole log is copied, and a fork of a finished run has nothing to do. For an event of another type theia refuses with exit code 1 and prints the 10 nearest valid points:\nfork: gateway.call is not a fork boundary — fork points are verdict/phase events (the attempt is the atom). Recent boundaries: e7fs5aa5 task.verdict ezs4raer task.phase\n--with is an instruction for the fork. Pass the text the roles must take into account on the new path. Without it the fork follows the same log from a clean slate. The instruction shows in theia show <forkId> --plain as a line user.note [msg] ….\n--budget is the fork's budget in dollars. The default is 5, not run.maxUsdPerRun. On --resume you see budget 5.00 usd remaining.\nExample output of theia fork:\nforked run2akdqckarw223cdvn75mka @ cbvlvdga → run2akdqckj62223mjar2dw7f events copied: 11; chain: intact djcvcjj5: no recorded commit — workspace will start clean from HEAD\ndrive it: theia run --resume run2akdqckj62223mjar2dw7f (gateway: hybrid — prefix replays free)\nExample output of theia compare for the parent run and its fork:\nA: run2akdqckarw223cdvn75mka (6 verdicts)\nB: run2akdqckj62223mjar2dw7f (7 verdicts)\nverdict sequences diverge at step 3 (3 shared): A tail: djcvcjj5 merger → merge_fail djcvcjj5 arbiter → arb_escalate djcvcjj5 lead → lead_discard B tail: djcvcjj5 reviewer → approve djcvcjj5 merger → merge_fail djcvcjj5 arbiter → arb_escalate djcvcjj5 lead → lead_discard\ncost A (post-baseline): $0.0000\ncost B (post-baseline): $0.0000\noutcomes: djcvcjj5: A=discarded B=discarded\nartifacts: A merged [] B merged [] (identical)\ntheia show shows the link: the fork has a line forked from 2akec5u2ns222lbndh3dce at a7zuqbcp, and the parent run has fork → <forkId> at <eventId> (<instruction>).\nForks are an advanced tool\nA fork has its own budget: $5 by default ( --budget ), not run.maxUsdPerRun.\nA fork gets no run branch of its own. When you resume it, theia prints the fork integrates in-checkout, and merges go straight into the branch checked out in your repository. The line gateway: hybrid means the hybrid gateway mode: the copied part of the log is not executed again.\nA fork without --at copies the whole history. A fork of a finished run then has nothing to do: resume: all tasks terminal (paused) — nothing to resume.\nA fork point in the verify phase without a commit is dangerous\nThe line no recorded commit — workspace will start clean from HEAD in the output of theia fork means the fork point has no recorded commit. If the task is in the verify phase there, the fork has no task copy, and after --resume the --verify command runs in the root of your working copy, not in a copy. A command that writes files or makes a commit changes your branch. The merge then fails: merger → merge_fail (1 evidence) “no workspace for task”. Choose a point task.phase … → implement or an implementer verdict, or do not pass --verify commands that change anything to the fork.\nAll options: theia fork, theia compare.\nCheck that the logs are intact\ntheia audit verify\nTheia checks the audit chain of every log in the store and prints one line per log: runs, plans and probe records, all without the prefix. Example output:\n2akdqck6vw2234dhcxwrue: intact\n2akdqckarw223cdvn75mka: intact\nBROKEN at <event> means the log was changed after it was written. The exit code is then 1. See theia audit verify.\nFree disk space\nEach run keeps copies of the repository under.theia/runs/. theia prune removes finished runs: their folders, their records and stored outputs that nothing uses any more. Unfinished and paused runs stay.\nFirst look at what would be removed. This example keeps the newest finished run:\ntheia prune --keep 1 --dry-run\nExample output:\nprune: 3 finished runs selected (0 unfinished kept): run2akdqckj62223mjar2dw7f 0d old no workspace run2akdqckarw223cdvn75mka 0d old 0.0 MB run2akdqck6vw2234dhcxwrue 0d old 0.0 MB workspaces: 0.0 MB · event scopes: 3 · orphaned blobs swept after untouched: unfinished runs, gateway recordings (request-keyed, shared), ai/* audit branches (your repo)\ndry-run: nothing deleted\nThen remove them. Select by age with --older-than, or by count with --keep:\ntheia prune --older-than <age> [--yes]\ntheia prune --keep <N> [--yes]\nOn a terminal theia asks delete N runs + X MB? [y/N]. Without a terminal add --yes, otherwise theia prints the list, then prune: aborted (nothing deleted), and exits with code 1. Example output after deletion:\npruned: 3 runs, 0.0 MB of workspaces, 14 orphaned blobs (0.0 MB)\ntheia prune does not delete the theia/run-* and ai/* branches. Git keeps an entry for each removed worktree; git worktree list marks it prunable. Remove these entries with:\ngit worktree prune\nThe branches themselves: Clean up. All details: theia prune.\nSee also\ntheia show, replay, diagnose, compare, fork\ntheia audit, prune\nControl a run\nYour first task","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/guides/knowledge.html":{"title":"Code index, memory and questions","content":"This page shows how Theia learns your project and answers questions about it: the code index, the project memory and theia ask. You will learn the working order, which commands cost money, and the traps to avoid.\nBefore you start\nYou need:\na workspace with a store, created by theia init;\na git repository: the code index and theia ask take the list of files from git.\nGit is required for the index and ask\nIn a folder that is not a git repository, theia index build, theia index status, theia index search and theia ask stop with fatal: not a git repository and exit code 1, and theia index status --brief prints theia: no index. Without git, the theia mem commands, theia index render and theia eval index-bench still work.\nThe index reads only files that git tracks. Run git add for a new file before you index it.\nWhat costs money Command Calls a language model Cost\ntheia index build, and theia index without a subcommand\nYes: up to two calls per new or changed file; the second one when the model's reply was unusable\nPaid\ntheia ask\nYes: one call per question\nPaid\ntheia mem maintain --llm\nYes: one call per summary\nPaid\ntheia index reindex, theia mem reindex\nNo, they compute embeddings\nFree with the local model, paid per token with OpenAI\ntheia index status, render, search; theia mem add, chunk, search, asof, supersede, maintain, context, recall, checkpoint; theia eval index-bench\nNo\nFree\nModel calls go through your routing and are paid through the harness it names, claude or codex. With OpenAI embeddings every search also sends its query to OpenAI to get a vector, at a small per-token cost. One exception to the table: with maintenance.indexPolicy: auto, the session start hook theia mem context can start a paid index build, see Keep it fresh automatically.\nWarning\ntheia index without a subcommand runs a full theia index build. In a new workspace this is one model call for every source file.\nThe code index\nThe code index holds one index entry per source file. The indexer role writes each entry once: the file's purpose, related files, public API and design notes. The entry is reused for free until the file content changes.\nThe working order:\ntheia index build creates entries for new and changed files.\ntheia index reindex publishes them to search.\ntheia index status checks that nothing is left behind.\nBuild the index\ntheia index build\nAt the end theia prints a report. Example output of a repeated build where nothing changed:\nindex: 0 written, 4 current, 0 failed, 0 model calls\nrate: — files/s, 4 zero-cost (100%), 0 model calls, $0.0000 locked (0ms)\nwritten counts entries a model created in this build, current counts unchanged files, and failed counts files without an entry; each of them gets a FAIL line with the reason. $… locked is what the build spent.\nThe index covers git-tracked source files in TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Go, Java, Kotlin, C, C++, C#, Objective-C, Swift, shell and SQL. Paths that contain test/ or fixtures/ are skipped. The exact rules are in Which files are indexed.\nFor a large repository, set a spending cap:\ntheia index build --budget 0.5\nThe --budget flag:\nwhy: to cap the money one build may spend;\nwhen: the first build of a large repository, or a model whose price you do not know yet;\nwhat to pass: a positive number in USD, for example 0.5;\nwithout it: there is no cap; the build calls the model for every new and changed file until it is done;\non failure: at the cap the remaining files get FAIL and stay missing or stale, exit code 1. Run the build again later: finished entries are not paid for again, you pay only for the files that are left. A value of 0 or a non-number stops the command before any work.\nIf another workspace on this computer already indexed the same files at the same paths, theia copies those entries for free. See Shared entry cache.\nPublish entries to search\nSearch does not read the entries directly. It reads a search generation: a published snapshot with a word index and, when embeddings work, meaning vectors. theia index build does not update it. After every build, run:\ntheia index reindex\nNew entries stay invisible until reindex\nOnce a search generation exists, entries built later are not found by theia index search and theia ask until the next theia index reindex. They already appear in theia index render. Before the very first reindex, search reads all entries directly, so this trap starts only after it.\nWith the default settings, the first reindex downloads the local embedding model, about 23 MB. Read Embeddings before you run it on a computer without internet access.\nCheck freshness\ntheia index status\nExample output:\nentries: 4 (3 current)\ndirty-marks: 2 paths since last build (post-commit hook)\nmissing (2): tests/y.ts src/new.ts\norphaned (1): src/main.go\nrun 'theia index build' to refresh\nstale: the file changed after its entry was built. This is what stale means everywhere in theia.\nmissing: a tracked source file has no entry.\norphaned: the entry belongs to a file that git no longer tracks. The next full build deletes it.\ndirty-marks: files the optional git hook noted after commits since the last build.\nWhen everything is up to date, the last line is index is fresh and the exit code is 0; otherwise the exit code is 1. For a status bar use theia index status --brief. It prints one line, for example theia: 3 indexed · 2 missing · 1 orphaned · 2 marked dirty.\nRead the project map\ntheia index render\nIt prints a short legend and the blueprint: one line per file, the most important files first. Example line:\nsrc/calc.ts[domain/billing/8]: F:Calculator helpers for invoices: add() and Invoice.withTax(). | R:src/main.go | A:add(a,b); class Invoice { withTax(rate) } | S:Tax is applied multiplicatively: total*(1+rate). No rounding.\nF is the file's purpose, R its related files, A its API and S its design notes. [domain/billing/8] is the layer, the business area and the importance, from 9 (core) to 1 (peripheral).\nFind code\ntheia index search invoice tax\nExample output:\n1.0164 [entry-scan] src/calc.ts [domain/billing/8] F: Calculator helpers for invoices: add() and Invoice.withTax(). R: src/main.go A: add(a,b); class Invoice { withTax(rate) } S: Tax is applied multiplicatively: total*(1+rate). No rounding.\nThe brackets name the search lanes that found the entry: bm25 matches words, vector matches meaning, symbol matches a code name such as RetryPolicy, and entry-scan matches words before the first reindex. Entries found by more lanes come first. The command shows up to 8 hits.\nEmbeddings\nEmbeddings let search match meaning, not only words. theia index reindex and theia mem reindex compute them, and searches use them.\nSetting in policy.yaml What computes embeddings Cost What it needs\ngateway.embeddings.provider: local (default)\nThe model all-MiniLM-L6-v2 on your computer\nFree\nThe model files, about 23 MB, downloaded automatically on first use\ngateway.embeddings.provider: openai\nOpenAI text-embedding-3-small\nPaid per token\nAn OpenAI key, see Keys and access\nCheck the state with theia doctor. Its embeddings line can say:\nembeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d) — FAIL (not fetched and autoDownload is off — theia setup --fetch-embeddings)\nOther states are — ready at <folder> and — not fetched (~23 MB; fetches itself on first use, or theia setup --fetch-embeddings).\nWithout embeddings the rest works: memory commands, theia index build, status, render, and search by words. With provider: openai and no key, theia index reindex publishes a word-only generation and prints lexical-only, searches print theia: vector lane off — … and still work, but theia mem reindex refuses to run.\nWhen search stops with \"query embedding failed\"\nWarning\nIf a search generation with vectors exists but the local model cannot be loaded, for example because gateway.embeddings.autoDownload is false and the model was never downloaded, these commands stop with exit code 1 instead of falling back to word search: theia index search, theia mem search, theia mem asof and theia ask. The message starts with theia: query embedding failed (fatal: embeddings-local: model 'all-MiniLM-L6-v2' missing at. The prompt hook of Claude Code and Codex then adds no memory: theia mem recall prints theia mem recall (fail-open): theia: query embedding failed … to stderr and exits with code 0, so the session is not broken.\nA reindex on an empty index or on empty memory leads to this state even without the model: it publishes an empty generation with vectors.\nHow to avoid it: run theia doctor before the first theia index reindex or theia mem reindex. Reindex only when the embeddings line says ready, or says not fetched with fetches itself on first use and the computer is online.\nVerified way out: point theia to the other built-in local model. Theia then sees that the stored vectors belong to a different model, skips meaning search and searches by words again. In.theia/config/policy.yaml, add the model line under gateway.embeddings and keep the keys you already have there. The example keeps autoDownload: false, the setting that usually leads to this state:\nspec: gateway: embeddings: autoDownload: false model: bge-small-en-v1.5\nSearches then print a notice and work:\ntheia: vector lane off — generation embedded by local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2/384, current embedder is local/bge-small-en-v1.5/384; run `theia index reindex` to rebuild\nDo not run reindex while the model is missing\nWith the swapped model, run neither theia index reindex nor theia mem reindex until the model bge-small-en-v1.5 is downloaded. They behave differently, and both outcomes are bad:\ntheia index reindex on an index with no entries does not fail. It prints generation 2akec5sjek223bivzvz2yv: 0 entries, published=true (embeddings: local/bge-small-en-v1.5/384) and publishes an empty generation with vectors again, now of the new model. Search breaks again; only the message now says model 'bge-small-en-v1.5' missing and ~34 MB, and the model swap no longer helps.\ntheia mem reindex on non-empty memory stops with memory: embedding failed during index build (fatal) and exit code 1. Nothing is published.\nWhen the model is available, remove the model line and run theia index reindex and theia mem reindex. To get the model, theia's message suggests theia setup --fetch-embeddings; this guide did not test that download, see theia setup.\nProject memory\nMemory keeps what the team decided and learned, so that agents and your Claude Code or Codex sessions do not have to ask again. It holds two kinds of records:\nfacts: short statements with a kind: decision, constraint, commitment, observation, entity or preference;\nchunks: verbatim text, for example the end of a session transcript that the hooks save.\nMemory commands do not call a language model and cost nothing, except theia mem maintain --llm.\nSave a fact\ntheia mem add decision \"We use PostgreSQL 16 for the billing database\"\ntheia mem add constraint \"Never call the payments API from tests\"\nExample output:\nadded fct2akdqbbrrc2235vtf5srr4\nThe id fct… names the fact. Saving almost the same sentence again replaces the old fact automatically; the output then ends with (superseded fct…). A changed value, such as \"PostgreSQL 17\", is a new fact. To retire the old one, supersede it with theia mem supersede.\nNever store secrets\nAlert\nNever put keys, tokens or passwords into theia mem add. Facts are stored exactly as typed, and no command deletes them. While a fact with a key is current, theia plan and theia ask stop with gateway fatal: gateway: prohibited content (api_key) — call blocked (THE-014) and exit code 1; for ask the message starts with ask:. The fix is in Secrets in memory. The key stays in the store's history, so revoke it.\nSearch memory\ntheia mem search PostgreSQL billing\nExample output:\n2.0164 [lexical-scan+recency] fct2akdqbbsuo223637hohwfp We use PostgreSQL 17 for the billing database\n1.0161 [recency] fct2akdqbbs562235xexqfulr Never call the payments API from tests\nSearch always adds the newest records through the lane recency, so it returns results for any query. A hit found only by recency does not match your words.\nLook back in time\ntheia mem asof searches memory as it was at the moment of an id: the facts that were current then, without later records.\ntheia mem asof <id> <query...>\n<id> is any theia id, for example the fct… id printed by theia mem add or the id of a run. Dates such as 2026-09-01 are not accepted. Details are in theia mem asof.\nMemory in multi-root workspaces\nWarning\ntheia mem add --root <name> saves a fact in a separate scope of that root. theia ask, theia mem context and theia mem recall never read that scope, so neither ask nor your Claude Code and Codex sessions see the fact. In a single-root repository --root root is accepted and hides the fact even from a plain theia mem search.\nSave facts without --root. In a multi-root workspace, use --root <name> only for notes you look up yourself with theia mem search, which reads every root.\nAsk a question\ntheia ask answers from memory, the code index and the last three runs, and lists the records it used.\ntheia ask \"which database do we use for billing?\"\nThe question can also come from stdin:\necho \"which database does billing use?\" | theia ask\nAfter the answer text theia prints the sources and the cost. Example of these two lines:\n[sources] fct:2akdqbbsuo223637hohwfp fct:2akdqbbs562235xexqfulr src/calc.ts hello.js\n[ask run2akdqct57o222gejfseu5t — $0.0000]\nExamples were captured on the test transport\nThe theia ask examples and cost lines in this guide were captured on the test transport fake, which spends no money, so the cost is always $0.0000. With a real model the last line shows the real amount. On fake the answer text itself is the stub fake-green: no verdict for role 'none', and theia index build always ends with FAIL, because the indexer role gets text instead of JSON.\nOne question is one model call. The default cap is 1 USD. Set another cap with --budget:\ntheia ask --budget 2 \"how is tax applied to invoices?\"\nThe --budget flag: why: to cap the price of one question; when: a question over a large index or an expensive model on the * route, or the opposite, when 1 USD is too much; what to pass: a positive number in USD; without it: a cap of 1 USD; on failure: when the call would exceed the cap, or you pass 0 or a negative number, the gateway refuses with ask: gateway fatal: gateway: usd budget exhausted for this run (THE-024) and exit code 1; the model is not asked and no money is spent. Two exceptions: with an empty store there is no call, and --budget 0 passes with exit code 0; the same question asked again is served from the record of the first answer before the cap is checked, and passes too. A non-number ( --budget abc ) or the flag without a value means \"no cap\", and theia does not warn about it.\nIf memory, the code index and run history are all empty, theia answers No records cover this … without a model, for $0. With at least one fact or run in the store, every question goes to the model.\n@path is not expanded in ask: the question is sent as typed. File inclusion works in theia run, theia plan, theia msg and theia probe.\nMemory saved with --root is not used.\nAll options: theia ask.\nKeep it fresh automatically\ntheia hooks install claude and theia hooks install codex connect memory to your sessions. A session starts with the map head and recent facts ( theia mem context ), each prompt gets matching memory ( theia mem recall ), and the end of a conversation is saved ( theia mem checkpoint ). For Claude Code the command also sets the status line theia index status --brief if you have none.\ntheia hooks install git notes changed files after each commit, and theia index status shows them as dirty-marks. It costs nothing and does not build the index.\nmaintenance.indexPolicy in policy.yaml decides whether theia builds the index on its own. alert, the default, only warns about stale entries when theia run and theia plan start. auto builds them at run and plan start and when a session starts, up to indexBuildBudgetUsd (1 USD by default) per build, and this costs money. manual never builds.\nAfter each run theia refreshes memory summaries for free, unless indexPolicy is manual.\nSee also\ntheia index\ntheia mem\ntheia ask\ntheia eval\npolicy.yaml settings\nClaude Code\nKnown limitations","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/tui/navigation.html":{"title":"Navigation and hotkeys","content":"This page lists the keys of every TUI screen, and the terminal settings that change how the screens look. What the screens show is described on the page TUI screens and elements.\nThe TUI works only with the keyboard. It has no mouse support.\nWhere keys work\nThe tables use these names for the screens:\nName Screen\nLive\ntheia run in a terminal, while the run works\nEnded\nthe \"run ended\" screen after a run finishes\nBrowser\ntheia show <runId> --tui\nThe keys were checked on macOS inside tmux. Keys marked \"from code\" were not seen working live.\nMove around the tree Key Action Where Notes\n↑ or k\nOne row up\nLive, Ended, Browser\nWhen the detail pane has the keys, it scrolls the pane instead.\n↓ or j\nOne row down\nLive, Ended, Browser\nThe same.\n→ or l\nOpen the row\nLive, Ended, Browser\nDoes nothing on a row without children.\n← or h\nClose the row. On a closed row or a row without children: go to the parent row\nLive, Ended, Browser\nL\nOpen all rows\nLive, Ended, Browser\nH\nClose all rows\nLive, Ended, Browser\ng\nFirst row\nLive, Ended, Browser\nG\nLast row\nLive, Ended, Browser\nPgDn / PgUp\nTen rows down / up\nLive, Ended, Browser\nAlways ten rows, whatever the window height.\ne\nNext row with a problem: ✖, ◆ or ‼\nLive, Ended, Browser\nAfter the last one it starts again from the top.\nf\nFollow mode: the cursor jumps to the newest activity\nLive, Ended, Browser\nAny cursor move turns it off. The live tree starts with follow mode on.\nF\nChange the follow level: round → tool → task\nLive, Ended, Browser\nAlso turns follow mode on. The bottom line shows f follow:<level>.\nCtrl+← / Ctrl+→\nThe same as ← / →\nLive, Ended, Browser\nIn the macOS Terminal app, Option+← and Option+→ work the same. From code.\nEsc\nClose the detail pane\nLive, Ended, Browser\nEsc never leaves the TUI.\nDetail pane Key Action Notes\nEnter\nOpen or close the details of the row under the cursor\nThe tree keeps the keys at first: ┤ tree · focus ├.\nTab\nMove the keys between the tree and the details\nWorks only while the pane is open.\n↑ ↓ j k\nScroll the details by one line\nWhen the pane has the keys: ┤ detail · focus ├.\nPgUp / PgDn\nScroll the details by ten lines\nThe same.\nEsc\nClose the details\nShow more or less Key Action\n+ or =\nMore detail: verbosity up, to 2 at most\n-\nLess detail: verbosity down, to 0 at least\nw\nShow a long row in full on several lines; press again to shorten it\nVerbosity levels:\n0: only tasks and rounds. Warnings and steering notes stay.\n1: the normal tree. This is the default.\n2: under each model call and tool, up to four dim lines: the first non-empty fields of its record. For a fake call they are blobRef, kind, latencyMs and masked; the set depends on the record.\nExample at verbosity 0, a working run:\n⣻ running · run run2akec5ebn2222agikedmpm · fast_path · $0.0000/$10.00 · 1 call · 7 events 9s\ntasks: 1 running · 0 done · anomalies 0 · idx ⚠1 [@ event 7/7 · live]\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ⣻ ▼ tsk…hb2ddoq5 [fast_path] verify r1 $0.0000 9.4s ✔ ├╴r1 implement implementer → ready 1 evidence 0.1s ⣻ ╰╴r2 verify … 9.2s ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow:round • e err • m steer • p prompt • +/- verbosity=0 •? • q stop\nExample at verbosity 2, a model call row under the cursor. The frame was captured with NO_COLOR, so the cursor row starts with ❯:\n✔ ├╴▼ r1 implement implementer → ready 1 evidence 0.1s\n❯✔ │ ├╴· gateway fake ok $0.0000 blobRef: 62a278c5331a7f9907b25d12090df24c603e457b430fd86b3e26a3c57e20b632 kind: ok latencyMs: 1 masked: {} · │ ╰╴command fake-green:implementer — synthesized (simulator run)\nExample of w on a long row in a window 60 columns wide:\n· │ ╰╴command fake-green:implementer — synthesized (simulat or run)\nTheia remembers the verbosity and the follow level for next time: see Remembered settings.\nSeek through the history\nThe browser ( theia show <runId> --tui ) shows a run as it was at any moment. The seek keys move over the records of the event log, and the tree is rebuilt for that moment.\nKey Action\n[ / ]\nOne record back / forward\n{ / }\nTen records back / forward\n0 or Home\nThe first record\n$ or End\nThe last record ( head )\nThe position is on the right of the second header line. The frames below were captured with THEIA_NO_SPINNER, so a static ● stands in for the turning glyph. Example: 0 in the browser:\n● running · run run2akec5ebn2222agikedmpm · fast_path · $0.0000/$10.00 · 0 calls · 1 event\ntasks: 0 running · 0 done · anomalies 0 · idx ⚠1 [@ event 1/23]\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── (no tasks yet) ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow • e err • [ ] seek • +/- verbosity=1 •? • q quit\nAfter ] three times:\n● running · run run2akec5ebn2222agikedmpm · fast_path · $0.0000/$10.00 · 0 calls · 4 events\ntasks: 1 running · 0 done · anomalies 0 · idx ⚠1 [@ event 4/23]\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ● ▼ tsk…hb2ddoq5 [fast_path] implement r0 $0.0000 1m11s · ├╴smoke ✔ ├╴workspace @ ac69078 (copy) ● ╰╴r1 implement … 1m11s ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow • e err • [ ] seek • +/- verbosity=1 •? • q quit\nThe time 1m11s in this example is counted up to the last record of the log, not up to this moment.\nSeek on the run-ended screen\nThe \"run ended\" screen of theia run also shows [ ] seek, but there the keys change only the counter [@ event k/N]. The tree stays at the final state. To step back through a run for real, open it with theia show <runId> --tui.\nIn the live tree the seek keys do nothing.\nOpen the full text Key Action Where Notes\nv\nOpen the full stored text of the row in $EDITOR, or in less\nLive, Ended, Browser\nChecked on model call rows. Close the program to return (in less, press q ).\np\nOpen the prompt of a model call that is still working\nLive\nFrom code. Checked only with no call working ( no call in flight ). With several calls, put the cursor on a task first.\nTo open the text of a model call: open the round with →, move down to its · gateway … row, press v.\nTraps:\nv on a test_run row, the result of a --verify command, says no stored body for this row. The output of your check command cannot be opened here.\nv on a task row also says no stored body for this row. Open a model call row inside a round instead.\np on a working call without a stored prompt says no prompt blob for this call (blob store absent or content blocked): the blob store is off, or the content was blocked. From code.\nDelete the temporary files\nv and p write the text (the blob ) to a file theia-blob-<id>.txt in the temporary folder and never delete it. The files keep full prompts and answers.\nTo list and delete them on macOS:\nls \" $TMPDIR \" /theia-blob-*.txt rm -f \" $TMPDIR \" /theia-blob-*.txt\nOn Linux the files are in $TMPDIR when it is set, otherwise in /tmp. This was not checked on Linux.\nSteer a working run Key Action Notes\nm\nOpen the input line for a steering note\nLive only. On the \"run ended\" screen and in the browser it does nothing. While you type, m is a letter.\nEnter\nSend the note\nAn empty note is not sent.\nEsc\nCancel the note\nWorks whether the input line or the tree has the keys.\nTab\nMove the keys between the input line and the tree; the draft stays\nWhile the tree has the keys, m also returns to the input line.\nText keys\nEdit the note\nSee Text input keys.\nCtrl+C does nothing while you type\nWhile the input line has the keys, Ctrl+C does nothing, and q types the letter q. To stop the run from there, press Esc (or Tab) first, then q.\nAnswer questions\nFrom code: the form was not seen live (see Clarify form ).\nKey Action\nText keys\nType the answer\nEnter\nGo to the next question. An empty answer skips the question\nEsc\nDecline all remaining questions\nTab\nMove the keys between the form and the tree\nStop and leave Key Live Ended Browser\nq or Ctrl+C, once\nGraceful stop: the current step finishes, the run pauses, the TUI closes. Exit code 0.\nLeave and print the summary. Exit code 0 if all tasks were merged or the run is paused, otherwise 1.\nLeave. Exit code 0.\nq or Ctrl+C, a second time\nHard stop: Theia kills its child processes at once. Exit code 130.\n—\n—\nEsc\nDoes not leave\nDoes not leave\nDoes not leave\nNo key leaves the TUI and keeps the run working. For the details and for ways to keep a run going, see Leave without stopping a task.\nText input keys\nThese keys work in the steering input line, in the clarify form, and in the task box of the home screen. Typing and Ctrl+U were checked live; the other keys are from code.\nKey Action Notes\n← / →\nMove the cursor by one character\nHome or Ctrl+A\nGo to the start\nEnd or Ctrl+E\nGo to the end\nCtrl+← / Ctrl+→\nOne word left / right\nIn the macOS Terminal app also Option+← / Option+→.\nBackspace / Delete\nDelete one character left / right\nCtrl+W\nDelete the word to the left\nIn the macOS Terminal app also Option+Delete.\nCtrl+U\nDelete from the start to the cursor\nCtrl+K\nDelete from the cursor to the end\nPaste\nInsert the whole pasted text\nA pasted line break does not send the text; it shows as ⏎.\nHome screen keys Key Action Notes\nText keys\nType a task into the box\nText input keys work here.\nEnter\nStart a run with the text\nDoes nothing while the box is empty. Fails in 0.2.201: see Home screen.\n?\nShow or hide the theia help text\nOnly while the box is empty; otherwise it types?.\nq\nLeave, exit code 0\nOnly while the box is empty; otherwise it types q.\nEsc\nLeave, exit code 0\nAlso with text in the box. The text is lost.\nCtrl+C\nLeave, exit code 0\nAlso with text in the box. The text is lost.\nTerminal size\nThe run tree and the browser need at least 20 columns and 5 lines.\nIn a smaller window Theia shows only terminal too small (19x4 < 20x5). In 0.2.201 this message can mix with pieces of the old screen, and it wraps when the window is narrower than the message. Make the window bigger, and the tree comes back.\nAbove that size every line is cut at the right edge with …. Example at 40 columns and 12 lines:\n✔ completed · run run2akec5ebn2222agike…\ntasks: 0 runnin… [@ event 23/23 · head]\n──────────────────────────────────────── ✖ ▼ tsk…h… discarded r6 $0.0000 40s · ├╴smoke ✔ ├╴workspace @ ac69078 (copy) ✔ ├╴▶ r1 implement … 1 evidence 0.1s ✔ ├╴▶ r2 verify veri… 1 evidence 40s ✔ ├╴▶ r3 review reviewer … 1 evidence ✖ ├╴▼ r4 merge merg… 1 evidence 0.1s · │ ╰╴command precheck:2akec5ec5k222ah… ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • …\nOn a narrow screen the bottom line first drops w wrap • v view, then +/- verbosity. At 40 columns the q hint is cut off, but q still works.\nThe detail pane takes about 40% of the height, and at least three lines.\nThe help (? ) and the home screen do not scroll. In a low window their lower lines are cut off; on the home screen the task box can disappear.\nWhen you resize the window, the screen is redrawn.\nColours, animation and fonts Setting Effect Works in\nNO_COLOR with any value\nNo colours at all. The cursor row starts with ❯. Not shown in theia help.\ntheia run, theia show --tui\n--no-spinner\nA static ● instead of the turning glyph; the text cursor does not blink\ntheia run only\nTHEIA_NO_SPINNER with any value\nThe same as --no-spinner. Not shown in theia help.\ntheia run, theia show --tui\nSyntax:\nNO_COLOR=1 theia show <runId> --tui\nTHEIA_NO_SPINNER=1 theia show <runId> --tui\ntheia run --task \"<text>\" --no-spinner\nExample with NO_COLOR=1, the first rows of the tree:\n❯✖ ▼ tsk…hb2ddoq5 [fast_path] discarded r6 $0.0000 40s · ├╴smoke ✔ ├╴workspace @ ac69078 (copy)\nMore about these variables: Environment variables.\nFonts and encoding:\nThe TUI draws with Unicode characters: ⣾, ✔, ✖, ◆, ‼, ▼, ├╴ and others. Use a UTF-8 terminal and a font that has these characters. There is no ASCII mode.\nIf glyphs show as _ and lines as qqqq after tmux attach, the tmux client is not in UTF-8 mode. Attach with the -u option: tmux -u attach -t <name>.\nScreen readers: the TUI has no special mode for them. It hides the terminal cursor and draws its own, so a screen reader may not follow the selection. The --plain view prints plain lines instead. This was not tested with a screen reader.\nRemembered settings\nTheia keeps two TUI settings in the file tui-state.json: the verbosity and the follow level. Other things are not kept: open rows, wrapped rows, the cursor, the detail pane, the history position.\nTo look at the file:\nmacOS\nLinux\ncat \" $HOME /Library/Application Support/theia/tui-state.json\"\nWhen that folder does not exist but the older ~/.config/theia/ does, Theia uses the older one:\ncat ~/.config/theia/tui-state.json\ncat ~/.config/theia/tui-state.json\nExample output after one + press in the tree:\n{\"verbosity\":2,\"followDepth\":\"round\"}\nThe file is in the user settings folder:\nSystem Folder\nAny, when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is set\n$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/theia/\nmacOS\n~/Library/Application Support/theia/, or ~/.config/theia/ when only that older folder exists\nLinux\n~/.config/theia/\nWindows\n%APPDATA%\\dokimelabs\\theia\\\nTheia reads the file when a tree opens and writes it when the tree closes. If the file is missing or broken, Theia uses the defaults: verbosity 1 and follow level round.\nSee also\nTUI screens and elements\nLeave without stopping a task\nEnvironment variables\nGlossary","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/tui/leave-running.html":{"title":"Leave without stopping a task","content":"A run in the TUI stops when you leave the screen, and dies when you close the terminal. This page shows what each way out does, and three checked ways to keep a task running while you do something else.\nNo key leaves the TUI and keeps the run going\nq and Ctrl+C stop the run. Closing the terminal window kills it. To keep a task running without watching it, start it inside tmux or in the background, as shown below.\nWhat each way out does What you do What happens Exit code The run afterwards\nq or Ctrl+C in a working run, once\nThe header shows ‖ stopping — in-flight verdicts landing…. No new step starts. The current step finishes, including a running --verify command. Then the run pauses, and the TUI closes.\n0\nPaused. Continue it with theia run --resume.\nq or Ctrl+C again, before the pause\nThe TUI closes at once. Theia kills its child processes, including the check command. Nothing is printed.\n130\nNot ended: theia show reports halted: (still open). Continue it with theia run --resume.\nq or Ctrl+C on the \"run ended\" screen\nTheia prints the summary.\n0 when all tasks were merged or the run is paused, otherwise 1\nEnded.\nEsc\nNothing: Esc does not leave.\n—\n—\nClose the terminal window\nTheia and the check command die at once, with no pause.\n—\nNot ended. Continue it with theia run --resume.\nkill -INT <pid> from another terminal\nThe same as q: once is a graceful stop, twice is a hard stop. After the second signal Theia prints hard stop: swept N child process(es)/group(s); resume with: theia run --resume <id>. Here <id> has no run prefix, for example 2akec6uuuw222d4oullrzb; theia run --resume accepts it.\n0 / 130\nPaused / not ended.\nkill -TERM <pid> from another terminal\nTheia dies at once, without cleanup. See kill -TERM.\n143\nNot ended.\nExample: the header after the first q:\n‖ stopping — in-flight verdicts landing… · run run2akdqcguzg2232gj4jthgl · fast_path · $0.0000/$10.00 · 1 call · 7 events 23s\nWhen the current step has finished, the TUI closes and prints:\ntsk2akdqcgviw2232gyenfrzy → review (2 rounds) detach demo\nhalted: paused · 1 gateway calls · $0.0000\naudit chain: intact\nbudget remaining: 10\nrun branch theia/run-4jthgl — no merges landed (branch left at base for inspection)\npaused — resume with: theia run --resume run2akdqcguzg2232gj4jthgl\nIn this case the \"run ended\" screen does not appear. To continue the run, see Way 3: pause now, resume later.\nClosing the terminal window\nClosing the window kills Theia and its check command at once. The run does not pause.\nThe log keeps every step up to that moment. theia show <runId> reports halted: (still open), and the home screen shows the run as in flight.\nContinue the run with theia run --resume <runId>, or close it: see Runs left open.\nWith a real engine, the claude or codex process runs in its own process group. Closing the window may not stop it, and nothing cleans it up after Theia dies. This comes from the source code; it was not checked with a real engine.\nThis was checked by closing a tmux window with the TUI in it, which closes the terminal the same way as closing a window.\nkill -TERM\nkill -TERM <pid> stops Theia without cleanup:\nThe terminal where the TUI ran stays in full-screen mode, and the cursor stays hidden.\nThe running check command keeps working without Theia. Find it with ps and stop it yourself.\nTo bring the terminal back, type these two commands in it, pressing Enter after each. The old screen may hide what you type.\ntput rmcup\ntput cnorm\nreset shows the cursor again but does not leave full-screen mode.\nTo stop a run from another terminal, use kill -INT <pid> instead: it pauses the run properly.\nFlags in the examples\nWays 1 and 2 start the same task. Each flag in those commands is explained by one scheme: why, when, what to pass, what happens without it, what happens on failure.\n--task \"Add a usage section to README.md\"\nWhy: the task text, what to do.\nWhen: on every new run. To continue an old run use --resume, without --task.\nWhat to pass: one or two sentences in quotes. The text can include a file with @path, see --task.\nWithout it: when standard input is not a terminal, Theia reads the task from there ( theia run < task.md ); the TUI still opens. With no text anywhere, Theia prints the syntax hint and exits with code 1.\nOn failure: the flag itself cannot fail. A badly described task ends as discarded or escalated; read the result in theia show.\n--verify \"make test\"\nWhy: the readiness criterion. The task counts as done only when the command returns code 0.\nWhen: the result has a checkable sign: tests, a build, a file. Unattended this matters most: while you are not watching, only the command separates done from not done.\nWhat to pass: a shell command in quotes, the one you would run yourself: make test, npm test, test -f greeting.txt. For several checks repeat the flag; they run in order. The command runs on your computer, without a sandbox, for at most 5 minutes.\nWithout it: the model judges the result, the verifier. That is one more model call, and an opinion rather than a check.\nOn failure: a non-zero code gives the verdict verify_fail, and the task goes back to the implementer for another attempt. After three failures in a row the task goes to the lead ( stop_and_fix ), who may discard it. In the --plain and --cli views Ctrl+C also kills the check command, and Theia records a false verify_fail: see The check command and Ctrl+C.\nMore: Why --verify, --verify.\n--plain (Way 2 only)\nWhy: one line per event instead of a screen. Such output can go to a file and be read with tail -f.\nWhen: the output goes to a file, to CI or to another program, wherever there is no terminal or none is needed. Way 1 does not need it: tmux provides a terminal, and the TUI works.\nWhat to pass: nothing; the flag takes no value.\nWithout it: theia run tries to open the TUI. When the output goes to a file there is no terminal, and Theia switches to plain lines on its own, but writes the notice view: --tui needs an interactive terminal (a TTY for output and keys) — degrading to --plain into the log.\nOn failure: it cannot fail.\n--non-interactive (Way 2 only)\nWhy: the run never waits for you: roles ask no questions, and the TUI does not open.\nWhen: nobody is at the keyboard: background, CI, input from /dev/null.\nWhat to pass: nothing.\nWithout it: in a terminal a clarifying question from a role opens the answer form and waits for you. In a terminal with this flag Theia prints view: --tui is interactive; --non-interactive degrades to --plain and uses plain lines.\nOn failure: if a role still asks a question, the task freezes as escalated ( ◆ ), and the log gets the anomaly clarify_unanswered. A second question in a row gives clarify_stalled and pauses the run. How to answer: Answer a question.\nMore: --plain, --non-interactive.\nDefaults that both commands leave as they are: the track fast_path (the task goes straight to the implementer) and the budget of $10 per run. For a task over several files add --track full_spec, see Choosing the track. For another money limit use --budget, see --budget.\nWay 1: run inside tmux\ntmux keeps a terminal session alive on its own. You can detach from the session, close the window, and attach again later. tmux is a separate program; install it with your package manager.\nStart a tmux session with a name. tmux opens the session in the current folder:\ntmux new -s tui-task\nIn the session, in the project folder, start the task as usual:\ntheia run --task \"Add a usage section to README.md\" --verify \"make test\"\nThe flags --task and --verify are explained in Flags in the examples.\nDetach: press Ctrl+b, release both keys, then press d. tmux prints:\n[detached (from session tui-task)]\nThe run keeps working. You can close the terminal window now.\nLater, list the sessions:\ntmux ls\nExample output:\ntui-task: 1 windows (created Sun Sep 13 19:47:52 2026)\nAttach again:\ntmux attach -t tui-task\nThe TUI shows the current state of the run. If the run ended while you were away, you see the \"run ended\" screen; press q to get the summary.\nWhen you no longer need the session, remove it:\ntmux kill-session -t tui-task\nWhat was checked: the run kept working while the session was detached. Closing a window that was attached to the session did not stop the run either. After the run ended, q printed the summary inside the session.\nTips:\ntui-task is only a name. Use any name, and give the same name to tmux attach -t.\nIf glyphs show as _ and lines as qqqq after you attach, the tmux client is not in UTF-8 mode. Attach with the -u option:\ntmux -u attach -t tui-task\nWay 2: run in the background with a log file\nIn the project folder, start the run in the background, then detach it from the shell:\ntheia run --task \"Add a usage section to README.md\" --verify \"make test\" --plain --non-interactive > ~/theia-run.log 2>&1 < /dev/null & disown\n--task, --verify, --plain and --non-interactive are explained in Flags in the examples. Here --plain gives one line per event, and --non-interactive keeps the run from waiting for you.\n> ~/theia-run.log 2>&1 sends all output to the log file. < /dev/null means the run never reads the terminal.\n& starts the run in the background. The shell prints the job number and the process id, for example [1] 77601.\ndisown removes the job from the shell, so closing the window does not stop the run.\nFollow the log:\ntail -f ~/theia-run.log\nExample output:\nrun run2akdqjis52222v3h2dmrut task tsk2akdqjisls222v27k5c277 (fast_path)\n3aae2f2i task.note [workspace_base] live ⏱ implementer tsk…k5c277 call started\n3puszqgk gateway implementer@fake → ok $0.0000\n2x37t4fz verdict 27k5c277 implementer → ready (1 evidence) “fake-green implementer”\n3ppb2zdj task.phase 27k5c277 ready → verify\nPress Ctrl+C to stop tail. The run keeps working.\nLook at the state at any time, from any terminal in the project folder:\ntheia show <runId>\n<runId> is in the log line run run2… task …. Example output while the run works:\ntsk2akdqjisls222v27k5c277 → verify (1 rounds) Add a usage section to README.md\nhalted: (still open) · 1 gateway calls · $0.0000\naudit chain: intact\nWhen the run has ended, the end of the log holds the summary:\ntail -n 6 ~/theia-run.log\nExample output:\nhalted: completed · 4 gateway calls · $0.0000\naudit chain: intact\nbudget remaining: 10\nrun branch theia/run-ryqdjt — no merges landed (branch left at base for inspection)\n5a4kgvvu task.note [post_run_maintenance]\nmaintenance: 0 summaries refreshed, 0 orphan generations GCed\nTo stop a background run gracefully, find its process id:\npgrep -fl \"theia run\"\nExample output:\n77601 theia run --task Add a usage section to README.md --verify make test --plain --non-interactive\nThen send it the INT signal:\nkill -INT <pid>\nThe log gets the line stopping gracefully — in-flight verdicts will land; resume with: theia run --resume <id>. The id in the hint has no run prefix, for example 2akecb4g2o222vjw2dnktw; theia run --resume accepts both that and the full run2akecb4g2o222vjw2dnktw. The current step finishes, without interrupting the check command, and the run pauses. A second kill -INT stops the run at once.\nWhat was checked on macOS, in zsh and in bash: after disown, closing the terminal window did not stop the run, and the run wrote its summary to the log. In zsh, background jobs run at a lower CPU priority ( ps shows the flag N ), so the check command can run a little slower.\nnohup alone does not keep Theia alive\nA run started with nohup theia run … & died when the terminal window closed: the log stopped in the middle, and theia show reported halted: (still open). In the same test, nohup sleep 300 survived. Use & with disown as shown above, or tmux.\nWay 3: pause now, resume later\nIn the TUI, press q once (or Ctrl+C once). Do not press it again: wait until the current step finishes. The run pauses, and the TUI prints paused — resume with: theia run --resume run2….\nLater, in the same project folder, continue the run:\ntheia run --resume <runId>\nExample of the start of the output:\nrun branch: theia/run-gzhh76 (resumed @ 38e8154)\nresuming run run2akdqjtrt6223o7agzhh76 (paused; budget 10.00 usd remaining)\nThe run continues from where it stopped. Steps that finished are not repeated.\nTo resume in the background, combine the command with Way 2, and then run disown:\ntheia run --resume <runId> --plain --non-interactive > ~/theia-run.log 2>&1 < /dev/null &\nResuming in the background was checked with --plain --non-interactive, the log redirect and &.\ntheia run --resume also continues a run whose process was killed ( halted: (still open) ). More: Control a run and theia run.\nResume in the TUI draws only part of the tree\ntheia run --resume <runId> in the TUI shows only what happens after the resume. The tasks from before the pause are missing, the model calls show under gateway calls (unattributed), and the header has no track and no budget:\n✔ completed · run run2akdqcguzg2232gj4jthgl · · $0.0000 spent · 3 calls · 12 events 0s\ntasks: 0 running · 0 done · anomalies 0 [@ event 12/12 · head]\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ✔ ▶ gateway calls (unattributed) ×3\nThe run itself continues correctly, and the summary after q is right. To see the whole tree, open the run in the browser once it has ended: theia show <runId> --tui shows all steps, before and after the pause. To follow a resumed run while it works, resume it with --plain or --cli.\nWatch from another terminal\ntheia show <runId> prints the current summary, and theia show <runId> --plain prints all events so far. theia show <runId> --tui opens the stored run browser. None of them update on their own: run the command again to see new steps.\nIf you need every detail: the variable THEIA_LOG_FILE makes theia run also append every event, with all its fields, to a file, even while the TUI owns the screen. Follow that file with tail -f from another terminal. See Environment variables.\nOther terminals cannot steer a new run\nIn 0.2.201, theia msg, theia answer and the MCP tool run_stop do not reach a run started with theia run --task or with the MCP tool run_start. The note or the stop request is written to the log, but the run does not read it and keeps working. These commands work only for a run continued with theia run --resume. See Control a run.\nTo steer a run started with theia run --task, press m in its own TUI. If it runs in tmux, attach to the session first.\nThe check command and Ctrl+C\nIn the TUI, q and Ctrl+C are keys, not signals. After the first press, a running --verify command finishes normally.\nIn the --plain and --cli views, Ctrl+C reaches the whole group of processes in the terminal, including the running check command. The command dies, and Theia records a false failure:\n6f65mawa verdict 7xcnwbqx verifier → verify_fail (1 evidence) “$ sleep 30 → exit null (no output)”\nThe task goes back to the implementer, and the run pauses.\nTo pause a --plain run without this, send kill -INT <pid> from another terminal: the signal reaches only Theia.\nA second Ctrl+C in --plain or --cli is a hard stop, the same as a second kill -INT: Theia kills its child processes, prints hard stop: swept N child process(es)/group(s); resume with: … and exits with code 130. The second press was checked with the kill -INT signal, not with the key.\nRuns left open\nA run whose process died without a pause (a closed window, kill -TERM, a second Ctrl+C) has no end record:\ntheia show <runId> reports halted: (still open);\nthe home screen shows ⣽ … in flight and counts the run in · N live;\nthe browser shows it as running.\nContinue such a run with theia run --resume <runId>, or close it:\ntheia run --abandon <runId>\nExample output:\nabandoned run2akdqcdzy6223oud53ns4c — workspaces kept; reclaim with 'theia prune'\nAfter that, the home screen shows the run as ✖ … abandoned and no longer counts it as live. To remove its branch as well, see Clean up after the task box and Run results.\nWarning\nIn 0.2.201, theia run --resume does not refuse a run closed with --abandon: it runs it again. Do not resume abandoned runs.\nSee also\nTUI screens and elements\nNavigation and hotkeys\nControl a run\ntheia run\nMCP server","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/config/models.html":{"title":"Models for tasks","content":"Theia has no model of its own. It gives every step of a task to an agent CLI on your machine: Claude Code ( claude ) or Codex ( codex ). This page shows how to choose the CLI and the model for each role, with examples that load in Theia 0.2.201.\nHow Theia picks a model\nEvery step of a run belongs to a role. For example, implementer writes code and reviewer reviews it.\ngateway.routing in policy.yaml maps each role to a transport. A transport is a named way to call a model: which CLI, which default model and how much access.\ngateway.models can set a different model for a role.\nThe transport starts the CLI with your subscription login. Logins are described in Keys and access.\nA route may point only to a transport listed in a TransportRegistry manifest. theia init writes.theia/config/registry.yaml with every Claude and Codex transport except codex-full.\nRoles Role What it does When it calls a model\nlead\ndecides what to do with a stuck task\ntheia run\nplanner\nsplits work into tasks\ntheia plan; theia run for tasks on the full_spec track\nimplementer\nchanges code\ntheia run\nfresh_implementer\na fresh attempt after two tries of implementer\ntheia run. Without its own route it uses the implementer route, not \"*\"\nverifier\nchecks the result\ntheia run, only when the task has no --verify commands\nreviewer\nreviews the change\ntheia run\narbiter\ndecides after a failed step\ntheia run\nmerger\nmerges a task into the run branch\nnever: Theia merges with git\nindexer\nwrites cards for the code index\ntheia index build and automatic index builds\nextractor\nsummarizes memory\ntheia mem maintain --llm\ndiagnostician\n—\nnever in 0.2.201: theia diagnose works by fixed rules\nnone\none-off questions\ntheia ask, theia probe. Uses routing.none if set, otherwise \"*\"\n\"*\" in gateway.routing is the route for every role that has no entry of its own.\nTransports Transport CLI Default model Access\nclaude\nclaude\nclaude-opus-5\nreads files and runs commands: tools Bash Read Grep Glob\nclaude-write\nclaude\nclaude-opus-5\nalso edits files: mode acceptEdits, tools Bash Read Grep Glob Edit Write\nclaude-index\nclaude\nclaude-sonnet-5\nno tools, thinking turned off; made for the code index\ncodex\ncodex\ngpt-5.6-luna\nread-only sandbox\ncodex-terra\ncodex\ngpt-5.6-terra\nread-only sandbox\ncodex-write\ncodex\ngpt-5.6-luna\nmay write in the task copy ( workspace-write )\ncodex-full\ncodex\ngpt-5.6-luna\nfull access to your machine; not in registry.yaml by default\ncodex-jailed\ncodex\ngpt-5.6-luna\nfull access inside a jail\nRoles that change code ( implementer, fresh_implementer ) need a write transport: claude-write or codex-write.\ncodex-full and codex-jailed belong to sandbox tiers. See Sandbox.\nTheia 0.2.201 has no other providers: no direct API calls and no other agent CLIs.\nHarness at init\ntheia init --harness claude or theia init --harness codex chooses the harness of a new store. Both write the same registry.yaml. Only gateway.routing in policy.yaml differs:\nclaude\ncodex\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: local } spec: run: {} gateway: routing: \"*\": claude implementer: claude-write fresh_implementer: claude-write indexer: claude-index\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: local } spec: run: {} gateway: routing: \"*\": codex implementer: codex-write fresh_implementer: codex-write reviewer: codex-terra\nWithout --harness, init checks which CLI is logged in. It picks codex when Codex answers (also when both answer), claude when only Claude Code answers, and codex with a warning when neither answers. Details: theia init.\nExamples\nPut one of these files into.theia/config/policy.yaml. If your file has other settings, for example under run:, keep them.\nEverything on Claude\nNeeds a Claude Code login. The reviewer uses claude-sonnet-5 instead of the default model.\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: local } spec: run: {} gateway: routing: \"*\": claude implementer: claude-write fresh_implementer: claude-write indexer: claude-index models: reviewer: claude-sonnet-5\nEverything on Codex\nNeeds a Codex login. Both implementer roles use gpt-5.6-terra instead of the default model.\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: local } spec: run: {} gateway: routing: \"*\": codex implementer: codex-write fresh_implementer: codex-write reviewer: codex-terra models: implementer: gpt-5.6-terra fresh_implementer: gpt-5.6-terra\nMixed: Codex writes, Claude reviews\nNeeds both logins. Codex writes the code, Claude Code reviews it and builds the code index.\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: local } spec: run: {} gateway: routing: \"*\": codex implementer: codex-write fresh_implementer: codex-write reviewer: claude indexer: claude-index models: reviewer: claude-opus-5 indexer: claude-sonnet-5\nAfter a change, check the logins with theia doctor and look at the gateway lines of the next run. See How to check.\nModel per role\ngateway.models maps a role to a model id:\nspec: gateway: models: <role>: <model id>\nThe key is a role, not a transport. Two roles on one transport can use different models.\nA role without an entry uses the default model of its transport (see Transports ).\nTheia passes the id to the CLI unchanged:\nClaude Code: claude -p … --model <model id>;\nCodex: codex exec … -m <model id>; when Codex continues a saved session: -c model=\"<model id>\".\nTheia does not check the id. A wrong id fails inside the CLI when the call starts. Which ids work is decided by the CLI and your account.\nSwitch the harness of an existing store\nWarning\nRunning theia init --harness claude in an existing store does not switch it. The command prints harness: claude, but policy.yaml stays as it was.\nEdit.theia/config/policy.yaml instead. From Codex to Claude:\nrouting: - \"*\": codex - implementer: codex-write - fresh_implementer: codex-write - reviewer: codex-terra + \"*\": claude + implementer: claude-write + fresh_implementer: claude-write + indexer: claude-index\nregistry.yaml already lists both families, so nothing else must change. Remove gateway.models entries that name models of the other family. Then check that the CLI you need is logged in:\ntheia doctor\nPer-call Codex settings\ngateway.harnessConfig adds Codex settings to every call of a role. Theia passes each pair as codex exec -c <key>=<value>, with the value written as JSON: text goes in double quotes, numbers and true / false go as they are.\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: local } spec: run: {} gateway: routing: \"*\": codex implementer: codex-write fresh_implementer: codex-write reviewer: codex-terra harnessConfig: arbiter: { model_reasoning_effort: low } indexer: { model_reasoning_effort: low }\nThe example asks Codex for low reasoning effort in the arbiter and indexer roles. On the command line it becomes:\n-c model_reasoning_effort=\"low\"\nWarning\nOnly Codex transports use gateway.harnessConfig. Claude transports ignore it.\nTheia does not check key names or role names. Codex decides whether they are valid. Use only keys from the Codex documentation for your Codex version.\nA value may only be text, a number or true / false. A list or a map stops the command when the file loads: config: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.gateway.harnessConfig.arbiter.model_reasoning_effort: invalid_union).\nA root can add its own values in its.theia/config/. See Rules for one root.\nCost and budget\nTheia prices every model call and adds the prices up. The budget of a run is 10 US dollars by default. It is set by run.maxUsdPerRun (see policy.yaml settings ) or by theia run --budget.\nClaude calls count at list price. Theia takes the price from Claude Code's own report, also when you pay with a subscription. A long run can pause on budget although your subscription costs nothing extra.\nCodex reports only tokens. Theia prices them by a built-in table: gpt-5.6-luna 0.20 and 1.20, gpt-5.6-terra 2 and 12 US dollars per million input and output tokens. A model that is not in the table costs 0 in the accounting.\nWhen the budget runs out, the run pauses. theia run --resume <runId> continues it with the budget that is left; add --budget <N> to set a new budget, 0 removes the limit. See Control a run.\nIf a subscription run keeps pausing on budget:\nRaise run.maxUsdPerRun in policy.yaml or in your user folder.\nStart the run with --budget 0.\nCount Claude calls as free with a Pricing manifest (below).\nPricing manifest: count Claude calls as free\nSave it as.theia/config/pricing.yaml:\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Pricing metadata: { name: subscription } spec: models: claude-opus-5: { inPerMTok: 0, outPerMTok: 0, cacheReadPerMTok: 0 } claude-sonnet-5: { inPerMTok: 0, outPerMTok: 0, cacheReadPerMTok: 0 } transports: claude: { costBasis: actual } claude-write: { costBasis: actual } claude-index: { costBasis: actual }\nmodels — prices in US dollars per million tokens, by model id. The keys are model ids, not transport names.\ntransports — costBasis: actual tells Theia to use these prices instead of Claude Code's report.\nList every Claude model you use. A model without a price keeps Claude Code's figure.\nThis file loads without errors. In the code, costBasis: actual replaces the figure from Claude Code's report with the price from the table; without actual the Claude Code report keeps priority. This guide did not check it on real Claude calls.\nRuns paused by settings\nSome setting problems do not stop the command at once. The run tries the step three times in a row, then pauses. This happens when:\na role has no route, or its route names a transport that is not registered;\nthe task text contains a secret (see Data protection in requests );\nthe text added to a prompt is longer than run.contextRefuseChars (see policy.yaml settings ).\ntheia run exits with code 0\nWhen the problem hits every role that has to work, the run pauses and theia run exits with code 0. In scripts check the halted: line, not only the exit code. When other roles still work, the task ends as discarded and the exit code is 1.\nExample output (no \"*\" route):\ns2kn3t6f!! anomaly environment_failure taskId=2akdqk6fy22235tie77psc role=lead phase=escalated reason=gateway fatal: gateway: no route for ro…\ntkpufktf!! anomaly dispatch_stalled taskId=2akdqk6fy22235tie77psc consecutive=3\nto3ersnm run.paused rounds=5 (resumable)\nhalted: paused · 1 gateway calls · $0.0000 ·!! 4 anomalies\npaused — resume with: theia run --resume run2akdqk6fgw2235sabsuw3u\nFix the file, then continue the same run:\ntheia run --resume <runId>\nExample output after the fix:\nresuming run run2akdqk6fgw2235sabsuw3u (paused; budget 10.00 usd remaining)\nksssgvgq gateway lead@fake → ok $0.0000\nHow to check\nLogins: theia doctor shows whether codex and claude are logged in. It does not show routing. See theia doctor.\nTransport of every call: the gateway lines of theia run --plain and theia show <runId> --plain print role@transport. See What a role really uses.\nModel: the TUI shows the model next to the role only when gateway.models sets it for that role. Without an entry the role uses the default model of its transport.\nModel of every call, read from the store\nNeeds sqlite3. Run it in the folder that holds.theia:\nsqlite3.theia/store.db \"select json_extract(payload,'\\$.role'), json_extract(payload,'\\$.transport'), json_extract(payload,'\\$.model') from events where type='gateway.call' order by id desc limit 10\"\nEvery line is role|transport|model, newest first. An empty model means the default model of the transport.\nExample output:\nreviewer|fake|\nimplementer|fake|\nSee also\nKeys and access\npolicy.yaml settings\nSettings files\nSandbox\ntheia init\ntheia run","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/config/keys.html":{"title":"Keys and access","content":"To do work, Theia needs a logged-in agent CLI. A few keys are optional. This page shows what each login and key is for, where Theia keeps it and how Theia protects secrets in requests to models.\nWhat you need What Needed for How to set it Where it is kept\nClaude Code login\nroles routed to claude, claude-write, claude-index\nclaude auth login\nClaude Code's own files\nCodex login\nroles routed to codex transports\ncodex login\nCodex's own files\nOpenAI API key\nonly embeddings with provider: openai\ntheia setup --openai-key or OPENAI_API_KEY\nconfig.yaml in your user folder\nGitHub token ( PAT )\nupdates and other versions\nthe installer, theia setup --pat or THEIA_GH_TOKEN\nconfig.yaml in your user folder\nOne logged-in CLI is enough. The harnesses line of theia doctor fails only when neither codex nor claude is logged in:\nharnesses: FAIL (neither codex nor claude is authenticated — one is required)\nClaude Code\nTheia runs Claude Code only with your Claude subscription login.\nLog in once: claude auth login, or start claude and use /login.\nTheia checks the login with claude auth status.\ntheia doctor line when you are not logged in:\nclaude code: unavailable (harness-claude: not logged in (run `claude login`; API-key fallback is refused — THE-013))\nAPI keys are not passed to Claude Code\nTheia removes ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN from the environment of every claude process it starts. A setup with only an API key does not work through Theia. Other variables pass through unchanged, for example ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL.\nCodex\nLog in once: codex login.\nTheia checks the login with codex login status. The output must say that you are logged in.\nCodex keeps its own settings in ~/.codex. The CODEX_HOME variable moves this folder. Codex reads it, and so do theia doctor and theia hooks install codex --trust.\nTheia passes its environment to codex unchanged.\ntheia doctor line when you are not logged in:\ncodex subscription: unavailable (harness-codex: not authenticated (codex login status: exit 1))\nTrust settings for Codex hooks are described in Codex.\nOpenAI key for embeddings\nTheia needs this key only when gateway.embeddings.provider is openai. The default provider, local, needs no key. Theia does not give this key to Codex.\nSave the key in your user folder:\ntheia setup --openai-key <key>\n<key> — your OpenAI API key. Or set the OPENAI_API_KEY variable. The variable wins over the saved key.\nWhen the provider is openai, theia doctor shows the result:\nembeddings: openai/text-embedding-3-small — key present\nWithout a key:\nembeddings: openai/text-embedding-3-small — FAIL (no OpenAI key — set OPENAI_API_KEY, theia setup --openai-key <key>, or policy provider: local)\nMore about embeddings: Code index, memory and questions.\nGitHub token (PAT)\nTheia releases are kept in a private GitHub repository. The token is needed for theia self update, for starting another version with --x-release and for the daily update notice. Everyday work does not need it.\nSave or change the token:\ntheia setup --pat <token>\n<token> — a GitHub token with access to Theia releases. How to get one: Install.\nThe installer saves the token it asks for. It does not replace a token that is already saved, so change a token with theia setup --pat.\nThe THEIA_GH_TOKEN variable wins over the saved token.\nGH_TOKEN and GITHUB_TOKEN are not read.\nTHEIA_PAT and THEIA_VERSION are read only by the installer script, not by theia.\nWith a token, the first command of each day (except theia version ) asks GitHub for the newest release. The check takes at most 1.5 seconds. See Update.\ntheia doctor lines:\ngithub pat: present\ngithub pat: absent (release download/self update unavailable)\nWhere keys are kept\ntheia setup writes config.yaml in your user folder. Which folder that is: Your user folder.\ngithub: pat: ghp_XXXXXXXX openai: apiKey: sk-XXXXXXXX\nEvery time Theia writes the file, it sets mode 0600: only your user can read it.\ntheia setup never prints key values:\nwrote github.pat to ~/.config/theia/config.yaml (mode 0600)\ntheia setup writes the whole file again. Keys it does not know are dropped, so do not add your own keys to this file.\nOther keys of this file ( update.channel, dev.root ): theia setup.\nFlag values in theia setup\ntheia setup --pat without a value writes nothing: setup: nothing to write — ….\nThe word after --pat always becomes the token. theia setup --pat --openai-key <key> fails with setup: unknown argument and writes nothing.\nSafe practice\nIn CI and shared shells pass keys through variables ( THEIA_GH_TOKEN, OPENAI_API_KEY ) instead of saving them in a file.\nDo not put config.yaml into a repository and do not share it.\nKeep.theia private. It holds prompts, model replies, command output and the event log. theia init adds.theia/ to.gitignore.\nTo replace a token, run theia setup --pat with the new token. The installer keeps the old one.\nSecrets in the task text are saved\nDo not paste secrets into the text of a task. Theia blocks the model call (see below), but it saves the task text as plain text in.theia/store.db.\nData protection in requests\nBefore any text goes to a model, Theia checks it with five detectors. What happens depends on the data class of the detector:\nprohibited — the call is blocked and nothing is sent;\nrestricted — the match is replaced with a placeholder and the call goes on.\nDetector What it finds Class Placeholder\npem_private_key\na private key block -----BEGIN … PRIVATE KEY-----\nprohibited\n[PRIVATE KEY]\napi_key\nkeys that start with sk- (20 or more characters after it), AKIA (AWS), ghp_ or gho_ (GitHub)\nprohibited\n[API KEY]\ncard_number\n13–19 digits that pass the card checksum (Luhn)\nprohibited\n[CARD NUMBER]\nseed_phrase\n12–24 short lower-case words in a row without common English words\nprohibited\n[SEED PHRASE]\nemail\ne-mail addresses\nrestricted\n[EMAIL]\nThe check covers the prompt of every role.\nCopies of prompts and replies that Theia stores in.theia/objects/ keep the placeholders. A blocked text is not stored there.\nThe code index builder replaces prohibited matches with placeholders instead of blocking, so a file with a key still gets its index card.\nA blocked call shows @blocked in the gateway line, and the run pauses (see Runs paused by settings ):\n6pcskjfa gateway implementer@blocked → fatal\n7sr2k4yw verdict 7lqxy5km implementer → no_progress (0 evidence) “gateway fatal: gateway: prohibited content (api_key) — call…”\nhalted: paused · 3 gateway calls · $0.0000 ·!! 4 anomalies\nA masked call goes on normally. The stored prompt then contains [EMAIL] instead of the address.\nChange a detector\ngateway.maskOverrides changes the class of a detector in your project. Values: prohibited (block), restricted (replace and continue), off (do not check).\nExample: account numbers in your data look like card numbers and block calls. Save this as.theia/config/masking.yaml:\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: masking } spec: gateway: maskOverrides: card_number: restricted\nWith this file, a text with a card-like number goes to the model with [CARD NUMBER] in its place. This file combines with policy.yaml because the two files set different keys. If another file also sets gateway.maskOverrides, the merge rule applies.\nLimits in 0.2.201\nThe detectors look only for the patterns in the table. Passwords, tokens of other formats, phone numbers and names are not found.\nThe dataClasses field of transports in registry.yaml has no effect. The field is required, but Theia does not use it to allow or deny anything.\nSee also\nModels for tasks\nEnvironment variables\nSettings files\nFirst setup\nCodex","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/config/policy.html":{"title":"policy.yaml settings","content":"This page lists the Policy settings that Theia 0.2.201 really reads: type, default and meaning. How several files combine and which one wins: Settings files.\nWhere these settings go\nThe settings go under spec of a kind: Policy manifest: in.theia/config/policy.yaml, in another.yaml file next to it, or in the config/ folder of your user folder. There are five sections: run, gateway, ui, maintenance, memory. Every section and every key is optional.\nExample:\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: local } spec: run: maxUsdPerRun: 5 preTask: - { run: \"npm ci\", mustPass: true } contextAlertChars: 100000 gateway: routing: \"*\": claude implementer: claude-write fresh_implementer: claude-write indexer: claude-index ui: defaultView: plain maintenance: indexPolicy: alert storeAlertGb: 10 memory: analyzer: snowball\nThis file sets a budget of 5 US dollars per run and runs npm ci before the first implement step of each task. It prints a notice when the text added to a prompt is longer than 100 000 characters. It sends the roles to Claude Code, makes plain the default view and warns when the store grows past 10 GB.\nTypes in the tables: int — a whole number; number — any number; bool — true or false; list — a YAML list; map — YAML keys with values. A value out of bounds or of the wrong type stops the command when the file loads. The texts of those errors and what to do about them: Mistakes in settings files.\nrun Budget and limits Key Type Default What it does\nmaxUsdPerRun\nnumber above 0\n10\nBudget of one run in US dollars. theia run --budget overrides it; --budget 0 removes the limit. 0 is not allowed in the file: config: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.run.maxUsdPerRun: too_small).\nmaxRoundsPerTask\nint 1–100\n10\nHow many rounds one task may use. theia run --max-rounds overrides it.\nmaxRescopes\nint 0–10\n2\nHow many times the lead may restart a task with a new direction. Each restart resets the round count. theia run --max-rescopes overrides it.\nstopAndFixAfterFailures\nint 1–10\n3\nFailed checks in a row before the task goes to the lead.\nstallPauseAfter\nint 0–50\n3\nSteps in a row without a result before the run pauses. 0 — never pause.\nfreshPerspectiveAfter\nint 1–10\n2\nImplement attempts before fresh_implementer takes over.\nmaxDelegationDepth\nint 0–3\n1\nHow deep subtasks may create their own subtasks.\nmaxDelegationFanout\nint 1–16\n4\nHow many subtasks one decision may create.\npoolSizes\nmap: role to int 1–8\n2 for each role\nHow many tasks one role handles at the same time. lead and merger always use 1. Role names are not checked.\nTask copies and access Key Type Default What it does\nworkspaceMode\ncopy or clone\ncopy\nHow the copy of the repository for a task is made. copy also copies untracked files such as node_modules and.env, so checks run without a new install. clone does not copy them.\nsandboxTier\nstandard, jailed-full or trusted-full\nstandard\nHow much access the roles that write code get. See Sandbox.\njail.backend\nauto or container\nauto\nIsolation technology for jailed-full and theia jail. See Sandbox.\njail.image\ntext\n—\nContainer image. Required with jail.backend: container.\nresources.memoryMax\ntext such as 3G or 512M\n—\nMemory cap for every process tree that Theia starts. Needs Linux with systemd. On other systems a run with any resources key refuses to start, unless jail.backend is container.\nresources.cpuQuota\nint 10–6400\n—\nCPU cap in percent; 100 is one core. Same limits as memoryMax.\nresources.tasksMax\nint 16–65536\n—\nCap on processes and threads. Same limits as memoryMax.\nchangesetPartialFailure\nrevert or freeze\nrevert\nFor work across several roots: when one part fails after others were merged, undo the merged parts ( revert ) or stop and name them ( freeze ).\nThis is how theia run refuses resources on macOS (exit code 1):\nrun: run.resources is configured but unenforceable here — run.resources needs linux cgroups (systemd-run); platform is darwin (unset run.resources to run uncapped DELIBERATELY)\nContext and checks before work Key Type Default What it does\ncontextAlertChars\nint from 1000\n200000\nPrints a notice when the text added to one prompt (rules, files included with @ ) is longer. Everything is still sent.\ncontextRefuseChars\nint from 1000\n—\nStops a step before the model call when the added text is longer. The run pauses.\nblueprintMinImportance\nint 1–9\n—\nLeaves entries below this importance out of the code map sent to agents and prints how many were left out. Agents can still reach them with search tools.\npreTask\nlist of up to 8 items { run, mustPass }\nempty\nShell commands before the first implement step of each task. With mustPass: true (the default) a failed command stops the task before any model call.\nplanVerifyPreflight\nalways or disable\ndisable\ntheia plan runs the check commands of a new plan once.\nA run with preTask prints a line for each task:\ngpcbw3xl pre-task hxo6hfs6 1 gate(s), 0 failed\nAgent tools and rules Key Type Default What it does\nmcpBelt.enabled\nbool\ntrue\nGives agents Theia's own MCP tools: code map, code search, symbol lookup, memory search.\nmcpBelt.tools\nlist of up to 16 names\ncodebase_blueprint, codebase_search, codebase_symbol, memory_search\nWhich tools. Also available: memory_add, memory_tree, run_audit, run_diagnose, run_check. Names are not checked when the file loads: a file with a typo loads, and the error mcp-server: unknown tool appears when the run starts the MCP server for the first model call. On the fake test transport the server does not start, so the error is not seen.\nrulesSoT.enabled\nbool\nfalse\nRules reach agents only through Context manifests. See rulesSoT mode.\nrulesSoT.maskFiles\nlist\nAGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md,.cursorrules,.cursor/rules,.ai\nFiles and folders blanked in task copies when rulesSoT is on.\nQuestions and sessions Key Type Default What it does\ninteraction\nterminal or bus\nterminal\nWhere clarifying questions go: to your terminal, or to the control bus, where theia answer or an MCP client answers them. theia run --interaction overrides it.\ninteractionWaitSeconds\nint from 5\n600\nHow long a question on the control bus waits for an answer.\nsessionContinuity\noff or sigma-shared\noff\nKeeps the stable start of prompts identical between rounds and records its checksum in the run. theia run --sessions and --no-sessions override it.\ngateway Key Type Default What it does\nrouting\nmap: role to transport\nwritten by theia init\nWhich transport serves each role; \"*\" — every other role. See Models for tasks.\nmodels\nmap: role to model id\nempty\nModel per role. See Model per role.\nharnessConfig\nmap: role to a map of settings (values are text, a number or bool)\nempty\nExtra Codex settings per role. Each pair is passed as codex exec -c key=value with the value written as JSON: text in quotes, for example -c model_reasoning_effort=\"low\". Claude transports ignore it. See Per-call Codex settings.\nmaskOverrides\nmap: detector to prohibited, restricted or off\nempty\nChanges the class of a detector. See Data protection in requests.\nmaxRetries\nint 0–8\n3\nRetries after a temporary error, such as a rate limit or a network error.\nretryBaseMs\nint from 10\n500\nPause before the first retry in milliseconds. The pause doubles on each retry.\nembeddings.provider\nlocal or openai\nlocal\nWhere embeddings are made: on your computer, or through the OpenAI API with your key (see OpenAI key ).\nembeddings.model\ntext\nall-MiniLM-L6-v2 for local, text-embedding-3-small for openai\nEmbedding model. Local models: all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (about 23 MB) and bge-small-en-v1.5 (about 34 MB).\nembeddings.autoDownload\nbool\ntrue\nThe local model downloads itself the first time it is needed. With false, download it first with theia setup --fetch-embeddings (not shown in theia help ).\nExample: a local model without automatic download, in.theia/config/embeddings.yaml:\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: embeddings } spec: gateway: embeddings: provider: local model: bge-small-en-v1.5 autoDownload: false\nAfter you change provider or model, rebuild the search with theia mem reindex and theia index reindex. Until then searches do not use the old vectors and print:\ntheia: vector lane off — generation embedded by local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2/384, current embedder is local/bge-small-en-v1.5/384; run `theia mem reindex` to rebuild\nui Key Type Default What it does\ndefaultView\nsummary, plain, cli or tui\ntui\nView of theia run when you give no view flag. Without a terminal tui and cli fall back to plain. theia show does not use this key.\nmaintenance Key Type Default What it does\nindexPolicy\nauto, alert or manual\nalert\nWhat happens with a stale code index. auto rebuilds stale cards with model calls when a run or a plan starts, when a Claude Code session starts, and in theia serve. alert only prints a notice. manual also skips the free cleanup after a run.\nindexBuildBudgetUsd\nnumber above 0\n1\nBudget of one automatic index build in US dollars.\nserveRefreshSeconds\nint from 10\n300\nHow often theia serve checks whether the index is up to date.\nstoreAlertGb\nnumber above 0\n5\nWarn when.theia is larger than this many gigabytes.\nindexCache\nbool\ntrue\nReuse index cards from other projects for identical files. The shared cache lives in your cache folder.\nWith indexPolicy: auto a run starts like this:\nindex: 1 stale — plan quality degrades; building (budget $0.5)…\nThe storage warning of theia doctor and of the end of a run:\nstorage:.theia at 3.8 MB (alert ≥0.00001 GB) — reclaim with 'theia prune'\nmemory Key Type Default What it does\nanalyzer\nsnowball or plain\nsnowball\nHow the word index of memory splits words. snowball also reduces words to their stem. After a change run theia mem reindex.\ntimeLane\nbool\ntrue\nExact dates in a question narrow memory search to facts of that time.\nsentenceGraph\noff, h0 or h1\noff\nAdds a sentence-level part to the memory index. Do not turn it on in 0.2.201, see the warning.\nmemory.sentenceGraph turns vector search off\nWith h0 or h1, memory search stops using embeddings, even right after theia mem reindex. Searches then find facts only by words and print:\ntheia: vector lane off — generation embedded by local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2/384+sent@2, current embedder is local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2/384; run `theia mem reindex` to rebuild\nKeep sentenceGraph: off. After switching back, run theia mem reindex.\nFields with no effect\nEvery Policy key on this page is used by Theia 0.2.201. Fields without effect exist only in TransportRegistry manifests: Theia uses only name there. The required fields provider, dataClasses and owner and the optional field region are checked for format and then ignored.\nSee also\nSettings files\nModels for tasks\nKeys and access\nRules and prompts\nSandbox","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/config/environment.html":{"title":"Environment variables","content":"Environment variables that change how theia works, with their default and who reads them. At the end there is a short list of names from tests and release scripts that you can ignore.\nVariables you can set Keys and versions Variable What it does When not set Read by\nTHEIA_GH_TOKEN\nGitHub token ( PAT ). Wins over github.pat in config.yaml.\nthe saved token\ntheia self update, --x-release, the daily update check, theia doctor\nOPENAI_API_KEY\nOpenAI key for embeddings with provider: openai. Wins over openai.apiKey in config.yaml. When it is set, the setup wizard does not ask for a key.\nthe saved key\ncommands that build or search embeddings, theia doctor, the setup wizard\nTHEIA_X_RELEASE\nStarts another Theia version, like the --x-release flag. The flag wins.\nthe running version\ntheia, before any command\nA wrong THEIA_X_RELEASE value stops every command, theia version included:\ninvalid release version 'bogus' (expected e.g. 1.2.3 or v1.2.3)\nKeys are described in Keys and access, other versions in Update.\nFolders Variable What it does When not set Read by\nXDG_CONFIG_HOME\nThe settings folder becomes $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/theia on every system.\nsee Your user folder\nall commands\nXDG_DATA_HOME\nThe data folder becomes $XDG_DATA_HOME/theia: stores of folders outside git, vendor files.\nLinux ~/.local/share/theia, macOS ~/Library/Application Support/theia\nall commands\nXDG_CACHE_HOME\nThe cache folder becomes $XDG_CACHE_HOME/theia: local models, index cache, downloaded versions.\nLinux ~/.cache/theia, macOS ~/Library/Caches/theia\nall commands\nAPPDATA, LOCALAPPDATA\nWindows base folders for settings, data and cache.\n~\\AppData\\Roaming, ~\\AppData\\Local\nall commands on Windows\nTHEIA_MODELS_DIR\nFolder for local embedding models.\nmodels in the cache folder\nembeddings, theia doctor, theia setup --fetch-embeddings\nTHEIA_INDEX_CACHE_DIR\nFolder of the shared index cache.\nindex-cache in the cache folder\ntheia index build and automatic index builds\nHF_ENDPOINT\nHugging Face mirror to download local embedding models from.\nhttps://huggingface.co\nmodel download\nOn macOS the old folders ~/.local/share/theia and ~/.cache/theia win when they exist and the new folders do not, the same way as for settings. The installer creates ~/.local/share/theia.\nOutput and logs Variable What it does When not set Read by\nTHEIA_LOG_FILE\ntheia run appends every event to this file: the same line as in --plain, and under it a ⤷ line with the event details as JSON. Useful while the TUI takes the screen. If the file cannot be created, for example the folder does not exist, Theia says nothing: the run goes on, there is no log.\nno file\ntheia run\nTHEIA_NO_SPINNER\nStatic symbols instead of animation. Same as theia run --no-spinner.\nanimation\nthe TUI of theia run and theia show\nNO_COLOR\nTurns colors off.\ncolors\nthe TUI and the home screen\nEDITOR\nProgram that opens a stored prompt or command output from the TUI. It may contain arguments, for example code --wait.\nless\nthe TUI\nTHEIA_LOG_FILE syntax:\nTHEIA_LOG_FILE=<file> theia run --task \"<text>\"\n<file> — the log file. <text> — the task.\nExample: the first lines of the file. The header names the run and its start time, then come pairs of lines \"event — details\":\n# theia run run2akec65qow2222b6kma2mn — 2026-09-15T07:55:37.623Z\nawcfkaqm run.created budget=$10 track=fast_path ⤷ {\"task\":\"t\",\"planId\":null,\"track\":\"fast_path\",\"trackOverride\":null,\"budgetUsd\":10,\"verify\":[\"true\"],\"record\":false,\"branch\":\"theia/run-kma2mn\",\"baseSha\":\"725a78e07c76216af93039fa26c41df48d5ff7c6\"}\na35avm37 task.created ah45rvrt [fast_path] t ⤷ {\"taskId\":\"2akec65r6c2222ah45rvrt\",\"desc\":\"t\",\"track\":\"fast_path\",\"depth\":0,\"deps\":[]}\nThe ⤷ lines exist only in the log; --plain does not print them. After the run, check that the file exists: with an unreachable path there is none.\nOther tools Variable What it does When not set Read by\nCODEX_HOME\nCodex's own folder. theia doctor and theia hooks install codex --trust look for config.toml there.\n~/.codex\nCodex, theia doctor, theia hooks install codex\nTHEIA_BASH\nWindows: the bash.exe that Theia runs commands with.\nGit Bash, found automatically\nall commands on Windows\nOTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT\nSends traces and metrics to an OpenTelemetry collector. See Metrics and tracing.\nno export\nall commands\nOTEL_SERVICE_NAME\nService name in traces and metrics.\ntheia\nall commands\nTHEIA_VENDOR_DIR\nFor platform developers. Use this folder of prompts, rules and skills instead of the installed vendor files. A path that does not exist stops exactly these commands with exit code 1: run, plan, ask, probe, fork, compare, diagnose, serve, eval, replay, msg, answer. Others, for example theia index status, still work.\nthe installed files\nthe listed commands, theia doctor\nMessage when the THEIA_VENDOR_DIR path does not exist:\ntheia: no vendor deliverables found (lib/vendor missing and no installed set) — reinstall via get-theia, or point THEIA_VENDOR_DIR at a vendor tree (THE-065)\nWhat Theia changes for agent CLIs\nTheia starts claude and codex with its own environment, with these changes:\nclaude does not get ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN, CLAUDECODE and any variable that starts with CLAUDE_CODE_. So a claude started by Theia from inside a Claude Code session starts clean.\nFor the claude-index transport Theia adds MAX_THINKING_TOKENS=0.\nAll other variables reach claude unchanged, for example ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL.\ncodex gets the environment unchanged, including CODEX_HOME and OPENAI_API_KEY.\nInternal names\nYou may see these names in code, logs or scripts. Do not set them.\nTests, evaluation and the launcher: THEIA_FAKE_RULEBOOK, THEIA_LAUNCHER_HOP, THEIA_SWEBL_NO_CAP, THEIA_DATASETS_DIR, THEIA_LIVE_LOCAL_EMBED.\nTheia sets them itself on Windows: MSYS_NO_PATHCONV, MSYS2_ARG_CONV_EXCL.\nRelease scripts of the Theia team, not read by theia: THEIA_BIN, EXPECT_VERSION, GH_TOKEN, RELEASES_PAT, PUBLISH_TOKEN, THEIA_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID, THEIA_BUILD_VERSION, THEIA_SEA_NODE.\nRead only by the installer script: THEIA_PAT, THEIA_VERSION. See Install.\nNot environment variables at all: THEIA_MCP_TOOLS, THEIA_CONTROL_TOOLS and THEIA_SKILL are names inside the code (tool name lists and the text of the /theia skill); THEIA_PATCH_EOF is an end-of-text marker inside an evaluation script. Setting them changes nothing.\nSee also\nKeys and access\nSettings files\nUpdate\nMetrics and tracing","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/config/rules-prompts.html":{"title":"Rules and prompts","content":"How your project rules reach the agents: files that the agent CLIs read themselves, rules that Theia adds to prompts, and your own additions to role prompts. The page describes only what works in 0.2.201.\nThree ways rules reach agents Way Files Who uses them\nNative rules files\nAGENTS.md for Codex, CLAUDE.md for Claude Code\nThe CLI itself. Theia leaves these files in the task copy\nContext manifest\nAny text or file you list. theia init creates one from the rules files it finds\nTheia adds the text to prompts\nPrompt additions\n.theia/prompts/<ROLE>.md,.theia/prompts/PROMPTS.md and others\nTheia adds the text to the prompt of a role\nWhat theia init finds\ntheia init looks for rules files at the top of the project, and in every root of a multi-root workspace:\nFile What init does\nAGENTS.md\nNothing. Codex reads it itself.\nCLAUDE.md\nNothing. Claude Code reads it itself.\n.cursorrules\nAdds an entry to.theia/config/context-rules.yaml.\n.github/copilot-instructions.md\nAdds an entry.\n.cursor/rules/, files at any depth\nAdds one entry for the whole folder.\n.ai/, files at any depth\nAdds one entry for the whole folder.\nNot found: GEMINI.md, AGENT.md,.windsurfrules,.clinerules/, CONVENTIONS.md and empty folders. Add such files yourself with a Context manifest.\nExample output of theia init:\nai rules: seeded 4 into ~/projects/app/.theia/config/context-rules.yaml (cursorrules, github-copilot-instructions-md, cursor-rules-rules, ai-rules)\nai rules: AGENTS.md left to codex's native read; CLAUDE.md left to Claude Code's native read (set run.rulesSoT.enabled to route natives through the audited context path instead)\nThe last hint is not accurate: run.rulesSoT hides AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md but does not add them to prompts. See rulesSoT mode.\nThe generated file:\n# generated by `theia init` — merge-managed rules manifest. # Re-running init only ADDS entries for rules files it has never seeded # (the memory is.context-rules.seeded beside this file). Edit or delete # entries freely — your changes are preserved. If you delete a rules FILE, # also delete its entry here (a missing `path:` refuses loudly at run # start, by design). apiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Context metadata: { name: detected-rules } spec: entries: - name: cursorrules source: { path: \".cursorrules\" } inject: always - name: github-copilot-instructions-md source: { path: \".github/copilot-instructions.md\" } inject: always - name: cursor-rules-rules source: { glob: \".cursor/rules/**\" } inject: always - name: ai-rules source: { glob: \".ai/**\" } inject: always\ntheia init run again adds only files it has never added before. Your edits stay. An entry you deleted stays deleted, and init prints ai rules: … already wired (context-rules.yaml).\nThe names of added entries are kept in.theia/config/.context-rules.seeded.\nIf you delete a rules file, delete its entry too. A missing file stops the next run at start.\ntheia doctor lists the rules files it finds:\nai rules: AGENTS.md (native·codex) · CLAUDE.md (native·claude) ·.cursorrules (injected) ·.github/copilot-instructions.md (injected) ·.cursor/rules/ (injected·2 files) ·.ai/ (injected)\nnative·codex and native·claude mean that the CLI reads the file itself. injected means that Theia adds the file to prompts. theia doctor decides this from the files on disk, not from the manifest: a file whose entry you deleted still shows injected.\nYour own rules: the Context manifest\nA Context manifest lists texts that Theia adds to agent prompts. Save this as.theia/config/team-rules.yaml:\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Context metadata: { name: team-rules } spec: entries: - name: house-style source: { inline: \"Write a unit test for every bug fix.\" } - name: review-checklist source: { path: docs/review-checklist.md } inject: { roles: [ reviewer ] } - name: db-rules source: { glob: \"docs/db/*.md\" } inject: { pathTriggers: [ \"migrations/**\" ] } budgetChars: 4000\nIn this example:\nhouse-style goes to every role;\nreview-checklist goes only to reviewer;\ndb-rules goes to a role only when the task changes files in migrations/. Its text is cut after 4000 characters.\nSource Value Notes\ninline\ntext up to 20 000 characters\n—\npath\na file path from the top of the project\nAbsolute paths do not work. A missing file stops the run at start.\nglob\na file pattern from the top of the project\n** — any depth, * — inside one folder,? — one character..git,.theia and node_modules are skipped. No match: a warning, the entry is skipped.\nEntry names: lower-case Latin letters, digits, - and _, up to 64 characters, unique across all files.\nbudgetChars (100–20 000) cuts the text of the entry. Without it the whole text is sent.\nWhen the text added to one prompt is too long, run.contextAlertChars prints a notice and run.contextRefuseChars stops the step. See policy.yaml settings.\nA missing file stops the run with this message:\ncontext: entry 'gone' names missing file docs/NOPE.md\nIn a prompt every entry is a block after the code map:\n--- context: house-style ---\nWrite a unit test for every bug fix.\nAt start the run prints the names of all loaded entries, also of entries that no role gets:\nhaceub5u context injected: house-style, review-checklist, db-rules\nWhen an entry is used\ninject: always (the default) — every role.\ninject: { roles: [...] } — only the listed roles.\ninject: { tracks: [fast_path] } or [full_spec] — only tasks on that track.\ninject: { pathTriggers: [...] } — only when paths match the patterns.\nWith several conditions, the entry is used when any of them matches.\npathTriggers and the task text\nTheia matches pathTriggers against the files that the task has already changed and committed. Paths written in the task text count only when there is no task copy, that is with theia run --in-place. In a normal run an entry with pathTriggers does not reach the first implement attempt, even when the task text names a matching file.\nrulesSoT mode\nrulesSoT makes Context manifests the only way rules reach agents. Save this as.theia/config/rules-sot.yaml:\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: rules-sot } spec: run: rulesSoT: { enabled: true }\nWith the mode on:\nIn every task copy Theia masks AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md,.cursorrules and every file in.cursor/rules/ and.ai/. A file tracked by git is replaced with the one line # masked by theia (rulesSoT): rules reach the model only via audited context manifests and marked skip-worktree in git, so the placeholder cannot land in a commit. An untracked file is deleted. Your own checkout does not change.\nCodex calls also get -c project_doc_max_bytes=0, so Codex does not read project documents itself.\nOther files to blank: run.rulesSoT.maskFiles in policy.yaml settings.\nrulesSoT does not add AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md to prompts\nWith the mode on, agents stop seeing AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md at all. To keep these rules, add them to a Context manifest, for example.theia/config/native-rules.yaml below. Theia reads the files from your checkout, so the masked copies do not matter.\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Context metadata: { name: native-rules } spec: entries: - name: agents-md source: { path: AGENTS.md } - name: claude-md source: { path: CLAUDE.md }\nRole prompts\nTheia builds the prompt of each role from the base prompt shipped with Theia and your additions. Additions are only appended; the base prompt cannot be replaced.\nFile in.theia/prompts/ Added to\n<ROLE>.md with the role name in upper case: IMPLEMENTER.md, FRESH_IMPLEMENTER.md, REVIEWER.md, VERIFIER.md, ARBITER.md, LEAD.md, PLANNER.md\nthe prompt of that role\nASK.md\ntheia ask\nPROBE.md\ntheia probe\nPROMPTS.md, section ### <name>\nthe role or command with that name: implementer, reviewer, ask, probe and so on. The name is case-insensitive. The section ends at the next line that starts with ###.\nOrder in the prompt: base prompt, then <ROLE>.md, then the section of PROMPTS.md, then the same files of the task's root, then the reply format.\nExample..theia/prompts/REVIEWER.md:\nReject changes that have no tests.\n.theia/prompts/PROMPTS.md:\n### implementer Keep functions under 40 lines. ### ask Answer in one short paragraph.\nWith these files the reviewer gets the first text, the implementer gets its section, and theia ask gets the ask section. No role gets the sections of other roles.\ntheia plan uses only the project's.theia/prompts/ for the planner. Prompts of roots are not used there.\nFor a store outside git the folder is prompts/ in the store folder. See Store and project roots.\nRules and skills shipped with Theia\nTheia ships vendor files: base role prompts, rule documents and skill documents. On a Mac set up with the installer and on Linux they are in ~/.local/share/theia/vendor/current/.\nprompts/kernel.yaml holds the base prompts of the roles. Theia always uses them.\nrules/*.md and skills/*.md are shipped, but no part of Theia 0.2.201 reads them. They do not reach agents.\nTo use one of these rules, copy the file into your repository and add a path entry to a Context manifest.\nNote\nFolders.theia/rules/ and.theia/skills/ and a theia skills command do not exist in 0.2.201. theia hooks install claude installs something else: a Claude Code skill for controlling Theia. See Claude Code.\nRules for one root\nIn a multi-root workspace a root may keep its own.theia/config/ and.theia/prompts/ folders without a store. See Store and project roots. For tasks in that root Theia adds:\nContext manifests of the root. Their paths start at the root. An entry with the same name replaces the project's entry.\nPrompt files of the root, after the project's files.\nPolicy with only two keys: run.preTask replaces the project's list, and gateway.harnessConfig is merged role by role.\nAny other Policy key or manifest kind in a root stops runs:\nconfig: api/.theia/config/p.yaml: root overlays accept only gateway.harnessConfig and run.preTask (spec.run: unrecognized_keys) — other policy keys live in the workspace home's.theia/config/\nSee also\nSettings files\npolicy.yaml settings\nStore and project roots\nClaude Code\nCodex","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/config/sandbox.html":{"title":"Sandbox","content":"What limits agents and check commands, which sandbox tiers exist, and where theia jail works.\nWhat you get by default\nThe default tier is standard. On this tier Theia itself isolates nothing. The only limits come from the modes in which Theia starts the engines.\nEngine Role How Theia starts the engine\nCodex\nImplementer ( implementer )\ncodex exec -s workspace-write: Codex allows writing only in the working folder\nCodex\nOther roles\ncodex exec -s read-only: read only\nClaude Code\nImplementer ( implementer )\nclaude -p with --permission-mode acceptEdits and the tools Bash Read Grep Glob Edit Write\nClaude Code\nOther roles\nclaude -p with the tools Bash Read Grep Glob; the indexer ( indexer ) works without tools\nAgents work in a copy of the repository,.theia/runs/<run>/ws-<task>, not in your working copy.\nTheia itself merges into the run branch with plain git.\nWhich engine works in which role is set by routing: see Models for tasks.\nRisks\nCheck commands and agents run on your machine\nTheia runs the commands from --verify with bash -lc directly on your machine, with your user's rights and no sandbox. A command runs in the task copy. Each command gets up to 5 minutes.\nClaude Code in Theia's roles can run shell commands: the Bash tool is allowed. Those commands also run with your user's rights.\nHow strictly Codex enforces the workspace-write and read-only modes is up to Codex. Theia does not check it.\nWhat to do:\nRun Theia only in repositories you trust.\nPass to --verify only commands that change nothing outside the project.\nFor full isolation, run Theia on a separate machine or in a virtual machine.\nSandbox tiers\nThe tier is set by the key run.sandboxTier in the policy file.theia/config/policy.yaml. It changes only the implementer roles: implementer and fresh_implementer.\nTier What the implementer gets What it needs Linux macOS\nstandard\nThe engine modes from the table above\nNothing\nYes\nYes\njailed-full\nFull access inside theia jail: the system is read-only, writing goes to the working folder\nA working theia jail and the codex-jailed transport\nYes, with bubblewrap\nNo\ntrusted-full\nFull access to the machine, no sandbox\nThe codex-full transport in registry.yaml\nYes\nYes\nstandard\nThe default. Nothing to set up.\njailed-full\nThe implementer is always Codex (transport codex-jailed ), even when the project is set up for Claude Code. The codex-jailed transport is already in the registry.yaml that theia init creates.\nThe tier needs a working sandbox: Linux with bwrap (bubblewrap) and user namespaces. Check the jail line in theia doctor. At the start of a run Theia prints a warning:\n!! sandboxTier jailed-full: implementer agents run danger-full-access INSIDE theia jail (RO host, RW workspace)\nOn macOS the run does not start:\nrun: sandboxTier jailed-full requires the theia jail, which is unavailable here: no sandbox backend on darwin yet (the container backend is the queued arm — docs/13); use standard or trusted-full (linux: bwrap + user namespaces; windows: AppContainer; see 'theia doctor')\ntrusted-full\nThe implementer is Codex (transport codex-full ) with full access to the machine and no sandbox.\nThe codex-full transport is not in registry.yaml by default. Without it the run does not start:\nrun: sandboxTier trusted-full requires the 'codex-full' transport in a TransportRegistry manifest (.theia/config) — the opt-in is manifest-recorded by design (R2)\nTo allow this tier, add a codex-full row to spec.transports in.theia/config/registry.yaml, following the codex-jailed row:\n- { name: codex-full, provider: openai-subscription, dataClasses: [ restricted, permitted ], owner: local }\nWith this transport the run starts with a warning:\n!! sandboxTier trusted-full: implementer agents run with FULL HOST ACCESS, unjailed (manifest-recorded opt-in)\nAlert\nWith trusted-full the model can change and delete any file your user can reach, not only the project files. Turn this tier on only on a separate machine or in a virtual machine.\nChoose a tier\nOpen.theia/config/policy.yaml and set the key in the spec.run section. Example for the default tier:\nspec: run: sandboxTier: standard\nLeave the other sections of the file, such as gateway, as they are. All policy keys are in policy.yaml settings.\ntheia jail\ntheia jail runs one command in a sandbox: the whole system is read-only, and writing is allowed only in the folders you allow.\ntheia jail [--rw=<path>]... [--] <command> [args]\nOn Linux it needs bwrap (bubblewrap) and user namespaces. On macOS the command does not work; every call ends like this:\ntheia jail -- echo hi\njail: unavailable — no sandbox backend on darwin yet (the container backend is the queued arm — docs/13); use standard or trusted-full\nThe code has a container mode ( --backend container, Docker or Podman). It does not depend on the system, and on macOS theia jail with it does reach Docker: without --image the command refuses, and with an image while Docker is not running it prints jail: unavailable — failed to connect to the docker API …. Running a command inside a container was not verified in this guide. All flags and the exact messages are in the theia jail reference.\nSupport by system Feature Linux macOS\nTier standard\nYes\nYes\nTier trusted-full\nYes\nYes\nTier jailed-full\nYes, with bwrap\nNo\ntheia jail\nYes, with bwrap\nNo\nResource limits: run.resources and theia jail --mem, --cpus, --pids\nYes, with systemd-run --user\nNo\ntheia doctor shows what is available on your machine:\ntheia doctor\nExample lines on macOS:\njail (none ): unavailable (no sandbox backend on darwin yet (the container backend is the queued arm — docs/13); use standard or trusted-full) — jailed-full tier unavailable\njail (container ): docker ready (image is per-policy)\nresources: unavailable (run.resources needs linux cgroups (systemd-run); platform is darwin) — run.resources would refuse\ndocker ready only means that docker --version worked ( podman is looked for first, then docker ). The line does not check that Docker is running or that an image exists.\nSee also\ntheia jail\ntheia doctor\npolicy.yaml settings\nModels for tasks\nPlatforms and run modes","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/config/workspaces.html":{"title":"Store and project roots","content":"Where Theia creates the store, how commands find it, and how to build a workspace from several repositories.\nWhere the store is created\ntheia init chooses the place:\nWhere you run it Command Where the store goes\nRoot of a git repository\ntheia init\n.theia at the root\nSubfolder of a git repository\ntheia init\n.theia at the repository root\nAny folder\ntheia init --here\n.theia in this folder\nFolder outside git\ntheia init\nThe user data folder, plus a row in the workspaces.yaml registry\nThe first-setup wizard does not follow these rules: it always creates.theia in the current folder. See First-setup wizard.\nIn a git repository\ntheia init finds the root of the working copy and puts.theia there. One working copy gets one store, whichever subfolder you run the command from.\nExample output from a subfolder:\nstore: ~/projects/app/.theia (worktree root — one store per checkout; pass --here to pin it to this subfolder instead)\ntheia init adds the line.theia/ to the.gitignore at the root.\nThe --here flag\n--here puts the store exactly in the current folder:\ntheia init --here\nExample output in the src subfolder:\nstore: ~/projects/app/src/.theia (detached — --here pins the store to this folder)\nThe line.theia/ is added to the.gitignore of that same folder, even outside git.\nIn a repository subfolder you get a separate store. Commands run from that subfolder and below find it, not the store at the root.\nOutside git\nIn a folder outside git, theia init does not create.theia. The store goes into the user data folder, and the link to your folder is written to the workspaces.yaml registry.\nExample output for the folder ~/notes:\nstore: ~/.local/share/theia/stores/notes-f941a13e (user-home store — this folder is not a git worktree and stays clean; registered in ~/.config/theia/workspaces.yaml; pass --here for a local.theia instead)\nThe store folder name is your folder's name in lower case plus 8 characters of a hash of the full path.\nA workspace outside git does not fully work\nIf the store was created for a folder outside git ( theia init or theia init --here ), the commands theia index status, theia index build, theia index search and theia ask fail with:\nfatal: not a git repository (or any of the parent directories):.git\ntheia index status --brief then prints theia: no index. The theia mem commands work. For work on code, create the store in a git repository.\nHow commands find the store\nA command looks for the store from the current folder upward. In each folder it checks, in order:\na.theia folder with a store.db file;\na row for this folder in the workspaces.yaml registry, if that store has a store.db.\nThe first store found is used. That is why commands work from any project subfolder:\ntheia index status --brief\nExample output from the src subfolder:\ntheia: 0 indexed\nMessages during the search:\nMessage What it means\ntheia: <folder>/.theia has no store.db — per-root overlay, continuing upward\nA.theia folder without a store. Theia treats it as extra settings and looks higher\ntheia: registry maps <folder> to <store> but no store.db is there — ignoring the row (re-run 'theia init' or edit <path>/workspaces.yaml)\nA registry row points to a store that does not exist. Theia skips the row\nIf there is no store anywhere above, the command exits with code 1:\ntheia: no.theia/ found from ~/projects/tmp upward (and no user-registry workspace) — run 'theia init' in the workspace home first\nNote\ntheia doctor does not look upward: its.theia store line checks only the current folder. Run theia doctor from the project root.\nThe workspaces.yaml registry\nThe registry links folders outside git to their stores.\nSystem Registry Stores\nLinux\n~/.config/theia/workspaces.yaml\n~/.local/share/theia/stores/\nmacOS after the installer\n~/.config/theia/workspaces.yaml\n~/.local/share/theia/stores/\nmacOS without the old folders\n~/Library/Application Support/theia/workspaces.yaml\n~/Library/Application Support/theia/stores/\nXDG_CONFIG_HOME and XDG_DATA_HOME change these folders. Why macOS has two folders is described in macOS: the settings folder can switch.\nShow the registry:\nLinux and macOS after the installer\nmacOS without the old folders\ncat ~/.config/theia/workspaces.yaml\ncat \" $HOME /Library/Application Support/theia/workspaces.yaml\"\nExample output:\nworkspaces: /Users/you/notes: /Users/you/.local/share/theia/stores/notes-f941a13e\nIf you no longer need a folder outside git, delete its row from the registry and its store folder.\nSeveral roots\nOne store can serve several repositories. The Workspace manifest lists them — a YAML file with kind: Workspace in the.theia/config/ folder. Without a manifest the workspace has one root named root: the folder that holds.theia.\nExample.theia/config/workspace.yaml:\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Workspace metadata: { name: workspace } spec: roots: - { name: app, path: \".\" } - { name: lib, path: \"../lib\" } primary: app\nField What it means\nroots\nThe list of roots: 1 to 16\nname\nThe root name: lower-case Latin letters, digits, _ and -, up to 40 characters. Starts with a letter or digit. Names do not repeat\npath\nThe repository path relative to the folder that holds.theia, or an absolute path. The folder must exist\nprimary\nThe default root. If omitted, the first in the list\nCheck that Theia sees the roots:\ntheia index status\nExample output:\nentries: 0 (0 current) app: 0 stale, 0 missing, 0 orphaned lib: 0 stale, 0 missing, 0 orphaned\nindex is fresh\nThe file name can be anything ending in.yaml: Theia reads every YAML file in.theia/config/ and recognizes the manifest by kind: Workspace. A workspace can have only one such manifest.\nThe first-setup wizard creates this file itself if you list sibling repositories.\nA Workspace manifest is not allowed in the config/ folder next to the user settings file: Theia stops with an error.\nIf a root path is not found, commands stop with a message like:\ntheia: workspace root '<name>' does not exist: <path> (fix the Workspace manifest)\nOther manifest errors (the command exits with code 1):\nMessage Cause\nconfig: ws2.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.roots.0.name: invalid_string)\nA root name that breaks the rules from the table above. 0 is the index of the root in the list, counted from zero\nconfig: ws2.yaml: invalid manifest (spec: custom)\nTwo roots with the same name, or primary names a root that is not in roots\nconfig: ws3.yaml: second Workspace manifest (one per workspace)\nTwo kind: Workspace files in.theia/config/\nWarning\ntheia index status --brief does not show these errors: it prints theia: no index and exits with code 0. Check the manifest with theia index status without --brief. See Mistakes in settings files.\nThe --root flag\n--root <name> picks the root for the commands theia index, theia mem and theia run. The name comes from the manifest. In a workspace without a manifest the only root is named root.\nAn unknown name stops the command with code 1:\ntheia: unknown root 'web' (declared: app, lib)\nIn a workspace with one root, theia run --root with any name is silently ignored: the name is checked only where there is more than one root. A typo in a script goes unnoticed this way, so check the root name against the manifest.\nHow the flag works in each command is described in the theia index, theia mem and theia run references.\nLimits\nThe first-setup wizard creates.theia in the current folder and does not use the theia init rules.\nOutside git the code index and theia ask do not work.\ntheia doctor checks for the store only in the current folder.\nRunning theia init again does not change the policy of an existing store: see theia init.\nSee also\ntheia init\nFirst setup\nCode index, memory and questions\nSettings files","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/integrations/codex.html":{"title":"Codex","content":"This page connects Theia to Codex, the codex CLI. After that, Codex sessions in the project get project memory at the start, matching memory with each prompt, and five Theia tools for the code map and memory.\nWhat you get\ntheia hooks install codex writes two files into the project:\n.codex/hooks.json — four hooks that pass memory to Codex and save the end of a session.\n.codex/config.toml — the Theia MCP server with five tools.\nCodex uses them only after two trust confirmations. See Two trust gates.\nCodex gets less than Claude Code:\nClaude Code Codex\nHooks\n4, the last is Stop (after every response)\n4, the last is SessionEnd (end of a session)\nMCP tools\n22, run control included\n5: code map and memory\n/theia skill\nyes\nno\nStatus line\nyes\nno\nInstall\nGo to the folder that holds.theia/. The command does not look in parent folders.\nRun:\ntheia hooks install codex\nExample output (the trust report is shortened here and shown in full below):\ninstalled hooks: SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, PreCompact, SessionEnd → ~/projects/app/.codex/hooks.json\ncreated [mcp_servers.theia] → ~/projects/app/.codex/config.toml\ngate 1 (project trust): NOT SATISFIED — the whole project-scope.codex/ surface is INERT until\n…\nai rules: none detected\nPass both trust gates.\nThe --trust flag also records trust for this project in your Codex settings. It is not shown in theia help.\ntheia hooks install codex --trust\nExample output:\nhooks already installed (~/projects/app/.codex/hooks.json unchanged)\nmcp server already configured (~/projects/app/.codex/config.toml unchanged)\n--trust: appended project trust → /Users/you/.codex/config.toml\ngate 1 (project trust): ok — /Users/you/projects/app is trusted in /Users/you/.codex/config.toml\nRunning the command again changes nothing. It prints hooks already installed …, mcp server already configured … and the trust report again.\nTheia finds your Codex settings the same way Codex does: in the folder from the CODEX_HOME variable, or in ~/.codex by default.\nWhat changes.codex/hooks.json Codex event Command What Theia does\nSessionStart\ntheia mem context\nPasses the start of the code map and the 8 newest memory facts.\nUserPromptSubmit\ntheia mem recall\nPasses up to 6 memory entries that match the prompt.\nPreCompact\ntheia mem checkpoint\nSaves the last 16 KB of the session file to memory.\nSessionEnd\ntheia mem checkpoint --final\nThe same save at the end of a session.\nEach command uses the full path of the Theia program ( /Users/you/.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia ) and has a 30-second limit. The file has the same format as Claude Code hooks. What the commands print is shown on the Claude Code page. The memory piece saved by SessionEnd has the kind session-final, the same as the piece from Stop in Claude Code.\n.codex/config.toml\n# theia hooks install codex [mcp_servers.theia] command = \"/Users/you/.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia\" args = [ \"serve\", \"--home\", \"/Users/you/projects/app\", \"--tools\", \"codebase_blueprint,codebase_search,codebase_symbol,memory_search,memory_add\", \"--source-ref\", \"session:codex\" ] default_tools_approval_mode = \"auto\"\nIf the file already exists, Theia adds this block at the end and does not touch your lines. If the block is already there, the file does not change.\nThe tool list is the policy value run.mcpBelt.tools plus memory_add. With default settings these are the five tools below.\n--source-ref session:codex marks memory facts saved from Codex.\n~/.codex/config.toml, only with --trust\n# theia hooks install codex --trust [projects.\"/Users/you/projects/app\"] trust_level = \"trusted\"\nThis is the only change outside the project folder.\nTwo trust gates\nTheia prints this report after every install, even when nothing changed:\ngate 1 (project trust): NOT SATISFIED — the whole project-scope.codex/ surface is INERT until /Users/you/.codex/config.toml carries: [projects.\"/Users/you/projects/app\"] trust_level = \"trusted\" Either run `codex` once in this directory and accept the trust prompt, or re-run `theia hooks install codex --trust` to append it for you. (A `-c projects.….trust_level` CLI override does NOT satisfy this gate — probed, ledger §4.)\ngate 2 (hook trust): new/changed hooks are review-gated per hash and SKIPPED until trusted — run `/hooks` once in an interactive codex session here to review and trust them; vetted automation may pass `--dangerously-bypass-hook-trust` per invocation.\nnote: the plugin-hooks path is retired at codex-cli 0.151.0 (`plugin_hooks` = removed; core `hooks` feature is stable) — project.codex/hooks.json is the supported surface.\nprobed, ledger §4 is an internal note of the Theia developers; you can ignore it.\nGate 1: trust the project\nUntil the project is trusted, Codex ignores the whole project.codex/ folder: no hooks and no MCP server. Do one of these:\nRun codex in the project folder once and accept the trust question.\nRun theia hooks install codex --trust.\nAccording to Theia's report, a one-time -c projects.….trust_level option on the Codex command line is not enough.\nCheck the result. In a trusted project, codex mcp list shows the server:\ncodex mcp list\nExample output in a trusted project:\nName Command Args\ntheia /Users/you/.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia serve --home /Users/you/projects/app --tools …\nIn a project that is not trusted, the server is missing:\nNo MCP servers configured yet. Try `codex mcp add my-tool -- my-command`.\nGate 2: trust the hooks\nCodex skips new or changed hooks until you review them:\nOpen an interactive codex session in the project folder.\nType /hooks.\nReview the four Theia hooks and trust them.\nFor scripts that you have checked, Codex accepts the --dangerously-bypass-hook-trust option for a single call. After any change to the hook files, review them again.\nNote\nThis guide checked the files, the trust entry and codex mcp list. It did not check how the hooks behave inside a Codex session, because that needs model calls.\nCheck with theia doctor\ntheia doctor has a line about Codex hooks for the project in the current folder. Example output:\ncodex hooks: feature stable/true; project trusted; hooks.json present\nOther states in this line: project NOT trusted (gate 1), hooks.json absent (theia hooks install codex) and codex not found — install/auth codex first. See theia doctor.\nWhat you can do from Codex\nIn a trusted project with trusted hooks:\nAt the start of a session, Codex gets the <theia-context> block: the start of the code map and recent memory facts.\nWith each prompt, Codex gets matching memory in the <theia-memory> block.\nThe end of each session is saved to memory.\nCodex can call five tools:\nTool What you can ask Codex to do\ncodebase_blueprint\nread the whole code map\ncodebase_search\nfind where a feature lives\ncodebase_symbol\nfind where a function or class is defined and who calls it\nmemory_search\nfind project decisions and rules in memory\nmemory_add\nsave a decision or a rule to memory\nThe parameters are on the MCP server page.\nCodex cannot start or steer Theia runs: it has no run or plan tools and no /theia skill. Start and control runs in the terminal, see Control a run, or from Claude Code.\nYou can register the full server with run tools by hand, see Register the server by hand. Run tools inside Codex were not checked for this guide.\nAfter a Theia update\n.codex/hooks.json and.codex/config.toml store the path of one version, like the Claude Code files. After an update, Codex keeps running the old version. theia hooks install codex does not fix the path: it adds a second set of hooks and leaves the server entry as it is.\nChecked fix: point both files at ~/.local/bin/theia, the link that updates move to the new version.\nmacOS\nLinux\nsed -i '' 's#/.local/share/theia/releases/[^/]*/theia#/.local/bin/theia#g'.codex/hooks.json.codex/config.toml\nOpen both files in an editor and replace /.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia with /.local/bin/theia.\nAfter the change, codex mcp list shows /Users/you/.local/bin/theia as the command. The hooks have changed, so review them again with /hooks. Do not run theia hooks install codex again afterwards.\nUndo\nThere is no theia hooks uninstall command. Remove the integration by hand.\nIf both files contain only Theia's entries, delete them and the empty folder:\nrm.codex/hooks.json.codex/config.toml\nrmdir.codex\nIf.codex/config.toml has your own settings, delete only the lines from # theia hooks install codex to default_tools_approval_mode = \"auto\". If.codex/hooks.json has your own hooks, delete only the four Theia entries.\nIf you used --trust, delete these three lines from ~/.codex/config.toml:\n# theia hooks install codex --trust [projects.\"/Users/you/projects/app\"] trust_level = \"trusted\"\nCheck:\ncodex mcp list\nExample output when no servers are left:\nNo MCP servers configured yet. Try `codex mcp add my-tool -- my-command`.\nSee also\nMCP server\nClaude Code\ntheia hooks install codex\nEnvironment variables","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/integrations/mcp-server.html":{"title":"MCP server","content":"theia serve is Theia's MCP server. Claude Code, Codex and other MCP clients start it to read the code map and project memory. In Claude Code it also lets Claude plan and start runs. This page lists every tool and shows how to register the server.\nHow clients start the server\nThe server talks MCP over standard input and output. An MCP client starts it as a child process. In daily work you do not start it yourself.\nClaude Code. theia hooks install claude writes.mcp.json. After you approve the server, Claude Code starts theia serve --home <project folder> with all 22 tools. See Claude Code.\nCodex. theia hooks install codex writes.codex/config.toml. Codex starts the server with 5 tools, and only in a trusted project. See Codex.\nWhile it runs, the server:\nchecks every 300 seconds whether the code map is up to date ( maintenance.serveRefreshSeconds ); the check makes no model calls;\nwith maintenance.indexPolicy: auto, rebuilds out-of-date entries of the intent index within the budget; this calls models;\nwrites its own messages to stderr, for example theia serve: MCP server on stdio (root ~/projects/app).\nThe server offers tools only. Requests for resources or prompts get the Method not found error. It introduces itself as theia version 0.1.0; this number is not the Theia version.\nTools\nOptional parameters end with?. Tool errors come back as a short text, for example unknown run 'bogus' — run_list shows known runs; in the MCP reply such a result is marked isError: true. A wrong parameter comes back differently, as a protocol error MCP error -32602: Input validation error: ….\nCode map Tool Parameters What it does\ncodebase_blueprint\n—\nReturns the whole blueprint: one line per indexed file, the most important first.\ncodebase_search\nquery; k? from 1 to 50, default 8\nSearches the code map. Each hit has the file path, its role, related files, its API and design notes. If the map is out of date, the reply also has a staleness block.\ncodebase_symbol\nname\nLooks up an exact function or class name: definitions with line numbers, callers, callees and import sites.\nMemory Tool Parameters What it does\nmemory_search\nquery; k? from 1 to 50, default 8; asOf? — an event or fact id\nSearches project memory: facts and saved text pieces. With asOf, it shows what was known at that point.\nmemory_add\nkind: decision, constraint, commitment, observation, entity or preference; text up to 4000 characters; entities? up to 20 names\nSaves one fact. If a nearly identical fact exists, it is replaced, and the reply has supersededId.\nmemory_tree\nfamily: session or entity; treeKey; asOf?\nShows one memory timeline: groups with summaries and the entries in them.\nNote\nIn a test, a close rewording of an existing fact was saved as a second fact, not as a replacement. Do not count on automatic replacement when the wording differs.\nAudit and diagnosis Tool Parameters What it does\nrun_audit\nscope?\nChecks the audit chains. broken: null means the chain is intact. Without scope, it checks all chains.\nrun_diagnose\nrunId\nLooks for the cause of a failed run in its event log. It only reads the log.\nrun_check\nindex?\nA tool for Theia's own agents. Outside runs it replies run_check is not configured for this invocation (no check commands).\nRun control\nOnly the full server has these tools (Claude Code by default). runId is the full run id, for example run2akdqkvcug222iaxzvmt6b.\nTool Parameters What it does\nrun_list\nn? from 1 to 50, default 10\nRecent runs: id, state ( live, paused or finished ), task counts, cost, questions waiting.\nrun_status\nrunId\nOne run: tasks and their phases, closedBy for closed tasks, questions waiting, recent warnings, cost and lastEventId.\nrun_events\nrunId; afterId?\nThe important events of a run, oldest first.\nrun_wait\nrunId; afterId?; timeoutSec? from 1 to 55, default 45\nWaits for new events. The first call without afterId returns the last 30 events at once. Pass lastEventId from each reply as the next afterId.\nrun_msg\nrunId; text up to 8000 characters; taskId?\nSends a steering note to a run.\nrun_answer\nrunId; questionId; answers — question text mapped to your answer\nAnswers a clarifying question of a run. An empty answers declines to answer.\nrun_stop\nrunId\nAsks a run to pause after the step in progress.\nrun_start\ntask? or planId?; budgetUsd? above 0, up to 100\nStarts theia run --non-interactive --plain --interaction bus as a separate process. Returns runId and log, the path of.theia/control/run-<number>.log.\nrun_probe\nrunId; question up to 2000 characters\nAsks a read-only question about a run through theia probe.\nPlans Tool Parameters What it does\nplan_list\n—\nStored plans: id, status, task count and goal.\nplan_show\nplanId\nOne plan in full, with its status.\nplan_create\ntask up to 64000 characters\nStarts theia plan and returns the plan id and text. The plan is not approved yet. The output goes to.theia/control/plan-<number>.log.\nplan_approve\nplanId\nApproves a plan. The tool does not ask the user; the /theia skill tells Claude to ask first.\nNot checked for this guide\nThe test setup used a stub model that cannot produce plans or questions. So plan_create, plan_show, plan_approve, run_start with planId, run_answer, run_probe and a successful run_diagnose were not checked in use. Their description above follows the source code.\nWhich tools call models or download Tool What it can cost\nrun_start\nA whole run: many model calls within the run budget.\nplan_create\nModel calls for planning.\nrun_probe\nOne model call.\ncodebase_search, memory_search\nWith gateway.embeddings.provider: openai, each query goes to the paid OpenAI embeddings API. With the local model, the first search may download it (about 23 MB) if downloads are allowed.\nThe server itself\nWith maintenance.indexPolicy: auto, index rebuilds call models.\nAll other tools\nNo model calls.\nRun control does not reach new runs\nWarning\nrun_msg, run_stop and run_answer work only with a run that was paused and then continued with theia run --resume. A run started fresh ignores them, and that includes every run from run_start. run_msg still replies reaches the live run at its next dispatch boundary, and run_stop still replies honored at the next dispatch boundary (in-flight work lands first). Questions from such a run never arrive.\nThe cause is in the 0.2.201 code: a fresh run does not connect the control bus; only theia run --resume connects it. So theia run … --interaction bus without --resume does not listen to the bus either. This is a bug of the version, see Limitations.\nThe checked order of steps is in Steer a run from Claude. More on pauses: Control a run.\nAlso seen in tests:\nA run killed from outside stays live in run_list and run_status.\nrun_start with a wrong planId returns an error, but each attempt leaves an empty branch theia/run-<suffix> in the repository and a run record that stays in run_list forever with tail: live, outcome: in flight and zero tasks. Such a run also counts towards N live in the Claude Code status line.\nrun_wait does not list the stop request itself. It shows run.paused when the pause happens.\nChoose the tool set Launch Tools\ntheia serve\n22: all groups\ntheia serve --no-control\n9: code map, memory, audit and diagnosis\ntheia serve --tools codebase_blueprint,memory_search\nonly the listed tools; run and plan tools cannot be listed\nNames allowed in --tools: codebase_blueprint, codebase_search, codebase_symbol, memory_search, memory_add, memory_tree, run_audit, run_diagnose, run_check. Any other name stops the server with mcp-server: unknown tool 'bogus' in filter (known: …).\nUse --no-control when a client should only read the code map and memory. Flag details: theia serve.\nRegister the server by hand\nAlways pass --home with the full path of the folder that holds.theia/. The command can be the version path that the installer writes, or ~/.local/bin/theia, which follows updates (see After a Theia update ).\nClaude Code\nFile.mcp.json in the project folder:\n{ \"mcpServers\": { \"theia\": { \"command\": \"/Users/you/.local/bin/theia\", \"args\": [ \"serve\", \"--home\", \"/Users/you/projects/app\" ] } } }\nTo give Claude only the code map and memory, add \"--no-control\" to args. Check the entry with claude mcp list: until you approve the server in Claude Code, it shows Pending approval.\nCodex\nThe same block works in the project file.codex/config.toml (only in a trusted project) and in your Codex settings ~/.codex/config.toml (for all folders):\n[mcp_servers.theia] command = \"/Users/you/.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia\" args = [ \"serve\", \"--home\", \"/Users/you/projects/app\" ]\nWith this block in ~/.codex/config.toml, codex mcp list in another folder shows (shortened):\nName Command Args … Status\ntheia /Users/you/.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia serve --home /Users/you/projects/app … enabled\nWithout --tools, Codex gets all 22 tools. Run and plan tools inside Codex were not checked for this guide.\nOther MCP clients\nAny MCP client that starts servers over standard input and output can use Theia. The command is the path to theia, the arguments are serve --home <project folder>. The server offers tools only, with no resources or prompts.\nUndo\nThere is no command to undo. You disconnect the server by removing its entry from the client.\nIf theia hooks install wrote the entry, remove the whole integration by the steps in Undo on the Claude Code page or Undo on the Codex page.\nIf you registered the server by hand, delete the theia entry under mcpServers in.mcp.json, or the [mcp_servers.theia] block from the Codex config.toml. The client will not start the server on its next start. Claude Code keeps its own record of your approval of the server; how to reset it is on the Claude Code page.\nThe server itself leaves behind only the logs of the processes that run_start and plan_create started. You can delete them:\nrm -rf.theia/control\nFacts saved with memory_add stay in the project store. How to remove the store: Uninstall.\nErrors you may see Message Cause What to do\ntheia: no.theia/ found from … upward (and no user-registry workspace) — run 'theia init' in the workspace home first\n--home points at a folder without a store, or the server started outside the project\nFix the --home path, or run theia init in the project.\nmcp-server: unknown tool '…' in filter (known: …)\nA wrong name in --tools\nUse a name from Choose the tool set.\ntheia serve: --metrics needs a port 1..65535, got 'abc'\nA wrong port\nPass a number from 1 to 65535.\ntheia: query embedding failed (fatal: embeddings-local: model 'all-MiniLM-L6-v2' missing …)\nThe local embeddings model is not on disk and downloads are off\nFetch the model as the full message says. See Code index, memory and questions.\ntheia: no vendor deliverables found (lib/vendor missing and no installed set) …\nTheia's shipped files are missing, or THEIA_VENDOR_DIR points at a missing folder\nUnset THEIA_VENDOR_DIR or reinstall Theia.\nError: listen EADDRINUSE: address already in use\nAnother process already uses the --metrics port\nUse another port. See Metrics and tracing.\nterryid: neither a body nor a full id: 'bogus'\nrun_diagnose got a text that does not look like a run id\nPass the full runId from run_list.\nrun_diagnose: no events for <id>\nThe id has the right shape, but no run has such a log\nTake the runId from run_list.\nFor platform developers\nTheia's own agents inside runs start the same server with a short tool list (policy value run.mcpBelt.tools ) and extra flags. You do not need these flags; they are listed on the theia serve page.\nSee also\ntheia serve\nClaude Code\nCodex\nMetrics and tracing\nControl a run","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/integrations/git.html":{"title":"Git","content":"theia hooks install git adds a small git hook. After each commit it notes which files changed, so that theia index status can show that the code map needs a refresh. The hook makes no model calls.\nBefore you install\nRun the command in the folder that holds both.theia/ and.git.\nCheck that git does not take hooks from another folder. When core.hooksPath is not set, this command prints nothing:\ngit config --get core.hooksPath\nIf it prints a folder, read Limits first.\nInstall\ntheia hooks install git\nExample output:\ninstalled post-commit dirty-marks hook → ~/projects/app/.git/hooks/post-commit\nRunning the command again changes nothing and prints:\npost-commit hook already installed (~/projects/app/.git/hooks/post-commit unchanged)\nIf.git/hooks/post-commit already exists, Theia keeps your lines and adds its own line at the end.\nWhat the hook does\nThe file.git/hooks/post-commit, with permission to run:\n#!/bin/sh\ngit diff-tree -r --name-only --no-commit-id HEAD >>.theia/index/dirty-paths 2>/dev/null || true # theia dirty-marks (T-W)\nAfter each commit, git adds the names of the changed files to.theia/index/dirty-paths. These are the dirty marks. theia index status shows them:\ntheia index status\nExample output after one commit:\nentries: 0 (0 current)\ndirty-marks: 1 paths since last build (post-commit hook)\nmissing (1): hello.js\nrun 'theia index build' to refresh\nThe marks are only a hint. Theia still decides freshness by file contents.\nA successful full index build clears the marks: theia index build without paths, or an automatic build.\nThe hook contains no path to the Theia program, so Theia updates do not affect it.\nMore about the output: theia index status.\nLimits\ncore.hooksPath is set\nWhen git has core.hooksPath, git runs hooks from that folder and ignores.git/hooks. Theia still writes.git/hooks/post-commit and prints installed …, but the hook never runs: after a commit,.theia/index/dirty-paths stays empty. Theia cannot install the hook into another folder.\ngit worktree\nIn a linked git worktree,.git is a file, not a folder. The command fails with exit code 1:\nENOTDIR: not a directory, mkdir '~/projects/app-wt/.git/hooks'\nOther errors, both with exit code 1:\ntheia hooks: no.git in ~/projects/app — the post-commit hook needs a git repo\ntheia hooks: no.theia/ in ~/projects/app — run 'theia init' first\nUndo\nThere is no theia hooks uninstall command. Delete Theia's line from.git/hooks/post-commit by hand.\nFind the line:\ngrep -n \"theia dirty-marks\".git/hooks/post-commit\nDelete it:\nmacOS\nLinux\nsed -i '' '/# theia dirty-marks/d'.git/hooks/post-commit\nOpen.git/hooks/post-commit in an editor and delete the line that ends with # theia dirty-marks (T-W).\nIf only #!/bin/sh is left in the file, you can delete the file.\nWhat Theia does not do with git\nTheia does not push branches and does not create pull requests. A run leaves a local run branch theia/run-<suffix>; you merge it or open a pull request yourself. See Run results.\nThere is no GitHub, Slack, webhook or Jira integration. Theia uses the GitHub token ( PAT ) only for its own downloads and update checks.\nSee also\ntheia hooks install git\ntheia index status\nCode index, memory and questions","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/integrations/observability.html":{"title":"Metrics and tracing","content":"Theia can publish numbers about its work for Prometheus, and it can send traces and metrics to an OpenTelemetry collector. This page shows both ways, what they really contain, and their limits.\nTwo ways Way What you get Which process sends data\ntheia serve --metrics <port>\nA Prometheus page /metrics\nOnly the running MCP server\nOTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT\nTraces and metrics sent over OTLP, the OpenTelemetry protocol\nAny theia command: run, plan, index, serve\nA run is a separate process. Its metrics never appear on /metrics; they leave only through OTLP.\nThe /metrics page\n--metrics <port> starts an HTTP page next to the MCP server. Claude Code and Codex start the server without this flag. To turn it on for Claude Code, add the flag to args in.mcp.json:\n{ \"mcpServers\": { \"theia\": { \"command\": \"/Users/you/.local/bin/theia\", \"args\": [ \"serve\", \"--home\", \"/Users/you/projects/app\", \"--metrics\", \"9464\" ] } } }\nTo try it by hand, start the server in the project folder. It waits for an MCP client and stops when its input closes:\ntheia serve --metrics 9464\nRead the page from another terminal:\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:9464/metrics\nExample output from a fresh server:\n# HELP theia_telemetry_denied_attrs_total attribute keys dropped by the PII boundary (THE-055)\n# TYPE theia_telemetry_denied_attrs_total counter\nWhat the page contains:\nOnly the counters of this server process. A fresh server has only the header of theia_telemetry_denied_attrs_total, with no values.\nWith maintenance.indexPolicy: auto, the server rebuilds the index itself. The page then gets values for theia_gateway_calls_total, theia_gateway_usd, theia_gateway_latency_ms, theia_index_model_calls_total and theia_index_build_ms.\nMetrics of runs are never on this page. Use OTLP for them.\nAny other path returns 404.\nThe page is open to your network\nThe server listens on all network interfaces, not only on 127.0.0.1. Other machines can read the page unless a firewall blocks the port, and Theia has no option to change this. The page has no prompts and no code — only counts, durations and costs of model calls — but it shows how you use models. Turn on --metrics only on a network you trust, or block the port in your firewall.\nCheck which address the port listens on:\nlsof -nP -iTCP:9464 -sTCP:LISTEN\nExample output. *:9464 means all interfaces:\ntheia 76196 you 15u IPv6 0xa58112701e43d0c6 0t0 TCP *:9464 (LISTEN)\nOne port — one server\nIf the port is already taken, a second server crashes at start with a Node.js stack trace and exit code 1. Example with port 19466:\ntheia serve: /metrics on:19466\nnode:events:497 throw er; // Unhandled 'error' event ^ Error: listen EADDRINUSE: address already in use:::19466\nIf two MCP clients start the server with the same port, the second client gets no Theia tools. Give each client its own port, or turn on --metrics in one client only.\nExport over OTLP\nPoint Theia at your collector before you start a command:\nexport OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:4318\nexport OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=theia\nVariable What it does Default\nOTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT\nThe base address of the collector's OTLP/HTTP input. Theia adds /v1/traces and /v1/metrics itself. If the address already ends with /v1/traces or /v1/metrics, Theia swaps in the right path for each signal.\nnot set: nothing is sent\nOTEL_SERVICE_NAME\nThe service name in the data ( service.name ).\ntheia\nWhat a test with a local receiver showed:\nA short run sent one POST to /v1/traces and one to /v1/metrics, both as JSON.\nThe service name in the data was the value of OTEL_SERVICE_NAME.\nFrom the source code: during long work, metrics go out every 10 seconds, and at exit Theia waits up to 3 seconds to send the rest. If the exporter itself cannot be loaded, Theia prints otel: OTLP boot failed (…) — tracing disabled and keeps working.\nAn unreachable collector is a different case. In a test with an address where nothing listens, Theia printed no message and did not slow down: the data simply did not arrive. So silence does not mean the export works; check that the collector receives data.\nSpans\nA span is one timed piece of work inside a trace.\nSpan Attributes Meaning\nkernel.round\nround, jobs\nOne scheduling round of a run.\nkernel.job\nrole, taskId, phase, attempt\nOne job of one role for one task.\ngateway.call\nrole, mode\nOne model call.\nMetrics\n✓ — seen in a test, in the OTLP export or on /metrics. The other names come from the source code.\nMetric Type Labels Meaning\ntheia_gateway_calls_total ✓\ncounter\ntransport, role, kind\nModel calls by result.\ntheia_gateway_usd ✓\nhistogram\ntransport\nCost of one call, USD.\ntheia_gateway_latency_ms ✓\nhistogram\nrole, transport\nDuration of one model call.\ntheia_gateway_startup_ms\nhistogram\ntransport\nFrom the engine start to its first output.\ntheia_gateway_teardown_ms\nhistogram\ntransport\nFrom the engine's last output to its exit.\ntheia_tool_ms\nhistogram\ntool\nDuration of a tool call inside the engine.\ntheia_anomalies_total\ncounter\nkind\nWarning events of runs.\ntheia_task_transitions_total ✓\ncounter\nevent, to\nTask phase changes.\ntheia_workspace_ms ✓\nhistogram\nphase\nPreparing and committing a task workspace.\ntheia_verify_ms ✓\nhistogram\n—\nCheck commands.\ntheia_merge_ms ✓\nhistogram\n—\nMerges.\ntheia_index_build_ms ✓\nhistogram\n—\nIndex builds.\ntheia_index_model_calls_total ✓\ncounter\n—\nModel calls made by the indexer.\ntheia_index_replies_healed_total\ncounter\n—\nIndexer replies that were repaired automatically.\ntheia_index_replies_reasked_total\ncounter\n—\nIndex entries recovered by asking again.\ntheia_resource_alarms_total\ncounter\nkind\nResource warnings: a process killed for memory, memory near the limit, CPU throttling, low memory on the machine.\ntheia_scope_memory_mb\nhistogram\n—\nMemory used by a run, MB.\ntheia_telemetry_denied_attrs_total ✓\ncounter\n—\nAttributes dropped by the privacy filter.\nWhat is never sent\nTheia does not send prompts, replies or code. Attributes whose names contain prompt, content, text, message, email, token, secret, key or payload are dropped and counted in theia_telemetry_denied_attrs_total. Long values are cut to 200 characters.\nUndo\npolicy.yaml has no telemetry settings. Both ways are turned on only by a flag and environment variables, so they are turned off in the same places.\nThe /metrics page: remove --metrics and the port number from args in.mcp.json or.codex/config.toml. On its next start, the client starts the server without the page. If you started theia serve --metrics by hand, just stop it.\nOTLP export: remove the variables from the environment where you start theia:\nunset OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT OTEL_SERVICE_NAME\nIf the variables are set in your shell profile, delete them there and open a new terminal. Without OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT, Theia sends nothing.\nObservability stack from the source repository\nThe theia-dev source repository has a ready stack in the ops/observability folder: an OpenTelemetry collector, Jaeger, Prometheus and Grafana with the \"Theia Run Observatory\" dashboard. The stack is not part of the Theia release: the installed files have no ops folder. You need access to the source repository.\nThe repository README starts it like this. These commands were not run for this guide.\ncd ops/observability\npodman-compose -f compose.yaml up -d\nexport OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:4318\nService Address\nGrafana with the \"Theia Run Observatory\" dashboard\nhttp://127.0.0.1:3000\nJaeger UI\nhttp://127.0.0.1:16686\nPrometheus\nhttp://127.0.0.1:9090\nOTLP input, HTTP and gRPC\n127.0.0.1:4318 and 127.0.0.1:4317\nGood to know, from the stack files:\nAll ports are bound to 127.0.0.1.\nGrafana opens without a login, with administrator rights.\nJaeger keeps traces in memory, so a restart clears them. Prometheus keeps data for 7 days.\nPrometheus also tries to read theia serve --metrics 9464 on your machine through the host name host.containers.internal.\nThe images use the latest tag.\nA variant with Kafka between two collectors starts with OTELCOL_CONFIG=via-kafka.yaml podman-compose -f compose.yaml --profile kafka up -d.\nWarning\nThe dashboard panel \"PII boundary (denied attrs, masked writes)/min\" also queries the metric theia_pii_total. Theia does not produce this metric, so that line of the panel is always empty.\nSee also\ntheia serve --metrics\nMCP server\nEnvironment variables","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/init.html":{"title":"theia init","content":"Reference for the theia init command.\ntheia init\nCreates a Theia store for a project: the store.db database, settings and service folders. Run it once per project, from the repository root.\ntheia init [--harness codex|claude] [--here] [--embeddings local|openai]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--harness\ncodex or claude\nBy login: codex if you are logged in to Codex; claude if only to Claude Code; otherwise codex\nThe engine that routing in the new policy.yaml is set up for\n--here\n—\nOff\nPuts the store in the current folder, not at the repository root and not in the user folder\n--embeddings\nlocal or openai\nNot written, local is used\nHow to get embeddings, written to the new policy.yaml. Not shown in theia help\nThe form --harness=claude also works. The form --embeddings=local does not: the command silently skips it and writes nothing. Put the value after a space: --embeddings local.\nThe command silently ignores unknown flags; the exit code stays 0.\nIf the store already exists, the flags change nothing: see Running it again.\n--harness\nChooses the engine for all roles. Without the flag Theia checks the Codex and Claude Code logins:\nLogged in to Choice Message\nCodex and Claude Code\ncodex\nharness: both codex and claude authenticated — seeding codex (the measured default); theia init --harness claude selects the other\nCodex only\ncodex\n—\nClaude Code only\nclaude\nharness: codex unavailable, claude authenticated — seeding claude routing\nNeither\ncodex\nThe message below\nIf you are logged in to neither engine:\nharness: neither codex nor claude answered its doctor probe — seeding codex routing anyway; authenticate one and see `theia doctor`\npolicy.yaml after theia init --harness claude:\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: local } spec: run: {} gateway: routing: \"*\": claude implementer: claude-write fresh_implementer: claude-write indexer: claude-index\nHow routing works and how to switch the engine later is described in Models for tasks.\n--here\nPuts the.theia store in the current folder. Without the flag, the store goes to the repository root in git, and to the user data folder outside git. See Store and project roots.\ntheia init --here\nExample output in the src subfolder:\nstore: ~/projects/app/src/.theia (detached — --here pins the store to this folder)\ninitialized ~/projects/app/src/.theia (harness: codex — switch any time: gateway.routing in.theia/config/policy.yaml)\nai rules: none detected\n--embeddings\nWrites to the new policy.yaml how to get embeddings: local is a local model, openai goes through OpenAI and needs a key. Without the flag the line is not written and local is used. The flag is not shown in theia help.\ntheia init --embeddings local\npolicy.yaml after this command, when you are logged in to neither engine:\napiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Policy metadata: { name: local } spec: run: {} gateway: routing: \"*\": codex implementer: codex-write fresh_implementer: codex-write reviewer: codex-terra embeddings: { provider: local }\nKeys for embeddings are described in Keys and access.\nExample\ntheia init --harness claude\nExample output at the repository root:\nstore: ~/projects/app/.theia (worktree root)\ninitialized ~/projects/app/.theia (harness: claude — switch any time: gateway.routing in.theia/config/policy.yaml)\nai rules: none detected\nstore: — where the store is and why it is there.\ninitialized — the store is ready. In brackets: the chosen engine and the file where you can change it.\nai rules: — AI rules files found in the project. See Rules and prompts.\nCreated files Path What it is\n.theia/store.db\nSQLite database: event log, plans, memory, index\n.theia/config/policy.yaml\nThe policy: role routing and other settings\n.theia/config/registry.yaml\nThe list of allowed transports\n.theia/prompts/\nYour additions to role prompts\n.theia/objects/\nFiles that runs save: check command output and more\n.theia/index/\nSearch files\n.gitignore\nThe line.theia/ is added if the store is in this folder. Outside git with --here the file is created if it did not exist\nFor a folder outside git without --here, the same files are in the store folder in the user data folder: ~/.local/share/theia/stores/<folder name>-<8 hash characters>. The folder itself stays clean and.gitignore does not change. The link between the folder and the store is written to the registry ~/.config/theia/workspaces.yaml:\nworkspaces: <full path to the folder>: <full path to the store>\nSee The workspaces.yaml registry.\nRunning it again\nRunning it again does not change the policy\nIf the store already exists, theia init does not rewrite policy.yaml or registry.yaml. The --harness and --embeddings flags then change nothing, but the output still names the new engine.\nExample: the store was created for Codex, then init runs again:\ntheia init --harness claude\nExample output:\nstore: ~/projects/app/.theia (worktree root)\ninitialized ~/projects/app/.theia (harness: claude — switch any time: gateway.routing in.theia/config/policy.yaml)\nai rules: none detected\npolicy.yaml still says \"*\": codex. How to switch the engine is described in Models for tasks.\nErrors and exit codes Code When\n0\nThe store was created or already existed\n1\nA wrong value for --harness or --embeddings\nError messages:\ninit: unknown harness 'gemini' (usage: theia init [--harness codex|claude])\ntheia init: --embeddings expects one of local|openai\nSee also\nFirst setup\nStore and project roots\nModels for tasks\ntheia doctor","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/setup-self-version.html":{"title":"theia setup, self update, version","content":"Reference for the user settings, update and version commands.\ntheia setup\nSaves user settings to config.yaml, or asks for them in the terminal. It never prints token or key values.\ntheia setup --interactive\ntheia setup [--pat <token>] [--openai-key <key>] [--channel release|prerelease] [--dev-root <path>]\ntheia setup --fetch-embeddings [<model>]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--interactive\n—\n—\nAsks for the GitHub token and the OpenAI key in the terminal. Must come first\n--pat\na GitHub token\n—\nSaves github.pat\n--openai-key\nan OpenAI key\n—\nSaves openai.apiKey\n--channel\nrelease or prerelease\nrelease\nSaves update.channel\n--dev-root\na path\n—\nSaves dev.root. Platform developers only\n--fetch-embeddings\na model name, optional\nthe default model\nDownloads the local embedding model. Must be the first and only flag. Not shown in theia help\nYou can combine --pat, --openai-key, --channel and --dev-root. The = form also works, for example --channel=release.\nAfter writing, the command prints which keys it wrote and to which file.\nWhere the file is and how the command changes it is described below: The settings file.\n--interactive\nAsks in the terminal for the GitHub token and the OpenAI key, if they are not set yet. Then checks the engine logins and tells you how to log in.\ntheia setup --interactive\nExample output:\nglobal setup — ~/.config/theia/config.yaml (written mode 0600)\nGitHub PAT for theia releases (updates + version pinning; Enter to skip):\nOpenAI API key for embeddings (semantic search; Enter to skip):\nwrote ~/.config/theia/config.yaml\nno harness is authenticated yet — sign in with one of: codex login (OpenAI subscription) claude /login (Anthropic subscription — run `claude` once)\nthen `theia doctor` confirms.\nworkspace setup: cd into your project and run `theia init` (or bare `theia`)\nIt needs a terminal for input and output. Without one it prints setup --interactive needs a terminal (use the flag form in scripts) and exits with code 1.\n--interactive must come first. theia setup --pat <token> --interactive fails with setup: unknown argument '--interactive'.\nThis command does not create a project store: use theia init for that.\n--pat\nSaves the GitHub token (PAT). You need it for theia self update, --x-release and the new version notice.\ntheia setup --pat <token>\n<token> is a GitHub token with access to the release repository. Example output:\nwrote github.pat to ~/.config/theia/config.yaml (mode 0600)\nA token typed in the command stays in your shell history. To avoid that, use theia setup --interactive.\nThe THEIA_GH_TOKEN variable wins over the saved token.\n--pat= with an empty value saves an empty token.\n--pat without a value writes nothing: the command prints setup: nothing to write and exits with code 1.\n--openai-key\nSaves the OpenAI key. You need it only if embeddings come from OpenAI. The OPENAI_API_KEY variable wins over the saved key. See Keys and access.\ntheia setup --openai-key <key>\n--channel\nSaves the update channel: release means releases only, prerelease adds pre-release builds for platform developers.\ntheia setup --channel release\nExample output:\nwrote update.channel to ~/.config/theia/config.yaml (mode 0600)\nAny other value is refused:\nsetup: unknown channel 'nightly' (supported: release, prerelease)\n--dev-root\nSaves the path to Theia's source code for the --dev flag. Platform developers only. The path is saved as given, without a check. What --dev does and which errors it prints without a source tree is described in For platform developers.\n--fetch-embeddings\nDownloads the local embedding model into the cache. The folder: THEIA_MODELS_DIR if set; otherwise $XDG_CACHE_HOME/theia/models; otherwise ~/.cache/theia/models. On macOS, when the folder ~/Library/Caches/theia exists, it is ~/Library/Caches/theia/models. The default model is all-MiniLM-L6-v2, about 23 MB. The flag is not shown in theia help.\ntheia setup --fetch-embeddings [<model>]\n<model> is a model name, optional. After the download the command prints embeddings ready: local/<model> (<dims>d) at <folder>. On error: setup: <message> and exit code 1. An unknown model name fails before the download: setup: embeddings-local: unknown model '<model>' (supported: …).\nThe flag must come first and without other flags. Otherwise the command fails with exit code 1:\nsetup: unknown argument '--fetch-embeddings' (usage: theia setup [--interactive] [--pat <token>] [--openai-key <key>] [--dev-root <path>] [--channel release|prerelease] | --fetch-embeddings [model])\nThe settings file\ntheia setup and the first-setup wizard write to one file:\nSystem File\nLinux\n~/.config/theia/config.yaml\nmacOS after the installer\n~/.config/theia/config.yaml\nmacOS, if the folder ~/Library/Application Support/theia exists\n~/Library/Application Support/theia/config.yaml\nXDG_CONFIG_HOME changes the folder on any system. Why macOS has two folders is described in macOS: the settings folder can switch.\nExample file:\ngithub: pat: ghp_XXXXXXXX openai: apiKey: sk-XXXXXXXX update: channel: prerelease\nThe file is rewritten completely\nEvery write rewrites config.yaml. Only the keys github.pat, openai.apiKey, dev.root and update.channel stay. Anything you added by hand is lost. An invalid update.channel value is removed. The file mode goes back to 0600.\nErrors and exit codes Code When\n0\nSettings written, or the model downloaded\n1\nNothing to write, an unknown argument, a wrong channel, no terminal for --interactive, a model download error\nMessages:\nsetup: nothing to write — pass --pat, --openai-key, --dev-root, --channel, --fetch-embeddings, or run theia setup --interactive\nsetup: unknown argument '--bogus' (usage: theia setup [--interactive] [--pat <token>] [--openai-key <key>] [--dev-root <path>] [--channel release|prerelease] | --fetch-embeddings [model])\nsetup --interactive needs a terminal (use the flag form in scripts)\ntheia self update\nDownloads and installs the latest version from your update channel, then updates the vendor files.\ntheia self update\nThe command has no flags. Any other word after self prints usage: theia self update and exits with code 1.\nSteps:\nTakes the token from THEIA_GH_TOKEN or github.pat. Without a token it prints self update: no GitHub PAT — theia setup --pat <token> (or THEIA_GH_TOKEN) and exits with code 1.\nFinds the latest version in the update.channel channel. On the prerelease channel it prints prerelease channel (update.channel) — resolving against -pre builds too.\nIf the latest version equals the current one, it prints already up to date (v<version>). On the prerelease channel: already up to date (v<version>, prerelease channel). Then only the vendor files are updated.\nOtherwise it downloads the archive into the cache, prints cached v<version> at <folder> and installs the version.\nUpdates the vendor files ( vendor ).\nHow the command installs the new version:\nHow Theia was installed What happens Message\nBy the installer: ~/.local/bin/theia is a link to the current program or into the releases folder\nThe version is copied into the data folder releases/<version>/ and the link is switched. The data folder: $XDG_DATA_HOME/theia, otherwise ~/.local/share/theia; on macOS, when ~/Library/Application Support/theia exists, that folder\ninstalled v<version> to <folder> and repointed <link>\nAs a plain file\nThe file is replaced in place, together with the lib folder next to it\nreplaced <path> with v<version>\nVendor file messages:\nMessage What it means\nvendor: up to date (<hash>)\nThe vendor files are current\nvendor: installed <hash> → <folder> (current repointed)\nA new set was downloaded, checked and switched on\nvendor: release v<version> is not published (pruned?) — the embedded set is authoritative for this version; nothing to fetch\nYour version's release is gone. The vendor files inside the program are used\nvendor: release v<version> carries no vendor-manifest.json — vendor deliverables unchanged\nThe release has no vendor file list. The files stay as they are\nvendor: manifest names tree <hash> but v<version> has no matching tarball — skipped (report this release)\nThe release has a list but no vendor archive. Report that release to the developers\nvendor update failed (binary update unaffected): <reason>\nThe vendor files were not updated. The program itself is updated\nvendor update failed: <reason>\nThe same in the already-up-to-date branch: the version did not change and the vendor files were not updated\nVersions are compared as strings. If your version is newer than the latest in the channel, the command installs an older one. After an update, check theia version.\nThe checksum is checked only if the release has checksums.txt with a line for the archive.\nOld versions and the cache copy stay on disk.\nThe exit code is 0 after an update and 1 on an error: no token, no access to GitHub, no release. A vendor file error does not change the code: the command exits with 0.\nSee Update and choose a version.\ntheia version\nPrints Theia's version. This command never shows the new version notice, so it is handy in scripts.\ntheia version\ntheia --version\ntheia -V\ntheia version\nExample output:\n0.2.201\nThe exit code is 0. If THEIA_X_RELEASE holds an invalid value, even this command fails: see Another version for one command.\nSee also\nFirst setup\nUpdate and choose a version\nKeys and access","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/doctor.html":{"title":"theia doctor","content":"Reference for theia doctor: the environment check.\ntheia doctor\nChecks the engine logins, the sandbox, search, embeddings, the settings files, the store in the current folder, and the vendor files. It makes no model calls and does not change your files.\ntheia doctor\nThe command has no flags and ignores extra arguments.\ntheia doctor\nRun it from the project root: some lines look only at the current folder. See Blind spots.\nExample output\nA Mac logged in to Claude Code, the command run outside a project:\ncodex subscription: unavailable (harness-codex: not authenticated (codex login status: exit 1))\nclaude code: ok\njail (none ): unavailable (no sandbox backend on darwin yet (the container backend is the queued arm — docs/13); use standard or trusted-full) — jailed-full tier unavailable\njail (container ): docker ready (image is per-policy)\nresources: unavailable (run.resources needs linux cgroups (systemd-run); platform is darwin) — run.resources would refuse\nlexical index: ok (tantivy binding loads)\nembeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d) — ready at ~/.cache/theia/models/Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\nuser config: present\ngithub pat: present.theia store: not initialized (theia init)\nai rules: none detected\ncodex hooks: feature stable/true; project NOT trusted (gate 1); hooks.json absent (theia hooks install codex)\nvendor deliverables: ok — 9ff60d108678 verified (21 files); matches this release\nThe exit code here is 0: Claude Code works and there are no other errors.\nCheck lines Line What it checks Exit code 1\ncodex subscription\nThe Codex login: codex login status\nNo\nclaude code\nThe Claude Code subscription login: claude auth status\nNo\nharnesses\nAppears only when you are logged in to neither engine\nYes\njail (bwrap …) or jail (none …)\nThe theia jail sandbox: bubblewrap on Linux. macOS has none\nNo\njail (container …)\nWhether a podman or docker command exists: <engine> ready (image is per-policy) or no podman/docker on PATH\nNo\nresources\nResource limits through systemd-run --user, Linux only: cgroup scopes ok (systemd-run --user) or unavailable (…)\nNo\nlexical index\nWhether the text search library loads\nNo, even on FAIL\nembeddings\nThe embedding model from the current folder's policy: is the local model downloaded, is there an OpenAI key\nYes, on FAIL\nuser config\nWhether the user settings file exists\nNo\ngithub pat\nWhether a GitHub token is in the settings file or in THEIA_GH_TOKEN\nNo\n.theia store\nWhether.theia/store.db exists in the current folder\nNo\nstorage\nAppears only when.theia is larger than maintenance.storeAlertGb, 5 GB by default: storage:.theia at <size> (alert ≥5 GB) — reclaim with 'theia prune'\nNo\nai rules\nAI rules files found in the current folder\nNo\ncodex hooks\nCodex hooks in the project: is the feature on, does Codex trust the project, does.codex/hooks.json exist\nNo\nvendor deliverables\nThe vendor files: each file checked against its checksum\nYes, on FAIL\nThe shell (git-bash) line appears only on Windows, and on FAIL it sets exit code 1. Version 0.2.201 has no Windows files.\nWords in the lines:\nWord Meaning\nok\nThe check passed\nunavailable (…)\nThe feature is not available here; the reason is in brackets. Not an error\nFAIL\nA problem\npresent and absent (…)\nThe file or token exists, or does not\nnot initialized (theia init)\nThe current folder has no store\nLine variants\nThe engine lines when you are logged in to neither engine:\ncodex subscription: unavailable (harness-codex: not authenticated (codex login status: exit 1))\nclaude code: unavailable (harness-claude: not logged in (run `claude login`; API-key fallback is refused — THE-013))\nharnesses: FAIL (neither codex nor claude is authenticated — one is required)\nA claude code line with API-key fallback is refused means Claude Code is set up with an API key, not a subscription login. Theia does not accept that login: see Keys and access.\nThe embeddings line:\nembeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d) — ready at ~/.cache/theia/models/Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\nembeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d) — not fetched (~23 MB; fetches itself on first use, or theia setup --fetch-embeddings)\nembeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d) — FAIL (not fetched and autoDownload is off — theia setup --fetch-embeddings)\nembeddings: openai/text-embedding-3-small — FAIL (no OpenAI key — set OPENAI_API_KEY, theia setup --openai-key <key>, or policy provider: local)\nembeddings: openai/text-embedding-3-small — key present\nembeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d) — FAIL (corrupt: <what is wrong> — theia setup --fetch-embeddings re-fetches)\nembeddings: FAIL (policy unreadable: <reason>)\nready at … — the local model is downloaded.\nnot fetched — not an error: the model downloads itself on first use, about 23 MB.\nFAIL (not fetched and autoDownload is off …) — the policy forbids the download and the model is missing. Download it with theia setup --fetch-embeddings.\nopenai/… — FAIL (no OpenAI key …) — the policy needs OpenAI and there is no key. See Keys and access.\nopenai/… — key present — the policy needs OpenAI and a key exists. The key itself is not checked.\nFAIL (corrupt: …) — the model files are damaged. theia setup --fetch-embeddings downloads them again.\nFAIL (policy unreadable: …) — policy.yaml in.theia/config cannot be read. Fix the file.\nThe vendor deliverables line:\nvendor deliverables: ok — 9ff60d108678 verified (21 files); matches this release\nmatches this release — the set matches the one shipped with the program.\ndiffers from this release's embedded set <hash> (auto-updated) — the set is newer: theia self update updated it.\nFAIL (…) — files are damaged or missing. Fix: theia self update or a reinstall.\nThe codex hooks line:\nfeature stable/true — the hooks feature in Codex is on; feature unknown — Codex did not report it;\nproject NOT trusted (gate 1) — Codex does not trust this project; project trusted — it does; project config unparseable — the Codex settings file cannot be read;\nhooks.json absent (theia hooks install codex) — Theia's hooks for Codex are not installed; hooks.json present — they are.\nIf there is no codex command, the whole line is:\ncodex hooks: codex not found — install/auth codex first\nSee Codex.\nExit code Code When\n0\nNo errors\n1\nThere is a harnesses: FAIL line, an embeddings line with FAIL, vendor deliverables: FAIL, or shell (git-bash): FAIL (Windows only)\nCheck the exit code:\ntheia doctor echo $?\nThe last output line is the exit code, for example 1.\nBlind spots\nWarning\nThe.theia store, embeddings and ai rules lines look only at.theia in the current folder. The command does not find a store higher up, or the store of a folder outside git, and prints not initialized, although other commands do find it.\ndocker ready only means that the docker command was found. The line does not check that Docker is running.\nlexical index: FAIL does not change the exit code: the command can exit with 0 while text search is broken. Read the lines, not just the exit code.\nThe embeddings line uses only the current folder's policy. It ignores the settings in the config/ folder next to the user settings file.\nWhat doctor runs\ntheia doctor runs only checking commands:\ncodex login status, codex features list and claude auth status;\npodman --version and docker --version;\non Linux, an empty command inside bwrap and a trial systemd-run --user.\nOnce a day, before the check, Theia may ask GitHub for the latest version number if a token is saved. The time of that check is written to the cache. See New version notice.\nSee also\nFirst setup\nSandbox\nKeys and access\nStore and project roots","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/run.html":{"title":"theia run","content":"theia run starts a run. Theia works on your task in a copy of the repository, checks the result, reviews it and merges it into a separate branch. The same command also continues a run ( --resume ) or closes it without a result ( --abandon ).\ntheia run\ntheia run --task \"<text>\" [options]\ntheia run --from-plan <planId> [options]\ntheia run [options] < <file>\ntheia run --resume <runId> [options]\ntheia run --abandon <runId>\n<text> — the task in plain words, the brief.\n<planId> — the id of an approved plan, pln….\n<file> — a text file with the task.\n<runId> — the run id, run…. The same id without the run prefix also works.\nWithout a task, without --from-plan, and with --resume or --abandon without a value, theia prints a short usage text and exits with code 1:\ntheia run --task \"…\" [--track fast_path|full_spec] [--verify \"cmd\"]... [--budget usd] [--record] [--in-place] [--plain|--cli|--tui] [--non-interactive] [--root <name>] --from-plan <planId> execute an APPROVED plan artifact (R3) (--track with --from-plan forces every task onto that track for this run) --resume <runId> resume a paused/killed run (R5) --plan plan mode (alias of theia plan) (or pipe the task: echo \"…\" | theia run …)\nWarning\nTheia ignores unknown options without a warning. A typo such as --budjet 3 leaves the default in place. Only the values of --track and --interaction are validated, see General rules.\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--task\ntext\n—\nThe task. Required unless you use --from-plan or pipe the text to stdin. See --task.\n--track\nfast_path | full_spec\nfast_path\nWhere a task starts: at implementation, or at planning. See --track.\n--verify\nshell command\n—\nCheck command after each attempt. Repeat the option for several commands. See --verify.\n--budget\nUS dollars\nrun.maxUsdPerRun, 10\nMoney limit for the run. 0 means no limit. See --budget.\n--max-rounds\nnumber\nrun.maxRoundsPerTask, 10\nRound limit per task. 0 means no limit.\n--max-rescopes\nnumber\nrun.maxRescopes, 2\nHow many times the lead may restart a task. 0 means no limit.\n--plain\n—\n—\nOne text line per event.\n--cli\n—\n—\nA board that redraws in place.\n--tui\n—\nui.defaultView, tui\nThe full-screen interface.\n--non-interactive\n—\noff\nNo questions and no TUI.\n--no-spinner\n—\noff\nStatic symbols instead of animation.\n--interaction\nterminal | bus\nrun.interaction, terminal\nWhere clarifying questions go. Has an effect only with --resume.\n--from-plan\nplan id\n—\nRun an approved plan.\n--plan\n—\n—\nThe same as theia plan. Not shown in theia help.\n--root\nroot name\n—\nMulti-root workspaces only: the root of the task.\n--branch\nbranch name\ntheia/run-<6 characters>\nName of the run branch.\n--base\ngit ref\nupstream, else origin/HEAD, else HEAD\nCommit the run branch starts from.\n--no-run-branch\n—\noff\nMerge straight into the branch you have checked out.\n--in-place\n—\noff\nWork in the folder itself, without a copy and without git. Not shown in theia help.\n--sessions, --no-sessions\n—\nrun.sessionContinuity, off\nExperimental: record prompt fingerprints.\n--record\n—\noff\nSave model replies in the store.\n--resume\nrun id\n—\nContinue a paused or stopped run. See --resume.\n--abandon\nrun id\n—\nClose a run without a result. See --abandon.\n--task\nThe text of the task. Put it in quotes. If you give no --task and stdin is not a terminal, theia reads the whole stdin as the task:\necho \"<text>\" | theia run [options]\ntheia run [options] < <file>\nThe text may include files, see Include files with @.\nInclude files with @\nWrite @path in the task text to include a file ( file inclusion ). Theia puts the file content under the task text in a block that starts with --- file: <path> (sha256 <12 characters>, <N> chars) ---. The log records the path, size and fingerprint of each file.\nThe path is relative to the current folder. It must start with a letter, a digit or _. Letters of any language work, for example @заметки.md.\n@/absolute/path, @./file and @../file are not expanded. They stay in the text as they are, without an error.\n@@name gives a literal @name. An address such as a@b.c is not a file reference.\nA missing file stops the command with exit code 1.\nA file with a secret stops the command with exit code 1. Secrets are a private key, an API key, a card number or a seed phrase. E-mail addresses are replaced by a mask.\nThere is no size limit by default. Above 200,000 characters theia prints a warning ( run.contextAlertChars ).\nError messages:\nrun: @file: no such file 'nope.txt' (relative to ~/projects/app; escape a literal with @@)\nrun: @key.pem: prohibited content (pem_private_key) — this file cannot enter a model prompt (THE-014)\nThe same @ rules work in theia plan, theia msg and theia probe. The (THE-014) code at the end is an internal rule number, see THE-….\n--track\nfast_path ( short path ) — the task goes straight to implementation. Use it for small changes.\nfull_spec ( full specification ) — the planner looks at the task first and may split it into subtasks. Then implementation starts. There is no approval step. For a plan that you approve first, use theia plan.\nWith --from-plan, --track puts every task of the plan on one track for this run. Theia prints track override: all 2 tasks forced to full_spec for this run (plan content unchanged).\nA wrong value stops the command: run: unknown track 'bogus' (supported: full_spec, fast_path), exit code 1.\n--verify\nTheia runs the command after each implementation attempt as bash -lc \"<command>\", on your computer, not in a sandbox.\nIt runs in the task's copy of the repository,.theia/runs/<run folder>/ws-<task>. With --in-place or outside git it runs in the project folder.\nExit code 0 means the check passed ( verify_pass ). Any other code means it failed ( verify_fail ). No model takes part.\nSeveral --verify options run in order. The first failure skips the rest: $ false → exit 1 (no output) (1 verify command(s) skipped after this failure).\nEach command may run for 5 minutes. A command that is stopped shows exit null.\nWithout --verify a model, the verifier, checks the result. The same applies in --in-place mode.\nA second failure in a row records the anomaly fresh_perspective: the next attempt is made by the fresh_implementer role. After 3 failed checks in a row theia records the anomaly stop_and_fix, freezes the task and passes it to the lead.\nIn a plan run, a task's own checks from the plan replace --verify.\n--budget\nThe budget in US dollars for the whole run. Decimals are allowed. The default is run.maxUsdPerRun in policy.yaml, 10.\n0 removes the money limit. Theia prints budget: 0 = unmetered (loop guards stay: maxTotalRounds, round/rescope budgets).\nWhen the money runs out, theia refuses the next model call and pauses the run, exit code 0. To continue, run theia run --resume <runId> --budget <usd>. See When the budget runs out.\nOne repeated call that fixes a broken model reply may go up to $0.75 over the limit.\nWarning\nTheia does not check the value. Text that is not a number, for example --budget abc, removes the limit. The start line then shows run.created budget=$NaN track=fast_path and the end shows budget remaining: NaN.\n--max-rounds\nThe limit of rounds per task for this run. It overrides run.maxRoundsPerTask (default 10). A round is one verdict on the task. When the limit is reached, theia records the anomaly round_budget and passes the task to the lead. 0 means no limit. A value that is not a number is ignored.\n--max-rescopes\nHow many times the lead may rescope a task, that is, send it back with new directions and a fresh round count. It overrides run.maxRescopes (default 2). Past the limit the task is discarded. On the command line 0 means no limit. In policy.yaml, 0 means no rescopes at all.\n--plain\nOne line per event, see Output. Works everywhere, including CI and pipes.\n--cli\nA board that redraws in place, one line per task with its phase and round. It needs a terminal. Without one, theia prints view: --cli needs a TTY — degrading to --plain.\n--tui\nThe full-screen TUI. It is the default on a terminal, set by ui.defaultView. It needs a terminal for output and keys, and no --non-interactive. Otherwise theia falls back to --plain and prints view: --tui needs an interactive terminal (a TTY for output and keys) — degrading to --plain or view: --tui is interactive; --non-interactive degrades to --plain. See Screens and elements.\nIf you give several view options, the last one wins.\n--non-interactive\nTheia asks no questions. A clarifying question freezes the task instead: the log records the anomaly clarify_unanswered. A second unanswered question in a row records clarify_stalled and pauses the run. The TUI is not used.\n--no-spinner\nStatic symbols instead of spinners in the TUI and on the board. The same as the variable THEIA_NO_SPINNER=1.\n--interaction\nWhere clarifying questions go:\nterminal — a form in the TUI or a text prompt in the terminal.\nbus — the control bus. The question is written to the run log and waits up to 600 seconds ( run.interactionWaitSeconds ) for an answer from theia answer.\nThe default is run.interaction, terminal. A wrong value stops the command: --interaction must be terminal|bus (got 'foo'), exit code 1.\nWarning\nIn 0.2.201 --interaction has an effect only together with --resume. A new run parses it but does not use it: theia run --task \"…\" --interaction bus behaves like a normal run. See Control a run.\n--from-plan\nRuns an approved plan. Theia creates one task per plan task, named [key] description, in dependency order. A task starts only when its dependencies are merged. Each task uses its own checks from the plan. A task without checks uses --verify, or the model verifier. The id line looks like run run2akdqddivk222d4b46etos plan pln2akdqddd5c2223ubqzsvlt @ fce3554ee5e2… → 2 tasks. The run.created line gains plan=<8 characters>.\nRefusals, all with exit code 1:\nA plan that is not approved:\nrun: plan pln2akdqddd5c2223ubqzsvlt is 'draft', not approved — approve it first (theia plan approve)\nAn unknown plan id: plan: unknown plan <id without pln>. Example output:\nrun branch: theia/run-3c5e57 (base HEAD (no origin remote) @ 02b18f7)\nvmk2jqgu run.created budget=$10 track=fast_path plan=zzzzzzzz\nplan: unknown plan 2akzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzz\nThe default budget is below the cost estimate of the plan (the plan floor ). Theia estimates the cost from earlier runs in this store that recorded call prices, and multiplies the average price per task by the number of tasks. Without such history, or with --budget 0, there is no check. The refusal text:\nrun: plan floor ~$<X> (<N> tasks × $<Y> avg/task from this store's recorded runs) exceeds budget $<Z> — pass --budget <n> explicitly, raise run.maxUsdPerRun, or --budget 0 (unmetered)\nWith an explicit --budget theia only warns and goes on:\nrun: WARNING — plan floor ~$<X> (<N> tasks × $<Y> avg/task from this store's recorded runs) exceeds budget $<Z>; proceeding on your explicit --budget (expect a budget pause; theia run --resume <id> --budget N continues)\nWarning\nIn 0.2.201 the refusal for an unapproved or unknown plan comes after theia has already written run.created, created the folder.theia/runs/<run folder>/trunk and the branch theia/run-…. An unfinished run is left behind, and theia prune does not take it. How to remove it: A refused plan run leaves a branch.\n--plan\ntheia run --plan … is the same as theia plan …, see theia plan. Subcommands work too: theia run --plan list. Not shown in theia help.\n--root\nFor multi-root workspaces: the root of the task. In a single-root repository theia ignores the option, even with a name that does not exist. In a multi-root workspace an unknown name stops the command: theia: unknown root '<name>' (declared: <list of roots>), exit code 1. See Storage and project roots.\n--branch\nThe name of the run branch. The default is theia/run- plus the last 6 characters of the run id. If the branch already exists, theia stops with exit code 1:\nrun: branch 'docs/demo' already exists — pass --branch <other-name>, or delete the stale one first (git branch -D docs/demo)\n--base\nThe commit the run branch starts from. By default theia takes the upstream of your current branch, for example origin/main. Without an upstream it takes origin/HEAD, and without a remote the local HEAD. Local commits that you have not pushed are not included unless you give --base HEAD. The start line shows the choice:\nrun branch: theia/run-ha55jw (base HEAD @ c4095c5)\nA ref that does not exist stops the command: run: --base 'nosuchref' does not resolve in <repository> (fetch it, or name a reachable ref), exit code 1.\n--branch and --base need a run branch. With --in-place, --no-run-branch or outside git, theia stops with exit code 1:\nrun: --branch/--base need the run-branch mode (single-root git repo, not --in-place/--no-run-branch)\n--no-run-branch\nNo run branch. Each finished task is merged straight into the branch checked out in your repository, as a commit Merge branch 'ai/<task>'. Tasks still work in copies. In this mode there is no run branch: … line at the start and no run branch … ahead of base line at the end.\n--in-place\nNo copy and no git. The roles change files directly in your folder, theia commits nothing, and the merge step only marks the task merged. With --verify the command runs in the project folder; without --verify a model checks the result. Theia uses the same mode automatically outside a git repository. It prints mode: in-place (no git repo or --in-place) — no worktree isolation. Not shown in theia help. Not available in multi-root workspaces: run: --in-place is not available in multi-root workspaces (R9), exit code 1.\nWarning\nIn this mode changes go straight into your files. Commit or stash your own work before the run.\n--sessions, --no-sessions\nOverride run.sessionContinuity for this run. --sessions prints sessions: sigma-shared (stable-prefix continuity, digests audited) and records prompt fingerprints in the log. The prompts do not change. This is an experimental setting.\n--record\nSaves each model reply in the store, so a later fork of the run can reuse it. Useful mainly for tests.\ntheia run --resume\ntheia run --resume <runId> [options]\nContinues a paused or stopped run from its log. Finished steps are not repeated. A step that was cut off starts again.\nExample output:\nrun branch: theia/run-elou46 (resumed @ 74a5168)\nresuming run run2akdqcg6bs222ldyelou46 (paused; budget 10.00 usd remaining)\nThe word in brackets is paused, live (after a hard stop or kill ) or finished. The amount may read unmetered.\nGive the full id or the id without run. A shorter part fails with terryid: malformed id '…'.\nThese options work with --resume: --plain, --cli, --tui, --non-interactive, --budget, --verify, --max-rounds, --max-rescopes, --interaction, --sessions, --no-sessions, --record, --in-place.\n--budget sets the amount left for the rest of the run. It does not add to the old budget. 0 removes the limit.\n--verify replaces the check commands of the run.\nTheia ignores --task, --track, --from-plan, --branch, --base, --no-run-branch and --root here.\nAfter a resume the run reads notes from theia msg at every step, see theia msg.\nTheia refuses with exit code 1 in these cases:\nresume: no events for <id> — no run with this id.\nresume: all tasks terminal (finished) — nothing to resume — nothing left to do. For a fork made without --at the brackets say paused.\nThe log was changed (code THE-005, see THE-… ):\nresume: audit chain BROKEN at <event id> — refusing to resume a tampered run (THE-005)\nA warning on stderr that does not stop the command: resume: recorded plan not loadable — per-task verify unavailable, falling back to run-level. It appears for a plan run whose plan could not be loaded: the tasks' checks from the plan are unavailable, and --verify applies.\nAn abandoned run can be resumed by mistake\nIn 0.2.201 --resume does not refuse a run that you closed with --abandon. It runs it again. The sign is the word finished in the first line: resuming run run2akec65eyw223ou46mazw5 (finished; budget 5.00 usd remaining). Check the run with theia show <runId> first. If it says halted: abandoned, start a new run instead.\ntheia run --abandon\ntheia run --abandon <runId>\nMarks a run as closed without a result: halted: abandoned. The command does not stop a running process and deletes nothing. Stop the run first with Ctrl+C or q. It works for a fork as well.\nExample output:\nabandoned run2akdqbnjho2237q4cxhpts — workspaces kept; reclaim with 'theia prune'\nThe run is already finished: abandon: run already finished — nothing to do (prune reclaims finished runs), exit code 1.\nUnknown id: abandon: no events for <id>, exit code 1.\nThe copies and records of an abandoned run stay until theia prune.\nOutput\nWith --plain theia prints one line per event. Each line starts with the last 8 characters of the event id.\nLine Meaning\nrun branch: theia/run-4lejtj (base HEAD (no origin remote) @ 0de439e)\nThe run branch and the commit it starts from.\nmode: in-place (no git repo or --in-place) — no worktree isolation\nIn-place mode: no copy, no git.\nrun run2akdqiyvvs222jx34lejtj task tsk2akdqiyyco222jxsd2vfzc (fast_path)\nThe run id and the task id.\nrun.created budget=$10 track=fast_path\nThe run started with this budget. budget=$null means no limit. plan=<8 characters> marks a plan run.\ntask.phase xsd2vfzc ready → implement\nThe task moved to a new phase.\nverdict xsd2vfzc verifier → verify_pass (1 evidence)\nA role's verdict. Model verdicts end with a short note in quotes.\ngateway <role>@<transport> → ok $<cost>\nOne model call ( gateway call ) and its cost.\n!! anomaly <kind> …\nSomething unusual: a limit reached, a failed call.\nuser.note [msg] <text>\nAn instruction from theia msg.\nrun.paused rounds=2 (resumable)\nThe run paused and can be resumed.\nrun.finished halted=completed rounds=4\nThe run ended.\nlive ⏱ implementer tsk…d2vfzc call started\nProgress. Not stored in the log.\nClosing lines:\ntsk2akdqiyyco222jxsd2vfzc → merged (4 rounds) Add a greeting.txt file with the text hello\nhalted: completed · 2 gateway calls · $0.0000\naudit chain: intact\nbudget remaining: 10\nrun branch theia/run-4lejtj @ 8d6868e — 2 merges ahead of base; review/PR from it (your checkout was not touched)\nThe final phase of each task. merged means the change is on the run branch. discarded means the task ended without a result.\nhalted: is the reason the run stopped, see halted. completed means nothing was left to do; tasks can still be discarded. paused means the run can be resumed. abandoned means the run was closed with --abandon. budget means the run reached the internal limit of 1000 rounds for the whole run; it has nothing to do with money. When there were anomalies the line is longer: halted: completed · 4 gateway calls · $0.0000 ·!! 2 anomalies.\nThe number of model calls and their total cost.\naudit chain: intact means the run log is complete and unchanged, see audit chain.\nbudget remaining: is the money left. unmetered means no limit.\nThe last line shows the run branch. The number counts commits after the base, not merges. no merges landed means there is nothing to take. With --no-run-branch and --in-place this line is absent.\nA paused run adds paused — resume with: theia run --resume <runId>. After the closing lines theia prints the note task.note [post_run_maintenance] and a routine cleanup line, for example maintenance: 0 summaries refreshed, 0 orphan generations GCed.\nExit codes Code When\n0\nEvery task ended merged (or planned ). Also when the run paused: Ctrl+C or q once, the budget ran out, or a guard paused the run.\n1\nAt least one task ended another way, for example discarded. Also for wrong arguments and refusals: unknown track, the branch exists, the base is not found, the plan is not approved or unknown, a file for @ is missing or contains a secret, nothing to resume.\n130\nHard stop: a second Ctrl+C, or a second q in the TUI.\n143\nThe process was killed with SIGTERM, for example by kill. Theia does not catch the signal, and the log ends at the last event. theia show prints halted: (still open), and theia run --resume continues the run with the word live in brackets.\nWhat a run creates\n.theia/ is added to.gitignore in the repository root.\n.theia/runs/<run folder>/trunk is a hidden git worktree with the run branch checked out. The folder name is the run id without run.\n.theia/runs/<run folder>/ws-<task> is a full copy of the repository for each task, the workspace. It includes untracked files such as node_modules and.env, so the roles can read them. These files are never committed. Theia deletes the copy after a successful merge and keeps it otherwise.\nThe git branch theia/run-<6 characters>, and ai/<task> for each merged task. Theia never pushes them.\nRecords in.theia/store.db.\nHow to remove them: Your first task and theia prune.\nEnvironment variables Variable What it does\nTHEIA_LOG_FILE\nAppends every event of the run to this file: the --plain line and the full data in JSON. The file starts with a line # theia run <runId> — <time>. Theia does not flush the buffer on exit, so the file may be incomplete: the last events are not written.\nTHEIA_NO_SPINNER\n1 gives static symbols, the same as --no-spinner.\nAll variables: Environment variables.\nSee also\nYour first task\nControl a run\nRun results\ntheia plan\ntheia msg, answer, probe\npolicy.yaml settings","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/plan.html":{"title":"theia plan","content":"theia plan asks the planner for a plan, saves it as a draft and lets you read, change and approve it before a run. The step-by-step guide is Plan before you run.\ntheia plan\nCreates a plan.\ntheia plan --task \"<text>\" [--non-interactive]\ntheia plan [--non-interactive] < <file>\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--task\ntext\n—\nThe task to plan. Without it, and with stdin not a terminal, theia reads the task from stdin. See --task.\n--non-interactive\n—\noff\nDo not ask the planner's questions on the terminal; save them in the draft. Not shown in theia help. See --non-interactive.\nOther options are ignored: --track, --budget and the like do not affect the plan. Without a task, and also with an unknown subcommand such as theia plan bogus, theia prints this usage text and exits with code 1:\ntheia plan [--task \"…\"] [--non-interactive] (or pipe the brief: theia plan < REQ.md)\ntheia plan approve|request-changes|add-context|show|list …\ntheia run --plan … does the same as theia plan ….\n--task\nThe text of the task, as in theia run --task. The same @ file rules apply; the error reads plan: @file: no such file …, exit code 1. The plan keeps the text exactly as the planner saw it.\n--non-interactive\nWithout this option theia asks the planner's questions (up to two rounds) and plans again with your answers. This happens only when both stdin and stdout are a terminal. The answers are also saved in memory, so the same question is not asked again.\nWith --non-interactive, or when stdin or stdout is not a terminal, theia does not ask. It saves a draft with the questions and a single task unresolved. It prints OPEN QUESTIONS — plan saved as draft pln…:, the questions as lines? <question> (<why>), and the hint answer with: theia plan add-context pln… \"…\" — then re-run theia plan or approve. The exit code is 0.\nWhat theia prints\ncontext collected (N blocks): and one line per block: the code map ( [blueprint] ), your context rules ( [context:<name>] ), memory hits ( [memory:…] ). Each line is cut at 90 characters.\nWhen the context is large, stderr gets a warning, but everything still loads:\nplan: collected context is large (<N> chars > run.contextAlertChars=200000) — everything still loads\nAbove run.contextRefuseChars, if you set it, theia stops before the model call with exit code 1:\nplan: collected context <N> chars exceeds run.contextRefuseChars=<M> — refusing before any model call (raise the policy, narrow the manifest, or set run.blueprintMinImportance)\nIf the planner's reply cannot be read: plan: planner reply off contract (…) — asking for a repair (one bounded retry). A second failure: plan: planner reply rejected twice — …, exit code 1. A reply that breaks the plan limits is rejected too; see the error texts there.\nThe plan, as plan show prints it.\nIf tasks have checks: verify pre-flight: off (opt in with policy run.planVerifyPreflight: always). With run.planVerifyPreflight: always theia runs each check once at the base before approval and prints one line per check:\nverify pre-flight: `<command>` passes at base\nverify pre-flight: `<command>` exit <N> at base — expected for red→green; if this is infrastructure (missing tool/framework), fix the command before approving\nWhen every check of a task already passes before any change, theia warns that such checks prove nothing:\nverify pre-flight: task '<key>' — every check passes at base (green→green attests nothing about this task's change); prefer a focused red→green first entry\nThe results appear later in plan show.\ncost estimate: ~$X (N tasks × $Y avg/task from recorded runs) or cost estimate: no run history — no estimate. The estimate comes from earlier runs in this store that recorded call prices.\nnext: theia plan approve pln… && theia run --from-plan pln….\nOnly theia plan --task calls a model: one planner role. The other subcommands make no model calls.\nPlan limits What Limit\nTasks in a plan\n1 to 32\nTask key\nlower-case letters, digits, _ and -; starts with a letter or digit; up to 40 characters\nTask description\nup to 2000 characters\nChecks of a task\none command, or a list of up to 8; each up to 300 characters\nDependencies of a task\nup to 16; each must be a key of this plan; no cycles and no dependency on itself\nRoots of a task\na root name follows the key pattern; the roots list holds up to 16\nContext blocks\nup to 128; a source up to 100 characters\nA planner reply that breaks these rules is rejected with exit code 1. The error texts for dependencies:\nplan: duplicate task key 'docs'\nplan: task 'docs' depends on unknown key 'nope'\nplan: task 'docs' depends on itself\nplan: dependency cycle through 'docs'\nPlan statuses Status Meaning\ndraft\nNew, or changed with add-context. Cannot be run.\nchanges_requested\nSomeone recorded what is wrong. Cannot be run.\napproved\nCan be run with theia run --from-plan.\ntheia plan list\nLists all plans in the store.\ntheia plan list\nOne line per plan: the id, the status in brackets, the number of tasks and the first 70 characters of the task text. The order follows the id, that is, the creation time. Example output:\npln2akdql5sgs225jjdujidwc [draft] 2 tasks — Add a greeting file and mention it in the README\nWith no plans the command prints nothing. The exit code is 0.\ntheia plan show\nPrints one plan.\ntheia plan show <planId>\nExample output:\nplan 2akdql5sgs225jjdujidwc [approved] digest e96fe336654a…\nbrief: Add a greeting file and mention it in the README\nnote: Keep the README change short [user] README.md is plain Markdown greet [fast_path] ✓ test -f greeting.txt Create greeting.txt with the text hello docs [fast_path] ⇐ greet ✓ grep -q greeting.txt README.md Mention greeting.txt in README.md\nThe first line: the id without pln, the status and the start of the digest.\nbrief: — the task text, its first 100 characters.\nnote: — the text from request-changes, if any.\n[user] … — context added with add-context, the first 80 characters of the block. The planner's context blocks appear the same way, with their source in brackets.\nOne entry per task: the key, the track, ⇐ dependencies, ✓ checks, and the description on the next line.\nIf checks were tried before approval, the end of the output adds verify pre-flight at base: with a line per check: ✔ or ✖, the exit code and the command.\nErrors, all with exit code 1:\nWithout an id: theia plan show <planId>.\nUnknown plan: plan: unknown plan <id>.\nMalformed id: terryid: malformed id 'not-an-id'.\nThe saved content does not match its digest: plan: <id> content does not match its digest — refusing (tamper/corruption).\ntheia plan request-changes\nRecords what is wrong with a plan.\ntheia plan request-changes <planId> <text>\nExample output:\nchanges requested on pln2akdql5sgs225jjdujidwc\nThe status becomes changes_requested and the text is saved as the note of the plan. The tasks and the digest do not change. Every word after the id is joined into the text, unknown flags included.\nErrors, both with exit code 1:\nWithout an id: theia plan request-changes <planId> \"text\".\nWithout text: theia plan request-changes <planId> \"what must change\".\ntheia plan add-context\nAdds information to a plan.\ntheia plan add-context <planId> <text>\nExample output:\ncontext added — NEW digest e96fe336654a…, status draft (re-approval required)\nThe text is added as a context block with the source user. The plan gets a new digest and the status draft, so it needs a new approval. Every word after the id is joined into the text.\nErrors, both with exit code 1:\nWithout an id: theia plan add-context <planId> \"text\".\nWithout text: theia plan add-context <planId> \"context\".\ntheia plan approve\nApproves a plan, so that theia run --from-plan can run it.\ntheia plan approve <planId>\nExample output:\napproved pln2akdql5sgs225jjdujidwc @ e96fe336654a…\nThe approval is tied to this digest. Approving an approved plan prints the same line again and writes no new event. Without an id theia prints theia plan approve <planId>; for an unknown plan it prints plan: unknown plan <id>; both exit with code 1.\nRunning a plan\ntheia run --from-plan <planId> [options]\nSee theia run --from-plan. A refused run for an unapproved or unknown plan leaves a branch and an unfinished run behind: clean up.\nExit codes Code When\n0\nThe plan was created, shown or changed. Also when plan --task without a terminal saved a draft with open questions.\n1\nNo task or an unknown subcommand; a missing id or text; an unknown or malformed plan id; a digest mismatch; a missing @ file; context above run.contextRefuseChars; the planner replied off contract twice or broke the plan limits.\nIds and storage\nA plan id starts with pln. Commands accept the full id or the id without pln.\nPlans live in the store.theia/store.db. Each plan has an id, a digest, a status, its content and a note.\nEach action is recorded in the plan's own log: created, changes requested, context added, approved. theia audit verify checks this log.\nThere is no command to edit or delete a plan. To change the tasks, create a new plan.\nSee also\nPlan before you run\ntheia run\nMCP server","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/run-control.html":{"title":"theia msg, answer, probe","content":"These commands talk to an existing run: msg gives it an instruction, answer answers its questions, probe asks a question about it without changing it.\ntheia msg\nAdds an instruction to the run log. The roles see it in their next prompts. No model is called.\ntheia msg <runId> [--task <taskId>] <text>\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<runId>\nrun id\n—\nThe run. The full id run… or the same without run.\n--task\ntask id or its last characters\n—\nSend the note to one task only. See --task.\n<text>\ntext\n—\nThe instruction. All words after the run id are joined, so quotes are optional but recommended. Unknown flags become part of the text too: theia msg <runId> --foo hello there records the note --foo hello there. @path includes a file, see file inclusion.\nWithout text or without an id theia prints theia msg <runId> [--task <taskId>] \"steering note\" and exits with code 1. An unknown run gives msg: no events for <id>, exit code 1.\nThe answer depends on the state of the run:\nRun state Output\npaused, including a fresh fork\nnoted — folds into guidance on: theia run --resume run2akdqcg6bs222ldyelou46\nrunning\na line noted — this run appears live: …, full text below\nfinished\nnoted — run already finished; the note stays on the audit trail (and guides any fork)\nThe full text for a running run:\nnoted — this run appears live: it folds cross-process notes at its next dispatch boundary (the store-as-control-bus poll; a live --tui run also steers in-process with its 'm' key; if the run predates the control bus, msg + resume still delivers)\ntheia show <runId> --plain shows each note as user.note [msg] <text>, with the text cut at 70 characters.\n--task\nSends the note to one task: only that task's prompts include it.\nGive the full task id tsk…, the id without tsk, or its last characters.\nOutput: noted for task …dlyndt2n — folds into its guidance only.\nIf the id is unknown, the note goes to the whole run and the lead sees it: task …nosuch is unknown — rerouted to run-level guidance (the lead sees it; the original addressee is recorded on the note).\nIf the task has already ended, the same happens, but instead of unknown the message names its phase, for example is merged or is discarded.\nIf the characters match several tasks, theia stops with msg: task <id> is ambiguous (…), exit code 1.\nWhen the run uses the note How the run was started When the note is used\nPaused, then continued with theia run --resume\nFrom the resume on, in every prompt.\nContinued with theia run --resume and still running\nFrom its next step.\nStarted with theia run --task or --from-plan and still running\nNot used. It reaches the run only if you pause and resume it.\nFinished\nNever. The note stays in the log.\nWarning\nIn 0.2.201 a running run that was started with theia run --task or --from-plan does not read notes from other processes, although msg says it will. Pause the run, send the note and resume it. In the TUI you can steer directly with the m key, see Navigation and hotkeys.\nExit codes Code When\n0\nThe note was recorded, whatever the state of the run.\n1\nMissing id or text; unknown run; a missing @ file; the task id matches several tasks.\ntheia answer\nLists the clarifying questions that a run sent over the control bus, and answers them. No model is called.\ntheia answer <runId>\ntheia answer <runId> --question <questionId> [<answer> …]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<runId>\nrun id\n—\nThe run.\n--question\nquestion id or its last characters\n—\nThe question to answer. Without it theia lists the open questions.\n<answer> …\ntext, one argument per question\n—\nAnswers, matched to the questions in order: every word without -- after --question <id>. Quote each answer. No answers means you decline the question. theia help shows only one answer.\nWithout a run id theia prints the usage text and exits with code 1:\ntheia answer <runId> list pending questions\ntheia answer <runId> --question <id> \"answer 1\" [\"answer 2\" …]\nAn unknown run gives answer: no events for <id>, exit code 1.\nListing:\nNo open questions: no pending questions, exit code 0.\nOtherwise, for each question: a line question <questionId> (task …<8 characters>), then? <question> and, if given, the reason in brackets. At the end: answer with: theia answer <runId> --question <id> \"…\" — answers match questions in order.\nAnswering:\nTheia writes the answer to the run log. Output: answered <8 characters> (<event id>) — a live run consumes it within seconds; a paused run hears it on resume.\nNo answers: declined <8 characters> (<event id>).\nUnknown question: answer: no pending question 'zzzz' on this run, exit code 1.\nAnswers were given, but none of them matched a question: answer: no answers matched (fewer questions than answers?), exit code 1.\n--question\nThe id of the question from the listing. The last characters of the id are enough.\nWarning\nIn 0.2.201 only a run continued with theia run --resume <runId> --interaction bus sends questions to the control bus. It waits up to 600 seconds ( run.interactionWaitSeconds ), polling the log every 2 seconds. An answer written after the run stopped waiting is not used, although the message says \"a paused run hears it on resume\": on resume the kernel does not read run.answer events. Pass the information with theia msg and resume the run. A question on the control bus could not be produced in our tests of this version.\nExit codes Code When\n0\nThe list was printed, even an empty one; an answer was recorded; a question was declined.\n1\nNo run id; unknown run; no open question with that id; no answer matched.\ntheia probe\nA question about a run ( probe ): its state, verdicts and context. Theia makes one model call with the none role and does not change the run.\ntheia probe <runId> <question> [--budget <usd>]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<runId>\nrun id\n—\nThe run to ask about.\n<question>\ntext\n—\nThe question. All words after the run id are joined; unknown flags become part of the question too. @path includes a file.\n--budget\nUS dollars\n1\nMoney limit for this call. See --budget.\nOutput: the model's answer, an empty line, then a line with the probe id, the run, the last event, the cost, and a reminder that the run was not changed. Example output on the fake test transport, which gives no answer for the none role:\nfake-green: no verdict for role 'none' [probe run2akec5db32222xyvv75jas of run2akec5byvo222bl7knwfxo @ …lbkslnog — $0.0000; parent untouched]\nWorks for a running, paused or finished run.\nThe probe gets its own id run… and its own log. It appears in theia audit verify, and theia prune does not remove it: its log has no run.created event.\nIf you ask the same question about an unchanged run again, theia can reuse the recorded answer without a new model call (the hybrid gateway mode).\nWithout a question or without an id theia prints theia probe <runId> \"question\" [--budget usd] and exits with code 1.\nUnknown run: probe: no events for <id without run>, exit code 1.\nA failed model call: probe: gateway <kind>: <reason>, exit code 1.\nIf the run log was changed, theia refuses with exit code 1 (code THE-005, see THE-… ):\nprobe: parent chain BROKEN at <event id> — refusing to read a tampered run (THE-005)\n--budget\nThe money limit for the probe call, in US dollars. The default is $1. Not shown in theia help.\nExit codes Code When\n0\nAn answer was received.\n1\nMissing id or question; unknown run; the audit chain is broken; a missing @ file; the model call failed.\nSee also\nControl a run\ntheia run\nMCP server","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/run-inspect.html":{"title":"theia show, replay, diagnose, compare, fork","content":"These commands read stored runs. show, replay and compare change nothing. diagnose changes nothing in the run but saves its result in memory. fork creates a new run from part of an existing one. None of them calls a model.\ntheia show\nPrints a stored run.\ntheia show <runId> [--plain | --cli | --tui]\ntheia show <runId> --task <taskId> [--diff]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<runId>\nrun id\n—\nThe run: the full id run… or the id without run. A shorter part does not work.\n--plain\n—\n—\nAll saved events, then the summary. See --plain.\n--cli\n—\n—\nThe final board. See --cli.\n--tui\n—\n—\nBrowse the run tree. Needs a terminal. See --tui.\n--task\ntask id or its last characters\n—\nCommits and changed files of one task. Wins over --plain, --cli and --tui. See --task.\n--diff\n—\noff\nWith --task: add the full diff. See --diff.\nWithout options theia prints the summary. Example output:\ntsk2akdql5ywk223qptn2mqab → merged (4 rounds) [greet] Create greeting.txt with the text hello\ntsk2akdql5ywk22bqovrzs5yx → merged (4 rounds) [docs] Mention greeting.txt in README.md\nhalted: completed · 4 gateway calls · $0.0000\naudit chain: intact\nOne line per task: the task id, its final phase, the number of rounds and the description.\nhalted: — the reason the run stopped, the number of model calls and their cost. See Output. A run without a run.finished event shows (still open): this is how a paused run, a killed process, a fresh fork and a run left behind by a refused --from-plan look.\naudit chain: intact or BROKEN, see audit chain.\nFor a fork: a line forked from <parent without run> at <8 characters of the event>. For a run with forks: one line per fork, fork → <fork without run> at <event> (<instruction from --with>); without --with the brackets say fork.\nIf the code index is out of date, stderr also shows index: 1 stale — the map may lag this run's tree.\nExample output for a fresh fork and for its parent:\ntsk2akec5bzeg222bkfnjuaoh → implement (1 rounds) Add greeting\nhalted: (still open) · 1 gateway calls · $0.0000\naudit chain: intact\nforked from 2akec5byvo222bl7knwfxo at kvtged3r\ntsk2akec5bzeg222bkfnjuaoh → discarded (6 rounds) Add greeting\nhalted: completed · 4 gateway calls · $0.0000\naudit chain: intact\nfork → 2akec5d3z6222vcka77nvi at l3rh4kfs (fork)\nfork → 2akec5d4uc222wheuo2hpq at kvtged3r (try harder)\nErrors, all with exit code 1:\nNo run id: the usage line theia show <runId> [--plain|--cli|--tui] [--task <id> [--diff]].\nUnknown run: theia show: no events for run run2akzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzz.\nA shortened or malformed id: terryid: neither a body nor a full id: 'cxwrue'.\nOtherwise the exit code is 0.\n--plain\nPrints every saved event of the run in the same format as theia run --plain, then the summary. Progress lines with live are not saved, so they are missing here. The format of the lines: Output.\n--cli\nPrints the final board and the audit chain line. Works without a terminal. Example output:\nrun …dhcxwrue $0.0000/$10.00 · 2 calls · 16 events tsk…dumszl6j fast_path merged r4 merger→merged\n— halted: completed\naudit chain: intact\nThe first line shows the end of the run id, the money spent and the budget, the number of model calls and the number of events. Then one line per task: the track, the phase, the round ( r4 ) and the last verdict.\n--tui\nOpens the run tree in the TUI, where you can browse the tasks and step through the events. It reads the log once and does not follow a run that is still going. See Screens and elements. Without a terminal theia prints view: --tui needs a TTY — degrading to the summary on stderr, shows the summary and exits with code 0.\n--task\nShows what one task changed: its branch ai/<task>, its commits, and a summary of changed files against the commit the task started from. Example output:\ntask tsk2akdql5ywk223qptn2mqab — ai/2akdql5ywk223qptn2mqab @ f87e49cecb50 (base a67ee9e260d3)\nattempts: f87e49c theia: [greet] Create greeting.txt with the text hello (attempt 0) greeting.txt | 1 + 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)\nGive the full task id tsk…, the id without tsk, or its last characters.\nIf the characters match several tasks: show: ambiguous task '<id>' (…), exit code 1.\nIf they match none: show: no task matching '<id>' in this run, exit code 1.\n--task without an id: theia show: --task needs a task id (suffix accepted), exit code 1.\nWarning\n--task works only for merged tasks of a run that used copies, because theia copies the branch ai/<task> into your repository at merge time. For any other task, and for every task of an --in-place run, even a merged one, theia prints the message below and exits with code 1, even if nothing was pruned. The copy of such a task stays in.theia/runs/<run folder>/ws-<task>.\nshow: audit branch ai/2akdqckb7s225cdjcvcjj5 not found in ~/projects/app (in-place run, or the branch was pruned)\nA third refusal, also with code 1, appears for runs of old versions that recorded no task base:\nshow: no recorded workspace base for task …cdjcvcjj5 (pre-R2 run) — inspect ai/2akdqckb7s225cdjcvcjj5 manually\n--diff\nTogether with --task, adds the full diff of the task after the summary. Example output:\ndiff --git a/greeting.txt b/greeting.txt\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..ce01362\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/greeting.txt\n@@ -0,0 +1 @@\n+hello\nExit codes Code When\n0\nThe summary, the events, the board or the task's changes were printed. Also when --tui without a terminal showed the summary.\n1\nNo id; unknown run; malformed id; --task without a value, with no match, with several matches, or without the ai/<task> branch in the repository.\ntheia replay\nReplay reads the log of a run again and rebuilds its final state. Nothing runs and no model is called. Use it to check that a log is complete and readable.\ntheia replay <runId>\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<runId>\nrun id\n—\nThe run.\nExample output:\nevents: 16; chain: intact tsk2akdqck7ds2234dumszl6j → merged\nverdicts: 4; gateway calls: 2\nsigma: {}\nevents: N; chain: intact — the number of events and the state of the audit chain. BROKEN at <event> means the log was changed.\nOne line per task with its final phase.\nverdicts and gateway calls — how many verdicts and model calls the log holds.\nsigma: — the shared state that the roles saved during the run, as JSON. {} means it is empty.\nExit codes:\nCode When\n0\nThe audit chain is intact.\n1\nThe chain is broken, the run is unknown ( replay: no events for <id without run> ), or the id is missing ( theia replay <runId> ).\ntheia diagnose\nNames the most likely reason why a run ended the way it did. Theia matches the log against a fixed list of rules. It makes no model call.\ntheia diagnose <runId>\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<runId>\nrun id\n—\nThe run.\nExample output, shortened:\nroot cause: rejected-by-review\nwhy: a task terminated discarded with no anomaly — the work was produced and judged wrong, not blocked\nfingerprint: 0b0ec207de4ff4b3… memory: 2akdqckhcs223li2axo3ft\nanomalies: none\ntaxonomy: —\ngateway: 4 calls, $0.0000 task 2akdqckb7s225cdjcvcjj5 → discarded; verdicts: ready→verify_pass→approve→merge_fail→arb_escalate→lead_discard evidence[command] precheck:2akdqckb7s225cdjcvcjj5 (no commits on the task branch)\nroot cause: — the rule that matched, see the table below. The text (fingerprint seen before — reused) is added when the same pattern was diagnosed before.\nwhy: — the explanation of the rule.\nfingerprint: and memory: — the fingerprint of the pattern, and the id of the memory record with the diagnosis.\nanomalies: and taxonomy: — the anomalies of the run and their classes.\ngateway: — the number of model calls and their cost.\nFor each task: its final phase, its chain of verdicts, and the evidence of each verdict.\nRoot cause Meaning\nverification-loop\nChecks failed 3 times in a row, and the task was frozen.\nrunaway-decomposition\nThe work was split into more subtasks than the limits allow.\nbudget-exhaustion\nThe money limit stopped the work.\nconfused-runner\nModels kept giving replies that are not allowed.\nreview-loop\nThe reviewer kept objecting while every check passed.\ncapability-gap\nThe implementer reported that it cannot do the task ( cant_do ).\nrejected-by-review\nA task was discarded without any anomaly: the work was done but judged wrong.\ninconclusive\nNo rule matched. The why: line reads no rule matched the evidence — the honest arm; inspect the trace, then extend the table.\nTheia checks the rules in this order, and the first match wins. It saves the diagnosis as a memory record in the scope diagnostics.\nExit codes Code When\n0\nThe diagnosis was printed, inconclusive included.\n1\nNo id ( theia diagnose <runId> ) or unknown run ( diagnose: no events for <id> ).\ntheia compare\nCompares two runs step by step. It is most useful for a run and its fork.\ntheia compare <runA> <runB>\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<runA>, <runB>\nrun ids\n—\nThe two runs to compare.\nExample output for a run and its fork:\nA: run2akdqckarw223cdvn75mka (6 verdicts)\nB: run2akdqckj62223mjar2dw7f (7 verdicts)\nverdict sequences diverge at step 3 (3 shared): A tail: djcvcjj5 merger → merge_fail djcvcjj5 arbiter → arb_escalate djcvcjj5 lead → lead_discard B tail: djcvcjj5 reviewer → approve djcvcjj5 merger → merge_fail djcvcjj5 arbiter → arb_escalate djcvcjj5 lead → lead_discard\ncost A (post-baseline): $0.0000\ncost B (post-baseline): $0.0000\noutcomes: djcvcjj5: A=discarded B=discarded\nartifacts: A merged [] B merged [] (identical)\nA: and B: — the two runs and their number of verdicts.\nverdict sequences diverge at step N (N shared): — where the verdicts start to differ, followed by up to 10 next verdicts of each run. When a run has no more verdicts, the list is replaced by (ends), for example B tail: (ends) for a fork that was not continued yet. Without any difference the line reads verdict sequences: IDENTICAL and there are no tails.\ncost A (post-baseline): — the money each run spent since its own start. For a fork, the copied history is not counted. For a run of an old version without a run.created or run.forked event the line reads cost A: n/a (run predates baselines — no run.created/run.forked).\noutcomes: — the final phase of each task in both runs. ← differs marks a difference. Two unrelated runs share no tasks, so every task shows a difference.\nartifacts: — the merge commits of each run.\nExit codes Code When\n0\nThe comparison was printed.\n1\nAn id is missing ( theia compare <runA> <runB> ) or a run is unknown ( compare: no events for <id> ).\ntheia fork\nCreates a new run from the history of an existing run up to an event, optionally with a new instruction ( fork ). The new run starts paused. Continue it with theia run --resume.\ntheia fork <runId> [--at <eventId>] [--with \"<text>\"] [--budget <usd>]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<runId>\nrun id\n—\nThe run to copy.\n--at\nevent id or its last characters\nthe last event\nWhere to cut the history. See --at.\n--with\ntext\n—\nAn instruction for the new run. See --with.\n--budget\nUS dollars\n5\nThe budget of the new run. Not shown in theia help. See --budget.\nExample output:\nforked run2akdqckarw223cdvn75mka @ cbvlvdga → run2akdqckj62223mjar2dw7f events copied: 11; chain: intact djcvcjj5: no recorded commit — workspace will start clean from HEAD\ndrive it: theia run --resume run2akdqckj62223mjar2dw7f (gateway: hybrid — prefix replays free)\nevents copied: N; chain: intact — the copied history. The new run has its own id and its own log.\nFor each unfinished task one of these lines: workspace at recorded <commit> when theia restored the copy of the task from a recorded commit; no recorded commit — workspace will start clean from HEAD when there was no commit; recorded <commit> unreachable (<reason>) — starts clean from HEAD when a commit was recorded but the repository no longer has it. Outside git the line is (no git repo — nothing to re-materialize) instead.\ndrive it: — the command to continue the new run.\ntheia show of the new run adds forked from <parent without run> at <event>. theia show of the old run adds fork → <fork without run> at <event> (<instruction>).\nErrors, all with exit code 1:\nNo run id: the usage line theia fork <runId> [--at <eventId>] [--with \"steering\"] [--budget usd].\nUnknown run: fork: no events for <id>.\nUnknown event: fork: event <id> not found in the parent run.\nAn event of another kind: see --at.\nThe log of the run was changed (code THE-005, see THE-… ):\nfork: parent chain BROKEN at <event id> — refusing to fork a tampered run (THE-005)\n--at\nThe event to cut the history at. It must be a task.verdict or task.phase event. The full id or any tail of it works: for example, the 8 characters at the start of a line in theia show <runId> --plain. Theia refuses other events and lists up to 10 latest events you can use:\nfork: run.created is not a fork boundary — fork points are verdict/phase events (the attempt is the atom). Recent boundaries: dj3nvedr task.verdict clbwpqmm task.phase\nWithout --at theia copies the whole history. A fork of a finished run then has nothing left to do.\n--with\nThe text of an instruction. Theia saves it as a note in the new run. The roles see it after theia run --resume, as with theia msg. It also appears in theia show of the old run.\n--budget\nThe budget of the new run in US dollars. The default is 5, whatever run.maxUsdPerRun says. Money spent in the copied history does not count. Not shown in theia help. --budget of theia run --resume replaces this value.\nContinue a fork\ntheia run --resume <forkId>\nExample output:\nfork resume: gateway in hybrid mode (recorded prefix replays free)\nfork resume: parent ran on branch 'theia/run-n75mka' — the fork integrates in-checkout (fork-owned branches are queued)\nresuming run run2akdqckj62223mjar2dw7f (paused; budget 5.00 usd remaining)\nWarning\nA fork has no run branch of its own. Its merges go straight into the branch checked out in your repository.\nThe copied history is not run again. Saved model replies are reused only if they exist, for example after theia run --record. Other calls cost money as usual.\nA fork of a finished run made without --at cannot be continued: resume: all tasks terminal (paused) — nothing to resume. For theia prune it counts as finished, because the copied history contains run.finished.\nA fork made with --at that you did not continue stays unfinished for theia prune and is never removed. Close it with theia run --abandon <forkId>, see What prune leaves behind.\nExit codes Code When\n0\nThe fork was created.\n1\nNo id; unknown run; broken audit chain; the event was not found or is not a fork boundary.\nSee also\nRun results\ntheia run\ntheia audit, prune","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/index-cmd.html":{"title":"theia index","content":"theia index builds and reads the code index: one index entry per source file with its purpose, related files, public API and design notes. This page lists every subcommand, flag, output line and exit code.\nRead before the first command\nThe code index works only in a git repository. In a folder that is not a git repository, build, status and search stop with fatal: not a git repository and exit code 1. status --brief prints theia: no index with exit code 0, and render works without git.\ntheia index without a subcommand runs theia index build: a full build that calls a model for every new or changed file and costs money.\ntheia index [build] [--root <name>] [--concurrency <N>] [--budget <usd>] [<path>...]\ntheia index status [--root <name>] [--brief]\ntheia index render\ntheia index reindex\ntheia index search <query...>\nAny other subcommand prints theia index: unknown subcommand '<name>' and exits with code 1. Flags go after the subcommand: theia index --budget 1 fails with theia index: unknown subcommand '--budget'.\nWhat costs money Subcommand Calls a language model Cost\nbuild\nYes: one call per new or changed file, a second call if the reply was unusable\nPaid through the harness that routing picks for the indexer role\nreindex\nNo. It computes embeddings\nFree with the local model, paid per token with OpenAI\nsearch\nNo. With OpenAI embeddings the query is sent to OpenAI to get a vector\nFree with the local model\nstatus, render\nNo\nFree\ntheia index build\nCreates entries for new and changed files and deletes entries of files that left git. Unchanged files cost nothing: theia compares each file's content with the content its entry was built from.\ntheia index build [--root <name>] [--concurrency <N>] [--budget <usd>] [<path>...]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<path>...\nFile paths relative to the repository root. In a multi-root workspace: <root>/<path>\nAll tracked source files\nBuild entries only for these files. In a multi-root workspace a path without the root name stops the command: index: multi-root build keys must be rootName/relpath (roots: api, web).\n--root\nRoot name\nAll roots\nBuild entries only for the files of this root.\n--concurrency\nInteger 1–64\n4\nHow many model calls run at the same time.\n--budget\nPositive number, USD\nNo cap\nStop spending at this amount.\ntheia index build\nExample output of a repeated build where nothing changed:\nindex: 0 written, 4 current, 0 failed, 0 model calls\nrate: — files/s, 4 zero-cost (100%), 0 model calls, $0.0000 locked (0ms)\nAfter a build, run theia index reindex. Until then theia index search and theia ask do not find the new entries.\nWhich files are indexed\nFiles that git tracks, in every root. A new file is skipped until you run git add for it.\nOnly these extensions:.ts.mts.cts.tsx.js.jsx.mjs.cjs.py.go.java.kt.kts.c.h.cpp.cc.cxx.hpp.hh.hxx.cs.m.mm.swift.sh.bash.zsh.ksh.sql. Markdown, JSON, YAML and other languages are not indexed.\nPaths that contain test/ or fixtures/ are skipped. This is a plain text match: src/test/w.ts is skipped, and so is src/latest/x.ts; tests/y.ts is indexed.\nThere is no file size limit. The model sees the first 16,000 characters of a file and a short list of its symbols and imports.\nFiles are taken in turn from each top-level folder, so a --budget cap does not spend everything in one folder.\nPaths\nWith paths, theia builds only those files. They do not have to be tracked by git or be in a supported language. A build with paths never deletes entries. An entry for an untracked file shows as orphaned in theia index status, and the next full build deletes it.\ntheia index build src/calc.ts\nA path that does not exist stops the command with ENOENT: no such file or directory, open '<full path>' and exit code 1. This is a raw Node message, not a theia one: the quotes hold the full path of the file.\n--budget\nCaps the money this build may spend, in USD. When the cap is reached, the remaining files fail and stay missing or stale. Run the build again later: entries that are already built are not paid for again.\ntheia index build --budget 0.5\nA value that is not a positive number stops the command: theia index build: --budget needs a positive USD amount, got '0'.\n--concurrency\nHow many model calls run in parallel, from 1 to 64. Each call can start a harness process, so high values load your computer.\ntheia index build --concurrency 2\nA wrong value stops the command: theia index build: --concurrency needs an integer 1..64, got '65'.\n--root\nBuilds only the files of one root of a multi-root workspace. Entry names in such a workspace start with the root name, for example web/src/main.ts. A build with --root never deletes entries.\ntheia index build --root web\nIn a single-root repository the only root is called root. Any other name stops the command: theia: unknown root 'foo' (declared: root).\nShared entry cache\nBefore calling a model, theia looks for an entry that another workspace on this computer already built for the same path with the same file content. A hit costs nothing. The cache is on by default ( maintenance.indexCache: true in policy.yaml ).\nThe cache is the index-cache folder in the user cache folder: $XDG_CACHE_HOME/theia when that variable is set; on macOS ~/Library/Caches/theia, or ~/.cache/theia when only that folder exists; on Linux ~/.cache/theia. The environment variable THEIA_INDEX_CACHE_DIR sets another folder.\nExample output when every entry came from the cache:\nindex: 0 written, 0 current, 0 failed, 0 model calls\nrate: 4000.0 files/s, 4 zero-cost (100%; 4 via the cross-workspace cache), 0 model calls, $0.0000 locked (1ms)\nEntries taken from the cache are counted neither in written nor in current. Only the rate line shows them.\nOutput\nWhile it works, the build prints progress to stderr, at most once every 5 seconds:\nindex: 2/2 built · 2 FAILED · $0.0000 · 250.0/s\nA progress line can also show the time left and full ≈ $<amount>: what the whole build will cost at the current rate.\nAt the end it prints a report. Example output of a build where two files failed:\nindex: 0 written, 3 current, 2 failed, 4 model calls\nrate: 625.0 files/s, 3 zero-cost (60%), 4 model calls, $0.0000 locked (8ms) FAIL tests/y.ts: entry reply is not JSON (no JSON value found) (reply 41 bytes); after re-ask: entry reply is not JSON (no JSON value found) (reply 41 bytes)\npruned 1 orphaned entries (files left the tree)\nWord Meaning\nwritten\nEntries a model created in this build.\ncurrent\nFiles that did not change. No call was made.\nfailed, FAIL\nNo entry was made. The reason follows the path.\nmodel calls\nCalls made, second attempts included.\n(N healed)\nReplies whose JSON theia had to repair.\n(N re-asked)\nEntries saved by a second call after an unusable reply.\nzero-cost\nFiles that needed no call: unchanged files and cache hits.\n$… locked\nMoney recorded for the calls of this build.\npruned N orphaned entries\nEntries of files that left git were deleted. Only a full build without paths and without --root does this.\nThe FAIL reason in the example comes from the test transport\nThe reason entry reply is not JSON (no JSON value found) (reply 41 bytes) in the example above is the reply of the test transport fake, on which the examples of this guide were captured: it answers the indexer role with a line of text instead of JSON, so on it theia index build always ends with FAIL and exit code 1. With a real model the reasons are different: a quota, a missing login, an unusable model reply.\nIf 10 calls in a row fail, for example because a quota is used up or a login is missing, theia stops calling the model. The remaining files get this reason:\nskipped: build aborted after 10 consecutive call failures (quota/auth/spawn — fix and re-run; the build is incremental)\nFix the cause and run the build again.\nExit codes\n0 — no file failed.\n1 — at least one file failed, or the command stopped with an error.\nA build without paths and without failures also clears the dirty-marks of the git hook.\ntheia index status\nCompares the index with the files on disk. It makes no model calls and costs nothing.\ntheia index status [--root <name>] [--brief]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--brief\n—\nOff\nPrint one line for a status bar.\n--root\nRoot name\nAll roots\nCheck one root only. Not shown in theia help.\ntheia index status\nExample output:\nentries: 4 (3 current)\ndirty-marks: 2 paths since last build (post-commit hook)\nmissing (2): tests/y.ts src/new.ts\norphaned (1): src/main.go\nrun 'theia index build' to refresh\nWhen nothing needs work, the last line is index is fresh. Each list shows up to 20 paths and then … N more. In a multi-root workspace a line per root is added, for example api: 0 stale, 1 missing, 0 orphaned. When a search generation exists, the first line ends with its age, for example · head 6m old.\nFreshness states State Meaning What to do\ncurrent\nThe entry matches the file.\nNothing.\nstale\nThe file changed after its entry was built. Uncommitted edits count. A theia update that changes how entries are built also makes entries stale.\ntheia index build\nmissing\nA tracked source file has no entry.\ntheia index build\norphaned\nThe entry belongs to a file that git no longer tracks.\nA full theia index build deletes it.\ndirty-marks\nFiles the git hook noted after commits since the last clean build. Only a hint.\ntheia index build\nThe stale state is what theia run and theia plan warn about at start.\nExit codes: 0 when nothing is stale, missing or orphaned; 1 otherwise, and on errors.\n--brief\nPrints one line and always exits with code 0, so a status bar never breaks. theia hooks install claude sets this command as the Claude Code status line if you have none.\ntheia index status --brief\nExample output:\ntheia: 3 indexed · 2 missing · 1 orphaned · 2 marked dirty\nParts that are zero are left out. During a live run the line can end with a run count, for example · 1 live, and when a run waits for an answer, with · 1 question waiting. On any error, including a folder without a store or without git, the line is theia: no index.\n--root\nNot shown in theia help. Checks the files of one root only.\ntheia index status --root web\nOther roots show as orphaned\nWith --root, the entries of all other roots are counted as orphaned, and the exit code is 1 even when the index is fine. Example output for --root web in a workspace with the roots api and web:\nentries: 2 (1 current) api: 0 stale, 0 missing, 1 orphaned web: 0 stale, 0 missing, 0 orphaned\norphaned (1): api/src/main.ts\nrun 'theia index build' to refresh\nstatus deletes nothing. To see the real state, run theia index status without --root.\ntheia index render\nPrints the project blueprint: a legend, then one line per entry, the most important files first. It costs nothing. The session hook theia mem context shows the first 40 lines of the same map.\ntheia index render\nExample output:\nRepo intent map — one line per code unit: path[layer/module/importance]: F:… | R:… | A:… | S:…\nlayer = architectural layer; module = business area; importance = 9 core … 1 peripheral (lines sorted by it, descending).\nF = the file's role. R = related file paths (its dependencies/consumers — follow these to enumerate what a change touches).\nA = exposed API surface. S = design decisions, invariants, gotchas, error/fallback behavior — the field to trust over guessing.\nsrc/calc.ts[domain/billing/8]: F:Calculator helpers for invoices: add() and Invoice.withTax(). | R:src/main.go | A:add(a,b); class Invoice { withTax(rate) } | S:Tax is applied multiplicatively: total*(1+rate). No rounding.\nhello.js[script/demo/1]: F:Prints hello to stdout. | R:- | A:- | S:No logic; smoke file.\nField Meaning\nlayer\nArchitectural layer: app, engine, domain, adapter, schema, config, test, docs, script or other.\nmodule\nBusiness area of the file.\nimportance\nFrom 9 (core) to 1 (peripheral). Only 9, 8, 7, 5, 3 and 1 are used.\nF\nWhat the file is for.\nR\nRelated files: what the file uses and what uses it.\nA\nPublic API. - when there is none.\nS\nDesign decisions, rules and pitfalls.\nWith no entries, only the legend is printed. Exit code: 0.\ntheia index reindex\nBuilds a new search generation from all entries and makes it the one that searches read. Run it after theia index build. It does not call a language model.\ntheia index reindex\nIt prints one line: generation <id>: <N> entries, published=true, and in brackets what it built:\n(embeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2/384) — a word index and meaning vectors from the local model;\n(lexical-only — <reason>) — a word index only, for example when the policy asks for OpenAI embeddings and no OpenAI key is set.\nExample output with OpenAI embeddings and no key:\ngeneration 2akdqd7sqc222u427gl6l7: 1 entries, published=true (lexical-only — gateway.embeddings.provider=openai but no OpenAI key (set OPENAI_API_KEY, `theia setup --openai-key <key>`, or switch the policy to provider: local))\nWith the default local embeddings theia loads the model all-MiniLM-L6-v2. If the model is not on disk, theia downloads it (about 23 MB), unless gateway.embeddings.autoDownload is false. See Embeddings.\nIf the local model cannot be loaded and the index has entries, the command stops with codebase-model: embedding failed during index build (fatal), and nothing is published.\nThe files of generations are kept in.theia/index/codebase/.\nExit codes: 0 when the generation was published, 1 otherwise.\nWarning\nOn an index with no entries, reindex publishes an empty generation with vectors even when the model is missing. After that, theia index search and theia ask stop with theia: query embedding failed. Do not run reindex before the model is available. The way out is described in When search stops with \"query embedding failed\".\ntheia index search\nFinds up to 8 entries for words or a code name. It costs nothing with local embeddings.\ntheia index search <query...>\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<query...>\nWords, quotes are not needed\n—\nWhat to look for. Required.\ntheia index search invoice tax\nExample output:\n1.0164 [entry-scan] src/calc.ts [domain/billing/8] F: Calculator helpers for invoices: add() and Invoice.withTax(). R: src/main.go A: add(a,b); class Invoice { withTax(rate) } S: Tax is applied multiplicatively: total*(1+rate). No rounding.\nEach hit starts with a score, the search lanes that found it in brackets, the path and [layer/module/importance]. The R: and A: lines are left out when empty.\nSearch lanes Lane How it finds entries\nbm25\nWord match in the published search generation.\nvector\nMeaning match in the published generation, when it has vectors from the current embedding model.\nentry-scan\nWord match straight over all entries. Used only while no search generation has been published.\nsymbol\nFiles that define a code symbol named like the whole query or one of its words, for example RetryPolicy.\nThe score is the number of lanes that found the entry plus a small bonus for its rank. 2.0164 means two lanes. Entries found by more lanes come first.\nNo hits: nothing is printed, exit code 0. No query: the line theia index search <query…>, exit code 1.\nAfter the first reindex, entries built later are not found by bm25 and vector until the next reindex.\nThe stderr line theia: vector lane off — <reason> means the search ran without meaning match.\nThe symbol lane reads the file list from git. Outside a git repository the command stops with an error.\nAutomatic index updates\nThe maintenance section of policy.yaml decides whether theia keeps the index fresh on its own:\nindexPolicy What theia does\nalert (default)\nPrints index: N stale — plan quality degrades; run 'theia index build' to refresh at theia run and theia plan start. Spends nothing.\nauto\nBuilds stale entries and publishes a search generation at run and plan start, in theia serve, and when a Claude Code or Codex session starts. At run and plan start it prints index: N stale — plan quality degrades; building (budget $1)…. Each build spends up to indexBuildBudgetUsd (default 1 USD). This costs money.\nmanual\nNever builds. At run and plan start it prints index: N stale — plan quality degrades; indexPolicy=manual. It also skips the free memory maintenance after runs.\nNot in theia help\ntheia index status --root <name> is not shown by the help.\n--root <name> is accepted anywhere in the arguments, not only after the subcommand: theia index --root web status works the same as theia index status --root web.\nThe help says --budget caps only automatic and background builds ( caps auto/background builds ). That is wrong: the cap also applies to a manual theia index build --budget.\nThe help promises bm25 + vector for reindex. With gateway.embeddings.provider: openai and no key, a word-only generation ( lexical-only ) is published.\nSee also\nCode index, memory and questions\ntheia mem\ntheia ask\ntheia hooks\npolicy.yaml settings\nGit","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/mem.html":{"title":"theia mem","content":"theia mem manages the project memory: short facts such as decisions and constraints, and verbatim text chunks. Your Claude Code and Codex sessions and theia ask read it. No mem subcommand calls a language model, except theia mem maintain --llm.\ntheia mem add <kind> <text...>\ntheia mem chunk [--date <date>] <text...>\ntheia mem search <query...>\ntheia mem asof <cut-id> <query...>\ntheia mem supersede <old-fact-id> <new-fact-id>\ntheia mem reindex\ntheia mem maintain [--llm]\ntheia mem context\ntheia mem recall\ntheia mem checkpoint [--final]\nadd, chunk, search and asof also accept --root <name>: see Memory scopes and --root. A wrong or incomplete call prints the usage line and exits with code 1.\nSubcommands at a glance Subcommand What it does Calls a model Shown in theia help\nadd\nSaves a fact\nNo\nYes\nchunk\nSaves verbatim text\nNo\nNo\nsearch\nFinds facts and chunks\nNo\nYes\nasof\nSearches memory as it was at a moment\nNo\nYes\nsupersede\nReplaces one fact with another\nNo\nNo\nreindex\nBuilds the memory search generation\nNo, embeddings only\nNo\nmaintain\nRefreshes summaries and deletes unused search files\nOnly with --llm\nYes, without --llm\ncontext\nPrints the session start text for hooks\nNo\nYes\nrecall\nPrints memory that matches a prompt, for hooks\nNo\nYes\ncheckpoint\nSaves the end of a transcript, for hooks\nNo\nYes, without --final\nFacts, chunks and ids\nA fact is one short statement with a kind, for example a decision. Its id starts with fct.\nA chunk is verbatim text, for example the end of a session transcript. Its id starts with chk. A chunk can carry the date it talks about.\nNothing is overwritten. A fact can be superseded: the old fact leaves search, but theia mem asof still shows it.\nEvery id contains the moment it was created. theia mem asof uses this to show memory as it was at that moment.\nMemory scopes and --root\nEach record belongs to a scope: the whole workspace (the default) or one root of a multi-root workspace. With --root <name> a command writes to and reads from the scope of that root.\nCommand Scope it uses\ntheia mem add, theia mem chunk\nThe workspace scope. With --root <name>, the scope of that root.\ntheia mem search, theia mem asof\nThe workspace scope. In a multi-root workspace also every root scope; hits from a root are tagged, for example {root:web}. With --root <name>, only that root.\ntheia mem context, theia mem recall, theia mem checkpoint, theia ask\nOnly the workspace scope.\nMemory written with --root is invisible to hooks and ask\nFacts saved with --root never reach theia ask, theia mem context or theia mem recall, so your Claude Code and Codex sessions do not see them either. In a single-root repository the only root is called root: --root root is accepted and creates a separate scope that even a plain theia mem search does not show.\nSafe usage:\nIn a single-root repository, do not use --root with theia mem.\nIn a multi-root workspace, save everything that sessions and theia ask should know without --root. Use --root <name> only for notes that you look up yourself with theia mem search.\nAn unknown root name stops the command with theia: unknown root 'foo' (declared: root) and exit code 1.\ntheia help is wrong about recall\ntheia help says that recall unions roots ( recall unions roots ). It does not: theia mem recall reads only the workspace scope. Only theia mem search and theia mem asof union the roots.\nNot in theia help\nThe subcommands chunk (with --date ), supersede, reindex; the flags maintain --llm and checkpoint --final. The help lists only add, search, asof, maintain, context, recall and checkpoint.\n--root <name> is accepted anywhere in the arguments, not only at the end.\ntheia mem add\nSaves a fact. It costs nothing.\ntheia mem add <kind> <text...> [--root <name>]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<kind>\ndecision, constraint, commitment, observation, entity or preference\n—\nKind of the fact. Required.\n<text...>\nText in quotes\n—\nThe fact. Required.\n--root\nRoot name\nWorkspace scope\nSaves the fact to the scope of one root. Read the warning in Memory scopes and --root first.\ntheia mem add decision \"We use PostgreSQL 16 for the billing database\"\nExample output:\nadded fct2akdqbbrrc2235vtf5srr4\nTheia also records the source cli and your system user name with the fact.\nKinds and automatic replacement Kind Typical content\ndecision\nA choice the team made.\nconstraint\nA rule that must always hold.\ncommitment\nSomething promised, with an owner or a date.\nobservation\nSomething noticed about the project.\nentity\nA fact about a component, service or table.\npreference\nA preferred style or tool.\nThe kind is shown in the hook output as [decision], [constraint] and so on. Theia treats all kinds the same, with one rule: when you save a fact whose words almost repeat a current fact of the same kind in the same scope (at least 92% of the distinct words match), the new fact replaces the old one. The output then names the replaced fact:\nadded fct2akdqbbsiw2235yybbevqd (superseded fct2akdqbbrrc2235vtf5srr4)\nA changed value is a new fact: \"PostgreSQL 16\" and \"PostgreSQL 17\" both stay current. Retire the old one yourself with theia mem supersede.\nNever put keys or tokens into facts\ntheia mem add saves the text exactly as typed. It does not mask keys, tokens or card numbers, and no command deletes a fact. See Secrets in memory.\ntheia mem chunk\nNot shown in theia help. Saves verbatim text, for example meeting notes. It costs nothing.\ntheia mem chunk [--date <date>] <text...> [--root <name>]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--date\nA date, see Date formats\nNo date\nThe day or period the text talks about. Must come right after chunk.\n<text...>\nText in quotes\n—\nThe text to save. Required.\n--root\nRoot name\nWorkspace scope\nSaves the chunk to the scope of one root.\ntheia mem chunk -- date 2026-09-02 \"Incident: the payments gateway was down for 2 hours\"\nExample output:\nchunk chk2akdqbhijc223wotx4bwzb\nBefore saving, theia replaces private keys with [PRIVATE KEY], API keys ( sk-…, AKIA…, ghp_…, gho_… ) with [API KEY] and card numbers with [CARD NUMBER]. E-mail addresses are kept as they are.\nDate formats You write Period that is saved\n2026-09-02 or 2026/09/02\nThat day\nSep 3, 2026\nThat day\nAugust 2026\nThat month\n2026\nThat year\nA search that names a date also finds chunks whose period includes it. For example, theia mem search what happened on 2026-09-02 finds the incident chunk above through the lane time.\nWarning\n2026-09 is saved as the whole year 2026, not as September. Write September 2026 instead.\nWords that are not a date stop the command: mem chunk: --date needs an explicit date (e.g. 2026-09-02), got 'yesterday', exit code 1.\n--date after the text is not read as a flag. It silently becomes part of the saved text.\ntheia mem search\nFinds up to 8 facts and chunks. It costs nothing with local embeddings.\ntheia mem search <query...> [--root <name>]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<query...>\nWords, quotes are not needed\n—\nWhat to look for. Required.\n--root\nRoot name\nWorkspace scope, plus every root in a multi-root workspace\nSearches only the scope of one root.\ntheia mem search billing database\nExample output:\n2.0164 [lexical-scan+recency] fct2akdqbbsuo223637hohwfp We use PostgreSQL 17 for the billing database\n2.0161 [lexical-scan+recency] fct2akdqbbsiw2235yybbevqd We use PostgreSQL 16 for the billing database\n1.0159 [recency] fct2akdqbbs562235xexqfulr Never call the payments API from tests\nEach line shows a score, the search lanes that found the record, a scope tag such as {root:web} when the record is not in the workspace scope, the id, and the first 100 characters.\nLane How it finds records\nlexical-scan\nWord match straight over all records. In theia mem search it works only while no memory search generation is published; in theia mem asof it always runs.\nrecency\nThe 10 newest current facts. Chunks are not included.\ntime\nChunks whose date matches a date written in the query.\nbm25\nWord match in the published memory search generation, after theia mem reindex. It replaces lexical-scan in theia mem search.\nvector\nMeaning match in that generation, when it has vectors from the current embedding model.\nsentence\nSentence-level match. Off by default.\nbm25@gen, vector@gen, sentence@gen\nThe same lanes over the generation in theia mem asof: the @gen suffix shows that the hit came from the generation, not from the records directly.\nSuperseded facts are not shown.\nThe recency lane always adds the newest facts, so search returns results for any query while memory is not empty. A hit found only by recency does not match your words.\nAfter theia mem reindex, the lexical-scan lane no longer runs in theia mem search: words are matched only in the generation ( bm25 ). Records added after the reindex are reachable only like this: facts through recency (the 10 newest) and time, chunks only through time. Run theia mem reindex again after you add records.\ntheia mem asof\nSearches memory as it was at a past moment: the facts that were current then, without records added later.\ntheia mem asof <cut-id> <query...> [--root <name>]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<cut-id>\nA theia id, for example a fact id ( fct… ) or a run id ( run… )\n—\nThe moment to look at. Required.\n<query...>\nWords\n—\nWhat to look for. Required.\n--root\nRoot name\nAs in theia mem search\nSearches only the scope of one root.\nExample: the fact fct2akdqbbsiw2235yybbevqd (\"PostgreSQL 16\") was later replaced by \"PostgreSQL 17\". A search as of its id returns the old fact:\ntheia mem asof fct2akdqbbsiw2235yybbevqd PostgreSQL billing\nExample output:\n2.0164 [lexical-scan+recency] fct2akdqbbsiw2235yybbevqd We use PostgreSQL 16 for the billing database\n1.0161 [recency] fct2akdqbbs562235xexqfulr Never call the payments API from tests\nDates are not accepted. theia mem asof 2026-09-01 PostgreSQL stops with terryid: neither a body nor a full id: '2026-09-01' and exit code 1. Take an id from the output of theia mem add, theia mem search, theia ask or a run.\ntheia mem supersede\nNot shown in theia help. Replaces an old fact with a newer one. The old fact leaves search, theia ask and the hooks, but stays in the history that theia mem asof reads.\ntheia mem supersede <old-fact-id> <new-fact-id>\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<old-fact-id>\nId of a current fact, as printed: fct…\n—\nThe fact to retire. Required.\n<new-fact-id>\nId of the fact that replaces it\n—\nThe replacement. Required.\nSave the new fact with theia mem add first, then pass both ids. Example output:\nsuperseded fct2akdqbbsiw2235yybbevqd -> fct2akdqbbsuo223637hohwfp\nTwo error cases, both with exit code 1:\nan id is malformed, for example fctnope, in either argument: terryid: malformed id 'fctnope';\nthe old id is well-formed but the fact is not current, because it was already replaced or no such fact exists: memory: supersede target 2akdqbbsiw2235yybbevqd is not a current fact.\nThe new id is not checked\nTheia does not check that <new-fact-id> exists. With a typo in it, the command still succeeds: the old fact disappears from search and points to a fact that does not exist. Copy both ids from the output of theia mem add or theia mem search, and check the result with theia mem search.\ntheia mem reindex\nNot shown in theia help. Builds and publishes a search generation for memory: a word index and meaning vectors, for the workspace scope and for every root scope that has records. It calls no language model; it computes embeddings.\ntheia mem reindex\nExample output:\nembeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2/384; lexical analyzer: snowball@1; sentence tier: off\ngeneration gen2akdqc7qm2222vt3dpoyqt [default]: 0 docs, published=true (30ms, — docs/s)\nThe first line goes to stderr: the embedding model, the word analyzer and whether sentence search is built. Then one line per scope.\nAfter the first reindex, theia mem search matches words only in the generation ( bm25 ), not over the records directly. Facts added later are found only by the lanes recency (the 10 newest) and time; chunks only by time. Run reindex again after you add records. theia mem asof is not affected: it always scans the records directly and prints the generation lanes as bm25@gen, vector@gen, sentence@gen.\nreindex needs a working embedding model. With OpenAI embeddings and no key it stops with theia: gateway.embeddings.provider=openai but no OpenAI key (…). When the local model cannot be loaded and memory is not empty, it stops with memory: embedding failed during index build (fatal).\nThe files of generations are kept in.theia/index/memory/.\nExit codes: 0 when every generation was published, 1 otherwise.\nWarning\nOn empty memory, reindex publishes an empty generation with vectors even when the model is missing. After that, theia mem search, theia mem asof and theia ask stop with theia: query embedding failed. Run theia doctor first. Reindex only when its embeddings line says ready, or says not fetched with fetches itself on first use and you are online. The way out is described in When search stops with \"query embedding failed\".\ntheia mem maintain\nRefreshes the summaries that theia keeps over groups of memory records and deletes search generations that are no longer used. No mem command prints these summaries. It is free unless you add --llm.\ntheia mem maintain [--llm]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--llm\n—\nOff\nWrites the summaries with a language model instead of a plain text digest. Costs money. Not shown in theia help.\ntheia mem maintain\nExample output:\nmaintain: 2 summaries refreshed (0 empty), 0 orphan generations GCed (0 artifacts removed)\nsummaries refreshed counts summaries brought up to date. orphan generations GCed and artifacts removed count old search generations and their files that were deleted.\nTheia runs the same free maintenance after every run and prints maintenance: … summaries refreshed, … orphan generations GCed, unless maintenance.indexPolicy is manual in policy.yaml.\nA failed reindex can leave an unpublished folder under.theia/index/. maintain does not delete it while no generation of that index has been published.\n--llm\nEach summary becomes one model call with the role extractor, sent through your routing. The default digest needs no model and no key.\ntheia mem maintain --llm\nCommands for session hooks\nClaude Code and Codex run these commands through the hooks that theia hooks install writes. You rarely run them yourself. They never break a session: on any error they print theia mem <subcommand> (fail-open): <message> to stderr and exit with code 0.\nHook event Command\nSessionStart\ntheia mem context\nUserPromptSubmit\ntheia mem recall\nPreCompact\ntheia mem checkpoint\nStop in Claude Code, SessionEnd in Codex\ntheia mem checkpoint --final\ntheia mem context\nPrints the text a new session starts with: the first 40 lines of the project blueprint, the 8 newest current facts of the workspace scope, and the state of the code index.\ntheia mem context\nExample output:\n<theia-context>\nRepo map (head):\nsrc/calc.ts[domain/billing/8]: F:Calculator helpers for invoices: add() and Invoice.withTax(). | R:src/main.go | A:add(a,b); class Invoice { withTax(rate) } | S:Tax is applied multiplicatively: total*(1+rate). No rounding.\nhello.js[script/demo/1]: F:Prints hello to stdout. | R:- | A:- | S:No logic; smoke file.\nRecent project memory:\n- [decision] We use PostgreSQL 17 for the billing database\n- [constraint] Never call the payments API from tests\n</theia-context>\n(index freshness: theia: 4 indexed)\nWith maintenance.indexPolicy: auto and stale index entries, it also starts an index build in the background, capped by indexBuildBudgetUsd, and prints (index: stale — background build started; indexPolicy=auto). That build costs money.\ntheia mem recall\nReads the hook JSON from stdin and prints up to 6 memory records that match the prompt. It prints nothing when the prompt is shorter than 8 characters, when stdin is not valid JSON, or when you run it in a terminal without input. It never downloads a model.\necho '{\"prompt\":\"which database do we use for billing?\"}' | theia mem recall\nExample output:\n<theia-memory>\n- [decision] We use PostgreSQL 17 for the billing database\n- [chunk] Incident: the payments gateway was down for 2 hours\n- [constraint] Never call the payments API from tests\n</theia-memory>\ntheia mem checkpoint\nReads the hook JSON from stdin, takes transcript_path and saves the last 16 KB of that file as a chunk, with keys and card numbers masked. This checkpoint keeps the end of a conversation before the session compacts it. It prints nothing.\necho '{\"transcript_path\":\"<file>\"}' | theia mem checkpoint [--final]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--final\n—\nOff\nMarks the chunk as the end of a session ( session-final instead of compact-checkpoint ). Not shown in theia help.\nSecrets in memory\nNever put keys, tokens or passwords into facts\ntheia mem add saves the text as typed. A key in a fact is stored and printed as is. Chunks mask keys and card numbers; facts do not. E-mail addresses are masked in neither.\nWhile such a fact is current, theia plan and theia ask stop with gateway fatal: gateway: prohibited content (api_key) — call blocked (THE-014) and exit code 1, whatever you ask. For ask the message starts with ask:.\nNo command deletes a fact.\nIf a key got into a fact:\nFind the fact id with theia mem search and a word from that fact.\nSave a clean fact without the key and note its id:\ntheia mem add observation \"The staging deploy token lives in the team vault, never in memory\"\nReplace the bad fact:\ntheia mem supersede <bad-fact-id> <clean-fact-id>\nCheck that theia ask answers again.\nThe key leaves search, plans and theia ask, but it stays in the store: theia mem asof with an earlier id still shows it. Treat the key as leaked and revoke it.\nExit codes\nadd, chunk, supersede, search, asof, reindex, maintain: 0 on success; 1 on a usage error, an unknown root, a wrong id, no store, or a failed reindex.\ncontext, recall, checkpoint: always 0.\nSee also\nCode index, memory and questions\ntheia ask\ntheia index\ntheia hooks\nStore and project roots\nClaude Code","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/ask.html":{"title":"theia ask","content":"theia ask answers one question about your project from what theia has stored: memory, the code index and recent runs. It makes at most one model call and lists the records it used.\nWarning\ntheia ask works only in a git repository. In a folder that is not a git repository it stops with fatal: not a git repository and exit code 1.\ntheia ask \"<question>\" [--budget <usd>]\n<command> | theia ask [--budget <usd>]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<question>\nText in quotes\nRead from stdin\nThe question. When you give both, the argument wins and stdin is ignored.\n--budget\nPositive number, USD\n1\nThe most this question may spend. 0 and a negative number: the call is refused; a non-number or the flag without a value: no cap. See Cost and --budget. Not shown in theia help.\nWithout a question, with no argument and nothing on stdin, theia prints theia ask \"question…\" [--budget usd] (or: echo \"q\" | theia ask) and exits with code 1.\nExamples\ntheia ask \"which database do we use for billing?\"\nThe question can come from another command through stdin:\necho \"which database does billing use?\" | theia ask\nWith a spending cap of 2 USD:\ntheia ask --budget 2 \"how is tax applied to invoices?\"\nWhere the answer comes from\nBefore calling a model, theia collects records:\nSource How many Notes\nMemory\nUp to 6 facts and chunks\nWorkspace scope only. Records saved with --root are not read.\nCode index\nUp to 6 index entries\nThe same search as theia index search.\nRuns\nThe last 3 runs\nResult and up to 4 verdicts of each.\nEach record is cut to 600 characters. The model is told to answer only from these records, to cite them, and to say which part of the question they do not cover.\n@path is not expanded in theia ask. The question is sent exactly as typed, and no file is attached. File inclusion works in theia run, theia plan, theia msg and theia probe.\nOutput\nTheia prints the answer text, an empty line, the sources and the cost. Example of the last two lines, captured on the test transport fake, where calls cost nothing. On it the answer text is the stub fake-green: no verdict for role 'none', so only the sources and cost lines are shown here:\n[sources] fct:2akdqbbsuo223637hohwfp fct:2akdqbbs562235xexqfulr src/calc.ts hello.js\n[ask run2akdqct57o222gejfseu5t — $0.0000]\nSource form Meaning\nfct:<id>\nA memory fact.\nchk:<id>\nA memory chunk.\nA file path\nAn index entry.\nrun:<id>\nA run.\nIn [sources] the ids have a colon after the kind ( fct:2akd… ), while theia mem search prints the same ids without it ( fct2akd… ). The last line shows the id of this question and what it cost.\nWhen no model is called\nIf memory, the code index and run history all return nothing, theia answers without a model, for $0:\nNo records cover this — memory, index, and run history are all empty for the question.\n[ask run2akdqbou4w223tksfh5bqz — $0.0000; no model call]\nThis happens only in an almost empty store. Memory search always adds the newest facts, and the last runs are always included. As soon as the store has one fact or one run, every question goes to the model.\nAsking exactly the same question again, while the same records are found, is served from the record of the first call and costs nothing.\nCost and --budget\nOne question is one model call through your routing, paid through the harness it names. The call carries no role ( none ), so the route comes from the \"*\" key of gateway.routing. --budget caps what the call may spend; the default is 1 USD.\nEdge values of --budget\n--budget 0 and a negative number do not mean \"no limit\". The call is refused with the message below and exit code 1; the model is not asked and no money is spent:\nask: gateway fatal: gateway: usd budget exhausted for this run (THE-024)\nThe refusal happens only where a call is really needed. Two exceptions pass with exit code 0 even with --budget 0: an empty store (the answer No records cover this …, no call) and the same question asked again over the same records (served from the record of the first call before the cap is checked).\nA non-number ( --budget abc ) and the flag without a value mean \"no cap\". Theia does not warn about it. Pass a positive number.\nLimits\nIt needs a git repository, see the note at the top of this page.\nA key or token in a current memory fact blocks the call, with exit code 1: ask: gateway fatal: gateway: prohibited content (api_key) — call blocked (THE-014). The fix is in Secrets in memory.\nIf a search generation with vectors exists but the local embedding model cannot be loaded, ask stops with theia: query embedding failed. The way out is in When search stops with \"query embedding failed\".\nMemory saved with --root is not read.\nOnce a search generation exists, index entries built later are not found until theia index reindex.\nChange the answer style\nTheia adds your own instructions to the built-in prompt of ask: the text of the file.theia/prompts/ASK.md, or the section ### ask of.theia/prompts/PROMPTS.md. See Rules and prompts.\nNot in theia help\n--budget <usd>: the help shows only ask \"q…\" and the question through stdin.\nExit codes\n0 — theia answered, or reported that no records cover the question.\n1 — no question was given, the call was refused (budget or secrets), no store was found, or the command stopped with an error.\nSee also\nCode index, memory and questions\ntheia mem\ntheia index\nmsg, answer, probe","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/hooks.html":{"title":"theia hooks","content":"theia hooks install connects Theia to Claude Code, Codex or git. This page gives the syntax, what each variant writes, the messages and the exit codes. Step-by-step guides: Claude Code, Codex, Git.\ntheia hooks\ntheia hooks install claude|codex|git [--trust]\nArgument Value Default What it does\ninstall\n—\n—\nThe only action.\nclaude\n—\n—\nHooks, status line, MCP server and the /theia skill for Claude Code. See theia hooks install claude.\ncodex\n—\n—\nHooks and the MCP server for Codex. See theia hooks install codex.\ngit\n—\n—\nThe post-commit hook. See theia hooks install git.\n--trust\n—\noff\nOnly with codex. Records trust for the project in the Codex settings. Not shown in theia help.\nRules for all variants:\nRun the command in the folder that holds.theia/. It does not search parent folders.\nExisting content stays: your hooks, your status line, other MCP servers, your lines in the git hook.\nA second run adds nothing when the entries are already there. A hook counts as present only when its command text is exactly the same.\nHook and server commands contain the full path of the Theia version that ran the install, for example /Users/you/.local/share/theia/releases/0.2.201/theia. This is also true when you start theia through ~/.local/bin/theia. See After a Theia update.\nThe command makes no model calls.\ntheia help describes hooks install claude only as memory hooks. The command also writes the status line,.mcp.json and the skill.\nExtra arguments are skipped without a message. --trust works only with codex: theia hooks install claude --trust and theia hooks install git --trust are normal installs.\nThere is no status and no uninstall action: install is the only one. Any other word, and a call without arguments, prints this usage and exits with code 1:\ntheia hooks install claude wire SessionStart/recall/checkpoint hooks into.claude/settings.json\ntheia hooks install codex wire project.codex/hooks.json + [mcp_servers.theia] (two trust gates, printed)\ntheia hooks install git wire the post-commit dirty-marks hook (freshness hint, zero model calls)\nExit codes:\nCode When\n0\nThe install finished, or everything was already in place.\n1\nA usage error; no.theia/ in the current folder; a settings file is broken; for git, also no.git or a linked git worktree.\ntheia hooks install claude\ntheia hooks install claude\nNo arguments.\nFile What is written\n.claude/settings.json\nHooks SessionStart → theia mem context, UserPromptSubmit → theia mem recall, PreCompact → theia mem checkpoint, Stop → theia mem checkpoint --final, each with \"timeout\": 30. statusLine → theia index status --brief, only if the file has no statusLine yet.\n.mcp.json\nServer theia with the arguments serve --home <project folder>: all 22 tools. Only if there is no theia entry yet.\n.claude/skills/theia/SKILL.md\nThe /theia skill. Only if the file does not exist.\nMessages:\nMessage Meaning\ninstalled hooks: SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, PreCompact, Stop → <file>\nHooks added.\nhooks already installed (<file> unchanged)\nAll four hooks were already there.\nstatusline wired: theia index status --brief\nStatus line added.\nstatusline: existing statusLine preserved (not overwritten)\nYour status line was kept.\nmcp server registered: theia serve → <file> (control tools included)\nServer entry added.\nmcp server: existing theia entry preserved\nA theia entry was already there.\n/theia skill installed → <file>\nSkill written.\n/theia skill: existing file preserved\nThe skill file was already there.\ntheia hooks: <file> is not valid JSON — fix it first (never clobbered)\nExit code 1. The broken file is not changed.\ntheia hooks: no.theia/ in <folder> — run 'theia init' first\nExit code 1.\nWarning\nThe files are written in this order:.claude/settings.json,.mcp.json, the skill. If.mcp.json is broken,.claude/settings.json is already changed when the command stops with exit code 1, and the skill is not installed.\nDetails and undo steps: Claude Code.\ntheia hooks install codex\ntheia hooks install codex [--trust]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--trust\n—\noff\nAdds trust_level = \"trusted\" for this project to the Codex settings. Not shown in theia help.\n--trust\nWithout the flag, Theia only prints what Codex needs for project trust. With it, Theia also appends this block to config.toml in the Codex settings folder: the folder from CODEX_HOME, or ~/.codex by default.\n# theia hooks install codex --trust [projects.\"/Users/you/projects/app\"] trust_level = \"trusted\"\nMessage: --trust: appended project trust → <file>. If the project is already trusted: --trust: already trusted (<file> unchanged). This is the only write outside the project folder.\nFiles:\nFile What is written\n.codex/hooks.json\nHooks SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit and PreCompact with the same commands as for Claude Code, and SessionEnd → theia mem checkpoint --final.\n.codex/config.toml\nThe [mcp_servers.theia] block: arguments serve --home <project folder> --tools <tools> --source-ref session:codex and default_tools_approval_mode = \"auto\". The tools are the policy value run.mcpBelt.tools plus memory_add. The block goes to the end of the file and is not added twice.\nMessages:\nMessage Meaning\ninstalled hooks: SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, PreCompact, SessionEnd → <file>\nHooks added.\ncreated [mcp_servers.theia] → <file>\nA new config.toml with the server block.\nappended [mcp_servers.theia] → <file>\nThe server block added to your existing config.toml.\nmcp server already configured (<file> unchanged)\nThe block was already there.\ngate 1 (project trust): …, gate 2 (hook trust): …, note: …\nThe trust report, printed on every run. See Codex.\ntheia hooks: <file> is not parseable TOML — fix it first (never clobbered)\nExit code 1. The file is not changed.\ntheia hooks: <file> is not valid JSON — fix it first (never clobbered)\nExit code 1.\nEnvironment variable: CODEX_HOME — the folder with Codex settings, ~/.codex by default.\ntheia hooks install git\ntheia hooks install git\nNo arguments. Needs.git and.theia/ in the current folder.\nFile What is written\n.git/hooks/post-commit\nA new file with #!/bin/sh and one line that appends the changed paths to.theia/index/dirty-paths, or that line added to the end of your existing hook. The file gets permission to run.\nMessages:\nMessage Meaning\ninstalled post-commit dirty-marks hook → <file>\nHook written.\npost-commit hook already installed (<file> unchanged)\nTheia's line is already in the file.\ntheia hooks: no.git in <folder> — the post-commit hook needs a git repo\nExit code 1.\nENOTDIR: not a directory, mkdir '<folder>/.git/hooks'\nExit code 1: the folder is a linked git worktree.\nWarning\nWith core.hooksPath set in git, the install reports success, but git never runs the hook. See Git.\nSee also\nClaude Code\nCodex\nGit\ntheia mem context\nAll commands","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/serve.html":{"title":"theia serve","content":"theia serve starts Theia's MCP server on standard input and output. MCP clients such as Claude Code and Codex start it themselves. The tools and client setup are on the MCP server page.\ntheia serve\ntheia serve [--home <path>] [--tools <names>] [--no-control] [--metrics <port>]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--home\npath of the folder that holds.theia/\nsearch from the current folder upward\nWhich project store to serve. Not shown in theia help.\n--tools\ntool names separated by commas\nall tools\nOffer only these tools. Run and plan tools are then off. Not shown in theia help.\n--no-control\n—\noff\nTurn off the 13 run and plan tools; 9 tools remain. With any --tools, they are off even without this flag. Not shown in theia help.\n--metrics\nport from 1 to 65535\noff\nA Prometheus page /metrics on this port.\ntheia help shows only serve [--metrics <port>].\nExamples of arguments for an MCP client. Each flag was checked on its own:\ntheia serve --home /Users/you/projects/app\ntheia serve --home /Users/you/projects/app --no-control\ntheia serve --home /Users/you/projects/app --tools codebase_blueprint,memory_search\ntheia serve --home /Users/you/projects/app --metrics 9464\n--home\nThe full path of the folder that holds.theia/. The installers always pass it, so the server finds the store wherever the client starts it. Without --home, the server looks for.theia/ from the current folder upward. If there is none, it stops with exit code 1:\ntheia: no.theia/ found from ~/projects upward (and no user-registry workspace) — run 'theia init' in the workspace home first\n--tools\nA list of names separated by commas, without spaces. Allowed names: codebase_blueprint, codebase_search, codebase_symbol, memory_search, memory_add, memory_tree, run_audit, run_diagnose, run_check.\nWith --tools, the run and plan tools are never offered, and the server's instructions to the client leave out the part about run control. Any other name, including a run tool name, stops the server with exit code 1:\nmcp-server: unknown tool 'run_list' in filter (known: codebase_blueprint, codebase_search, codebase_symbol, memory_search, memory_add, memory_tree, run_audit, run_diagnose, run_check)\n--no-control\nTurns off run_list, run_status, run_events, run_wait, run_msg, run_answer, run_stop, run_start, run_probe, plan_list, plan_show, plan_create and plan_approve. The code map, memory, audit and diagnosis tools stay. Use it for a client that should only read.\n--metrics\nStarts an HTTP server next to the MCP server. GET /metrics returns Prometheus text; any other path returns 404. The server prints theia serve: /metrics on:9464 to stderr.\nAlert\nThe page listens on all network interfaces, not only on 127.0.0.1, and there is no option to change that. Use it only on a network you trust, or block the port in your firewall. If the port is busy, the server crashes at start with exit code 1.\nA wrong value stops the server with exit code 1:\ntheia serve: --metrics needs a port 1..65535, got 'abc'\nWhat the page contains: Metrics and tracing.\nWhile the server runs\nstderr shows theia serve: MCP server on stdio (root <folder>). stdout is reserved for MCP.\nThe server checks whether the code map is up to date: once right at start, then every 300 seconds ( maintenance.serveRefreshSeconds ). The check makes no model calls.\nWith maintenance.indexPolicy: auto, it rebuilds stale index entries within maintenance.indexBuildBudgetUsd, and this calls models. stderr then shows lines like theia serve: reconciled index (0 written, 1 stale remain).\nWith any maintenance.indexPolicy other than manual, after the check the server also maintains project memory: it builds short summaries of entries without model calls.\nThe server stops when the client closes the connection.\nHow to remove the server from a client: Undo.\nExit codes Code When\n0\nThe client closed the connection.\n1\nA wrong --metrics value; an unknown name in --tools; no store found; Theia's shipped files are missing; the --metrics port is busy (the crash prints a Node.js stack trace).\nFor platform developers\nTheia's own agents inside runs use these flags. They are not shown in theia help, and users do not need them.\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--source-ref\ntext\nmcp\nLabel saved with facts written by memory_add. The Codex install passes session:codex.\n--check-cwd\npath\n—\nFolder where run_check runs its commands.\n--check-cmd\nshell command, can be repeated\n—\nCommands that run_check may run. Without --check-cwd and --check-cmd, run_check replies that it is not configured.\nSee also\nMCP server\nMetrics and tracing\ntheia hooks\nAll commands","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/jail.html":{"title":"theia jail","content":"Reference for theia jail: run one command in a sandbox.\ntheia jail\nRuns a command so that the whole system is read-only and writing is allowed only in the folders you name. The network stays available. The command works on Linux with bubblewrap. On macOS it always refuses.\ntheia jail [--rw=<path>]... [--mem <size>] [--cpus <percent>] [--pids <number>] [--] <command> [args]\ntheia jail --backend container --image <image> [--rw=<path>]... [--] <command> [args]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--rw\na path: --rw=<path> or --rw <path>\n—\nAllows writing in this folder. Can be given several times. The path must exist\n--mem\na size, for example 3G\n—\nMemory limit. Not shown in theia help\n--cpus\npercent of one CPU, for example 200\n—\nCPU limit: 200 means two CPUs. Not shown in theia help\n--pids\na number, for example 512\n—\nLimit on the number of processes. Not shown in theia help\n--backend\nauto or container\nauto\nIsolation method: auto is bubblewrap on Linux, container is Docker or Podman. Not shown in theia help\n--image\nan image name\n—\nThe image for --backend container. Not shown in theia help\n--\n—\n—\nEnd of flags. Optional: the first word that does not start with -- starts the command\n<command>\nthe command and its arguments\n—\nWhat to run. Required\nThe exit code is the exit code of the command you ran. When the sandbox is unavailable or the arguments are wrong, the exit code is 1.\n--rw\nAllows writing in a folder. The rest of the system stays read-only. If the folder does not exist, the command fails with jail: --rw path does not exist: <path>. The path is checked only after the sandbox itself: on macOS with --rw=/nonexistent you see the no sandbox backend on darwin refusal instead (see below).\n--mem, --cpus, --pids\nResource limits for the command. They need Linux with systemd-run --user and cgroup delegation. Without that the command does not start and prints a message like:\njail: caps requested but unenforceable — <reason>\n--backend container\nRuns the command in a Docker or Podman container. If both exist, Podman is chosen. The image must already be pulled: Theia does not download images. This mode is not verified in this guide: do not rely on it.\nWithout --image the command refuses:\ntheia jail --backend container -- true\nExample output:\njail: unavailable — run.jail.backend container needs run.jail.image (engine docker is ready)\nengine docker is ready only means that docker --version worked. This check does not look at whether Docker is running; the jail (container) line of theia doctor judges the same way. Theia tries to start a container only when an image is given. With --image and Docker not running, the command refuses with exit code 1:\ntheia jail --backend container --image alpine:nonexistent-tag -- true\nExample output:\njail: unavailable — failed to connect to the docker API at unix:///var/run/docker.sock; check if the path is correct and if the daemon is running: dial unix /var/run/docker.sock: connect: no such file or directory\nStart Docker and repeat the command. An image that is not on disk also leads to a refusal: Theia starts the engine with downloads disabled and suggests the pull command.\nLinux\nYou need bwrap (bubblewrap) on your PATH and working user namespaces.\nBefore running, Theia checks the sandbox with an empty command inside bwrap. If the check fails, it prints jail: unavailable — <reason> and exits with code 1.\nInside the sandbox: the system is read-only, /dev and /proc are fresh, writing goes to the --rw folders, and the network is shared.\nThe command ends together with theia jail.\nmacOS\nmacOS has no regular sandbox. Every call without --backend container ends like this, also with --rw, with --mem and without --:\ntheia jail -- echo hi\nExample output:\njail: unavailable — no sandbox backend on darwin yet (the container backend is the queued arm — docs/13); use standard or trusted-full\nThe exit code is 1. What --backend container prints on macOS without --image and with Docker not running: --backend container. The jailed-full sandbox tier is not available on macOS either: see Sandbox.\nWindows\nVersion 0.2.201 has no Theia files for Windows: see Platforms and run modes.\nUsage errors\nWith no command, with --rw without a value, or with a wrong --backend, Theia prints a hint and exits with code 1:\ntheia jail [--rw=PATH]... [--mem 3G] [--cpus 200] [--pids 512] [--backend container --image IMG] [--] <cmd> [args...] host read-only; RW only where granted; caps: MemoryMax / CPUQuota% / TasksMax per tree\nSee also\nSandbox\ntheia doctor\nPlatforms and run modes","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/audit-prune.html":{"title":"theia audit, prune","content":"theia audit verify checks that stored logs were not changed. theia prune removes old finished runs to free disk space. Neither command calls a model.\ntheia audit\ntheia audit has one subcommand, verify. Without it, or with another word, theia prints theia audit verify [scope] and exits with code 1.\ntheia audit verify\nChecks the audit chain of stored logs. Every event carries a fingerprint of the previous one, so a changed or removed event breaks the chain.\ntheia audit verify [<scope>]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<scope>\nlog id\nall logs\nCheck only this log. A run id works with or without the run prefix. Extra arguments are ignored.\nWithout an argument theia checks every log in the store: each run, each plan, each probe ( theia probe ), and the service logs plan (planner calls) and probe (the index used by a probe). It prints one line per log, without the run prefix. Lines of runs, plans and probes are mixed.\nExample output:\n2akdqck6vw2234dhcxwrue: intact\n2akdqckarw223cdvn75mka: intact\n2akdqckj62223mjar2dw7f: intact\nintact means the log is complete and unchanged.\nBROKEN at <event id> means the log was changed at this event.\nThe exit code is 0 when every checked log is intact, and 1 when any log is broken.\nWarning\nAn id that does not exist is also reported as intact: nosuchscope: intact, exit code 0. Check the spelling of the id.\ntheia prune\nRemoves finished runs: their folders under.theia/runs/, their records in the store, and stored outputs that nothing refers to any more. Theia looks for references in every remaining event and store record, plans and recorded model replies included.\ntheia prune --older-than <age> [--keep <N>] [--dry-run] [--yes]\ntheia prune --keep <N> [--dry-run] [--yes]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--older-than\nnumber plus d, h or m, for example 7d, 48h, 90m\n—\nSelect finished runs that started longer ago than this. See --older-than.\n--keep\nwhole number, 0 or more\n—\nKeep the newest N finished runs and select the rest. See --keep.\n--dry-run\n—\noff\nShow what would be removed and delete nothing. See --dry-run.\n--yes\n—\noff\nDelete without asking. Needed without a terminal. Not shown in theia help. See --yes.\nGive --older-than, --keep, or both. With both, a run is removed only if it matches both. Without either, theia prints its usage text and exits with code 1:\ntheia prune (--older-than 7d | --keep N) [--dry-run] [--yes] removes FINISHED runs: their kept workspaces, event scopes, and blobs nothing left references. Unfinished/paused runs never prune. --older-than human age: 7d, 48h, 90m --keep keep the newest N finished runs --dry-run price the pass, delete nothing --yes skip the confirmation (required when not a TTY)\nA run counts as finished when its log has both run.created and run.finished: a run that ended normally, a run closed with theia run --abandon, and a fork that was driven to the end or was made without --at from a finished run (then run.finished was copied). Unfinished runs, paused runs and forks made with --at that were never continued are never removed.\nExample output with --dry-run:\nprune: 3 finished runs selected (0 unfinished kept): run2akdqckj62223mjar2dw7f 0d old no workspace run2akdqckarw223cdvn75mka 0d old 0.0 MB run2akdqck6vw2234dhcxwrue 0d old 0.0 MB workspaces: 0.0 MB · event scopes: 3 · orphaned blobs swept after untouched: unfinished runs, gateway recordings (request-keyed, shared), ai/* audit branches (your repo)\ndry-run: nothing deleted\nExample output after deletion:\npruned: 3 runs, 0.0 MB of workspaces, 14 orphaned blobs (0.0 MB)\nWhen nothing matches:\nprune: nothing to remove (4 runs known, 0 unfinished — unfinished never prune)\nAfter deletion theia show and theia replay for these runs print no events.\nExit codes:\nCode When\n0\nRuns were removed, --dry-run finished, or nothing matched.\n1\nWrong or missing arguments, or you did not confirm.\n--older-than\nThe age of a run is counted from its start. The value is a number with d (days), h (hours) or m (minutes). Decimals work, for example 1.5d. The list shows the age in whole days, rounded: a run that is 90 minutes old shows as 0d old. A wrong value stops the command: prune: --older-than needs 7d / 48h / 90m, got '7x', exit code 1.\n--keep\nKeeps the newest N finished runs and selects all older finished runs. --keep 0 selects every finished run. A wrong value stops the command: prune: --keep needs a non-negative integer, got '-1', exit code 1.\n--dry-run\nPrints the list, the size of the copies and what stays untouched, then dry-run: nothing deleted. Nothing is removed. Run it first.\n--yes\nOn a terminal theia prints the list and asks delete N runs + X MB? [y/N] before it deletes anything. Only y or yes confirms, in any case. --yes skips the question. Without a terminal theia cannot ask. Without --yes it prints the list and prune: aborted (nothing deleted), exit code 1:\nprune: 1 finished runs selected (0 unfinished kept): run2akec4zct2222capt6cy53 0d old 0.0 MB workspaces: 0.0 MB · event scopes: 1 · orphaned blobs swept after untouched: unfinished runs, gateway recordings (request-keyed, shared), ai/* audit branches (your repo)\nprune: aborted (nothing deleted)\nWhat prune leaves behind\nUnfinished and paused runs. This includes the unfinished record that a refused theia run --from-plan leaves, and a fork made with --at that was never continued. To remove such a record, close the run with theia run --abandon <runId> first, see A refused plan run leaves a branch.\nSaved model replies. They are shared between runs.\nGit branches ai/* and theia/run-* in your repository.\nGit worktree entries of removed runs. The folder.theia/runs/<run folder>/trunk is deleted with the run, so git worktree list marks these entries prunable. Remove them with git:\ngit worktree prune\nRecords of theia probe: their log has no run.created event, so prune does not treat them as runs.\nSee also\nRun results\nYour first task: clean up\ntheia run","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/reference/eval.html":{"title":"theia eval","content":"theia eval holds benchmarks. For users, only theia eval index-bench is useful: it shows for free how the code index avoids paying twice for the same file. The other subcommands are tools for the developers of the platform.\ntheia eval index-bench [<N>]\ntheia eval calibrate\ntheia eval swebl <verb> [options]\ntheia eval memory <verb> [options]\ntheia eval demo create --dir <path> [options]\nWith no subcommand or an unknown one, theia prints theia eval index-bench [files=48] | calibrate | swebl <fetch|draw|prep|run|ladder|grade|report> | demo create | memory <fetch|draw|retrieval|report> and exits with code 1. That line omits memory qa, which exists.\ntheia eval index-bench\nBuilds an index of generated files three times and shows how many model calls each build needed. It runs the real index builder with a simulated model: no real model, no network, no money. It works in any folder, even without a store.\ntheia eval index-bench [<N>]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n<N>\nInteger 1–10000\n48\nNumber of generated files.\ntheia eval index-bench 8\nExample output:\nIndex build bench — 8 files (production IntentIndex, scripted gateway)\n| phase | model calls | wall ms | files/s | reused | usd locked |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| cold build | 8 | 42 | 190.5 | 0% | $0.0168 |\n| unchanged rebuild | 0 | 0 | — | 100% | $0.0000 |\n| one-file touch | 1 | 1 | 8000.0 | 88% | $0.0021 |\nWhen a phase took 0 ms, the files/s column prints —.\nPhase What it shows\ncold build\nA new index: one model call per file.\nunchanged rebuild\nNothing changed: no calls.\none-file touch\nOne file changed: one call.\nreused is the share of files that needed no call. The usd locked column uses a fixed made-up price of $0.0021 per call; it is not a real cost. A wrong <N> stops the command with theia eval: files must be an integer 1..10000, got '0' and exit code 1.\nFor platform developers\nWarning\nThe subcommands below are tools for developing Theia itself:\nthey download data from Hugging Face and GitHub;\nthey call real models through codex and spend money;\nthey look for task rows and manifests in the install folder ~/.local/share/theia ( refs/swe-bench-live-tasks/, docs/eval/ ), where they do not exist, and write results to developer paths such as ~/work/scratchpad/tmp/theia-swebl;\nparts of them assume Linux.\nDo not expect them to work out of the box in 0.2.201. This section only lists their syntax, taken from the program's argument parsing.\ntheia eval calibrate\nReads the model calls and verdicts recorded in the store and computes per-role uncertainty thresholds. It needs a store and makes no model calls. The claude and codex harnesses do not record the data it needs, so it prints:\nno logprob-bearing transport recorded — mechanism armed, awaiting data\nWithout a store it stops with theia: no.theia/ found from <cwd> upward (and no user-registry workspace) — run 'theia init' in the workspace home first and exit code 1.\ntheia eval swebl\nRuns the SWE-bench-Live benchmark ladder.\ntheia eval swebl fetch --split <split> [--ids <id>,<id>]\ntheia eval swebl draw --split <split> --n <k> [--seed <int>]\ntheia eval swebl prep <instance> [--posture raw|theia] [--capability trusted|standard] [--index-budget <usd>] [--regate]\ntheia eval swebl run <instance> --arm S0|S0.5|S1 [--pass <k>] [--budget <usd>] [--wall-min <minutes>] [--split <split>] [--tui]\ntheia eval swebl ladder --split <split> --n <k> [--workers <a>/<b>]\ntheia eval swebl grade <instance> [--gold|--empty|--arm <arm> [--pass <k>]|--patch <file>] [--split <split>]\ntheia eval swebl report [--split <split>] [--md <path>]\nfetch downloads task rows through the Hugging Face datasets API. prep clones the project from GitHub and runs its test suite. prep with the theia posture and run call codex and spend money.\nDefaults: draw --n 3 --seed 20260905; prep --posture theia --capability trusted --index-budget 1; run --budget 1 (2 for S1 ), --wall-min 15, --split cs; ladder --workers 3/1.\ngrade with --arm or --patch first requires passing --gold and --empty self-tests.\nResults are written under ~/work/scratchpad/tmp/theia-swebl. Task rows are looked up in ~/.local/share/theia/refs/swe-bench-live-tasks/<id>.json, manifests in ~/.local/share/theia/docs/eval/. An installed release does not have these files.\nVerified stops: fetch without --split → swebl fetch: --split <cs|ts|js|…> required; draw before fetch → swebl draw: no fetched ids for split 'cs' — run fetch first; report with no attempts → swebl report: no attempts recorded for split 'cs'; prep without a task row → ENOENT: no such file or directory, open '/Users/…/.local/share/theia/refs/swe-bench-live-tasks/<id>.json'. Exit code 1 in every case.\ntheia eval memory\nRuns the memory benchmark on public datasets.\ntheia eval memory fetch [--datasets <dir>]\ntheia eval memory draw --set small|medium|large [--seed <N>] [--out <path>]\ntheia eval memory retrieval --set small|medium|large [--arms <arm>,<arm>] [--only <dataset>,<dataset>] [--k <N>] [--out <dir>] [--cache <dir>] [--manifest <path>]\ntheia eval memory qa --set small|medium|large [--arms <arm>,<arm>] [--only <dataset>,<dataset>] [--k <N>] [--concurrency <N>] [--reasoning <level>] [--out <dir>] [--cache <dir>] [--manifest <path>]\ntheia eval memory report <rows.json> [--arms <arm>,<arm>]\nfetch downloads LongMemEval from Hugging Face and LoCoMo from GitHub into the folder THEIA_DATASETS_DIR, or datasets in the user cache folder.\ndraw needs every dataset fetched. Without them the command exits with code 1 and this message:\nmemory bench: LoCoMo not fetched (<cache>/theia/datasets/locomo10.json) — run `theia eval memory fetch`\nretrieval and qa read the manifest docs/eval/memory-<set>.json from the current folder. Without it the command exits with code 1 and this message:\nmemory bench: no draw manifest at <cwd>/docs/eval/memory-small.json — run `theia eval memory draw --set small`\nretrieval needs a working embedding model. qa also calls a model for every question. The --cache <dir> flag of qa sets the cache folder instead of memory-bench-cache next to the datasets.\nDefaults: draw --seed 20260902; retrieval --k 20; qa --arms hybrid,hybrid+stem --k 10 --concurrency 3.\nreport without a rows file prints the usage lines and exits with code 1.\ntheia eval demo create\nCreates the demo workspace used in Theia presentations: a C# project with a known task.\ntheia eval demo create --dir <path> [--index-budget <usd>] [--skip-index] [--force]\nArgument Value Default What it does\n--dir\nFolder path\n—\nWhere to create the workspace. Required. The folder must be empty unless --force is given.\n--index-budget\nPositive number, USD\n0.5\nSpending cap of the index build.\n--skip-index\n—\nOff\nDoes not build the index.\n--force\n—\nOff\nDeletes the folder contents and starts again.\nWhat it does: clones dotnet-state-machine/stateless from GitHub, runs its test suite with dotnet, runs theia init, writes a demo policy that routes all roles to codex, saves one fact and one chunk, then builds the index with codex within the index budget.\nIt also needs the task row ~/.local/share/theia/refs/swe-bench-live-tasks/dotnet-state-machine__stateless-644.json. An installed release does not have it, so the command stops while reading that file, before cloning, with ENOENT: no such file or directory, open '…'. This was not run: with the file in place the command would go on to clone from GitHub.\nThese checks stop the command before any work: demo create: --dir <path> required; demo create: --index-budget must be a positive number, got '0'; demo create: <dir> exists and is not empty — refusing (pass --force to recreate).\nNot in theia help\neval memory qa is in theia help but missing from the usage line of theia eval.\neval memory retrieval --manifest, eval memory qa --manifest and eval memory qa --cache are not shown even in the usage lines of theia eval memory itself.\nThe <N> limit of index-bench (1–10000) is named nowhere in the help.\nExit codes\nindex-bench: 0; 1 for a wrong <N>.\ncalibrate: 0, also when there is no data; 1 without a store.\nswebl, memory, demo: without the needed subcommand or argument, the usage lines and 1; 1 on any of the stops listed above.\ntheia eval without a subcommand or with an unknown one: 1.\nSee also\ntheia index\nCode index, memory and questions","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/troubleshooting.html":{"title":"Troubleshooting","content":"Common problems: what you see, why it happens and what to do. Search this page for the error text (Cmd+F or Ctrl+F). Known limitations of this version are listed in Known limitations.\nCheck these first\nThe version: you should see 0.2.201.\nYour setup: run theia doctor from the project root.\ntheia version\ntheia doctor\nIf a run went wrong, look at its log and its likely cause: theia show <runId> --plain and theia diagnose <runId>. How to find a run id is in All commands.\nInstall and start The installer says \"no PAT\"\nget-theia: error: no PAT — set THEIA_PAT or run interactively (the releases repo is private)\nCause. The installer has no GitHub token and nowhere to ask for one: it runs without a terminal.\nFix. Run the installer in a normal terminal window; it asks for the token with hidden input. See Install.\nThe installer says \"cannot reach dokimelabs/theia-releases\"\nget-theia: error: cannot reach dokimelabs/theia-releases (check the PAT's repo access and network)\nCause. The token has no access to the private release repository, has expired, or there is no network.\nFix. Check that the token was issued for the dokimelabs/theia-releases repository with read access to contents (Contents: read) and has not expired. Check access to github.com.\nThe installer refuses on an Intel Mac or on Windows\nget-theia: error: Intel macOS (darwin/x86_64) is not supported — releases target darwin/aarch64 (Apple silicon) and linux x86_64/aarch64\nCause. Version 0.2.201 has no builds for Intel Macs or Windows. See Systems and run modes.\ntheia is not found after install\nCause. The installer puts a link to the program into ~/.local/bin, and that folder is not in your PATH. The installer warns about this with a NOTE: add … to PATH line.\nFix. Add the folder to PATH. For zsh:\necho 'export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\"' >> ~/.zshrc\nOpen a new terminal window. For bash, use the ~/.bashrc file.\nEvery command fails with \"invalid release version\"\ninvalid release version 'garbage' (expected e.g. 1.2.3 or v1.2.3)\nCause. The THEIA_X_RELEASE variable is set to a wrong value. It breaks every command, even theia version.\nFix. Remove the variable, and remove it from your shell startup files too:\nunset THEIA_X_RELEASE\n\"release v… is not available\"\nrelease v0.1.14 is not available: not cached and no GitHub PAT configured — cannot download looked in cache: ~/.cache/theia/releases/0.1.14 fix: check the version tag, configure a GitHub PAT (theia setup --pat <token> or THEIA_GH_TOKEN), and ensure network access to github.com — theia never silently falls back to the running version.\nCause. --x-release or THEIA_X_RELEASE asks for a version that is not in the cache, and there is no GitHub token to download it.\nFix. Save a token ( theia setup --pat, see Keys and access ), or remove the flag and the variable.\n\"no vendor deliverables found\" or \"vendor prompts broken\"\ntheia: no vendor deliverables found (lib/vendor missing and no installed set) — reinstall via get-theia, or point THEIA_VENDOR_DIR at a vendor tree (THE-065)\ntheia: vendor prompts broken — <what is wrong> fix: theia self update (re-verifies by hash), or reinstall; THEIA_VENDOR_DIR overrides (THE-065)\nCause. The shipped file set ( vendor deliverables ) is missing or damaged; it holds the role prompts. Without it run, plan, ask, probe, fork, compare, diagnose, serve, eval, replay, msg and answer do not work.\nFix. Run theia self update or reinstall Theia. version, setup, self, doctor and init keep working meanwhile.\nself update says \"no GitHub PAT\"\nself update: no GitHub PAT — theia setup --pat <token> (or THEIA_GH_TOKEN)\nFix. Save a GitHub token with theia setup --pat, or set the THEIA_GH_TOKEN variable. See Keys and access.\ntheia setup says \"unknown argument\"\nsetup: unknown argument\nCause. A flag that takes a value uses the next word. In theia setup --pat --openai-key <key> the word --openai-key becomes the token, and the key is left over.\nFix. Give each flag its value right after it: --pat <token>, --openai-key <key>.\nThe wizard created.theia in a subfolder\nCause. The first-run wizard creates the store in the current folder. theia init puts it at the repository root.\nFix. If the store was just created and holds none of your runs, delete the.theia folder in that subfolder, go to the repository root and run theia init:\nrm -rf.theia\ncd <repository root>\ntheia init\nRun the first command exactly in the subfolder where the wizard created.theia.\ntheia --dev says \"no development root\"\n--dev: no development root — pass a path (theia --dev <path> …) or set dev.root in ~/.config/theia/config.yaml (theia setup --dev-root <path>)\nCause. The --dev flag is for platform developers: it runs Theia from a source tree. An installed version has no such tree, so the command stops with code 1. The flag does not print the help. When a path is given but it is not a Theia source tree, the text differs:\n--dev: /tmp/x does not look like a theia dev tree (missing apps/theia/src/main.ts)\nFix. Remove --dev from the command. How the flag works for developers is described in For platform developers and Global flags.\nThe GitHub token disappeared on macOS\nSymptom. theia doctor shows github pat: absent, although you saved a token.\nCause. The folder ~/Library/Application Support/theia appeared. Theia now reads settings from there and no longer sees ~/.config/theia.\nFix. If the new folder is empty, delete it, and Theia reads ~/.config/theia again:\nrmdir \" $HOME /Library/Application Support/theia\"\nIf the folder already holds files, save the token again with theia setup --pat; it goes into the new folder. See First setup.\ntheia doctor harnesses: FAIL\nharnesses: FAIL (neither codex nor claude is authenticated — one is required)\nCause. Neither Codex nor Claude Code is signed in.\nFix. Sign in to at least one engine: codex login (OpenAI subscription) or claude auth login (Anthropic subscription; the /login command inside claude does the same). Then run theia doctor again.\nClaude Code: \"API-key fallback is refused\"\nclaude code: unavailable (harness-claude: not logged in (run `claude login`; API-key fallback is refused — THE-013))\nCause. Theia works with Claude Code only through a subscription sign-in. The ANTHROPIC_API_KEY key is not used.\nFix. Sign in to Claude Code with your subscription. See Keys and access.\n.theia store: not initialized in a project subfolder\nCause. theia doctor checks only the.theia folder in the current folder and does not look higher.\nFix. Run theia doctor from the project root. Other commands find the store from subfolders too.\nembeddings: … FAIL (not fetched and autoDownload is off …)\nembeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d) — FAIL (not fetched and autoDownload is off — theia setup --fetch-embeddings)\nCause. The local embedding model is not downloaded, and automatic download is off.\nFix. Download the model, about 23 MB:\ntheia setup --fetch-embeddings\nOr allow the download: gateway.embeddings.autoDownload: true in policy.yaml.\nembeddings: openai/… — FAIL (no OpenAI key …)\nembeddings: openai/text-embedding-3-small — FAIL (no OpenAI key — set OPENAI_API_KEY, theia setup --openai-key <key>, or policy provider: local)\nIn theia mem reindex, theia index reindex and theia ask the same cause looks like this:\ntheia: gateway.embeddings.provider=openai but no OpenAI key (set OPENAI_API_KEY, `theia setup --openai-key <key>`, or switch the policy to provider: local)\nCause. The settings choose the openai provider for embeddings, but there is no OpenAI key.\nFix. Set the key with OPENAI_API_KEY or theia setup --openai-key, or switch back to provider: local. See Keys and access.\nai rules shows (injected) for a removed entry\nCause. The ai rules line of theia doctor looks at rule files on disk, not at the entries in your settings.\nFix. Trust the context … injected: … line at the start of a run: it shows what really goes into requests. See Rules and prompts.\njail (none …): unavailable on macOS\nThis is not an error. macOS has no theia jail sandbox, so only the jailed-full tier is unavailable. The standard tier works. See Sandbox.\njail (container): docker ready, but Docker is not running\nCause. theia doctor only checks that the docker command exists. It does not check that Docker itself runs.\nRuns \"no.theia/ found\"\ntheia: no.theia/ found from ~/projects/app upward (and no user-registry workspace) — run 'theia init' in the workspace home first\nFix. Go to the repository root and run theia init. See First setup.\nA run does not see your latest commits\nCause. A run starts from the upstream branch, for example origin/main, not from your local HEAD. The base is shown in the first output line: run branch: theia/run-… (base origin/main @ …).\nFix. Push your commits, or start the run with --base HEAD. See Your first task.\ngit switch to the run branch fails\nfatal: 'theia/run-7adeu2' is already used by worktree at '~/projects/app/.theia/runs/2akdqayjgo2232jt7adeu2/trunk'\nCause. The run branch is already checked out in a hidden Theia working copy.\nFix. Take the result with a merge: while on your own branch, run git merge theia/run-…. See Run results.\nThe run branch cannot be deleted\nerror: cannot delete branch 'theia/run-7adeu2' used by worktree at '~/projects/app/.theia/runs/2akdqayjgo2232jt7adeu2/trunk'\nFix. First remove the working copy at the path from the message, then the branch:\ngit worktree remove <path from the message>\ngit branch -d theia/run-<suffix>\n\"branch '…' already exists\"\nrun: branch 'docs/demo' already exists — pass --branch <other-name>, or delete the stale one first (git branch -D docs/demo)\nFix. Give --branch another name, or delete the old branch.\n\"@file: no such file\"\nrun: @file: no such file 'nope.txt' (relative to ~/projects/app; escape a literal with @@)\nCause. The path after @ is read from the current folder. Paths like @./… and @/… are not inserted at all.\nFix. Check the path. To write the @ sign itself, double it: @@.\n\"prohibited content … cannot enter a model prompt\"\nrun: @key.pem: prohibited content (pem_private_key) — this file cannot enter a model prompt (THE-014)\nCause. The inserted file contains a secret: a private key, an API key, a card number or a seed phrase. Such a file never goes to a model. theia plan --task with the same @file prints the same text with the plan: prefix. The (THE-014) code at the end is an internal rule number, see THE-….\nFix. Remove the secret from the file, or do not insert that file. See Keys and access.\nThe run paused: the budget ran out\n!! anomaly budget_exhausted role=implementer transport=…!! anomaly budget_freeze taskId=… role=implementer\nrun.paused rounds=1 (resumable)\nhalted: paused · 1 gateway calls · $0.0000 ·!! 2 anomalies\npaused — resume with: theia run --resume run2akdqcio5w223v4bg3eng3\nCause. The run's budget money is used up. The run is not discarded; it is paused. Theia counts Claude calls at list price even on a subscription, so the default budget (10 US dollars) can run out in that case too.\nFix. Continue the run with a new budget in US dollars:\ntheia run --resume <runId> --budget 5\nThe new value replaces what is left of the budget; it is not added to it. To pause less often, raise run.maxUsdPerRun in policy.yaml or start runs with --budget 0. See Models for tasks.\nhalted: completed, but exit code 1\nCause. completed means \"no work is left\", not \"success\". At least one task was discarded ( discarded ). Other words like this are collected in the glossary section Misleading statuses.\nFix. Look at the cause:\ntheia diagnose <runId>\ntheia show <runId> --plain\nAfter Ctrl+C the log shows verify_fail … exit null\nverdict … verifier → verify_fail (1 evidence) “$ sleep 10 → exit null (no output)”\nCause. In --plain and --cli modes the check command receives the Ctrl+C signal too. This is a false failure.\nFix. Continue the run with theia run --resume <runId>. To pause in the TUI, press q: it does not interrupt the check command. Without the TUI, send the signal only to the Theia process from another terminal: kill -INT <pid>.\ntheia msg says \"noted\", but the run ignores it\nnoted — this run appears live: it folds cross-process notes at its next dispatch boundary (…)\nCause. In version 0.2.201 a new run does not read guidance from other processes. Only a run continued with --resume reads it.\nFix. Pause the run, send the guidance, then continue the run. The order is in Control a run.\nThe same goes for theia answer: it promises that a paused run hears the answer on resume, but the answer is not used. Send the information with theia msg and continue the run.\n\"plan … is 'draft', not approved\"\nrun: plan pln… is 'draft', not approved — approve it first (theia plan approve)\nFix. Approve the plan with theia plan approve and start again.\nThe refusal has already created a theia/run-… branch, a working copy and an unfinished run. theia prune does not remove them. The same happens when the word after --from-plan is not a plan id: the command prints terryid: malformed id '…' (see below ), and the branch and the run folder are already there. Remove them by hand, using the run folder from.theia/runs/:\ngit worktree remove.theia/runs/<run folder>/trunk\ngit branch -d theia/run-<suffix>\ntheia run --abandon <run folder>\nAfter that, theia prune can delete the run record. See Plan before you run.\n\"nothing to resume\"\nresume: all tasks terminal (finished) — nothing to resume\nCause. Every task of the run has already finished; there is nothing to continue. For a fork of a finished run made without --at, the word in brackets differs — (paused) — with the same meaning:\nresume: all tasks terminal (paused) — nothing to resume\n\"unknown track\"\nrun: unknown track 'nope' (supported: full_spec, fast_path)\nFix. Pass one of the two values to --track: fast_path or full_spec. See theia run.\n\"--base '…' does not resolve\"\nrun: --base 'nosuchref' does not resolve in ~/projects/app (fetch it, or name a reachable ref)\nCause. The branch or commit given in --base is not in the repository: a typo, or the branch has not been fetched yet.\nFix. Run git fetch, or name an existing branch, commit or HEAD. With --in-place the --branch and --base flags do not work at all:\nrun: --branch/--base need the run-branch mode (single-root git repo, not --in-place/--no-run-branch)\n\"plan floor … exceeds budget\"\nrun: plan floor ~$12.50 (5 tasks × $2.50 avg/task from this store's recorded runs) exceeds budget $10 — pass --budget 12.5 explicitly, raise run.maxUsdPerRun, or --budget 0 (unmetered)\nCause. The plan's cost estimate from earlier runs (the plan floor ) is above the default budget. Theia refuses before the first model call.\nFix. Pass the budget explicitly: theia run --from-plan <planId> --budget 13. With an explicit --budget Theia only warns and goes on.\n\"audit chain BROKEN … (THE-005)\"\nresume: audit chain BROKEN at <event id> — refusing to resume a tampered run (THE-005)\nCause. The run's audit chain is broken: store.db was changed outside Theia or is damaged. fork and probe answer the same way, with the words parent chain BROKEN.\nFix. Such a run cannot be continued or forked. Check the other records with theia audit verify and start a new run. If store.db is damaged, restore it from a backup.\n\"run already finished\" from --abandon\nabandon: run already finished — nothing to do (prune reclaims finished runs)\nThis is not an error. The run has already finished; there is nothing to close. theia prune reclaims the space.\n\"terryid: neither a body nor a full id\" or \"malformed id\"\nterryid: neither a body nor a full id: 'ovfyt3'\nCause. The command expects a full id ( run… ) or the 22 characters after the prefix, but got a short tail, a date or some other word. show, diagnose, msg, replay, fork and mem asof answer this way. Only show --task, msg --task, fork --at and answer --question accept a short tail. The word terryid is the name of the id format.\nThe second text appears in 0.2.201 only in theia run --from-plan, when the word after the flag is not a plan id, for example the next flag:\nterryid: malformed id '--plain'\nBy then the theia/run-… branch, the folder in.theia/runs/ and an unfinished run already exist — remove them as described in \"plan … is 'draft', not approved\".\nFix. Copy the full id from the run output (the run run… line) or from the theia home screen. See All commands.\n\"no events for …\"\ntheia show: no events for run run2akec5t4y2223u65sptkum\ndiagnose: no events for run2akec5t4y2223u65sptkum\nCause. The id is well-formed, but this store has no such run: a typo in the id, or the command runs in another project. Every command that takes a run id answers this way: show, diagnose, replay, compare, fork, probe, msg, answer, run --resume, run --abandon. The word before the colon is the command name. This is the most common error after a typo.\nFix. Copy the whole id from the run output or from the theia home screen, and check that you are in the same project: every.theia store has its own runs. See All commands.\ntheia show says halted: (still open)\nCause. The run was stopped hard: with a second Ctrl+C, by closing the terminal window, or by a signal. No pause record was written.\nFix. Continue the run with theia run --resume <runId>; the interrupted step runs again. Or close the run: theia run --abandon <runId>.\ntheia prune says \"aborted\"\nprune: aborted (nothing deleted)\nCause. The command runs without a terminal, so nobody can confirm the deletion.\nFix. Add the --yes flag. First check what would be deleted with --dry-run. See audit, prune.\nTwo similar cases sit next to this one. Without --keep and without --older-than the command prints only its usage and exits with code 1. The age 0d is rejected:\nprune: --older-than needs 7d / 48h / 90m, got '0d'\ntheia show --task says the branch was not found\nshow: audit branch ai/… not found in ~/projects/app (in-place run, or the branch was pruned)\nCause. The task was not merged. The ai/… branch reaches your repository only on merge, and the message names a different cause.\nFix. Look at the changes right in the task copy: git -C.theia/runs/<run folder>/ws-<task folder> diff.\nSettings \"invalid manifest (spec.run: unrecognized_keys)\"\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.run: unrecognized_keys)\nCause. The spec.run section of.theia/config/policy.yaml has an unknown key, most often a typo. The message names the section but not the key. With such a file every command fails except theia doctor: it prints all its lines and hides the error in the embeddings line:\nembeddings: FAIL (policy unreadable: config: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.run: unrecognized_keys))\nNeighbouring errors of the same kind. Broken YAML, for example an unclosed bracket:\nconfig: policy.yaml: YAML parse failed: Flow map in block collection must be sufficiently indented and end with a } at line 4, column 1:\nA typo in the kind field:\nconfig: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (kind: invalid_union_discriminator)\nIn a Context manifest the error names the entry number, counting from zero: config: context-x.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.entries.0.source: unrecognized_keys).\nFix. Compare the section's keys with the policy.yaml settings reference. The table of all settings errors is in Errors in settings files.\nA run paused because of settings\n!! anomaly environment_failure taskId=… role=lead phase=escalated reason=gateway fatal: gateway: no route for ro…!! anomaly dispatch_stalled taskId=… consecutive=3\nrun.paused rounds=5 (resumable)\nhalted: paused · 1 gateway calls · $0.0000 ·!! 4 anomalies\nCause. A step gave no result three times in a row, and the run paused. The text after reason= in the environment_failure line shows why:\nno route for role — the role has no route in gateway.routing;\ntransport 'nope' is not manifest-registered (docs/11 §6) — the route points at a transport that is not in registry.yaml, most often a typo;\nis registered but not constructed — the transport is listed but not created;\nprohibited content — the task text holds a secret;\ncontext refuse — text added to the request is longer than run.contextRefuseChars.\ntheia run exits with code 0 in this case. In scripts, check the halted: line.\nFix. Fix policy.yaml or the task text, then continue the same run: theia run --resume <runId>. See Models for tasks.\n\"no route for role\" right after adding a route to the project\nCause. Settings merge only one level deep. The project's gateway.routing replaced the same section from user settings as a whole, and the \"*\" route from there was lost.\nFix. Write the whole gateway.routing section, including \"*\", in the file that must win. See Settings files.\nAn entry for a role in routing or models has no effect\nCause. The role name is misspelled, for example implementor. Theia silently skips such entries.\nFix. Write role names exactly as in the Roles table.\ntheia init --harness claude printed harness: claude, but runs still use Codex\nCause. theia init does not rewrite an existing policy.yaml.\nFix. Change gateway.routing in.theia/config/policy.yaml by hand. See Switch the engine of an existing store.\nA secret in the task text: → fatal and a pause\ngateway implementer@blocked → fatal\nCause. A detector found a secret in the task text, for example a key or a card number, and did not let the request reach the model. The run pauses.\nFix. Remove the secret from the text and start a new run. Note that the original text is already saved in.theia/store.db as it is.\nIf the detector was wrong, for example an account number looks like a card number, lower the detector's class: gateway.maskOverrides: { card_number: restricted }. The number is then replaced with [CARD NUMBER], and the run goes on. See Keys and access.\nWith run.rulesSoT, agents stopped following AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md\nCause. This mode empties AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md in task copies but does not add them to requests itself.\nFix. Add Context entries with path: AGENTS.md and path: CLAUDE.md. See Rules and prompts.\nA rule with pathTriggers does not reach the implementer\nCause. In a normal run Theia matches pathTriggers against the task's changed files, and that list is empty at first.\nFix. Use roles or always. With --in-place, matching against the task text works. See Rules and prompts.\nA Context entry with an absolute path: \"names missing file\"\ncontext: entry 'readme' names missing file /Users/me/projects/app/README.md\nCause. Absolute paths in Context entries are not supported, and the error calls an existing file missing. theia run stops with code 1 right after the run branch: … line. With a relative path the same entry works: context injected: readme.\nFix. Copy the file into the repository and give a path relative to the project root.\n\"run.resources needs linux cgroups\"\nrun: run.resources is configured but unenforceable here — run.resources needs linux cgroups (systemd-run); platform is darwin (unset run.resources to run uncapped DELIBERATELY)\nCause. policy.yaml has a run.resources section, but resource limits work only on Linux with systemd-run. theia run stops with code 1. The resources: unavailable (…) line in theia doctor is only a warning.\nFix. On macOS, remove the run.resources section from policy.yaml.\n\"sandboxTier jailed-full requires the theia jail\"\nrun: sandboxTier jailed-full requires the theia jail, which is unavailable here: no sandbox backend on darwin yet (the container backend is the queued arm — docs/13); use standard or trusted-full (linux: bwrap + user namespaces; windows: AppContainer; see 'theia doctor')\nCause. The jailed-full sandbox tier works only where theia jail works, which means Linux. theia run stops with code 1.\nFix. On macOS keep the standard tier (the default). See Sandbox.\nTUI screen Enter on the home screen: \"kernel: no vendored prompts\"\nkernel: no vendored prompts — the composition root must call setVendorPrompts from the resolved vendor dir (reinstall, or set THEIA_VENDOR_DIR); prompts do not exist in code (THE-065)\nCause. A bug in release 0.2.201: the task box on the home screen does not work. Setting THEIA_VENDOR_DIR does not help.\nFix. Start tasks with theia run --task \"…\". Clean up what the failed start left behind:\ntheia run --abandon <runId>\ngit worktree remove.theia/runs/<run folder>/trunk\ngit branch -d theia/run-<suffix>\nAfter that the home screen shows the run as abandoned and stops counting it as live.\nThe terminal is \"broken\" after a crash\nSymptom. After kill -TERM or a crash, the screen does not scroll and the cursor is gone.\nCause. Theia exited without returning the terminal to normal mode.\nFix. Leave the alternate screen and turn the cursor back on:\ntput rmcup\ntput cnorm\nA run started with nohup died when the window closed\nCause. Theia does not keep the protection from the terminal-close signal that nohup sets. The run is left in the halted: (still open) state.\nFix. Run inside tmux, or in the background with < /dev/null & and disown. Continue the interrupted run with theia run --resume <runId>. See Leave without stopping a task.\ntheia run --resume … --tui shows an incomplete tree\nSymptom. After the resume the tree has no old tasks, calls are labeled unattributed, and the track and budget are blank.\nCause. The live screen sees only the events after the resume.\nFix. Resume the run with --plain or --cli, and look at the full tree in theia show <runId> --tui.\nCtrl+C does nothing while typing a steering note\nFix. Leave the input field first with Esc or Tab, then press q. See Navigation and hotkeys.\ntheia-blob-*.txt files pile up in the temp folder\nCause. The v and p keys save the full request text to a temporary file and never delete it.\nFix. On macOS, list and delete these files:\nls \" $TMPDIR \" /theia-blob-*.txt\nrm -f \" $TMPDIR \" /theia-blob-*.txt\ntmux shows q and _ instead of frames\nCause. The tmux client is not in UTF-8 mode.\nFix. Attach to the session with the -u flag: tmux -u attach -t <session name>.\n\"terminal too small\"\nterminal too small (<cols>x<rows> < 20x5)\nCause. The window is narrower than 20 columns or shorter than 5 rows. The brackets show the current size and the minimum.\nFix. Make the terminal window bigger. The minimum size is 20×5 characters.\n\"degrading to --plain\"\nview: --tui needs an interactive terminal (a TTY for output and keys) — degrading to --plain\nThis is not an error. The output does not go to a terminal, so Theia shows the run line by line, as with --plain.\nCode index, memory and questions index or ask fails with \"not a git repository\"\nfatal: not a git repository (or any of the parent directories):.git\nCause. The store was created for a folder without git. The code index and ask work only in a git repository.\nFix. Use the index in a git repository: run theia init at its root. mem commands work without git too.\n\"query embedding failed\"\ntheia: query embedding failed (fatal: embeddings-local: model 'all-MiniLM-L6-v2' missing at … — download disabled; run `theia setup --fetch-embeddings` (or allow download) to install ~23 MB)\nCause. A vector index is published, but the local embedding model is not downloaded and download is off. There is no word-only fallback in this case.\nFix. Download the model with theia setup --fetch-embeddings, or allow the download ( gateway.embeddings.autoDownload: true ).\nIf you cannot download the model right now, add the line model: bge-small-en-v1.5 to the gateway.embeddings section of.theia/config/policy.yaml. Search then prints vector lane off and searches by words. When the model is available, remove that line and run theia index reindex or theia mem reindex.\n\"memory: embedding failed during index build\"\nmemory: embedding failed during index build (fatal)\nCause. theia mem reindex cannot compute embeddings: usually the local model is not downloaded and autoDownload is off. The command itself does not name the cause; the embeddings line of theia doctor does.\nFix. Run theia doctor and follow the hint in the embeddings line, for example download the model with theia setup --fetch-embeddings.\nindex search does not find new files\nCause. theia index build writes new cards but does not refresh search.\nFix. After a build, run theia index reindex. See Code index, memory and questions.\n\"theia index: unknown subcommand '--budget'\"\nCause. Flags go after the subcommand.\nFix. Write it like this: theia index build --budget 1.\ntheia: vector lane off — …\nThis is a warning. Meaning-based search was skipped; word search works. The reason follows the dash. Rebuilding search usually helps: theia index reindex or theia mem reindex.\nIf the text contains +sent@2, the memory.sentenceGraph setting with h0 or h1 is to blame. Set memory.sentenceGraph: off back and run theia mem reindex. See policy.yaml settings.\n\"--date needs an explicit date\"\nmem chunk: --date needs an explicit date (e.g. 2026-09-02), got 'yesterday'\nFix. Give an explicit date, for example 2026-09-02. The --date flag must come right after chunk. Write months in words, for example September 2026: 2026-09 is taken as the whole year.\nmem asof does not accept a date\nterryid: neither a body nor a full id: '2026-09-01'\nCause. mem asof expects a record id ( fct…, run… ), not a date. See mem.\n\"usd budget exhausted\" from ask\nask: gateway fatal: gateway: usd budget exhausted for this run (THE-024)\nCause. --budget is 0 or below zero.\nFix. Pass a positive value or leave the flag out: the default ask budget is $1.\nplan, ask or probe fails with \"prohibited content (api_key)\"\nplan: gateway fatal: gateway: prohibited content (api_key) — call blocked (THE-014)\nCause. A current memory fact holds a key. theia mem add does not mask secrets. Facts from memory go into the model request, and the gateway blocks that request. The same happens in theia probe: probe: gateway fatal: gateway: prohibited content (api_key) — call blocked (THE-014).\nFix. Save the fact without the key and replace the old one with it. theia mem add and theia mem search print fact ids:\ntheia mem add <kind> \"<text without the key>\"\ntheia mem supersede <old fct…> <new fct…>\nThe key stays in history; theia mem asof still shows it. So revoke the key with the service that issued it.\nA fact is not visible in ask, the hooks or mem search\nCause. The fact was saved with the --root flag. Only theia mem search --root <name> reads that memory; the hooks and ask do not see it. In a single-root project, --root root hides the fact even from plain mem search.\nFix. Save shared facts without --root. See mem.\nindex status --root shows files of other roots as orphaned\nFix. Run theia index status without --root: it shows the state of all roots at once.\nIntegrations After an update Claude Code or Codex runs the old version\nCause. Hooks, the status line and.mcp.json store the path of one specific version ( …/releases/0.2.201/theia ). Old versions are not deleted, so they keep running.\nFix. Replace the version path in the files with ~/.local/bin/theia, or remove Theia's entries and install the integration again. Both ways are described in Claude Code and Codex.\nTheia hooks run twice\nCause. Installing again after an update added a second set of hooks with its own path.\nFix. Remove all Theia entries from the settings and install the hooks once. See Claude Code.\n\".mcp.json is not valid JSON\" from theia hooks install claude\ntheia hooks: ~/projects/app/.mcp.json is not valid JSON — fix it first (never clobbered)\nCause. The project already has a.mcp.json file with a JSON error. The command exits with code 1 halfway: the hooks in.claude/settings.json and the status line are already written, while the MCP server and the /theia skill are not yet there. The same text naming.claude/settings.json means the Claude Code settings file is broken; then nothing is written.\nFix. Fix the JSON in.mcp.json and run theia hooks install claude again. Running it again is safe.\nCodex has no theia server\nNo MCP servers configured yet. Try `codex mcp add my-tool -- my-command`.\nCause. Codex does not trust the project, so it does not read its.codex/ folder.\nFix. Start codex in the project folder and confirm trust, or run theia hooks install codex --trust. The hooks also need approval with /hooks inside Codex. See Codex.\nClaude Code: the theia server waits for approval\ntheia: … serve --home … - ⏸ Pending approval (run `claude` to approve)\nFix. Start claude in the project folder and approve the theia server. See Claude Code.\ntheia hooks install git has no effect\nCause. git has core.hooksPath set. Theia writes the hook into.git/hooks, but git runs hooks from another folder. The command still reports success.\nFix. Check whether core.hooksPath is set:\ngit config --get core.hooksPath\nIf the command prints anything, Theia's hook in.git/hooks will not run. See Git.\n\"ENOTDIR\" from theia hooks install git\nENOTDIR: not a directory, mkdir '~/projects/app-wt/.git/hooks'\nCause. The command ran in a linked git worktree, where.git is a file, not a folder. The error appears when that worktree has its own.theia store. Without one the command stops earlier:\ntheia hooks: no.theia/ in ~/projects/app-wt — run 'theia init' first\nFix. Install the hook from the main working copy of the repository.\n\"EADDRINUSE\" from theia serve --metrics\nError: listen EADDRINUSE: address already in use:::19466\nCause. The port is already taken by another theia serve process.\nFix. Pick another port. See Metrics and tracing.\nSee also\nKnown limitations\nAll commands\nGlossary","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/guides/plan-and-run.html":{"title":"Plan before you run","content":"For a bigger task, ask theia for a plan first: a list of tasks with their order and checks. You read the plan, change or approve it, and then theia runs exactly the version you approved.\nWarning\ntheia plan calls the planner model, so it costs money, like a run.\nHow it works\ntheia plan --task \"…\" — the planner writes a plan. Theia saves it as a draft with an id pln….\ntheia plan show <planId> — you read it.\nIf needed: theia plan add-context adds information, theia plan request-changes records what is wrong.\ntheia plan approve <planId> — you approve it.\ntheia run --from-plan <planId> — theia runs the approved plan.\nApproval is tied to the digest of the plan, a fingerprint of its content. Any change sends the plan back to draft, so a plan runs only in the form that you approved.\nStep 1. Create a plan\ntheia plan --task \"<text>\" [--non-interactive]\ntheia plan [--non-interactive] < <file>\nWhat theia does:\nIt collects context: the code map, your context rules and relevant memory. It prints context collected (N blocks): and one line per block.\nIt asks the planner. The planner returns either tasks or questions.\nTasks: theia saves a draft and prints the plan, a cost estimate and the next commands: next: theia plan approve pln… && theia run --from-plan pln…. The estimate is the average price per task across earlier runs in this store, multiplied by the number of tasks. Without earlier runs, or when all of them cost $0, theia prints cost estimate: no run history — no estimate. When tasks have check commands, the line verify pre-flight: off (opt in with policy run.planVerifyPreflight: always) comes after the plan: theia did not run those commands ahead of time, see verify pre-flight.\nQuestions: on a terminal theia asks them, up to two rounds, and plans again with your answers. With --non-interactive, or without a terminal, it saves a draft with the open questions and exits with code 0:\nOPEN QUESTIONS — plan saved as draft pln…:? <the planner's question>\nanswer with: theia plan add-context pln… \"…\" — then re-run theia plan or approve\nSuch a draft holds one placeholder task with the key unresolved. Do not approve it: answer with theia plan add-context, then create a new plan.\nIf the reply of the planner cannot be read, theia prints plan: planner reply off contract (…) — asking for a repair (one bounded retry) and asks once more. A second failure ends with plan: planner reply rejected twice — … and exit code 1.\n--non-interactive is for places where nobody can answer questions: CI, a script, a background job. It takes no value. Without the flag theia asks its questions on the terminal, and without a terminal it behaves as if the flag were set. If the planner still asks questions, the plan is not lost: it is saved as a draft, as shown above.\nThe text can include files with @path, see file inclusion. All options: theia plan.\nStep 2. Read the plan\ntheia plan list\nExample output:\npln2akdql5sgs225jjdujidwc [draft] 2 tasks — Add a greeting file and mention it in the README\nShow one plan:\ntheia plan show <planId>\nExample output:\nplan 2akdql5sgs225jjdujidwc [draft] digest 548c1b240150…\nbrief: Add a greeting file and mention it in the README greet [fast_path] ✓ test -f greeting.txt Create greeting.txt with the text hello docs [fast_path] ⇐ greet ✓ grep -q greeting.txt README.md Mention greeting.txt in README.md\ngreet and docs are task keys. [fast_path] is the track.\n⇐ greet means docs waits until greet is merged.\n✓ … is the check command of the task. It replaces --verify for this task.\nThe indented line is the description of the task.\nStep 3. Add information or ask for changes\nAdd information that the plan must take into account:\ntheia plan add-context <planId> \"<text>\"\nExample output:\ncontext added — NEW digest e96fe336654a…, status draft (re-approval required)\nThe text becomes part of the plan. The plan gets a new digest and goes back to draft.\nRecord what is wrong with the plan:\ntheia plan request-changes <planId> \"<text>\"\nExample output:\nchanges requested on pln2akdql5sgs225jjdujidwc\nThe status becomes changes_requested, and plan show shows your text as note: …. The tasks stay as they are. For a different set of tasks, create a new plan with a clearer text.\nStep 4. Approve the plan\ntheia plan approve <planId>\nExample output:\napproved pln2akdql5sgs225jjdujidwc @ e96fe336654a…\nAfter the changes and the approval, plan show looks like this:\nplan 2akdql5sgs225jjdujidwc [approved] digest e96fe336654a…\nbrief: Add a greeting file and mention it in the README\nnote: Keep the README change short [user] README.md is plain Markdown greet [fast_path] ✓ test -f greeting.txt Create greeting.txt with the text hello docs [fast_path] ⇐ greet ✓ grep -q greeting.txt README.md Mention greeting.txt in README.md\nStep 5. Run the plan\ntheia run --from-plan <planId> [options]\nTheia creates one task per plan task, named [key] description. Both tasks enter implement at once, but docs starts only after greet is merged. Example output with --plain, shortened:\nrun branch: theia/run-m6ql2b (base HEAD (no origin remote) @ a67ee9e)\no6je6ctj run.created budget=$10 track=fast_path plan=jdujidwc\nov43wwvt task.created ptn2mqab [fast_path] [greet] Create greeting.txt with the text hello\np4hwzov4 task.created ovrzs5yx [fast_path] [docs] Mention greeting.txt in README.md\nrun run2akdql5yio223qpzm6ql2b plan pln2akdql5sgs225jjdujidwc @ e96fe336654a… → 2 tasks\npcolpyou verdict ptn2mqab verifier → verify_pass (1 evidence)\npxuotora verdict ptn2mqab merger → merged (1 evidence)\npwmiqzj4 verdict ovrzs5yx verifier → verify_pass (1 evidence)\norgtsjnl verdict ovrzs5yx merger → merged (1 evidence)\npmnkihfy run.finished halted=completed rounds=8\ntsk2akdql5ywk223qptn2mqab → merged (4 rounds) [greet] Create greeting.txt with the text hello\ntsk2akdql5ywk22bqovrzs5yx → merged (4 rounds) [docs] Mention greeting.txt in README.md\nhalted: completed · 4 gateway calls · $0.0000\naudit chain: intact\nbudget remaining: 10\nrun branch theia/run-m6ql2b @ 881481f — 4 merges ahead of base; review/PR from it (your checkout was not touched)\nThe result is on the run branch, as in Your first task.\nThe options of theia run work here too: --budget, the views, --base and the others, see --from-plan. --verify applies only to tasks without a ✓ command of their own: for the others the command from the plan wins.\n--track full_spec or --track fast_path puts every task of this run on one track and prints track override: all 2 tasks forced to full_spec for this run (plan content unchanged). The plan itself does not change.\nTheia compares the budget with the cost estimate (called the plan floor in messages): the average price per task across earlier runs, multiplied by the number of tasks. Without earlier paid runs there is no comparison. If the default budget is below the estimate, theia stops before the first model call with exit code 1. The message format:\nrun: plan floor ~$<sum> (<N> tasks × $<avg> avg/task from this store's recorded runs) exceeds budget $<budget> — pass --budget <sum> explicitly, raise run.maxUsdPerRun, or --budget 0 (unmetered)\nIf you set --budget yourself and it is below the estimate, theia only warns and goes on. Expect a budget pause, see When the budget runs out. The warning format:\nrun: WARNING — plan floor ~$<sum> (<N> tasks × $<avg> avg/task from this store's recorded runs) exceeds budget $<budget>; proceeding on your explicit --budget (expect a budget pause; theia run --resume <id> --budget N continues)\n--budget 0 turns off both the limit and this comparison.\nA refused plan run leaves a branch\nWarning\ntheia run --from-plan for a plan that is not approved stops with exit code 1. By then it has already created a run branch, a hidden git worktree and an unfinished run record. theia prune never removes unfinished runs, so clean up by hand. The same happens with an unknown plan id ( plan: unknown plan … ) and with an id in the wrong format ( terryid: malformed id '…' ). In the last case there is no run record: only the branch and the worktree remain.\nExample output of the refused command:\nrun branch: theia/run-2bkse3 (base HEAD (no origin remote) @ a67ee9e)\nc4bz76iv run.created budget=$10 track=fast_path plan=jdujidwc\nrun: plan pln2akdql5sgs225jjdujidwc is 'draft', not approved — approve it first (theia plan approve)\nThe same refusal for an unknown id and for a malformed id:\nrun branch: theia/run-jywuib (base HEAD (no origin remote) @ 8907ddc)\n4qdyskd5 run.created budget=$10 track=fast_path plan=zzzzzzzz\nplan: unknown plan 2akzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzz\nrun branch: theia/run-rjtklt (base HEAD (no origin remote) @ 8907ddc)\nterryid: malformed id 'plnnope'\nFind the worktree:\ngit worktree list\nExample output:\n~/projects/app a67ee9e [main]\n~/projects/app/.theia/runs/2akdql5u3g223ldx2bkse3/trunk a67ee9e [theia/run-2bkse3]\nRemove the worktree and the branch, and close the run record. The run folder name is the run id without run. It ends with the same 6 characters as the branch.\ngit worktree remove.theia/runs/<run folder>/trunk\ngit branch -d theia/run-<suffix>\ntheia run --abandon <run folder>\nExample output:\nDeleted branch theia/run-2bkse3 (was a67ee9e).\nabandoned run2akdql5u3g223ldx2bkse3 — workspaces kept; reclaim with 'theia prune'\ntheia run --abandon takes the full run id or its 22 characters, which is exactly the folder name. It closes the run record and deletes nothing. When there is no record, as after terryid: malformed id, theia answers abandon: no events for <run folder> with exit code 1, and this step is not needed. For a run that already finished the answer is abandon: run already finished — nothing to do (prune reclaims finished runs), exit code 1.\nAfter --abandon a later theia prune removes the record.\nSee also\ntheia plan\ntheia run --from-plan\nYour first task\nMCP server","keywords":[],"tags":[]},"en/glossary.html":{"title":"Glossary","content":"This page explains the words Theia prints: in command output, on the TUI screen, in theia doctor and in settings files. Each term appears exactly as Theia writes it. The explanation uses plain words.\nTerms are grouped by topic. If you only know the word, open the A–Z list.\nAll terms A–Z\nSymbols: $ · Σ · ‖ · ↻ · ⋯ · ⏱ · ■ · ⚙ · ✎ · ✔ ✖ ◆ ◌ ‼ » · ❯ · ⤷\nA: --abandon · abandoned · absent · accept_unverified · ai rules · ai/<task> · algedonic ·!! anomaly · anomaly · answer ▸ · approve · approved · arb_continue · arb_escalate · arbiter · arbitrate · ask · asof · audit branch · audit chain · autoDownload\nB: base · @blocked · blob · blocked on · blueprint · blueprint floor · bm25 · board ( --cli ) · body · brief · --budget · budget · budget remaining · budget_exhausted · budget_freeze\nC: cant_do · changes_requested · changeset_partial · channel · checkpoint · chk… · chunk · clarify · [clarify] · clarify_stalled · clarify_unanswered · Claude Code · claude code · claude-index · claude-write · --cli · clock.read · Codex · codex hooks · codex subscription · codex-full · codex-jailed · codex-write ·.codex/hooks.json · command · commitment · compact-checkpoint · completed · config.yaml · constraint · context injected · context-rules.yaml · control bus · cost estimate · ctx ⚠N · current\nD: data class · dataClasses · decision · default · delegation_depth · delegation_fanout · delegation_refused · detached · detail · --dev · diagnostician · diagnostics · digest · dirty-marks · dirty-paths · discard · discarded · dispatch boundary · dispatch_stalled · doctor · done · draft\nE: [EMAIL] · embeddings · entity · entry-scan · environment_failure · escalated · [@ event k/N] · event log · evidence · extractor\nF: F6 · fact · FAIL · fail-open · FAILED · failed · (FAILED) · fake · fast_path · fct… · [file_ref] · fingerprint · follow · fork · forked· · fresh · fresh_implementer · fresh_perspective · freshness · frozen · full_spec\nG: gate 1 · gate 2 · gateway · gateway call · gateway.maskOverrides · gateway.models · gateway.routing · generation · generation.published · github pat · guidance\nH: halted · hard stop · harness · harnesses · head · --here · home screen · /hooks · hooks · hybrid\nI: idx ⚠N · illegal_verdict · implement · implementer · importance · in flight · --in-place · index entry · index.build · index.entry · index is fresh · indexBuildBudgetUsd · indexer · indexPolicy · intent index · --interaction bus · is available\nJ: jail · jailed-full\nK: keybar · kind: Context · kind: Pricing · kind: Workspace\nL: lane · layer · lead · lead_accept · lead_discard · lead_rescope · lead_reverify · lexical index · lexical-only · lexical-scan · lineage · live · locked\nM: maintenance · manifest · marked dirty · masked · maskOverrides · --max-rescopes · --max-rounds · maxTotalRounds · MCP ·.mcp.json · memory · memory.fact · merge · merge_fail · merged · merger · merges ahead of base · /metrics · missing · mode: in-place · module · msg · [msg]\nN: (N evidence) · N live · no commits on the task branch · --no-spinner · NO_COLOR · no_progress · --non-interactive · none · not fetched · not initialized · note\nO: observation · OPEN QUESTIONS · OPENAI_API_KEY · orphan generations GCed · orphaned · overlay\nP: PAT · @path · paused · pending · permitted · phase · --plain · plan · plan floor · plan.op · planned · planned_without_subtasks · planner · pln… · Policy, policy.yaml · post-baseline · [post_run_maintenance] · posture · pre-task · pre-task gates · precheck · preference · prefix replays free · prerelease · present · primary · probe · prohibited · prunable · published=true\nQ: questions waiting\nR: R5, T-W · R9 · random.draw · ready · reaper · receipt · recency · recent runs · --record · registry.yaml · release · replay · replay_miss · Repo intent map · Repo map (head) · rerouted · rescope · rescope_budget · resources · restricted · resumable · --resume · review · reviewer · rework · role · --root · root · root cause · root:<name> · round · round_budget · run · run branch · run ended · run tree · run.answer · run.asked · run.blueprintMinImportance · run.changeset · run.contextAlertChars · run.created · run.finished · run.forked · run.freshPerspectiveAfter · run.interaction · run.maxRescopes · run.maxRoundsPerTask · run.maxUsdPerRun · run.paused · run.planVerifyPreflight · run.probed · run.preTask · run.question · run.resources · run.rulesSoT · run.sandboxTier · run.sessionContinuity · run.stallPauseAfter · run.stop_request · run.stopAndFixAfterFailures · run_answer · run_msg · run_start · run_stop · runId · running\nS: sandbox tier · scope · search generation · session · session-final · sessionId · --sessions · SessionStart · setup wizard · sigma · SKILL.md · stale · standard · start · statusLine · steer · steering · (still open) · stop_and_fix · stopped (…) · stopping · storage: · store · store.db · summaries refreshed · summary · supersede · symbol\nT: --task · task · task ▸ · task.created · task.note · task.phase · taxonomy · terryid · test_run · THE-005 · THE-013 · THE-014 · THE-024 · THE-065 ·.theia · theia compare · theia diagnose · theia serve · /theia skill ·.theia store · <theia-context> · <theia-memory> · theia/run-… · THEIA_GH_TOKEN · THEIA_LOG_FILE · THEIA_VENDOR_DIR · THEIA_X_RELEASE · tool · --track · track · track override · transport · TransportRegistry · trunk · trusted-full · tsk…xk6rzynf · TUI · --tui · tui-state.json\nU: ui.defaultView · unavailable · unmetered · unresolved · user config · user-home store · user.note\nV: vector · vector lane off · vendor deliverables · verbosity · verdict · verifier · --verify · verify · verify pre-flight · verify_fail · verify_pass\nW: Workspace · workspace · workspace home · [workspace_base] · workspaces.yaml · worktree · worktree root · ws-<task>\nX: --x-release\nRuns and tasks run\nA run is one execution of a task or a plan with theia run. A run has a run… id, a budget, an event log and its own git branch. At the end Theia prints a summary: each task's outcome, the halted: line and the log check.\nWhere you see it: the line run run… task tsk… (fast_path) at the start of a run, the summary halted: completed · 4 gateway calls · $0.0000, the recent runs: list on the home screen.\nSee also: runId, task, halted, run branch, event log, Your first task, theia run.\ntask\nA task is one unit of work inside a run. Its id starts with tsk. A run with --task creates one task; a run from a plan creates one task per plan item. On the full_spec track the planner may split a task into subtasks. Each task has its own phase, rounds, repository copy and outcome: merged or discarded.\nWhere you see it: task.created xk6rzynf [fast_path] …, the summary tsk… → discarded (6 rounds) …, tsk…xk6rzynf rows on the board and in the TUI.\nSee also: brief, phase, round, workspace, tsk…xk6rzynf.\nbrief\nThe brief is the task text you pass with --task or on standard input: echo \"…\" | theia run. You can include a file in it with @. Theia stores the text as is, even if the model call is blocked later. Do not put keys or passwords in it.\nWhere you see it: theia run --task \"…\", theia plan --task \"…\", the task.created line in --plain, the task text row in the TUI tree.\nSee also: @path, task, data class.\n@path\nIncluding a file with @. Theia replaces @path in a brief with the file name and appends the file content to the text as a block with the header --- file: <path> (sha256 …, N chars) ---. It works in run, plan, msg and probe, but not in ask. The path is relative to the current folder. @@ gives a literal @.\nPaths that start with /,./ or../ are not included: the word stays in the text and there is no error. A file with a secret, such as a private key, is refused: the command stops with prohibited content. Email addresses in the file become [EMAIL], and the block header shows masked email×1.\nWhere you see it: the header --- file: README.md (sha256 bc70e26f40b8, 7 chars) ---, the error run: @file: no such file 'nope.md' (…), the task.note [file_ref] event.\nSee also: brief, data class, masked, Argument rules.\ntrack\nThe track decides where a task starts. fast_path (the short track) hands the task straight to the implementer. full_spec (the full-spec track) runs the planner first. Set it with --track; the default is fast_path. Plan approval exists only in the theia plan → theia run --from-plan flow: a run with --track full_spec does not wait for approval.\nWhere you see it: run.created … track=fast_path, task.created … [full_spec] …, the TUI task row tsk…x [fast_path].\nSee also: fast_path, full_spec, plan, track override.\nfast_path\nThe short track: the task goes straight to the implement phase, with no planner. It is the default track. It suits small changes.\nWhere you see it: --track fast_path, task.phase … ready → implement, [fast_path] in task lines.\nSee also: track, full_spec, implement.\nfull_spec\nThe full-spec track: the task starts in the plan phase. The planner thinks the work through and may split it into subtasks; then the implementer works. The first line of such a task is task.phase … start → plan.\nWhere you see it: --track full_spec, [full_spec] in task lines, r1 plan planner → ready in the TUI.\nSee also: track, planner, plan, planned.\nround\nA round is one step of a task: one role worked and gave a verdict. The TUI numbers rounds r1, r2 …; the run summary shows (6 rounds). rounds=N in run.finished and run.paused lines is the total number of verdicts over all tasks of the run.\nThe limit is 10 rounds per task: the run.maxRoundsPerTask setting or the --max-rounds flag, where 0 removes the limit. When the limit is used up, the round_budget anomaly appears and the task goes to the lead. A rescope resets the limit counter, but round numbers in the output keep growing.\nWhere you see it: TUI r4 merge merger → merge_fail, board r6, summary tsk… → discarded (6 rounds), run.finished halted=completed rounds=6.\nSee also: verdict, round_budget, rescope, phase.\nevent log\nThe event log is the list of everything that happened in a run: tasks created, phase changes, verdicts, model calls, anomalies, steering notes. It lives in.theia/store.db. Each event stores a fingerprint of the previous one, so tampering shows. --plain prints the log one line per event, and theia show <runId> --plain shows it after the run.\nlive lines are not stored in the log. To save the full output with details to a file, set the THEIA_LOG_FILE variable.\nWhere you see it: --plain lines such as x5dhtxgs task.note [workspace_base], the commands theia show <runId> --plain, theia replay, theia audit verify.\nSee also: audit chain, --plain, live, THEIA_LOG_FILE, replay.\nnote\nThe word note means different things in Theia:\ntask.note — a service note in the log; the brackets name its source, such as [workspace_base] or [post_run_maintenance].\nuser.note — your steering or your answers to questions: user.note [msg] …, user.note [clarify] ….\nText in quotes “…” after a verdict — the role's short comment.\nnote: in theia plan show — your request for plan changes.\nnoted — … — the reply of theia msg.\nnote <source> — a service note row in the TUI tree.\nSee also: task.note, user.note, steering, verdict.\nlive\nThe word live means different things in Theia:\nlive lines in --plain: live ⏱ implementer tsk…6rzynf call started, live ↻ … retry …, live $ …. They are progress hints. They are not written to the log, so theia show --plain does not show them.\n[@ event 21/21 · live] in the TUI header — the screen follows a running run.\n(live; …) in the resuming run … line — the log has neither an end nor a pause: the run was interrupted without a pause.\nN live in the status line and on the home screen — the number of runs with no end or pause record. Killed runs count too.\nSee also: event log, [@ event k/N], --resume, statusLine, in flight.\nsigma\nΣ (sigma) is the run's shared notes that Theia passes to roles in every request: your steering, answers to clarifying questions, the lead's direction after a rescope. Theia rebuilds them from the event log. Every model call starts fresh; from earlier rounds a role gets these notes, the reviewer's objections and the check result.\nWhere you see it: sigma: {} in theia replay output, the sigma keys: … line in --plain, sessions: sigma-shared.\nSee also: steering, clarify, replay, session.\nsession\nThe word session means different things in Theia:\nA harness session — a chat inside Claude Code or Codex. Theia keeps only its number ( sessionId ) in the call details.\n--sessions and the line sessions: sigma-shared — an experimental mode: the shared part of a prompt goes first, and Theia records its fingerprint ( task.note [session_prefix] ). It does not continue a chat. It is off by default ( run.sessionContinuity: off ).\nSessionStart — the Claude Code and Codex hook event where Theia adds context ( theia mem context ).\ncompact-checkpoint and session-final — labels of transcript pieces that hooks save.\nSee also: harness, sigma, hooks, checkpoint.\n--non-interactive\nThe --non-interactive flag: the run asks nothing. The TUI screen does not open; --plain is used instead. If a role asks a clarifying question, the task freezes in the escalated phase instead of waiting for an answer. Use it in scripts, CI and background runs.\nWhere you see it: view: --tui is interactive; --non-interactive degrades to --plain, theia run … --non-interactive, theia plan --non-interactive.\nSee also: clarify, --plain, TUI, Leave without stopping a task.\nTHEIA_LOG_FILE\nAn environment variable with a file path. The run appends every --plain line with the full event data (the ⤷ {…} line) and the live lines to that file. It also works while the TUI is open, so you can watch the file from another terminal with tail -f. In 0.2.201 very short runs, under a second, may leave the file empty.\nWhere you see it: the line # theia run run… — <time> at the top of the file, the error THEIA_LOG_FILE: … — logging disabled.\nSee also: event log, live, Environment variables.\nRoles role\nA role is a kind of agent work in a run: plan, write code, check, review, merge, settle a dispute. A role is not a model. Routing ( gateway.routing ) decides which harness and model work in each role. The output shows the roles lead, planner, implementer, fresh_implementer, verifier, reviewer, merger, arbiter, indexer and none.\nTwo more roles exist in settings but do not show in run output: extractor writes memory summaries in theia mem maintain --llm, and diagnostician is unused in 0.2.201 — theia diagnose works without a model.\nWhere you see it: verdict … reviewer → approve, gateway implementer@<transport> → ok, live ⏱ lead tsk… call started.\nSee also: gateway.routing, transport, Roles.\nlead\nThe lead is the role that decides what happens to a stuck task in the escalated phase. Its options: rescope the task with a new direction ( lead_rescope ), accept the work ( lead_accept ), check it again ( lead_reverify ), discard the task ( lead_discard ) or ask you a question ( clarify ). The lead handles one task at a time.\nWhere you see it: verdict … lead → lead_discard, gateway lead@<transport>, TUI r6 escalated lead → lead_discard.\nSee also: escalated, rescope, lead_accept, lead_discard, clarify.\nplanner\nThe planner is the role that thinks the work through. It works in the plan phase of full_spec tasks and in theia plan. It answers planned with subtasks, ready (coding can start) or clarify (it needs your answer).\nWhere you see it: verdict … planner → ready, TUI r1 plan planner → ready.\nSee also: full_spec, plan, planned, clarify.\nimplementer\nThe implementer is the role that writes code. It works in the implement phase, in a separate copy of the repository. It answers ready (done), cant_do (cannot do it) or algedonic (a serious problem). Theia itself makes the commits, not the model.\nWhere you see it: verdict … implementer → ready (1 evidence), live ⏱ implementer tsk… call started.\nSee also: implement, workspace, fresh_implementer.\nfresh_implementer\nThe fresh implementer is a second implementer that starts the task again. After two attempts in the implement phase (the run.freshPerspectiveAfter setting) Theia hands the task to it and logs the fresh_perspective anomaly. It does not see the notes of earlier attempts, but it sees your steering and the reviewer's objections. Without its own line in gateway.routing it uses the same transport as implementer.\nWhere you see it:!! anomaly fresh_perspective taskId=… dispatches=2, gateway fresh_implementer@<transport>.\nSee also: implementer, fresh_perspective, gateway.routing.\nverifier\nThe verifier is the role of the verify phase. If you passed --verify commands, the commands do the check: Theia runs them, the exit code decides, and no model is called. Without commands a model checks the work. The verifier answers pass or fail; the output shows the events verify_pass and verify_fail.\nWhere you see it: verdict … verifier → verify_pass (1 evidence), verifier → verify_fail (1 evidence) “$ false → exit 1 (no output)”.\nSee also: verify, verify_pass, verify_fail, stop_and_fix.\nreviewer\nThe reviewer is the role of the review phase. It looks at the task's changes and answers approve (accept, go to merge), rework (needs more work) or discard (reject). After rework and discard the task goes to the arbiter; it is not discarded right away.\nWhere you see it: verdict … reviewer → approve (1 evidence).\nSee also: review, approve, rework, discard.\narbiter\nThe arbiter is the role of the arbitrate phase. It decides what to do after rework, discard, merge_fail or cant_do: one more attempt ( arb_continue, back to the implementer) or hand the task to the lead ( arb_escalate ).\nWhere you see it: verdict … arbiter → arb_escalate (1 evidence).\nSee also: arbitrate, arb_continue, arb_escalate, lead.\nmerger\nThe merger is the step of the merge phase. It is not a model: Theia itself merges the task branch into the run branch with plain git ( git merge --no-ff ). The answer is merged or merge_fail. Merges run one at a time.\nWhere you see it: verdict … merger → merged (1 evidence), merger → merge_fail (1 evidence) “no commits on the task branch”.\nSee also: merge, merged, merge_fail, run branch, precheck.\nindexer\nThe indexer is the role that writes code index entries: a short card for each file. It works in theia index build and in automatic index builds. Its calls are not part of a run.\nWhere you see it: theia index build, the line indexer: claude-index in policy.yaml.\nSee also: intent index, index entry, theia index build.\nnone\nnone is the role of a one-off question with no task. theia probe and theia ask use it. Its route comes from the \"*\" line in gateway.routing unless there is a none line.\nWhere you see it: gateway none@<transport> → ok in theia show <id> --plain for a probe or ask record.\nSee also: probe, ask, gateway.routing.\nTask phases phase\nA phase is the step a task is at now. Working phases: plan, implement, verify, review, merge, arbitrate, escalated. Final phases: merged (the work landed), discarded (dropped), planned (a plan item with subtasks). A phase change shows as task.phase <task> <event> → <phase>.\nWhere you see it: task.phase xk6rzynf ready → implement, the phase column on the board, discarded r6 in the TUI task row.\nSee also: implement, verify, review, merge, arbitrate, escalated, merged, discarded, task.phase, How a run works.\ndraft\nThe word has two meanings:\nThe phase of a new task. The task leaves it at once for plan or implement, so you rarely see it.\nA plan status: the plan was created or changed and is not approved yet. theia run --from-plan refuses such a plan.\nWhere you see it: pln… [draft] 2 tasks — … in theia plan list, context added — NEW digest …, status draft (re-approval required).\nSee also: plan, approved, phase.\nimplement\nThe phase where the implementer writes code in the task's repository copy. From here the task goes to verify (event ready ), to the arbiter ( cant_do ) or to the lead ( algedonic, no_progress ).\nWhere you see it: task.phase … ready → implement, TUI r1 implement implementer → ready.\nSee also: implementer, workspace, verify.\nverify\nThe word has several meanings:\nThe check phase after the implementer.\nThe --verify \"<command>\" flag; you can pass it several times. Theia runs the commands in order in the task copy with bash -lc, and the exit code decides. The commands run directly on your machine, not in a sandbox, with a 5-minute limit each. Without commands a model checks the work.\n✓ <command> in theia plan show — a plan item's own check command.\nlead_reverify — the lead asks for another check.\nWarning\nIn --plain and --cli, the first Ctrl+C in the terminal also kills a running check command. Theia records it as a failure: verify_fail with exit null.\nWhere you see it: task.phase … ready → verify, verdict … verifier → verify_fail (1 evidence) “$ false → exit 1 (no output)”.\nSee also: verifier, verify_pass, verify_fail, stop_and_fix, lead_reverify.\nreview\nThe phase where the reviewer looks at the task's changes. Next: approve leads to merge; rework and discard lead to the arbiter.\nWhere you see it: task.phase … verify_pass → review.\nSee also: reviewer, approve, rework, discard.\nmerge\nMerge ( merge ) is the phase where Theia merges the task's work into the run branch with plain git. No model works here. Merges run one at a time. After success the task copy is deleted, and the task branch ai/<task> stays in your repository. Do not confuse the merge phase with the merged outcome.\nWhere you see it: task.phase … approve → merge.\nSee also: merger, merged, merge_fail, run branch, ai/<task>.\narbitrate\nThe phase where the arbiter settles a dispute: one more attempt ( arb_continue → implement ) or hand the task to the lead ( arb_escalate → escalated ). A task comes here after rework, discard, merge_fail and cant_do.\nWhere you see it: task.phase … merge_fail → arbitrate.\nSee also: arbiter, arb_continue, arb_escalate.\nescalated\nThe task is stuck and waits for the lead. It gets here through arb_escalate, stop_and_fix, algedonic, no_progress and an unanswered clarifying question. The TUI marks such a task with ◆ and counts it as frozen. If the run paused or was closed, the task stays escalated.\nWhere you see it: task.phase … arb_escalate → escalated, TUI ◆ ▼ tsk…fd7pn6tz [fast_path] escalated r9, tasks: … 1 frozen.\nSee also: lead, frozen, no_progress, stop_and_fix.\nplanned\nThe final phase of a plan item. The planner answered planned and split the task into subtasks: the subtasks do the work, and the item itself is closed. If planned comes without subtasks, Theia treats it as ready and logs the planned_without_subtasks anomaly.\nSee also: planner, full_spec, planned_without_subtasks.\nmerged\nA task outcome: the work landed on the run branch. In the line task.phase … merged → merged the word appears twice: first the event (the merge worked), then the new phase. In --in-place mode there is no merge, and merged only marks the work as accepted.\nWhere you see it: verdict … merger → merged (1 evidence), summary tsk… → merged (4 rounds), the done count in the TUI.\nSee also: merge, run branch, --in-place, done.\ndiscarded\nA task outcome: the work was dropped and did not land on the run branch. Only the lead discards, with the lead_discard event; the reviewer's discard just sends the task to the arbiter. The task copy stays for inspection. theia run with a discarded task exits with code 1. The TUI marks such a task with ✖ and counts it as failed.\nWhere you see it: task.phase … lead_discard → discarded, summary tsk… → discarded (6 rounds), tasks: 0 running · 0 done · 1 failed.\nSee also: lead_discard, discard, failed, completed.\ndone\nThe word has two meanings:\nIn the TUI, N done is the number of tasks with a good outcome: merged or planned.\nA final phase done exists in Theia's code, but in 0.2.201 no step leads to it.\nWhere you see it: tasks: 0 running · 1 done · … in the TUI header.\nSee also: merged, planned, run tree.\nVerdicts and log events verdict\nA verdict is a role's answer to its step: a short word plus evidence references. The model answers with a word from its list ( pass, approve, continue, rescope …), and Theia prints the event ( verify_pass, approve, arb_continue, lead_rescope ). That is why --plain shows verify_pass, not pass.\nThe verdict line shows what the role answered. The task.phase line right after it shows what Theia applied. They can differ: for example, Theia applies the third verify_fail in a row as stop_and_fix.\nWhere you see it: verdict <task> <role> → <event> (N evidence) “<comment>”, TUI r2 verify verifier → verify_pass.\nSee also: evidence, task.phase, phase, round.\nstart\nThe start event of a full_spec task: the task moves from draft to plan.\nWhere you see it: task.phase … start → plan.\nSee also: full_spec, ready.\nready\n\"Done, next.\" One word for three moves: a new fast_path task goes to implement; the planner finished and the task goes to implement; the implementer finished and the task goes to verify. The phase after the arrow tells which move happened.\nWhere you see it: task.phase … ready → implement, task.phase … ready → verify, implementer → ready.\nSee also: fast_path, implement, verify.\ncant_do\nThe implementer reports that it cannot do the task. The task goes to the arbiter. theia diagnose calls this case capability-gap.\nSee also: implementer, arbitrate, root cause.\nalgedonic\nA serious-trouble signal from the implementer or the planner: the problem is bigger than this attempt. The task goes straight to the lead, skipping check and review.\nSee also: escalated, lead.\nverify_pass\nThe check passed: every --verify command exited with code 0, or the model verifier answered pass. The task goes to the reviewer.\nWhere you see it: verdict … verifier → verify_pass (1 evidence), task.phase … verify_pass → review.\nSee also: verify, verifier, verify_fail.\nverify_fail\nThe check failed: a command returned a non-zero code, and the commands after it are skipped. The task goes back to the implementer. After three failures in a row ( run.stopAndFixAfterFailures ) Theia breaks the loop with the stop_and_fix event.\nWhere you see it: verifier → verify_fail (1 evidence) “$ false → exit 1 (no output)”, task.phase … verify_fail → implement.\nSee also: verify, stop_and_fix, lead_reverify.\napprove\nThe reviewer accepted the changes. The task goes to merge.\nWhere you see it: verdict … reviewer → approve (1 evidence), task.phase … approve → merge.\nSee also: reviewer, merge.\nrework\nThe reviewer asks for more work. The task goes to the arbiter, which usually returns it to the implementer. Later attempts get the reviewer's objection. If rework repeats while checks pass, theia diagnose calls it review-loop.\nSee also: reviewer, arbitrate, root cause.\ndiscard\nThe reviewer rejects the changes. The task is not discarded by this: it goes to the arbiter. Only the lead can discard a task, with the lead_discard event.\nWhere you see it: task.phase … discard → arbitrate.\nSee also: lead_discard, discarded, arbitrate.\nmerge_fail\nThe merge failed. The reason is in the verdict comment, for example no commits on the task branch (the task copy has no commits), uncommitted work left in workspace (uncommitted edits remain) or a conflict. The task goes to the arbiter.\nWhere you see it: verdict … merger → merge_fail (1 evidence) “no commits on the task branch”.\nSee also: merger, precheck, arbitrate.\narb_continue\nThe arbiter decided to try again. The task returns to implement.\nSee also: arbiter, implement.\narb_escalate\nThe arbiter hands the task to the lead. The task moves to escalated.\nWhere you see it: verdict … arbiter → arb_escalate (1 evidence).\nSee also: arbiter, escalated, lead.\nrescope\nA rescope is the lead restarting a stuck task with a new direction. The output shows it as the lead_rescope event. The task goes back to the implementer, its round counter resets, and the lead's text becomes steering for this task. The lead may also replace the check commands.\nA task can be rescoped 2 times ( run.maxRescopes ). On the third time Theia logs the rescope_budget anomaly and discards the task. The --max-rescopes flag changes the limit for one run. In the flag 0 means \"no limit\", while in policy.yaml 0 means \"no rescopes\".\nSee also: lead_rescope, rescope_budget, round, lead.\nlead_rescope\nThe lead rescopes the task: it returns to implement with a new direction. See rescope.\nSee also: rescope, lead.\nlead_accept\nThe lead accepts the work as done, and the task goes to merge. Theia allows this only after a passed check ( verify_pass ). Without one, Theia logs the accept_unverified anomaly and counts the step as no_progress.\nSee also: lead, merge, accept_unverified.\nlead_reverify\nThe lead asks to check the work again without a new implementer step: the task returns to verify. The lead may also replace the check commands.\nSee also: lead, verify.\nlead_discard\nThe lead discards the task. It is the only way to the discarded outcome. Theia also ends up here when the lead has no rescopes left.\nWhere you see it: verdict … lead → lead_discard (1 evidence), task.phase … lead_discard → discarded.\nSee also: discarded, rescope_budget, discard.\nno_progress\nThe step produced nothing. Theia creates this event itself, not the model: the model call failed, the model's reply broke the format twice, the role answered with a word that is not allowed, or the round limit ran out. The task moves to escalated. After three no_progress in a row ( run.stallPauseAfter ) the run pauses with the dispatch_stalled anomaly.\nWhere you see it: verdict … lead → no_progress (0 evidence) “gateway fatal: gateway: transport 'nope' is not manifest-re…”.\nSee also: escalated, dispatch_stalled, environment_failure, illegal_verdict.\nevidence\nEvidence is the references a role attaches to a verdict: a command it ran, a check result, a commit. It is not a retelling but pointers: a kind and a reference. Kinds: command, test_run, diff, file, artifact, gateway_call. For a commit the TUI detail pane suggests git show <sha>.\nWhere you see it: (1 evidence) in a verdict line, TUI rows under a round such as command precheck:… — no commits on the task branch, evidence[command] … in theia diagnose.\nSee also: verdict, test_run, detail.\ntest_run\nAn evidence kind: the result of --verify commands. The reference is a fingerprint of the full output; the output itself is in.theia/objects. In 0.2.201 the TUI v key on such a row says no stored body for this row.\nWhere you see it: TUI test_run 74d01a0c051c… — exit 0: true, evidence[test_run] 74d01a0c… (exit 0: true) in theia diagnose.\nSee also: evidence, verify,.theia.\nrun.created\nThe first event of a run: budget and track, plus plan=<id> for a run from a plan. budget=$null means \"no limit\".\nWhere you see it: xu3xnxmu run.created budget=$10 track=fast_path.\nSee also: budget, track, unmetered.\nrun.finished\nThe last event of a run: halted=<outcome> rounds=<number of verdicts>. A run with this event counts as finished ( finished ). theia run --abandon writes the same event with halted=abandoned.\nWhere you see it: x3qwug7h run.finished halted=completed rounds=6.\nSee also: halted, round, abandoned.\nrun.paused\nThe pause event: rounds=N (resumable). You can continue the run with theia run --resume <runId>.\nWhere you see it: e7s432s2 run.paused rounds=8 (resumable).\nSee also: paused, --resume.\nrun.forked\nThe event at the start of a fork: which run it came from ( from= ), at which event ( at= ), and the reason in quotes.\nWhere you see it: run.forked from=<id> at=<id> “<reason>”, forked from <id> at <id> in theia show.\nSee also: fork.\ntask.created\nThe task creation event: the short task number, the track in brackets and the task text.\nWhere you see it: xeskq65r task.created xk6rzynf [fast_path] add a greeting line to hello.js.\nSee also: task, track, tsk…xk6rzynf.\ntask.phase\nThe phase change event: <task> <event> → <new phase>. Left of the arrow is the event Theia applied; right of it is the phase the task moved to.\nWhere you see it: wlqjldhd task.phase xk6rzynf ready → implement.\nSee also: phase, verdict.\ntask.note\nA service note in the log. The source is in brackets:\n[workspace_base] — the commit the task copy was made from;\n[post_run_maintenance] — cleanup after the run;\n[file_ref] — files included with @;\n[session_prefix] — the fingerprint of the shared prompt part with --sessions;\n[context_alert] and [context_refuse] — the added context is too large;\n[index_auto_build] — an automatic index build before the run.\nTwo notes print in their own way: context injected: … (added rules) and pre-task … N gate(s), M failed (checks before a task starts).\nWhere you see it: x5dhtxgs task.note [workspace_base], TUI workspace @ 209a8f6 (copy) and note post_run_maintenance.\nSee also: note, kind: Context, pre-task, maintenance.\nuser.note\nAn event with your steering note ( [msg] ) or your answers to clarifying questions ( [clarify] ). The TUI marks these rows with ».\nWhere you see it: user.note [msg] <text>, TUI » steer [msg] check the merge precheck first.\nSee also: steering, clarify.\ntool\nA line about an agent action inside the harness: $ is a command, ✎ a file edit, ⚙ another tool.! after the sign means the action failed. While the call runs, the same lines appear marked live.\nWhere you see it: tool $ <command>, tool rows under a model call in the TUI, live $ ….\nSee also: live, gateway call, detail.\nrun.stop_request\nA graceful stop request from another process. The MCP tool run_stop writes it. In 0.2.201 only a resumed run acts on it — see control bus.\nWhere you see it: the run.stop_request line in --plain.\nSee also: control bus, stopping, MCP.\nrun.question\nA clarifying question put on the control bus in --interaction bus mode. theia answer <runId> shows it. In 0.2.201 such questions come only from a resumed run.\nWhere you see it: question <id> (task …xxxxxxxx) in theia answer, N questions waiting in the status line.\nSee also: clarify, control bus, run.answer.\nrun.answer\nAn answer to a question from the control bus. theia answer <runId> --question <id> \"…\" and the MCP tool run_answer write it.\nWhere you see it: answered <id> (…) in theia answer output.\nSee also: run.question, control bus, theia answer.\nrun.changeset\nAn event of a run in a project with several roots: related merges in different repositories landed together. The TUI shows it as changeset (N merges). If the set landed only partly, Theia logs the changeset_partial anomaly.\nSee also: root, kind: Workspace, changeset_partial.\nrun.probed, run.asked\nQuestion records. theia probe stores its question and answer as a separate record with a run.probed event; theia ask does the same with run.asked. The run itself does not change. Such records have no run.finished event, so theia show prints halted: (still open) for them, and the home screen labels them probe and ask.\nWhere you see it: theia show <id> --plain on a question record, home screen · run… probe, the scope list of theia audit verify.\nSee also: probe, ask, (still open), audit chain.\nplan.op and other event names\nSome log events have no line of their own in the output: plan.op is a plan action ( created, approved, changes_requested, context_added ); clock.read and random.draw are recorded clock readings and random numbers for replaying a run; memory.fact, memory.chunk and memory.tombstone are memory records; index.entry and index.build are code index records; generation.published is the publication of a search generation. In --plain and theia show --plain these events are printed as they are: the event id and the type name. You do not need to read them.\nWhere you see it: in their own log scopes ( index, plan, memory), which theia audit verify checks ( index: intact, plan: intact ).\nSee also: event log, plan, memory, search generation, replay.\nStops, pauses and anomalies halted\nhalted is a run's outcome: why it stopped working. Values:\ncompleted — nothing is left to do. It is not success: tasks may have been discarded. See completed.\npaused — the run is paused and can continue. See paused.\nabandoned — the run was closed with theia run --abandon.\nbudget — an internal cap of 1000 verdicts per run was hit. It has nothing to do with money: when the money runs out, the run pauses ( budget_freeze ).\n(still open) — the log has no end: the run is still going, was killed, or it is a probe or ask record.\nThe TUI header shows the same value: ✔ completed, ‖ paused, ✖ stopped (abandoned), ✖ stopped (budget).\nWhere you see it: the summary halted: completed · 4 gateway calls · $0.0000, the event run.finished halted=completed rounds=6, the board — halted: completed.\nSee also: completed, paused, abandoned, (still open), budget, run.finished.\ncompleted\ncompleted is a value of halted: the run has nothing left to do. It does not mean the work succeeded. A run where every task was discarded also ends with halted: completed, and theia run exits with code 1. Check each task's outcome: merged means the work landed, discarded means it was dropped.\nThe TUI still marks such a run with a green check ✔ completed, and the home screen shows ✔ too.\nWhere you see it: halted: completed · 4 gateway calls · $0.0000, TUI ✔ completed · run run…, home screen ✔ run… completed $0.0000.\nSee also: halted, discarded, ✔ ✖ ◆ ◌ ‼ ».\npaused\nA paused run starts no new steps, keeps its log intact and can continue with theia run --resume <runId>. theia run exits with code 0 on a pause. A pause happens:\nafter the first Ctrl+C in the terminal or q in the TUI;\nwhen the budget runs out ( budget_freeze );\nwhen a loop guard fires ( dispatch_stalled, clarify_stalled );\non a run_stop request — only for a resumed run.\nWhere you see it: run.paused rounds=8 (resumable), halted: paused, paused — resume with: theia run --resume run…, TUI ‖ paused, home screen ◆ run… paused.\nSee also: --resume, stopping, budget_freeze, dispatch_stalled, Control a run.\nabandoned\nThe run was closed without a result by theia run --abandon <runId>. Theia appends the run's end halted: abandoned to the log and deletes nothing: task copies stay, and theia prune frees the space. The command does not stop a run that is still working in another terminal: press Ctrl+C there.\nWarning\nIn 0.2.201 theia run --resume does not refuse to continue an abandoned run.\nWhere you see it: abandoned run… — workspaces kept; reclaim with 'theia prune', halted: abandoned, TUI ✖ stopped (abandoned), home screen ✖ run… abandoned.\nSee also: halted, (still open), --resume, theia prune.\n(still open)\nThis is how theia show reports the outcome when the log has neither an end nor a pause. Reasons: the run is still going, it was killed (a second Ctrl+C, a closed terminal window), or it is a probe or ask record. A killed run stays open until you continue it ( --resume ) or close it ( --abandon ).\nWhere you see it: halted: (still open) · 0 gateway calls · $0.0000, home screen ⣽ run… in flight.\nSee also: halted, in flight, hard stop, abandoned.\n--resume\ntheia run --resume <runId> continues a run from where it stopped. Theia reads the log: finished steps are not repeated, an interrupted step starts again, and the budget is recalculated from recorded spending. The word in brackets in the resuming run … line is the state from the log: paused, live (the run was killed without a pause) or finished. A resumed run reads the control bus; a freshly started run does not.\nWhere you see it: resuming run run… (paused; budget 10.00 usd remaining), run branch: theia/run-47z2qh (resumed @ 3c0d7a9), resume: all tasks terminal (finished) — nothing to resume.\nSee also: paused, control bus, budget remaining, Control a run, theia run.\nstopping\nA graceful stop. The first Ctrl+C or q in the TUI asks the run to stop: no new steps start, the current ones finish, then the run pauses.\nWarning\nIn --plain and --cli the running --verify command also receives Ctrl+C from the terminal. It dies, and Theia records a false failure verify_fail with exit null. The TUI does not have this problem.\nWhere you see it: TUI ‖ stopping — in-flight verdicts landing…, stopping gracefully — in-flight verdicts will land; resume with: …, board stopping — finishing in-flight verdicts….\nSee also: paused, hard stop, dispatch boundary, Leave without stopping a task.\nhard stop\nA hard stop is the second Ctrl+C or the second q in the TUI. Theia ends its child processes at once and exits with code 130. The log gets no pause record, so theia show reports halted: (still open), but the run can continue with --resume. The line reaper: swept N … at the end of a normal run means Theia closed leftover processes.\nWhere you see it: hard stop: swept 3 child process(es)/group(s); resume with: theia run --resume … (after a signal; the TUI key path prints nothing).\nSee also: stopping, (still open), --resume.\nanomaly\nAn anomaly is Theia's warning that something went wrong in a run: a guard fired, a model call failed, a reply broke the rules. Each anomaly is a log event with a kind and a class ( taxonomy ). There are eleven classes: reasoning — a model reasoning error, planning — a task-splitting problem, action — a wrong action, memory — a memory problem, environment — an environment failure, task_spec — a bad task statement, security — a security rule broken, communication — a failure in the exchange between roles, trust — a trust violation, emergent — unexpected behaviour, termination — a limit that fired. theia diagnose counts anomalies per class in its taxonomy: line. An anomaly does not stop the run by itself; what happens next depends on the kind. The kinds are described below.\nWhere you see it:!! anomaly stop_and_fix taskId=… fails=3, summary ·!! 2 anomalies, TUI ‼ anomaly <kind> tsk…x ×3, header anomalies 6, taxonomy: reasoning=1 termination=1 in theia diagnose.\nSee also: root cause, ✔ ✖ ◆ ◌ ‼ », event log.\nstop_and_fix\nA guard against endless check failures. After three verify_fail in a row ( run.stopAndFixAfterFailures ) Theia logs the stop_and_fix anomaly and sends the task to the lead with the stop_and_fix event. theia diagnose calls this verification-loop.\nWhere you see it:!! anomaly stop_and_fix taskId=… fails=3, task.phase … stop_and_fix → escalated.\nSee also: verify_fail, escalated, root cause.\nround_budget\nAn anomaly: the task used up its round limit ( run.maxRoundsPerTask, 10 by default). The step counts as no_progress, and the task goes to the lead. The guard does not fire on steps that close the task or ask you ( approve, lead_accept, lead_reverify, clarify ).\nSee also: round, no_progress.\nrescope_budget\nAn anomaly: the lead has no rescopes left for the task ( run.maxRescopes, 2 by default). Theia discards the task with the lead_discard event.\nSee also: rescope, lead_discard.\nfresh_perspective\nA marker anomaly: after two attempts in implement, a fresh implementer ( fresh_implementer ) takes the task. It is not an error but a change of approach.\nWhere you see it:!! anomaly fresh_perspective taskId=… dispatches=2.\nSee also: fresh_implementer.\ndispatch_stalled\nAn anomaly: three steps in a row on one task ended with no_progress ( run.stallPauseAfter ). Usually the environment is failing: no harness login, a wrong route, no network. The run pauses so it does not waste calls. Fix the cause and continue the run with --resume.\nWhere you see it:!! anomaly dispatch_stalled taskId=… consecutive=3, then run.paused rounds=8 (resumable).\nSee also: no_progress, environment_failure, paused.\nenvironment_failure\nAn anomaly: a model call failed because of the environment, not the agent. For example, the transport is not registered, there is no harness login, or the harness process died. The reason is in the reason= field.\nWhere you see it:!! anomaly environment_failure taskId=… role=lead phase=escalated reason=gateway fatal: ….\nSee also: no_progress, dispatch_stalled, TransportRegistry.\nclarify_unanswered\nAn anomaly: nobody answered a clarifying question — there is no terminal, --non-interactive is set, or you declined. The task freezes in escalated, and the lead learns that there was no answer.\nSee also: clarify, clarify_stalled.\nclarify_stalled\nAn anomaly: the questions went nowhere — two unanswered questions in a row, or three answers in a row with no other steps. The run pauses. The next move is yours: send steering or change settings.\nSee also: clarify, paused, steering.\nillegal_verdict\nAn anomaly: a role answered with a word that is not allowed in this phase. The step counts as no_progress. In 0.2.201 the same anomaly also appears when a pre-task check or a context size refusal stopped the task.\nSee also: no_progress, pre-task, kind: Context.\nplanned_without_subtasks\nAn anomaly: the planner answered planned but gave no subtasks. Theia treats the answer as ready, and the task goes to the implementer instead of closing without code.\nSee also: planned, planner.\naccept_unverified\nAn anomaly: the lead tried to accept the work ( lead_accept ) without a passed check. Theia does not accept it and counts the step as no_progress.\nSee also: lead_accept, verify_pass.\ndelegation_refused\nAn anomaly: a role that may not split tasks proposed subtasks. Only the planner and the lead can split a task. No subtasks are created.\nSee also: planner, lead.\ndelegation_depth\nAn anomaly: subtasks are nested too deep. One level is allowed by default ( run.maxDelegationDepth ). No new subtasks are created.\nSee also: delegation_fanout, planned.\ndelegation_fanout\nAn anomaly: one answer proposed more subtasks than allowed ( run.maxDelegationFanout, 4 by default). Theia creates only the first four.\nSee also: delegation_depth, planned.\nreplay_miss\nAn anomaly of the recorded-call replay mode: there is no saved answer for a request. Platform developers use it to check repeatability. It is not related to theia replay, which calls no model.\nSee also: --record, hybrid.\nchangeset_partial\nAn anomaly of a run with several roots: some related merges landed and some did not. Theia either reverts what already landed ( revert, the default) or freezes the tasks ( freeze ), and names the unfinished set. The run.changesetPartialFailure setting chooses.\nSee also: run.changeset, root.\nMoney budget\nThe budget is a run's money limit in US dollars. The default is 10 (the run.maxUsdPerRun setting). The --budget flag changes it, and 0 removes the limit. Theia records the price of each gateway call and subtracts it from the budget.\nWhen the money runs out, the run does not stop for good: it pauses with the budget_exhausted and budget_freeze anomalies and halted: paused. You can continue with a new budget: theia run --resume <runId> --budget <N>.\nThe word also appears in other meanings:\nhalted: budget — not money but the cap of 1000 verdicts per run.\nround_budget and rescope_budget — round and rescope limits of a task.\n--budget of theia fork (default 5), theia probe and theia ask (default 1), theia index build — a separate limit for these commands.\nindexBuildBudgetUsd — the limit for automatic index builds.\nWhere you see it: run.created budget=$10 track=fast_path, $0.0000/$10.00 in the TUI header and on the board, budget remaining: 10.\nSee also: budget remaining, unmetered, budget_freeze, halted, gateway call, Control a run.\nbudget remaining\nbudget remaining: 10 at the end of a run is how many dollars of the budget are left. unmetered means there is no limit. When a run resumes, Theia recalculates the rest from recorded spending and prints it in the resuming run … line.\nWhere you see it: budget remaining: 10, budget remaining: unmetered, resuming run run… (paused; budget 10.00 usd remaining).\nSee also: budget, unmetered, --resume.\nunmetered\nNo money limit: this is what --budget 0 does. Loop guards stay on: round and rescope limits and the verdict cap. The run.created event records the missing limit as budget=$null.\nWhere you see it: budget: 0 = unmetered (loop guards stay: maxTotalRounds, round/rescope budgets), budget remaining: unmetered, board $0.0000 spent.\nSee also: budget, budget remaining, halted.\nbudget_exhausted\nAn anomaly: the gateway refused a model call because the run's budget ran out. budget_freeze follows right after, and the call shows → fatal in --plain.\nWhere you see it:!! anomaly budget_exhausted role=implementer transport=…, gateway fatal: gateway: usd budget exhausted for this run (THE-024).\nSee also: budget_freeze, budget.\nbudget_freeze\nAn anomaly: the run pauses because the money ran out. It appears once per run. Continue with a new budget: theia run --resume <runId> --budget <N>.\nWhere you see it:!! anomaly budget_freeze taskId=… role=implementer, then run.paused … (resumable).\nSee also: budget_exhausted, budget, paused.\ngateway call\nA gateway call is one model request through the gateway. Theia logs every such call with its role, transport, result and price, even a refused one. --verify commands, git merges and answers reused from a recording are not gateway calls.\nWhere you see it: gateway <role>@<transport> → ok $0.0000, summary 4 gateway calls, TUI header 4 calls, TUI · gateway <transport> ok $0.0000.\nSee also: gateway, budget, transport, locked.\nlocked\n$0.0000 locked is the price fixed at the moment of the call. Theia does not recalculate it, even if rates change later.\nWhere you see it: rate: … $0.0000 locked (6ms) in theia index build.\nSee also: gateway call, kind: Pricing.\nkind: Pricing\nAn optional manifest with model rates (dollars per million tokens) and a price basis per transport: costBasis: list or actual. Use it when the default prices do not fit you, for example for a subscription.\nSee also: manifest, locked, Settings files.\nrun.maxUsdPerRun\nThe default run budget in policy.yaml: 10 dollars. The --budget flag wins over it. The setting's name appears in the run --from-plan refusal when the budget is below the plan's cost estimate.\nWhere you see it: run: plan floor ~$… exceeds budget $… — pass --budget N explicitly, raise run.maxUsdPerRun, or --budget 0 (unmetered).\nSee also: budget, cost estimate, policy.yaml settings.\nGit run branch\nThe run branch is the git branch theia/run-<6 characters> where Theia merges finished tasks. The six characters are the end of the run id. The branch starts from the base, and your current branch does not change. A hidden git working copy in.theia/runs/<runId>/trunk holds the branch, so git switch to it fails. Take the work with git merge.\nAt the end of a run Theia says what is on the branch: — N merges ahead of base or — no merges landed (branch left at base for inspection). The number in merges ahead of base counts commits, not merges. The --branch flag sets your own branch name, --base your own base, and --no-run-branch turns the run branch off: merges then go straight into your current branch.\nWhere you see it: run branch: theia/run-esbyig (base HEAD (no origin remote) @ 4fb42d7), run branch theia/run-7adeu2 @ 0d391ae — 2 merges ahead of base; ….\nSee also: base, trunk, ai/<task>, merge, Run results.\nbase\nThe base is the commit the run branch starts from. Theia takes the upstream of your current branch; without one, origin/HEAD; otherwise the local HEAD. Local commits that are not in the upstream yet do not get into the run's work. To include them, pass --base HEAD. The [workspace_base] note and the TUI row workspace @ 209a8f6 (copy) show which commit a task copy was made from.\nWhere you see it: run branch: theia/run-5yrymu (base origin/main @ d2946e5), (base HEAD (no origin remote) @ 4fb42d7).\nSee also: run branch, workspace.\ntrunk\nThe folder.theia/runs/<runId>/trunk is a hidden git working copy where the run branch is checked out. While it exists, git does not let you switch to the run branch or delete it. Before deleting the branch, run git worktree remove.theia/runs/<runId>/trunk.\nSee also: run branch,.theia, prunable, Run results.\nprunable\nA git mark, not a Theia one. git worktree list puts it on a worktree entry whose folder is already gone: for example, after theia prune removed.theia/runs/<runId>/trunk. The entry itself stays in.git/worktrees. git worktree prune removes it; the theia/run-… branch is left alone.\nWhere you see it: ~/projects/app/.theia/runs/2akdq…/trunk abc1234 [theia/run-7adeu2] prunable in git worktree list.\nSee also: trunk, run branch, theia prune, Run results.\nai/<task>\nThe task branch ai/<task> with the task's commits. It appears in your repository only after the task merged, and it stays there: theia prune does not delete it. theia show <runId> --task <id> [--diff] reads it. A discarded task has no such branch, and the command says audit branch ai/… not found … (in-place run, or the branch was pruned); the reason in that message is wrong.\nWhere you see it: untouched: … ai/* audit branches (your repo) in theia prune, the evidence note merge --no-ff ai/<task>.\nSee also: merge, run branch, theia show.\nprecheck\nThe check before a merge: the task copy must have commits after the base and no uncommitted edits. If the check fails, there is no merge, and the evidence reads precheck:<task> with the reason.\nWhere you see it: TUI command precheck:2akdqcea5s223wgiwm7ieg — no commits on the task branch, evidence[command] precheck:… (no commits on the task branch) in theia diagnose.\nSee also: merge_fail, merger.\n--in-place\nA mode with no copy and no git: agents edit files right in your folder, there is no run branch, and merge only marks the work as accepted ( merged ). Theia turns this mode on by itself when the folder is not a git repository. It is not available in a project with several roots.\nWhere you see it: mode: in-place (no git repo or --in-place) — no worktree isolation.\nSee also: workspace, merged, run branch.\nStore and project store\nThe store is Theia's data folder for a project:.theia with a store.db file inside. It holds the event log, plans, memory, the code index, project settings and task copies. Where it lives:\nin a git repository — at the root of the working copy, even if you ran theia init in a subfolder;\noutside git — in the user data folder, and the project folder stays clean;\nwith theia init --here — exactly in the current folder.\nOther commands find the store by walking up from the current folder.\nWhere you see it: store: …/.theia (worktree root), theia: no.theia/ found from <cwd> upward (and no user-registry workspace) — run 'theia init' in the workspace home first.\nSee also:.theia, store.db, worktree root, user-home store, detached, Store and project roots.\n.theia\nThe store folder in a project. Inside:\nstore.db — the database;\nconfig/ — project settings: policy.yaml, registry.yaml and other manifests;\nprompts/ — your additions to role prompts;\nobjects/ — full command output, model requests and replies;\nindex/ — search files;\nruns/ — task copies and run branches;\ncontrol/ — logs of commands started through MCP.\nTheia adds.theia/ to.gitignore. The folder holds task texts and model replies, so treat it as private data. When it grows past 5 GB ( maintenance.storeAlertGb ), a line storage:.theia at … — reclaim with 'theia prune' appears after a run and in theia doctor.\nSee also: store, store.db,.theia store, theia prune.\nstore.db\nThe SQLite database file in the store. It holds the event logs of all runs, plans, memory facts, code index entries and saved model replies. This file is what makes a real store different from a.theia folder with only settings.\nSee also: store, overlay, event log.\nworkspace\nIn Theia this word means different things:\nThe repository copy for a task:.theia/runs/<runId>/ws-<task> on the branch ai/<task>. The copy also gets untracked files such as node_modules and.env, but they are never committed. After a successful merge the copy is deleted; for discarded and stuck tasks it stays. In the TUI: workspace @ 209a8f6 (copy).\nkind: Workspace — the manifest of a project with several roots.\nThe project folder that owns the store (workspace home).\nworkspace-write — the Codex access mode of the codex-write transport: writes only inside the working folder.\nSee also: kind: Workspace, workspace home, --in-place, ai/<task>.\nworkspace home\nThe project folder that owns the store: it contains.theia with store.db. Root paths in the Workspace manifest are relative to it.\nWhere you see it: … — run 'theia init' in the workspace home first.\nSee also: store, kind: Workspace, root.\nworktree root\nThe top folder of a git working copy. By default theia init puts the store there, even when you run it from a subfolder: one store per working copy.\nWhere you see it: store: …/.theia (worktree root), (worktree root — one store per checkout; pass --here to pin it to this subfolder instead).\nSee also: store, detached.\nuser-home store\nA store for a folder outside git. It lives in the user data folder ( stores/<name>-<hash> ), and the project folder stays clean. workspaces.yaml records which store belongs to which folder. The first-run setup wizard does not do this: it always creates.theia in the current folder.\nWhere you see it: store: …/stores/my-notes-dfef89b4 (user-home store — this folder is not a git worktree and stays clean; …).\nSee also: workspaces.yaml, store, setup wizard, Store and project roots.\ndetached\ntheia init --here puts the store exactly in the current folder, not at the working copy root and not in the user folder. Theia calls such a store detached.\nWhere you see it: store: …/.theia (detached — --here pins the store to this folder).\nSee also: store, worktree root.\nworkspaces.yaml\nA file in the user settings folder: which store belongs to which folder outside git. theia init writes it. If a row points to a missing store, Theia skips the row and says so.\nWhere you see it: theia: registry maps <folder> to <store> but no store.db is there — ignoring the row (…).\nSee also: user-home store, user config.\nroot\nIn Theia this word means different things:\nA repository in a project with several roots. It has a short name, and the --root <name> flag narrows index, mem and run to one root. A regular project has one root named root.\nroot:<name> — a memory part written with --root; theia mem search shows it as {root:web}.\nworktree root — the top folder of a git working copy, the default place of the store.\nroot cause — the likely cause of a failure in theia diagnose.\nroot: null in the TUI task details — the task has no separate root.\nWhere you see it: theia: unknown root 'nope' (declared: root).\nSee also: kind: Workspace, scope, worktree root, root cause.\nkind: Workspace\nThe manifest of a project with several roots: several repositories share one store. spec.roots lists the roots (a name and a path from the store folder), and spec.primary names the default root. Run tasks carry a root label, and related merges in different roots land together ( run.changeset ).\nWhere you see it:.theia/config/workspace.yaml from the first-run wizard, multi-root workspace: proj, lib (primary: proj).\nSee also: root, run.changeset, manifest, Store and project roots.\noverlay\nA.theia folder without store.db inside one of the project roots. It holds only that root's own settings and prompts. When looking for the store, Theia skips such a folder and keeps walking up.\nWhere you see it: theia: …/.theia has no store.db — per-root overlay, continuing upward.\nSee also: root, store.db, Store and project roots.\nPlans plan\nThe word has two meanings.\nA plan is a saved task list from theia plan: each task has a key, a track, dependencies and check commands. A plan has a content fingerprint ( digest ) and a status: draft, approved or changes_requested. Only an approved plan can run: theia run --from-plan <planId>. Any edit ( theia plan add-context ) produces a new fingerprint and returns the plan to draft.\nThe plan phase is the step of a full_spec task where the planner works.\nWhere you see it: pln2akdqddd5c2223ubqzsvlt [draft] 2 tasks — … in theia plan list, approved pln… @ fce3554ee5e2…, run run… plan pln… @ fce3554ee5e2… → 2 tasks, task.phase … start → plan.\nSee also: approved, draft, changes_requested, digest, full_spec, Plan before you run, theia plan.\napproved\nThe status of an approved plan. Only such a plan runs with theia run --from-plan. Theia refuses a plan in any other status.\nWhere you see it: run: plan pln… is 'draft', not approved — approve it first (theia plan approve).\nSee also: plan, draft.\nchanges_requested\nA plan status after theia plan request-changes <planId> \"…\": you asked for changes. The plan's fingerprint stays the same, and your request shows in theia plan show as a note: line.\nWhere you see it: changes requested on pln….\nSee also: plan, approved.\nOPEN QUESTIONS\nThe planner has questions, and they cannot be answered in the terminal, for example because of --non-interactive. Theia saves a draft plan with a single unresolved item and prints the questions. Add your answer with theia plan add-context <planId> \"…\", then create the plan again or approve it.\nWhere you see it: OPEN QUESTIONS — plan saved as draft pln…:, answer with: theia plan add-context pln… \"…\" — then re-run theia plan or approve.\nSee also: plan, clarify, unresolved.\nunresolved\nThe key of the placeholder task in a plan draft that was saved with open questions ( OPEN QUESTIONS ). Its description starts with Resolve open questions, then re-plan: …. It is not a task to run but a reminder: answer the questions with theia plan add-context <planId> \"…\" and create the plan again. Such a draft is not meant for approval.\nWhere you see it: unresolved in theia plan show <planId> after OPEN QUESTIONS — plan saved as draft pln….\nSee also: OPEN QUESTIONS, plan, draft, Plan before you run.\nverify pre-flight\nA trial run of a plan's check commands on the base commit, before approval. It is off by default: run.planVerifyPreflight: disable. A command that fails at the base is fine when the task is meant to fix it. But if the command itself is broken, for example a tool is missing, fix it before you approve.\nWhere you see it: verify pre-flight: off (opt in with policy run.planVerifyPreflight: always).\nSee also: plan, verify.\ncost estimate\nA plan's cost estimate from past runs in this store: number of tasks × average task price. Without run history there is no estimate. In theia run --from-plan the same estimate is called the plan floor: when the default budget is below it, the start is refused before the first model call.\nWhere you see it: cost estimate: ~$… (N tasks × $… avg/task from recorded runs), cost estimate: no run history — no estimate.\nSee also: budget, plan, plan floor, run.maxUsdPerRun.\nplan floor\nThe lowest cost estimate of a plan run: the average task price across this store's earlier paid runs × the number of plan tasks. theia run --from-plan compares the budget with it before the first model call. When the budget is the default ( run.maxUsdPerRun ) and lies below the floor, the start is refused with code 1: run: plan floor ~$… exceeds budget $… — pass --budget N explicitly, raise run.maxUsdPerRun, or --budget 0 (unmetered). When the budget comes from the --budget flag, Theia only warns ( run: WARNING — plan floor … ) and goes on: expect a budget pause. Without a history of paid runs there is no estimate and no check.\nWhere you see it: run: plan floor ~$12.50 (5 tasks × $2.50 avg/task from this store's recorded runs) exceeds budget $10 — …, run: WARNING — plan floor … proceeding on your explicit --budget.\nSee also: cost estimate, budget, unmetered, theia run --from-plan.\ntrack override\ntheia run --from-plan <planId> --track <track> puts every plan task on one track, for this run only. The plan and its fingerprint do not change.\nWhere you see it: track override: all 2 tasks forced to full_spec for this run (plan content unchanged).\nSee also: track, plan.\nSteering and questions steering\nSteering is your message to a run while it works: what to take into account, what to change. Theia records it as a user.note [msg] event and adds it to the shared notes (Σ) that the next steps receive. Ways to steer:\nthe m key in the TUI: the note reaches the run in the same process at once. The input line names the addressee: steer → tsk…x ▸ (the task under the cursor) or steer → run ▸ (the whole run);\ntheia msg <runId> \"…\"; with --task <id>, for one task only;\ntheia fork <runId> --with \"…\" — steering for a fork.\nTheia reroutes a note for a finished or unknown task to the whole run and says so.\nWarning\ntheia msg from another terminal reaches a paused run (when it resumes) and a resumed run. A freshly started run does not read the note, although theia msg replies noted — this run appears live …. See control bus.\nWhere you see it: noted for task …fd7pn6tz — folds into its guidance only, noted — folds into guidance on: theia run --resume run…, task …xk6rzynf is terminal — rerouted to run-level guidance (…), TUI » steer [msg] ….\nSee also: control bus, user.note, sigma, Control a run, theia msg.\ncontrol bus\nThe control bus is a way to control a run from another process without a separate server. A command or tool appends an event to the run's log, and the run reads new events between steps. theia msg, theia answer, the --interaction bus question mode and the MCP tools run_msg, run_answer, run_stop work this way.\nWarning\nIn 0.2.201 only a resumed run reads the bus — one started with theia run --resume <runId>. A run started with theia run --task … or --from-plan, and every run started by the MCP tool run_start, does not read it. The events land in the log, but the run carries on as if they were not there: theia msg, theia answer, --interaction bus and run_stop have no effect on it. The replies noted — this run appears live: … and honored at the next dispatch boundary are misleading in this case.\nWhat works: the m and q keys in the TUI of the same process, Ctrl+C in the run's terminal, and bus commands after theia run --resume <runId>. The steps are in Control a run.\nWhere you see it: noted — this run appears live: it folds cross-process notes at its next dispatch boundary …, N questions waiting in the status line.\nSee also: steering, run.stop_request, run.question, dispatch boundary, MCP, Control a run.\ndispatch boundary\nThe moment between a run's steps. At this moment the run checks for a stop request and new steering. A step already in progress, including a check command, finishes first. Steering does not get into a model call that is already running.\nWhere you see it: honored at the next dispatch boundary (in-flight work lands first) (the run_stop reply), … at its next dispatch boundary (the theia msg reply).\nSee also: control bus, stopping, steering.\nclarify\nA clarifying question ( clarify ) means a role cannot go on without your answer. Only the planner and the lead can ask, and each question comes with a reason. Where the question appears:\nin the TUI — a form in the detail pane: clarify — task …x (1/2) and the input line answer ▸. Enter goes to the next question, an empty answer skips, Esc declines the rest;\nin a plain terminal — the line the planner needs answers before proceeding: (the lead's questions too) and? … questions;\nin --interaction bus mode — a run.question event, answered with theia answer.\nTheia saves answers as memory facts Q: … A: … so it does not ask again. Without an answer the task freezes in escalated ( clarify_unanswered ). A second unanswered question in a row pauses the run ( clarify_stalled ). With --non-interactive the question stays unanswered at once.\nSee also: clarify_unanswered, clarify_stalled, run.question, --non-interactive, fact.\nprobe\nA probe is a one-off question to a model about a run's state: theia probe <runId> \"…\". The run does not change: Theia writes only a separate run.probed record. The question is one model call with a default limit of 1 dollar ( --budget ). Asking the same question again is answered for free from the recording. theia prune does not delete such records.\nWhere you see it: [probe run2akdqbfu2w223occvaxl3w of run2akdqb2i4k223pwvesbyig @ …bjr37ync — $0.0000; parent untouched], home screen · run… probe.\nSee also: none, ask, fork, theia probe.\nInspecting runs audit chain\nThe audit chain protects the log from tampering. Each event stores a fingerprint of the previous one, and Theia checks that the chain is unbroken. intact means the log is whole; BROKEN means it was changed after writing. A run with a broken chain cannot be resumed or forked.\ntheia audit verify checks the chains of all records: runs, probe and ask questions, plans and the index. In 0.2.201 it also prints intact for a name that does not exist.\nWhere you see it: audit chain: intact in the summary, 2akdqb2i4k223pwvesbyig: intact and index: intact in theia audit verify, TUI ‼ audit chain BROKEN at ….\nSee also: event log, replay, theia audit verify.\nreplay\nReplay ( replay ): theia replay <runId> reads the run's log again and recalculates the outcome. No task runs, no model is called, nothing changes. The command prints the number of events and the chain state, the final task phases, the number of verdicts and calls, and the shared notes sigma: {…}. If the chain is broken, the exit code is 1.\nWhere you see it: events: 22; chain: intact, verdicts: 6; gateway calls: 4, sigma: {}.\nSee also: audit chain, sigma, fork, theia replay.\nfork\nA fork is a new run that copies the original run's log up to a chosen event and then goes another way. The command: theia fork <runId> --at <event> --with \"…\". --at is a verdict or task.phase event; the short number from show --plain works. --with is steering for the new path. Start the fork with theia run --resume <new id>. A fork's default budget is 5 dollars.\nWarning\nA fork of a finished run made without --at cannot continue: all its tasks are already finished.\nA resumed fork merges its work into your current branch, not into a run branch.\nWhere you see it: forked run… @ bjr37ync → run…, drive it: theia run --resume run… (gateway: hybrid — prefix replays free), forked from … at … and fork → … at … (<reason>) in theia show, home screen forked·<outcome>.\nSee also: hybrid, run.forked, post-baseline, Run results, theia fork.\nhybrid\nA gateway mode for forks, probe and ask. If an answer to the same request is already saved, Theia reuses it for free; new requests go to the model and are saved. The words prefix replays free on a fork only mean that the copied part is not run again. Saved answers of the original run exist only if it ran with --record.\nWhere you see it: (gateway: hybrid — prefix replays free), fork resume: gateway in hybrid mode (recorded prefix replays free).\nSee also: --record, fork.\n--record\nThe theia run --record flag saves every model reply in the store, so it can be reused later without a call. It is mainly for experiments with forks. theia prune does not delete saved replies.\nWhere you see it: untouched: unfinished runs, gateway recordings (request-keyed, shared), … in theia prune.\nSee also: hybrid, fork, replay_miss.\npost-baseline\nSpending after a run's starting point. For a fork it is spending after the split: the copied part does not count. This is how theia compare compares the cost of two runs.\nWhere you see it: cost A (post-baseline): $0.0000.\nSee also: fork, theia compare.\nroot cause\nThe likely cause of a failure that theia diagnose names from a rule table, without a model. Values:\nverification-loop — the check failed again and again ( stop_and_fix );\nreview-loop — the reviewer repeated an objection while checks passed;\nrejected-by-review — the task was discarded with no anomalies: the work was done but judged wrong;\ncapability-gap — the implementer answered cant_do;\nbudget-exhaustion — the money ran out;\nconfused-runner — a role broke the reply format again and again;\nrunaway-decomposition — the task was split beyond the limits;\ninconclusive — no rule matched.\ntheia diagnose saves the result as a memory fact. A repeat of the same set of signs is marked (fingerprint seen before — reused).\nWhere you see it: root cause: rejected-by-review, why: …, fingerprint: 0b0ec207de4ff4b3… memory: 2akdqbfsxs223o53kulbqg.\nSee also: anomaly, fact, theia diagnose.\nCode index intent index\nThe code index is a set of short cards about project files. A model (the indexer role) writes each file's card once, and the card is reused until the file changes. Only new and changed files cost money. theia index build builds the index, and the cards live in store.db. Theia gives them to agents in every request as the blueprint.\nNew cards are not visible to search ( theia index search, theia ask ) until a new search generation is built with theia index reindex.\nWhere you see it: index: 0 written, 0 current, 1 failed, 2 model calls, rate: … zero-cost (0%) … $0.0000 locked, theia index status.\nSee also: index entry, blueprint, stale, search generation, Code index, memory and questions.\nindex entry\nAn index entry is one file's card. Its line looks like this: path[layer/module/importance]: F:… | R:… | A:… | S:….\nlayer — the architectural layer: app, domain, adapter, config, test and others;\nmodule — the business area of the file;\nimportance — from 9 (core) to 1 (peripheral);\nF — what the file is for, R — related files, A — its public API, S — decisions, constraints and pitfalls.\nWhere you see it: hello.js[script/demo/1]: F:Prints hello to stdout. | R:- | A:- | S:No logic; smoke file. in theia index render, F: and S: lines in theia index search.\nSee also: intent index, blueprint, indexer.\nblueprint\nThe blueprint is all index cards as one text: the most important first, with a legend at the top. Theia adds the blueprint to every agent request. You can see it with theia index render. The Claude Code and Codex hooks add its first 40 lines at session start ( Repo map (head): ).\nThe run.blueprintMinImportance setting leaves cards below a given importance out of requests. Theia reports this with a blueprint floor: … line.\nWhere you see it: Repo intent map — one line per code unit: path[layer/module/importance]: F:… | R:… | A:… | S:…, blueprint floor: importance ≥N — injecting K of M entries (…).\nSee also: intent index, index entry, <theia-context>.\nstale\nA stale index entry: the file changed after its card was written, or Theia's index builder version changed. Theia checks index freshness without a model, by file fingerprints.\nNote: the number in the warning index: N stale — plan quality degrades; … at the start of a run and in the TUI hint idx ⚠N adds stale and missing ( missing ) entries together. That is why you can see index: 1 stale while theia index status lists the file only under missing.\nStatuses in theia index status: current — the card is up to date, stale — outdated, missing — no card, orphaned — the file is gone. index is fresh means there are no stale, missing or orphaned entries.\nWhere you see it: index: 1 stale — plan quality degrades; run 'theia index build' to refresh, index: 1 stale — the map may lag this run's tree (in theia show ).\nSee also: missing, orphaned, current, dirty-marks, idx ⚠N, indexPolicy.\nmissing\nA file tracked by git that has no index card yet. It counts toward index: N stale. theia index build creates the card.\nWhere you see it: missing (1): and the file list in theia index status, theia: 0 indexed · 1 missing.\nSee also: stale, intent index.\norphaned\nA card for a file that is no longer tracked: it was deleted, renamed or removed from git. A full theia index build deletes such cards.\nWhere you see it: orphaned (1): in theia index status, pruned N orphaned entries (files left the tree) after theia index build.\nSee also: stale.\ncurrent\nAn up-to-date card: the file has not changed since the card was written. Such a file costs nothing during an index build.\nWhere you see it: entries: 4 (3 current) in theia index status, index: 0 written, 1 current, 0 failed.\nSee also: stale, intent index.\ndirty-marks\nMarks left by the post-commit git hook: which files changed since the last index build. The hook from theia hooks install git writes them. They are only a hint: the fingerprint comparison is the truth about freshness. The marks live in.theia/index/dirty-paths and are cleared after a clean full build.\nWhere you see it: dirty-marks: 2 paths since last build (post-commit hook), theia: … · 2 marked dirty.\nSee also: stale, hooks, Git.\nsearch generation\nA search generation is a published snapshot of search files: a word index and, when embeddings work, a vector index. theia index reindex builds it from index cards, and theia mem reindex from memory. Search reads the latest published generation, so new cards are not visible until a new one is built. lexical-only is a generation without vectors. Cleanup deletes old generations ( orphan generations GCed ).\nWhere you see it: generation 2akdqci45k223ouugqzblp: 0 entries, published=true (embeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2/384), head 6m old.\nSee also: embeddings, head, lane, maintenance.\nhead\nThe word head means different things:\nhead 6m old in theia index status — the age of the published search generation;\n[@ event 22/22 · head] in the TUI — you are looking at the latest log event;\nHEAD in base HEAD (no origin remote) — the current git commit.\nSee also: search generation, [@ event k/N], base.\nindexPolicy\nThe maintenance.indexPolicy setting in policy.yaml: how to keep the index fresh.\nalert (the default) — only warn at the start of a run or plan;\nauto — before a run or plan, in theia serve and at agent session start, rebuild stale cards within indexBuildBudgetUsd (1 dollar). These are model calls;\nmanual — stay quiet and skip cleanup after a run.\nWhere you see it: index: N stale — plan quality degrades; building (budget $1)…, (index: stale — background build started; indexPolicy=auto).\nSee also: stale, maintenance, policy.yaml settings.\nmaintenance\nCleanup after a run: memory summaries are refreshed and old search generations are deleted. No model is called. Cleanup is skipped with indexPolicy: manual. theia mem maintain does the same by hand.\nWhere you see it: maintenance: 0 summaries refreshed, 0 orphan generations GCed, task.note [post_run_maintenance], maintain: 0 summaries refreshed (0 empty), 0 orphan generations GCed (0 artifacts removed).\nSee also: search generation, memory, indexPolicy.\nMemory and search memory\nMemory is facts and notes about the project in the store: decisions, constraints, agreements. There are two kinds of records: facts ( fct… ) and text chunks ( chk… ). Nothing is overwritten: a new fact replaces an old one, and the old one stays in history. theia mem search, theia ask and the Claude Code and Codex hooks read memory. Theia writes answers to clarifying questions to memory by itself.\nWhere you see it: added fct2akdqbd5ag223ebx7s4jf2 after theia mem add, - [decision] … in the <theia-context> block.\nSee also: fact, chunk, supersede, scope, lane, Code index, memory and questions.\nfact\nA fact is one short statement in memory. A fact has a kind: decision, constraint, commitment, observation, entity, preference. Its id starts with fct. Add a fact with theia mem add <kind> <text>. In 0.2.201 secrets in a fact's text are not hidden, so do not put keys or passwords in facts.\nWhere you see it: 2.0164 [lexical-scan+recency] fct2akdqbd5ag223ebx7s4jf2 we use tabs for indentation, - [decision] …, [sources] fct:… in theia ask.\nSee also: memory, supersede, chunk, theia mem add.\nchunk\nA piece of text saved in memory word for word, for example a transcript tail from a hook. Its id starts with chk. Keys, card numbers and similar secrets in it are replaced with placeholders. Add a chunk by hand with theia mem chunk \"…\".\nWhere you see it: chunk chk…, - [chunk] … in the <theia-memory> block, chk:… in theia ask.\nSee also: memory, checkpoint, masked.\nsupersede\nSuperseding a fact: a new fact replaces an old one. The old fact disappears from normal search but stays in history and shows in theia mem asof at a moment before the replacement. It happens automatically when a new fact nearly repeats an old one, or by hand: theia mem supersede <old> <new>.\nWhere you see it: added fct… (superseded fct…), superseded <old> -> <new>.\nSee also: fact, asof.\ncheckpoint\nA checkpoint is the tail of an agent transcript (up to 16 KB) that a hook saves as a memory chunk before context compaction or at the end of a session. Labels: compact-checkpoint and session-final. The Claude Code Stop hook fires after every reply, so such chunks pile up quickly.\nWhere you see it: theia mem checkpoint and theia mem checkpoint --final in hook settings.\nSee also: hooks, chunk, fail-open.\nscope\nThe word scope means different things:\nA part of memory: shared by the project ( default ) or one root ( root:<name>, shown as {root:web} ). theia diagnose writes its results to the diagnostics part.\nA settings level: built-in values, user settings, project settings, root settings. The level closest to the task wins.\nA chain name in theia audit verify: a run, question or plan id, plus index and plan.\nSee also: root, memory, manifest, audit chain.\nasof\ntheia mem asof <id> <query> searches memory as it was at the moment of the record with that id. It needs a Theia id ( fct…, run… and others), not a date.\nWhere you see it: terryid: neither a body nor a full id: '2099-01-01' when you pass a date.\nSee also: supersede, terryid.\nlane\nA search method. Theia shows in square brackets which methods found a result:\nbm25 — word search in the search generation;\nvector — meaning search with embeddings;\nlexical-scan — word search right in memory records;\nentry-scan — the same over index cards when there is no generation yet;\nsymbol — files that declare a name from the query;\nrecency — the newest memory records, always added;\ntime — records with a date from the query.\nThe number before the brackets grows with the number of methods that found the record. Because of recency, memory search almost always finds something.\nWhere you see it: 2.0164 [lexical-scan+recency] fct… …, 1.0164 [entry-scan] src/calc.ts [domain/billing/8], theia: vector lane off — <reason>.\nSee also: embeddings, search generation, memory.\nembeddings\nEmbeddings (numeric meaning vectors) power meaning-based search. By default a local model all-MiniLM-L6-v2 of about 23 MB computes them: it is free and works offline, but it downloads on first use. The other option is the openai provider: it needs an OpenAI key ( OPENAI_API_KEY or theia setup --openai-key ) and costs money. Runs do not need embeddings.\nVectors from one model are not searched with another. After a model change Theia prints vector lane off and searches by words only until you rebuild search.\nWhere you see it: embeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d) — not fetched (~23 MB; …) in theia doctor, theia: vector lane off — ….\nSee also: autoDownload, lane, search generation, Keys and access.\nautoDownload\nThe gateway.embeddings.autoDownload setting: whether Theia may download the local embedding model by itself. The default is true. With false and no model, theia doctor reports FAIL; download the model with theia setup --fetch-embeddings.\nWhere you see it: embeddings: local/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d) — FAIL (not fetched and autoDownload is off — theia setup --fetch-embeddings).\nSee also: embeddings, FAIL.\nask\ntheia ask \"question\" answers a question about the project from memory, the code index and the three latest runs. It is one model call (role none, default limit 1 dollar), followed by a list of sources. ask answers without a model call, for free, only when nothing at all was found. File inclusion with @ does not work here.\nWhere you see it: [sources] fct:2akdqbd5ag223ebx7s4jf2 run:2akdqb2i4k223pwvesbyig, [ask run2akdqbfumk223ofyzfk5ix — $0.0000], home screen · run… ask.\nSee also: memory, lane, none, probe, theia ask.\n<theia-context>\nThe text block that the session-start hook ( theia mem context ) adds to Claude Code or Codex: the head of the blueprint and the latest memory facts. An index freshness line follows the block.\nWhere you see it: Recent project memory: inside the block, (index freshness: theia: 0 indexed · 1 missing) after it.\nSee also: hooks, blueprint, <theia-memory>.\n<theia-memory>\nThe block that the prompt-submit hook ( theia mem recall ) adds to your message in Claude Code or Codex: memory records similar to the request. If nothing is found or the message is shorter than 8 characters, there is no block.\nSee also: hooks, memory, <theia-context>.\nfail-open\nAn error in a hook does not break the agent session: Theia writes it to standard error and ends the hook with code 0. theia mem context, theia mem recall and theia mem checkpoint work this way.\nWhere you see it: theia mem recall (fail-open): <message>.\nSee also: hooks, checkpoint.\nSettings and models Policy, policy.yaml\nThe policy is the main project settings file:.theia/config/policy.yaml, a kind: Policy manifest. Sections: run (runs and limits), gateway (routing, models, embeddings), ui (default view), maintenance (index freshness), memory (memory search). theia init writes the file only if it does not exist, and running theia init again does not change it.\nValidation is strict: a typo in a key stops every command with a message like config: policy.yaml: invalid manifest (spec.run: unrecognized_keys). The message names the section but not the key. Settings from different files merge one level deep: a gateway.routing map from a closer file replaces the whole map.\nSee also: manifest, gateway.routing, policy.yaml settings, Settings files.\nmanifest\nA manifest is a Theia YAML settings file with the fields apiVersion: theia.dev/v1alpha1, kind, metadata and spec. Kinds: Policy, TransportRegistry, Workspace, Context, Pricing. Manifests live in the project's.theia/config/. The user settings folder ( config/ ) accepts only Policy, Pricing and TransportRegistry. Theia reads.yaml and.yml files in alphabetical order.\nSee also: Policy, policy.yaml, TransportRegistry, kind: Workspace, kind: Context, kind: Pricing, Settings files.\nTransportRegistry\nThe list of transports allowed for use:.theia/config/registry.yaml. A route to a transport that is not in the list fails. The fake transport is always allowed. Each row has the fields name, provider, dataClasses and owner; in 0.2.201 only name affects anything. The default list does not include the codex-full transport.\nWhere you see it: gateway fatal: gateway: transport 'nope' is not manifest-registered (docs/11 §6).\nSee also: transport, gateway.routing, data class, sandbox tier.\ngateway\nThe gateway is the single door that every model request goes through. It checks the text for secrets, picks the transport by routing, watches the budget, retries a request after a temporary error, fixes the price and logs the call.\nWhere you see it: the line gateway <role>@<transport> → ok $0.0000, the summary N gateway calls, the error gateway: no route for role '…' (set gateway.routing).\nSee also: gateway call, gateway.routing, transport, data class, budget.\ngateway.routing\nRouting: which transport each role uses. It is a map in policy.yaml; the \"*\" line applies to every role without its own line. Without a line, fresh_implementer uses the same transport as implementer. theia init --harness claude or --harness codex writes a ready map. Theia silently ignores a typo in a role name.\nWhere you see it: initialized …/.theia (harness: codex — switch any time: gateway.routing in.theia/config/policy.yaml), gateway: no route for role 'reviewer' (set gateway.routing).\nSee also: transport, gateway.models, role, Models for tasks.\ngateway.models\nThe model for a role: a \"role → model id\" map in policy.yaml. Theia passes the model to the harness ( --model for Claude Code, -m for Codex) and does not check the name: a wrong name fails inside the harness. Without a line for the role, the transport's default model works.\nSee also: gateway.routing, transport, Models for tasks.\ntransport\nA transport is a named way to call a model: which harness, with which permissions and which default model. Built-in transports: codex, codex-terra, codex-write, codex-jailed, codex-full, claude, claude-write, claude-index and the test transport fake. For example, claude-write can edit files, while claude works without file-editing tools. In --plain the transport follows @: reviewer@claude. @blocked means the secret check stopped the call.\nSee also: harness, gateway.routing, TransportRegistry, @blocked, Models for tasks.\nharness\nA harness is an external CLI agent that the model works through: Claude Code ( claude ) or Codex ( codex ). The harness uses the subscription you signed in to in the CLI itself; Theia does not use an Anthropic API key for Claude Code. theia init --harness claude or --harness codex picks the harness family, and routing sets the details. fake is a test harness with no model, for developers; replies like fake-green … come only from it.\nWhere you see it: codex subscription: …, claude code: … and harnesses: FAIL (…) in theia doctor.\nSee also: transport, gateway.routing, harnesses, Models for tasks, Keys and access.\nkind: Context\nA manifest with rules and reference texts that Theia adds to agent requests as text. An entry's source is a string, a file or a path pattern; it applies always, or for chosen roles, tracks or paths. theia init creates context-rules.yaml by itself when it finds rules files such as.cursorrules. Write file paths relative to the project: an absolute path does not work.\nIf the added text is larger than run.contextAlertChars (200,000 characters), Theia warns but loads everything. The hard limit run.contextRefuseChars is not set by default.\nWhere you see it: context injected: … in --plain, TUI context injected (N entries), context alert: task … injects N chars (> contextAlertChars=200000) — loading everything.\nSee also: ai rules, ctx ⚠N, manifest, Rules and prompts.\npre-task\nCommands from run.preTask that Theia runs before the implementer's first step. If a required command ( mustPass ) fails, the task freezes before any model call and waits for the lead.\nWhere you see it: pre-task <task> N gate(s), M failed, TUI pre-task gates: N run, M failed.\nSee also: illegal_verdict, Policy, policy.yaml, policy.yaml settings.\nrun.rulesSoT\nThe \"rules only through Theia\" mode. In task copies, rules files ( AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md,.cursorrules and others) are replaced with a stub, and Codex does not read its project files. AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md themselves do not reach requests then: add them with a Context manifest. The mode is off by default.\nSee also: kind: Context, ai rules, Rules and prompts.\nSecurity and sandbox data class\nA data class says how risky it is to send text to a model. The gateway checks every request with simple patterns:\nprohibited — must not be sent: private keys, API keys ( sk-…, AKIA…, ghp_… ), card numbers, seed phrases. The call stops.\nrestricted — may be sent only masked: email addresses become [EMAIL].\npermitted — the text goes as is.\nThe patterns do not catch passwords, phone numbers, names or keys of other shapes. A brief with a secret is still stored in the store. The dataClasses field in registry.yaml has no effect in 0.2.201.\nSee also: masked, @blocked, gateway.maskOverrides, @path, Keys and access.\nmasked\nA masked fragment: the gateway replaced risky text with a placeholder before sending — [EMAIL], [API KEY], [CARD NUMBER], [PRIVATE KEY] or [SEED PHRASE]. The number of replacements shows in the call data ( masked={\"email\":1} ) and in the header of an included file ( masked email×1 ).\nSee also: data class, @blocked, @path.\n@blocked\nThe secret check stopped the call: the text contains prohibited data. In --plain the transport is replaced by blocked, and the verdict comment names the pattern that fired. If every role is blocked this way, the run pauses.\nWhere you see it: gateway implementer@blocked → fatal, gateway fatal: gateway: prohibited content (api_key) — call blocked (THE-014).\nSee also: data class, masked, gateway.maskOverrides.\ngateway.maskOverrides\nExceptions for secret patterns in policy.yaml. For a pattern you can set off (do not check), restricted (mask but do not stop) or prohibited (stop). Pattern names: pem_private_key, api_key, card_number, seed_phrase, email. Use it when a pattern is wrong on your data, for example when it takes long numbers for card numbers.\nSee also: data class, masked, policy.yaml settings.\nsandbox tier\nThe sandbox tier says how much freedom agents have on your machine. The run.sandboxTier setting:\nstandard (the default) — the agent writes only in the task copy, and Theia does the git work;\njailed-full — full access inside the theia jail sandbox; in 0.2.201 it works on Linux only;\ntrusted-full — full access to the whole machine with no sandbox. It needs a codex-full transport row in registry.yaml, and Theia prints a loud warning.\nIn jailed-full and trusted-full the implementers always work through Codex.\nWhere you see it:!! sandboxTier trusted-full: implementer agents run with FULL HOST ACCESS, unjailed (manifest-recorded opt-in), jailed-full tier unavailable in theia doctor.\nSee also: jail, posture, TransportRegistry, Sandbox.\nposture\nPosture is Theia's working word for a chosen set of settings. Most often it means the sandbox tier: how much agents may change the system. The word appears in theia help ( seeded posture for theia eval demo create ) and in platform benchmark options. There is no setting with this name.\nSee also: sandbox tier, Sandbox.\njail\nA sandbox for a command: theia jail -- <command> runs the command with the system read-only, writable only in allowed folders. On Linux it works through bwrap; there is also a container option (Docker or Podman). There is no built-in sandbox for macOS in 0.2.201.\nWhere you see it: jail: unavailable — no sandbox backend on darwin yet (…); use standard or trusted-full, the jail (none ) and jail (container ) lines in theia doctor.\nSee also: sandbox tier, Sandbox, theia jail.\nrun.resources\nMemory, CPU and process-count limits for a run's commands and agents: memoryMax, cpuQuota, tasksMax. They work only on Linux through systemd. On another system a run with these limits refuses to start.\nWhere you see it: resources: unavailable (run.resources needs linux cgroups (systemd-run); platform is darwin) — ….\nSee also: jail, Sandbox.\nInstall and update PAT\nA GitHub PAT (personal access token) is the key to Theia's private release repository. It is needed for theia self update, for downloading versions with --x-release and for the daily update check. Everyday work does not need it. It is stored in config.yaml ( github.pat ). The THEIA_GH_TOKEN variable wins over the file, and Theia does not read GH_TOKEN. Theia never prints the token value.\nWhere you see it: github pat: present or absent (release download/self update unavailable) in theia doctor, self update: no GitHub PAT — theia setup --pat <token> (or THEIA_GH_TOKEN).\nSee also: user config, is available, channel, Keys and access.\nchannel\nThe release channel decides which versions theia self update follows. release (the default) means regular releases. prerelease adds test builds -pre.N, for platform developers. theia setup --channel sets it, and it is stored in config.yaml ( update.channel ).\nWhere you see it: prerelease channel (update.channel) — resolving against -pre builds too, already up to date (v0.2.201).\nSee also: PAT, user config, Update and pick a version.\nvendor deliverables\nVendor deliverables are the set of files shipped with Theia: role prompts ( prompts/kernel.yaml ), rules and skills. In 0.2.201 only the prompts are used; nothing reads the shipped rules and skills. theia doctor checks the files by fingerprint, and theia self update updates them. Without these files run, plan, ask and other model commands do not work.\nWhere you see it: vendor deliverables: ok — 9ff60d108678 verified (21 files); matches this release, theia: no vendor deliverables found (…).\nSee also: doctor, Update and pick a version, Rules and prompts.\nuser config\nThe personal settings file config.yaml in the user settings folder: the GitHub token ( github.pat ), the OpenAI key ( openai.apiKey ), the release channel ( update.channel ) and a developer path ( dev.root ). Mode 0600: only the owner can read the file. theia setup and the first-run wizard write it; theia setup drops keys it does not know. The folder is ~/.config/theia on Linux. On macOS it is ~/Library/Application Support/theia; if that folder does not exist but ~/.config/theia does, Theia uses ~/.config/theia.\nWhere you see it: user config: present or absent (<path> — theia setup) in theia doctor, wrote github.pat to <path> (mode 0600).\nSee also: PAT, channel, setup wizard, Settings files.\nsetup wizard\nThe first-run setup wizard. It opens instead of the home screen when you run theia with no command in a terminal and there are no personal settings or no store yet. It asks for the GitHub token and the OpenAI key (you can skip them), offers to create a store in the current folder, choose a harness and add other roots. Unlike theia init, the wizard always creates.theia in the current folder, even in a repository subfolder.\nWhere you see it: theia setup — Enter accepts the default, empty answers skip., initialize a theia workspace in …? [Y/n], done — try: theia doctor, then theia run --task \"…\".\nSee also: home screen, store, user config, First setup.\nis available\nA dim line about a new version: theia v<new> is available (running v<yours>) — theia self update. Theia checks for updates at most once a day, only with a GitHub token, and for at most 1.5 seconds. The check stays silent on any error. theia version does not run the check.\nSee also: PAT, channel, Update and pick a version.\n--x-release\nThe global flag --x-release <version> (or the THEIA_X_RELEASE variable) runs the command with another Theia version from the cache, downloading it if needed. If the version cannot be obtained, the command fails instead of running the current version. A wrong THEIA_X_RELEASE value breaks every command, even theia version.\nSee also: PAT, Update and pick a version, Global flags.\n--dev\nA flag for platform developers: run Theia from a source tree, at the path after the flag or at dev.root in ~/.config/theia/config.yaml. An installed version has no source tree, so the flag stops the command with code 1 and prints --dev: no development root — pass a path (theia --dev <path> …) or set dev.root in ~/.config/theia/config.yaml (theia setup --dev-root <path>). It does not print the help. With a foreign path the answer differs: --dev: <path> does not look like a theia dev tree (missing apps/theia/src/main.ts).\nSee also: --x-release, For platform developers, Global flags.\nIntegrations hooks\nHooks are short commands that Claude Code, Codex or git run by themselves on events. theia hooks install sets them up:\nclaude — hooks for session start ( theia mem context ), message submit ( theia mem recall ), context compaction and stop ( theia mem checkpoint ), a status line, the MCP server in.mcp.json and the /theia skill;\ncodex — the same memory hooks in.codex/hooks.json and the MCP server;\ngit — a post-commit hook that leaves marks for the index.\nThere is no uninstall command; remove hooks by hand. Hooks call Theia by the full path of the installed version, so after an update they keep running the old version.\nWhere you see it: installed post-commit dirty-marks hook → …/.git/hooks/post-commit, statusline wired: theia index status --brief, mcp server registered: theia serve → …/.mcp.json (control tools included).\nSee also: MCP, /theia skill, statusLine, dirty-marks, fail-open, Claude Code, Codex, theia hooks.\nMCP\nMCP (Model Context Protocol) is a standard way to give an agent tools. theia serve starts Theia's MCP server over standard input and output. Tools:\ncode map: codebase_blueprint, codebase_search, codebase_symbol;\nmemory: memory_search, memory_add, memory_tree;\nrun inspection: run_audit, run_diagnose;\ncontrol: run_list, run_status, run_events, run_wait, run_msg, run_answer, run_stop, run_start, run_probe, plan_list, plan_show, plan_create, plan_approve.\nCodex gets a smaller set without the control tools. The run_msg, run_answer and run_stop tools have no effect on freshly started runs, including runs from run_start: see control bus.\nWhere you see it: theia serve: MCP server on stdio (root …), mcp server registered: theia serve → …/.mcp.json (control tools included).\nSee also: control bus, hooks, /theia skill, /metrics, MCP server, theia serve.\n/theia skill\nThe /theia skill is an instruction for Claude Code on how to work through Theia: plan, human approval, start, watch, answer questions. theia hooks install claude puts it into.claude/skills/theia/SKILL.md. The skill itself runs nothing; it relies on Theia's MCP tools. It is not installed for Codex. In 0.2.201 the skill's steps with run_msg and run_stop have no effect on runs from run_start. Do not confuse this skill with the skills in vendor deliverables.\nWhere you see it: /theia skill installed → …/.claude/skills/theia/SKILL.md.\nSee also: MCP, hooks, control bus, Claude Code.\nstatusLine\nThe status line at the bottom of Claude Code. theia hooks install claude wires the theia index status --brief command into it if you do not have a status line yet. It shows index freshness and the number of open runs and waiting questions.\nWhere you see it: theia: 0 indexed · 1 missing · 1 live, statusline: existing statusLine preserved (not overwritten).\nSee also: stale, live, hooks, Claude Code.\ngate 1\nCodex's first condition: the project must be trusted ( project trust ). Until it is, Codex sees neither the hooks nor the MCP server from the.codex/ folder. The condition is met when you run codex once in this folder and accept the trust prompt, or run theia hooks install codex --trust.\nWhere you see it: project NOT trusted (gate 1) in the codex hooks line of theia doctor, gate 1 (project trust): NOT SATISFIED — ….\nSee also: gate 2, codex hooks, Codex.\ngate 2\nCodex's second condition: new and changed hooks must be reviewed and trusted ( hook trust ). Until then Codex skips them. Approve them with /hooks in an interactive Codex session in this folder.\nWhere you see it: gate 2 (hook trust): new/changed hooks are review-gated per hash and SKIPPED until trusted — in theia hooks install codex.\nSee also: gate 1, hooks, Codex.\n/metrics\nA Prometheus metrics page: theia serve --metrics <port>. It shows counters of the theia serve process only. The port listens on all network interfaces.\nWhere you see it: theia serve: /metrics on:<port>.\nSee also: MCP, Metrics and tracing.\nTUI screen TUI\nThe TUI (text user interface) is the full-screen mode of theia run and theia show --tui. It is the default view in a terminal. The screen shows the run tree, step details, live activity and key hints. Without a terminal, or with --non-interactive, Theia switches to --plain and says why. There is no key to detach and leave the run working.\nWhere you see it: view: --tui needs an interactive terminal (a TTY for output and keys) — degrading to --plain, view: --tui is interactive; --non-interactive degrades to --plain.\nSee also: run tree, home screen, board ( --cli ), --plain, Screens and elements, Leave without stopping a task.\nhome screen\nThe home screen is what theia with no command opens in a terminal when personal settings and a store already exist. It shows only the problem lines of theia doctor, the index freshness line, up to five latest runs with outcome and cost, and the task ▸ task box.\nWarning\nIn 0.2.201 starting a task from the task ▸ box does not work: the run fails with kernel: no vendored prompts … and stays open ( ⣽ … in flight ). Start tasks with theia run --task \"…\".\nWhere you see it: recent runs:, ✖ run2akdqctmhw222mff47z2qh abandoned $0.0000, task ▸ ▏, Enter run ·? help · q quit.\nSee also: setup wizard, in flight, ✔ ✖ ◆ ◌ ‼ », Screens and elements.\nrun tree\nThe run tree is the main part of the TUI. Tasks are rows like tsk…x [fast_path], with phase, rounds, cost and time on the right. Inside a task: its text, the copy workspace @ <sha> (copy), rounds like r1 implement implementer → ready, and under a round the model calls ( · gateway … ), tools and evidence. The top level also holds anomalies ‼, your steering » and service notes. Successful tasks collapse; problem tasks expand by themselves.\nSee also: ✔ ✖ ◆ ◌ ‼ », round, detail, keybar, Screens and elements.\nboard ( --cli )\nThe board ( --cli ) is a compact run view that redraws in place. The first line: run, money, calls and events. Then one line per task: track, phase, rounds and the last verdict. At the bottom, the state: running, halted: … or stopping — finishing in-flight verdicts…. The board needs a terminal; theia show <runId> --cli prints the last frame.\nWhere you see it: run …wvesbyig $0.0000/$10.00 · 4 calls · 22 events, tsk…xk6rzynf fast_path discarded r6 lead→lead_discard “…”, — halted: completed.\nSee also: --plain, TUI, summary.\n--plain\nThe plain view: one line per log event, no redrawing. Use it in scripts, CI and files. The lines show Theia's event names, not the model's words: verify_pass, not pass; arb_escalate, not escalate. live lines are only hints and are not written to the log.\nWhere you see it: theia run … --plain, theia show <runId> --plain, view: --cli needs a TTY — degrading to --plain.\nSee also: event log, verdict, live, board ( --cli ).\nsummary\nThe run summary: each task's outcome, the halted: line with the number of calls and the cost, and the log check. theia show <runId> with no flags and theia run at the end print it.\nWhere you see it: tsk2akdqb2ilc225pxk6rzynf → discarded (6 rounds) …, halted: completed · 4 gateway calls · $0.0000, audit chain: intact.\nSee also: halted, audit chain, --plain.\n✔ ✖ ◆ ◌ ‼ »\nTUI signs:\n✔ — good: a task outcome merged or planned, a round whose verdict is not on the bad list, a run that is completed;\n✖ — bad: a discarded task; a round with discard, merge_fail, verify_fail, cant_do, algedonic or no_progress; a stopped run ( stopped (…) );\n◆ — stuck: an escalated task or an unanswered question; on the home screen, a paused run;\n⣷ ⣯ ⣟ … (or ● with --no-spinner ) — work in progress, ◌ — waiting;\n‼ — an anomaly, » — your steering, · — task text or evidence, ▼ and ▶ — expanded and collapsed, ‖ — paused or stopping, ❯ — the cursor when colors are off.\nThe green check means \"not a bad event\", not \"a good outcome\". Rounds lead → lead_discard and arbiter → arb_escalate are marked ✔ too, and a run where every task was discarded shows ✔ completed.\nSee also: run tree, completed, frozen, failed, Screens and elements.\nrunning\nThe run or the task is working now. The TUI header says running, the task counter N running, the board — running. A run killed without a pause also looks running in theia show --tui until it is resumed or closed.\nSee also: stopping, (still open), in flight.\nfailed\nIn the TUI, a task with the discarded outcome: tasks: … 1 failed. A task has no separate failed outcome. (FAILED) in row details means an agent action failed.\nWhere you see it: tasks: 0 running · 0 done · 1 failed · anomalies 0.\nSee also: discarded, ✔ ✖ ◆ ◌ ‼ », FAIL.\nfrozen\nIn the TUI, a task in the escalated phase that waits for the lead: tasks: … 1 frozen, the ◆ sign.\nWhere you see it: tasks: 0 running · 0 done · 1 frozen · anomalies 6.\nSee also: escalated, ✔ ✖ ◆ ◌ ‼ ».\npending\nIn the TUI, a waiting task: its dependencies are not ready yet ( blocked on tsk… ), or the run has stopped while the task is unfinished. The sign is ◌.\nSee also: blocked on, ✔ ✖ ◆ ◌ ‼ ».\nblocked on\nThe task waits for other tasks: work starts when they end with merged or planned. The TUI shows it to the right of the task: blocked on tsk…x.\nSee also: pending, plan.\nidx ⚠N\nA hint in the TUI header: when the screen opened, N index entries were stale or missing. You can work, but agents see an incomplete blueprint.\nWhere you see it: tasks: 0 running · 0 done · 1 failed · anomalies 0 · idx ⚠1.\nSee also: stale, intent index.\nctx ⚠N\nA hint in the TUI header: N times the context added to requests was larger than the run.contextAlertChars threshold.\nSee also: kind: Context.\n[@ event k/N]\nThe log position in the TUI header: you are looking at event k of N. · live means the screen follows a running run; · head means the latest event. In theia show <runId> --tui the [ and ] keys move one event, { and } ten events, Home and End to the start and the end, and the tree is rebuilt for that moment. After a live run ends these keys only move the counter; to rewind, open theia show <runId> --tui.\nWhere you see it: [@ event 22/22 · head], [@ event 21/22].\nSee also: run ended, head, fork, Navigation and hotkeys.\nfollow\nFollow mode: the cursor moves to new activity by itself. The f key turns it on and off; F picks the level: task, round or tool.\nWhere you see it: f follow:round in the key bar.\nSee also: verbosity, keybar, Navigation and hotkeys.\nverbosity\nHow much detail the tree shows: 0 — only tasks and rounds (anomalies and steering always show), 1 — the normal view, 2 — also call and tool lines. The keys are + and -. Theia remembers the choice together with the follow level in tui-state.json.\nWhere you see it: +/- verbosity=1 in the key bar.\nSee also: follow, keybar.\nkeybar\nThe key hint line at the bottom of the TUI. It shows only the keys that work now: move — cursor, fold — collapse or expand, zoom — details (Enter), pane — switch pane (Tab), follow — follow mode, err — next error, seek — rewind, wrap — wrap the row, view — full text in $EDITOR or less, verbosity — detail level, steer — steering ( m ), prompt — the prompt of a running call ( p ), stop or quit — the q key.? opens the full help.\nWhere you see it: ↑↓ move • ←→ fold • ⏎ zoom • ⇥ pane • f follow • e err • [ ] seek • w wrap • v view • +/- verbosity=1 •? • q quit.\nSee also: follow, verbosity, [@ event k/N], detail, steering, Navigation and hotkeys.\ndetail\nThe detail pane under the tree, opened with Enter. It shows everything about the row under the cursor: the verdict with its comment and evidence, questions, call data, the task's phase history. For a verdict in a stored run it offers a ready fork command theia fork <run> --at <evt>. The Tab key moves control between the tree and the pane.\nWhere you see it: ┤ detail ├, ┤ tree · focus ├, phases: in task details.\nSee also: run tree, fork, keybar, Screens and elements.\nblob\nThe stored full text of one exchange with a model: the prompt and the reply of a call. Theia keeps it apart from the event log and leaves a blobRef reference in the call record. In the TUI the v key opens the text of the selected call and p the prompt of the call in flight; both write the text to a temporary file theia-blob-<id>.txt and never delete it. theia prune removes texts that nothing references any more ( orphaned blobs ).\nWhere you see it: blobRef: 62a278c5… in the detail pane, no stored body for this row, blob absent (recorded elsewhere), no prompt blob for this call (blob store absent or content blocked), 14 orphaned blobs (0.0 MB) in theia prune.\nSee also: gateway call, detail, in flight, Navigation and hotkeys.\nin flight\nWork is still going on:\non the home screen, ⣽ run… in flight — the run has no end or pause record; a killed run looks the same;\nin the TUI, ⋯ implementer tsk…x call in flight… · 12s [0 tools] — a model call is running right now. The p key opens its prompt, and no call in flight means there are no calls now.\nSee also: (still open), live, home screen.\nrun ended\nThe run is over, and the TUI stays open so you can look at the tree. The q key closes the screen, and then the summary prints to the normal terminal.\nWhere you see it: ■ run ended — 1 discarded · browse the tree; q quits.\nSee also: TUI, [@ event k/N], summary.\ntheia doctor doctor\ntheia doctor checks whether the machine is ready: harness logins, the sandbox, search, embeddings, personal settings, the store, rules, Codex hooks and vendor deliverables. No model is called. Each line reads name: state. The exit code is 1 when a counted FAIL is present. The home screen shows only the problem lines.\nSee also: unavailable, FAIL, not initialized, theia doctor.\nunavailable\nA capability is not available on this machine; the reason is in brackets. By itself it is not an error. For example, codex subscription: unavailable while Claude Code works is fine.\nSee also: doctor, FAIL, harnesses.\nFAIL\nA real problem. In theia doctor the harnesses, embeddings, vendor deliverables and shell (git-bash) (Windows only) lines with FAIL give exit code 1, while FAIL in the lexical index line does not change the exit code. In theia index build, a FAIL <file>: <reason> line and the N FAILED counter mean that a file's card could not be made.\nWhere you see it: harnesses: FAIL (neither codex nor claude is authenticated — one is required), index: 1/1 built · 1 FAILED · $0.0000 · 166.7/s.\nSee also: doctor, unavailable, failed.\nnot initialized\nIn theia doctor it means there is no.theia with store.db in the current folder. Doctor looks only at the current folder. From a project subfolder, or for a store in the user folder, it says not initialized (theia init), although other commands find the store. Run theia doctor from the project root.\nWhere you see it:.theia store: not initialized (theia init).\nSee also:.theia store, store, doctor.\nharnesses\nA theia doctor line that appears only when you are signed in to neither Codex nor Claude Code. Signing in to one of them is enough.\nWhere you see it: harnesses: FAIL (neither codex nor claude is authenticated — one is required).\nSee also: harness, codex subscription, claude code, Keys and access.\ncodex subscription\nA theia doctor line: whether you are signed in to the Codex CLI with a subscription ( codex login ). unavailable means there is no login or codex is not installed.\nWhere you see it: codex subscription: unavailable (harness-codex: not authenticated (codex login status: exit 1)).\nSee also: harness, harnesses, Keys and access.\nclaude code\nA theia doctor line: whether you are signed in to Claude Code with a subscription. Theia does not use an Anthropic API key for Claude Code, so a key-based login does not count here.\nWhere you see it: claude code: ok, claude code: unavailable (harness-claude: not logged in …).\nSee also: harness, harnesses, Keys and access.\nlexical index\nA theia doctor line: whether the built-in word search library (tantivy) loads. FAIL here means a broken installation, but it does not change the exit code.\nWhere you see it: lexical index: ok (tantivy binding loads).\nSee also: lane, doctor.\n.theia store\nA theia doctor line: whether there is a store in the current folder. Values: ok or not initialized (theia init).\nSee also: not initialized, store.\nai rules\nWhich AI rules files were found in the project and how they reach the model: (native·codex) — the harness reads the file itself, (injected) — Theia adds it through a Context manifest. none detected means there are no such files.\nWhere you see it: ai rules: none detected in theia doctor, ai rules: none detected after theia init.\nSee also: kind: Context, run.rulesSoT, Rules and prompts.\ncodex hooks\nA theia doctor line about Codex hooks: whether the hooks feature is on in Codex itself, whether the project is trusted ( gate 1 ) and whether.codex/hooks.json exists.\nWhere you see it: codex hooks: feature stable/true; project NOT trusted (gate 1); hooks.json absent (theia hooks install codex).\nSee also: gate 1, hooks, Codex.\nIds runId\nA run id: run plus 22 characters, for example run2akdqb2i4k223pwvesbyig. Other objects have their own prefixes: tsk — task, pln — plan, fct — fact, chk — memory chunk, gen — search generation. Ids grow over time, so their order matches creation order. Commands accept the full id or the 22 characters without the prefix.\nThere is no command that lists runs. You can see the id at the start of a run ( run run… task tsk… ), in the resume with: hint, on the home screen and in theia audit verify output.\nSee also: body, tsk…xk6rzynf, terryid.\nbody\nAn id without its prefix: 22 characters, for example 2akdqb2i4k223pwvesbyig. Theia prints ids in this form in theia audit verify, in fork lineage lines and in the hint after the first Ctrl+C. Commands accept this form too.\nWhere you see it: 2akdqb2i4k223pwvesbyig: intact, forked from 2akdqb2i4k223pwvesbyig at bjr37ync.\nSee also: runId, tsk…xk6rzynf.\ntsk…xk6rzynf\nA short number is the last characters of an id, for the screen: 8 characters in --plain, on the board and in the TUI ( xk6rzynf, tsk…xk6rzynf ), and 6 characters in live lines ( tsk…6rzynf ). Only show --task, msg --task, fork --at and answer --question accept a short tail.\nSee also: runId, body.\nterryid\nThe name of Theia's id format. The word appears in errors when a string cannot be read as an id. There are two texts:\nterryid: neither a body nor a full id: '…' — the argument is shorter than a full id and is not the 22 characters after the prefix. show, diagnose, msg, replay, fork and mem asof answer this to a short tail like ovfyt3 and to a date given instead of an id.\nterryid: malformed id '…' — the string has the length of an id but is not one. In 0.2.201 theia run --from-plan answers this when the word after the flag is not a plan id.\nA well-formed id that belongs to nothing gives a different error: <command>: no events for <id>.\nWhere you see it: terryid: neither a body nor a full id: 'ovfyt3', terryid: malformed id '--plain'.\nSee also: runId, asof.\ndigest\nA digest is the SHA-256 hash of some content. The same content gives the same digest; any change gives another one. Theia uses digests for a plan ( digest fce3554ee5e2… ), an included file ( sha256 bc70e26f40b8 ), check command output ( test_run 74d0… ) and vendor deliverables.\nSee also: plan, @path, test_run, audit chain.\nInternal codes THE-…\nNumbers of Theia's internal development rules in brackets at the end of error messages. The meaning is in the text before the brackets; the number itself can be ignored. The ones you meet most often:\nTHE-005 — the run's audit chain is broken: resume, fork and probe refuse to work with such a run ( … chain BROKEN at <event> — refusing to … a tampered run (THE-005) ), and theia audit verify prints BROKEN.\nTHE-013 — Claude Code is set up with an API key, while Theia accepts only a subscription login ( API-key fallback is refused — THE-013 ).\nTHE-014 — a secret reached a model prompt: a file included with @path in a task or plan text ( @key.pem: prohibited content (pem_private_key) — this file cannot enter a model prompt (THE-014) ) or the prompt text itself ( gateway: prohibited content (api_key) — call blocked (THE-014) ).\nTHE-024 — the money budget of a run or of ask is used up ( usd budget exhausted for this run (THE-024) ).\nTHE-065 — the shipped files with the role prompts are missing or damaged ( no vendor deliverables found … (THE-065), vendor prompts broken … (THE-065) ).\nSee also: R5, T-W, audit chain, @path, budget_exhausted, vendor deliverables.\nR5, T-W\nNumbers of internal tickets and design document sections in help text and messages: (R5), (R11), — T-W, (F6), docs/10 §4, docs/13. They are references for platform developers, and you can ignore them. Treat the word receipt in help text the same way.\nSee also: THE-….\nWords with several meanings\nOne word in Theia's output can mean different things. The place where it stands tells which. Each such word has its own article:\nnote — the service note task.note, your steering user.note, a role's comment, a plan request, the noted — … reply.\nlive — progress lines in --plain, · live in the TUI header, (live; …) on resume, N live in the status line.\nsession — a harness session, the --sessions mode, the SessionStart hook, conversation chunk labels.\nverify — the phase, the --verify flag, a plan item's check command, lead_reverify.\ndraft — the phase of a new task and the status of an unapproved plan.\ndone — the N done counter in the TUI and the unreachable done phase.\nbudget — the run's money, halted: budget (verdicts, not money), round_budget, rescope_budget, separate limits for fork, probe, ask, index build.\nworkspace — the task copy ws-<task>, the kind: Workspace manifest, the project folder, Codex's workspace-write mode.\nroot — a project root, root:<name> in memory, worktree root, root cause.\nscope — a memory part, a settings level, a chain name in audit verify.\nhead — the age of a search generation, the last event in the TUI, the HEAD commit.\nplan — a saved plan and the plan phase of the full_spec track.\nready — one event for three moves: into implement for a new task, into implement after the planner, into verify after the implementer. docker ready in theia doctor (the docker command exists) and ready at … for embeddings (the model is downloaded) are unrelated uses of the same word.\nmerge and merged — the merge phase and a task outcome; discard and discarded — the reviewer's verdict and a task outcome.\nFAIL and failed — a problem in theia doctor and index build, the counter of discarded tasks in the TUI, (FAILED) on an agent action.\nMisleading statuses\nWords that look like a judgement but are not one. Check what stands next to them.\ncompleted does not mean success: halted: completed is printed even when every task was discarded, and theia run then exits with code 1. Check each task's outcome: merged or discarded.\n✔ means \"not a bad event\", not \"a good outcome\". The rounds lead → lead_discard and arbiter → arb_escalate are marked ✔ too, and a run with no merge at all shows as ✔ completed.\nhalted: budget is not money but the cap of 1000 verdicts. When the money runs out, the outcome is halted: paused.\nrunning, in flight, N live — a run killed without a pause looks like this too: it simply has no end record. Continue it ( --resume ) or close it ( --abandon ).\nnoted — this run appears live … from theia msg does not mean the note will arrive: a new run does not read notes from other processes, only a run continued with --resume does.\nresuming run … (finished; …) — --resume continues even a closed run ( halted: abandoned ). The word in brackets is the state in the log, not a permission.\n<name>: intact from theia audit verify for a scope that does not exist — the chain is intact because there is nothing to check.\nsuperseded <old> -> <new> from theia mem supersede — the replacement is recorded even when the new id does not exist.\ndocker ready in theia doctor — the docker command exists, not that Docker is running. See jail.\n(injected) in the ai rules line — the rules file was found on disk, not that it will enter a prompt: theia doctor does not read Context entries. Trust the context injected: … line at the start of a run.\nbudget=$NaN and budget remaining: NaN — the --budget flag got something that is not a number. No limit applies, but Theia does not call it unmetered either.\nSee also\nWhat Theia is\nAll commands\nKnown limitations\nTroubleshooting","keywords":[],"tags":[]}}